window.COURSE = {"course":"IE408 — Project Management","student":"","currentTrack":"2026","trackNames":{"2026":"This semester · 2026/27 · Dr. B. Duraković","2025":"Archive · 2025/26 (previous semester, different instructor)"},"brandcfg":{"key":"ie408.v1","brand":"Critical Path","code":"IE408","sub":"IE408 · Project Management<br>study traveler","eyebrow":"Schedule baseline · 2026/27 Fall · Dr. B. Duraković","hero":"IE408 Study Traveler","tagline":"Every chapter is an activity. Find the critical path through them.","testHint":"Midterm (40%, week 8) covers Meredith & Mantel ch. 1–5 and 8 — and the archive barely touches ch. 1–5. Get the textbook. For a 4.00 you need 95 overall, so with perfect homework the two exams must still average 94.4%."},"deadlines":[{"date":"2026-11-16","label":"Mid-term exam (40%) — week 8 · Meredith & Mantel ch. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8 · DATE NOT PUBLISHED, estimate only","pts":40},{"date":"2027-01-18","label":"Final exam (50%) — DATE NOT PUBLISHED, estimate only · scope unconfirmed, assume cumulative","pts":50}],"chapters":[{"id":"00","title":"Course Map, Grading & the 4.00 Arithmetic","slides":"2026/27 · official syllabus","md":"# IE408 — Project Management (Course Map) · 2026/27 Fall\n\n**Source:** the official syllabus at `ecampus.ius.edu.ba/syllabus/ie408-project-management`, read 7 October 2026.\nThis supersedes everything in `study/archive-2025/`, which came from a different semester and, as it turns\nout, a differently ordered course.\n\n## The essentials\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Lecturer** | Assoc. Prof. Dr. **Benjamin Duraković** — bdurakovic@ius.edu.ba |\n| **Assistant** | Muhamed Bisic — mbisic@ius.edu.ba |\n| **Office** | A F1.15 — Tue 15:00–17:00, Wed 14:00–15:00, Fri 15:00–17:00 |\n| **Credits** | 6 ECTS · 2 teaching + 2 practice hours weekly, face to face |\n| **Prerequisite** | Junior standing, 108+ ECTS |\n| **Email rule** | Institutional email with **`[IE408]` in the subject line**. MS Teams chat will not be answered. |\n\n## Grading — and what a 4.00 actually requires\n\n| Component | Weight | Count | AI allowed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Final exam** | **50%** | 1 | No |\n| **Mid-term exam** | **40%** | 1 | No |\n| **Homework** | **10%** | 5 | No |\n\n> ### 90% of this course is two closed-book exams.\n>\n> Homework is 10% across five assignments, so roughly **2% each**. There is **no project, no presentation\n> and no quiz**. That is a far less forgiving structure than ENS208 or CS313: there is almost no coursework\n> cushion, and a bad exam cannot be repaired elsewhere.\n\n**IUS grade scale** — note where the A boundary sits:\n\n| Score | Letter | Coefficient |\n|---|---|---|\n| **95–100** | **A** | **4.0** |\n| 85–94 | A− | 3.7 |\n| 80–84 | B+ | 3.3 |\n| 75–79 | B | 3.0 |\n| 70–74 | B− | 2.7 |\n| 65–69 | C+ | 2.3 |\n| 55–64 | C | 2.0 |\n| 45–54 | E | 1.0 |\n| 0–44 | F | 0.0 |\n\nFor a 4.00 you need **95+**, not 90+, and the homework is too small to carry you there:\n\n| Homework result | Exams must then average |\n|---|---|\n| All five perfect (10/10) | **94.4%** |\n| One homework lost (8/10) | **96.7%** |\n| Two homeworks lost (6/10) | **98.9%** |\n| No homework at all | over 100% — **an A becomes impossible** |\n\nEach homework is worth about 2%, so skipping one costs more than it looks: it does not just remove 2 points,\nit raises the required exam average by **2.2 points**. Treat all five as compulsory.\n\n**AI is not permitted on any component.** All three say \"No\".\n\n## Weekly schedule\n\n| Week | Topic | Reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | General introduction; project vs. operations | Syllabus + Ch. 1 |\n| 2 | Project initiation; charter and plan development | Ch. 2 |\n| 3 | Project environment; organisation and stakeholders | Ch. 3 |\n| 4 | Project planning, selection and quality | Ch. 4 |\n| 5 | Scope; **work breakdown structure (WBS)** | Ch. 5 |\n| 6–7 | Schedule; network techniques, **PERT and CPM** | Ch. 8 |\n| **8** | **MID-TERM EXAM — chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8** | — |\n| 9 | Critical path method | |\n| 10 | Project scheduling; Gantt charts | |\n| 11 | Schedule uncertainty | |\n| 12 | Budget; resource allocation and time-phased budgets | |\n| 13 | Project acceleration; cost–time trade-offs (crashing) | |\n| 14 | Project audit and risk management | |\n| 15 | Topic review | |\n\n**Dates are not published.** The syllabus gives week numbers only. No calendar date appears for the\nmidterm or the final, and the five homeworks have no stated topics or due dates.\n\n## Textbooks and software\n\n- **Required:** Meredith & Mantel Jr., *Project Management: A Managerial Approach*, **6th edition**\n  (Wiley, ISBN 978-0471-715375).\n- Also listed: **PMBOK 4th edition**, **ProjectLibre** software, class notes.\n\n> ### ⚠️ The copy in this folder is the 7th edition, and the chapter numbers differ\n>\n> `Meredith-Mantel-Project-Management-A-Managerial-Approach-7e.pdf` is the **7th** edition. It permutes\n> chapters 4, 5 and 6 relative to the 6th that the syllabus cites:\n>\n> | 6th ed. (the syllabus) | 7th ed. (this PDF) | On the midterm? |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | 4 Project Organization | **5** The Project in the Organizational Structure | yes |\n> | 5 Project Planning (the WBS) | **6** Project Activity Planning | yes |\n> | 6 Conflict and Negotiation | **4** Negotiation and the Management of Conflict | **no** |\n>\n> Chapters 1, 2, 3 and 8 are the same in both. The digests in `study/book/` are numbered the\n> **6th-edition** way, so `B04` is project organization and `B05` is project planning, matching the\n> syllabus rather than the PDF. **Ask Dr. Duraković to confirm the scope**, because the difference decides\n> whether conflict and negotiation is examined.\n\n## The textbook digests\n\nAll six midterm chapters are digested in `study/book/`, with 166 flashcards between them:\n\n| File | 6e ch. | Topic | Cards |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `B01` | 1 | Projects in contemporary organizations | 18 |\n| `B02` ★ | 2 | Strategic management and **project selection** — payback, NPV, IRR, scoring models | 30 |\n| `B03` | 3 | The project manager | 20 |\n| `B04` | 4 | Project organization — functional, pure project, matrix | 22 |\n| `B05` | 5 | Project planning and the **WBS** | 28 |\n| `B06` | 6 | Conflict and negotiation — **not on the midterm** | 18 |\n| `B08` ★ | 8 | Scheduling: PERT and CPM | 30 |\n\n★ = contains calculations. Those two chapters are where the midterm's numeric questions will come from.\n\n> **ProjectLibre is examinable.** Learning outcomes 2 and 5 name it explicitly — \"demonstrate use of Gantt\n> charts, critical path method, **ProjectLibre**\" and \"use project management software\". It is free and\n> open source. Install it early and build the chapter-A05 network in it; a tool you first open in week 10\n> is a tool you will fight.\n\n## Learning outcomes\n\n1. Explain project fundamentals and management processes.\n2. Demonstrate use of Gantt charts, critical path method, ProjectLibre.\n3. Critically review project plans, emphasising risk identification.\n4. Apply course concepts to real-life projects.\n5. Use project management software.\n\nOutcome 3 is mapped to the homework, not the exams. Outcomes 1 and 2 are what the **midterm** tests; the\n**final** is mapped to all five.\n\n## Workload\n\n150 hours total: 30 lectures, 9 tutorials, 10 homework, 45 home study, **24 midterm revision, 32 final\nrevision**. The university's own model therefore budgets **56 hours of exam revision**, more than a third\nof the course. Plan for it rather than discovering it in week 7.\n\n## Policies\n\n- **Academic integrity** — \"All work submitted must be your own. Plagiarism, cheating, or any form of\n  academic dishonesty will result in disciplinary action.\"\n- **Attendance** — per IUS Study Rules and Regulations; excessive absence can affect assessment eligibility.\n- **Technology** — laptops for note-taking only, phones silenced, recording needs permission.\n\n## How the archive relates to this course\n\nThe 2025/26 study guide in `study/archive-2025/` covers **the same techniques in a different order**, under\na different instructor. It is genuinely useful for the second half of the course and genuinely thin for the\nfirst half — which is the half the 40% midterm examines.\n\n**Read `archive-vs-2026.md` before using the archive for anything.** It maps every archive chapter onto this\nyear's weeks and lists what the archive does not cover at all.\n\n## Open questions for the first lecture\n\n- [ ] Midterm and final **dates**.\n- [ ] The five **homework topics and due dates**, and whether they require ProjectLibre.\n- [ ] Whether the **final is cumulative** or covers only post-midterm material.\n- [ ] Whether the **pencil-only rule** and supplied **Z-tables** from the 2025/26 archive still apply —\n      these came from a different instructor and must not be assumed.\n- [ ] Whether **PMBOK 4th ed.** is genuinely needed or is a nominal listing.\n","lesson":"","cards":[],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 00"},{"id":"cmp","title":"Archive vs This Year — What Carries Over","slides":"2026/27 · week-by-week mapping","md":"# The 2025/26 archive versus this year's course — what carries over and what does not\n\n_Written 7 October 2026, after the official 2026/27 syllabus was read. Read this before using anything in\n`study/archive-2025/`._\n\n> ### Update, 7 October 2026 — the textbook arrived and the gap is closed\n>\n> All six midterm chapters are now digested in `study/book/` (166 cards). This file still describes what the\n> **archive** does and does not cover, which remains true and useful, but \"get the textbook\" is done.\n>\n> It also turned out that the **6th and 7th editions permute chapters 4, 5 and 6**, so the midterm's\n> \"chapters 1–5 and 8\" is not what the 7th-edition PDF's chapter numbers suggest. `exam-prep.md` has the\n> mapping. Conflict and negotiation is **not** in the stated scope.\n\n## The headline\n\n> **The archive covers the final well and the midterm badly.**\n\nThe 2025/26 guide is strong on PERT, CPM, Gantt, budgeting, crashing and Z-scores — all of which this year\nfall in **weeks 9 to 14, after the midterm**. The 2026/27 midterm examines **chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8**,\nwhich is weeks 1 to 7: initiation, project environment, selection, scope and WBS. The archive barely\ntouches four of those six chapters.\n\n**The midterm is 40% of the course.** So the part of the syllabus the archive helps least with is the part\ncarrying the second-largest weight, and it arrives first.\n\n## Three differences that matter\n\n| | 2025/26 archive | 2026/27 actual |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Instructor** | unknown, not named in the guide | **Benjamin Duraković** |\n| **Assessment** | guide implies quizzes; never states weights | **Final 50%, midterm 40%, 5 homeworks 10%. No quiz, no project.** |\n| **Topic order** | techniques front-loaded from week 2 | managerial foundations first, techniques from week 6 |\n| **Software** | not mentioned at all | **ProjectLibre**, named in two learning outcomes |\n| **Textbook** | not mentioned | **Meredith & Mantel, 6th ed.** + PMBOK 4th ed. |\n\n⚠️ **The instructor is different, so every \"the instructor says\" note in the archive is unsourced for this\nyear.** That specifically includes the pencils-only rule, the supplied Z-tables, the multiple-choice\nanswer-key warning and the claim that the final focuses on weeks 8–15. Do not plan around any of them until\nDr. Duraković confirms them.\n\n## Week-by-week mapping\n\n| 2026/27 week | Topic | Archive chapter | Coverage |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | General introduction; project vs. operations | A01 (part) | **partial** — the five process phases and the iron triangle are there; \"project vs. operations\" is not |\n| 2 | Project initiation; charter and plan development | — | **none** |\n| 3 | Project environment; organisation and stakeholders | — | **none** |\n| 4 | Project planning, **selection** and quality | A02 (quality only) | **partial** — quality and the eight dimensions are covered; **project selection methods are not** |\n| 5 | Scope; **work breakdown structure** | A01 (scope), A07 (WBS in passing) | **partial** — WBS appears only as one box in the cost-baseline chain |\n| 6–7 | Schedule; network techniques, PERT and CPM | **A03, A04, A05** | **full and strong** |\n| **8** | **MIDTERM — ch. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8** | | |\n| 9 | Critical path method | **A05** | **full** — the verified worked network |\n| 10 | Project scheduling; Gantt charts | **A06** | **full** |\n| 11 | Schedule uncertainty | **A10** | **full** — σ_p and Z-scores |\n| 12 | Budget; resource allocation, time-phased budgets | **A07** | **full** — the verified 216,000 KM table |\n| 13 | Project acceleration; cost–time trade-offs | **A09** | **full** — crashing and the least-cost method |\n| 14 | Project **audit** and risk management | A08 (risk only) | **partial** — risk is well covered; **project audit is not covered at all** |\n| 15 | Topic review | A11 | partial — the synthesis diagram carries over; the exam-rule section does not |\n\n## What the archive does not cover\n\nThese are this year's topics with no archive material behind them. All but one sit **before the midterm**.\n\n1. **Project initiation, the project charter, plan development** — week 2, midterm chapter 2.\n2. **Project environment, organisational structures, stakeholder management** — week 3, midterm chapter 3.\n3. **Project selection** — week 4, midterm chapter 4. Meredith & Mantel treats selection with numeric and\n   scoring models, which is calculable material the archive has nothing on.\n4. **Work breakdown structure in depth** — week 5, midterm chapter 5.\n5. **Project audit** — week 14.\n6. **ProjectLibre** — throughout, and named in the learning outcomes.\n\n**Items 1 to 4 are the midterm**, and all four are now covered by `study/book/B01`–`B05`. What remains\nuncovered anywhere is **project audit** (week 14) and **ProjectLibre**.\n\n## How to use the two tracks\n\n**Before the midterm (weeks 1–7).** Work from `study/book/B01`–`B05`, not from the archive. The one archive\nchapter worth reading early is **A02 on quality**, which maps to week 4. Then from week 6 the archive\nbecomes genuinely valuable: **A03, A04 and A05** cover PERT, network diagramming and CPM, which are\nchapter 8 and the calculable half of the midterm, and `book/B08` gives the same material in the textbook's\nown notation plus the near-critical-path result the archive lacks.\n\n**After the midterm (weeks 9–14).** The archive is close to a complete course. A05, A06, A10, A07 and A09\nline up almost exactly with weeks 9, 10, 11, 12 and 13, and every worked example in them has been\nrecomputed and verified. Add project audit for week 14 from the textbook.\n\n**Throughout.** Build the archive's worked network in ProjectLibre. One file gives you the Gantt chart, the\ncritical path and the resource histogram, and it doubles as software practice for outcomes 2 and 5.\n\n## The one thing to carry across unchanged\n\nThe verified worked network in `archive-2025/05-critical-path-method.md` is the single most reusable asset\nin either track. Eight activities, a 15-month critical path, a 216,000 KM budget, a 3.11-month² variance\nand a full crashing table — all cross-checked. It exercises weeks 6, 7, 9, 10, 11, 12 and 13 of this year's\nsyllabus from one dataset. Learn it cold.\n\n## Open items\n\n- [ ] Confirm the exam logistics with Dr. Duraković — pencils, calculator, whether Z-tables are supplied.\n- [ ] Confirm whether the final is cumulative.\n- [ ] Get the five homework topics and due dates.\n- [x] ~~Obtain Meredith & Mantel and ingest chapters 1–5~~ — done 7 Oct 2026; digests in `study/book/`.\n- [ ] **Confirm the midterm chapter numbers follow the 6th edition**, since the 7th renumbers 4, 5 and 6.\n- [ ] Install ProjectLibre.\n","lesson":"","cards":[],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 cmp"},{"id":"fe","title":"★ Financial Evaluation (tutorial 05.11) — MIDTERM","slides":"2026/27 · payback, ARR, NPV, NPV with inflation","md":"# Financial Evaluation of Projects — Tutorial 05.11.2026 ★ MIDTERM\n\n_The tutorial of 05.11.2026, from your handwritten notes (5 photos → `tutorials/2026-11-05 financial evaluation/notes-p1…p5.jpeg`). **The tutor said this part will be on the midterm** (midterm 40%, week 8). **NPV will be on the midterm, but practise the first three models first: Payback (PB), Average Rate of Return (ARR) and NPV.** NPV with inflation comes after those. Every number in the notes was recomputed: the notes are right, apart from one small multiplication slip (below), and one page leaves a column blank, which is completed here. Book background: `book/B02` (M&S 6e ch. 2, \"Project selection\")._\n\n---\n\n## 1. The four models and their formulas (notes p. 1)\n\n| # | Model | Formula | Decision rule |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Payback period (PB)** | PB = a₀ / annual cash inflow | shorter is better (compare with the firm's cutoff) |\n| 2 | **Average rate of return (ARR)** | ARR = average annual profit / average project investment | higher is better (compare with the required rate) |\n| 3 | **Net present value (NPV)** — \"→ Exam\" | NPV = a₀ + Σ_{t=1}^{n} CF_t / (1 + r)ᵗ | **accept if NPV > 0**; among projects, the higher NPV wins |\n| 4 | **NPV with inflation** | CF′_t = CF_t · (1 + i)ᵗ, then the NPV formula with CF′_t | as for NPV |\n\nSymbols: **a₀ = initial investment, entered as a negative number (a cost)** · CF_t = cash flow in year t · t = year · r = discount rate · i = inflation rate.\n\n**PV factor** = 1 / (1 + r)ᵗ. At r = 10% (rounded to 3 decimals, as the tutor does): **0.909, 0.826, 0.751, 0.683** for years 1–4.\n\n**Why the tutorial starts with \"App A = 19, B = 19\" (notes p. 1).** Both applications got the **same score, 19, from the weighted and unweighted scoring models** (`book/B02`, scoring models). The scoring models could not separate them, so **the financial models are applied to break the tie**. That is the story behind the whole example: two projects tied on scoring, then ranked by PB, ARR and NPV.\n\n**Study priority (tutor):** ① Payback ② ARR ③ **NPV (certain to be on the midterm)** ④ NPV with inflation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The tutorial's worked example, checked\n\n**Data.** App(lication) A: initial cost 80,000, annual cash 20,000. App B: initial cost 60,000, annual cash 15,000. Both last 4 years, r = 10%.\n\n### Model 1 — Payback\n- PB_A = 80,000 / 20,000 = **4 years**\n- PB_B = 60,000 / 15,000 = **4 years**\n\nPayback cannot separate them.\n\n### Model 2 — Average rate of return\nAverage annual profit A = 18,000 and B = 15,000 (given); average investment taken as the initial cost.\n- RR_A = 18,000 / 80,000 = **0.225 = 22.5%**\n- RR_B = 15,000 / 60,000 = **0.25 = 25%**, so B is better.\n\n⚠ **Two conventions for the denominator.** The tutor divided by the **initial** investment. The textbook (M&S) allows \"the initial **or** average investment\", and \"average investment\" is often (initial + salvage)/2, which is half the cost when salvage is 0. With that convention, RR_A would be 45%. **Use the tutor's convention on the midterm, and state it in one line.** Also note that the profit (18,000) can differ from the cash flow (20,000), for example after depreciation. Use the profit for ARR and the cash flow for payback and NPV.\n\n### Model 3 — NPV at 10%\n| Year | CF(A) | PV factor | PV |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 20,000 | 0.909 | 18,180 |\n| 2 | 20,000 | 0.826 | 16,520 |\n| 3 | 20,000 | 0.751 | 15,020 |\n| 4 | 20,000 | 0.683 | 13,660 |\n| | | **Σ** | **63,380** |\n\n- **NPV_A = −80,000 + 63,380 = −16,620** ✓\n- **NPV_B = −60,000 + 15,000 × (0.909 + 0.826 + 0.751 + 0.683) = −60,000 + 47,535 = −12,465** ✓ (B's PVs: 13,635 · 12,390 · 11,265 · 10,245)\n\nThe notes say \"Project B chosen, less money lost\". ⚠ Strictly, **both NPVs are negative, so by the NPV rule both should be rejected**. B is only the less bad choice if one of them must be done. Write both sentences on the exam.\n\n(With exact factors instead of 3 decimals, NPV_A = −16,603 and NPV_B = −12,452. The rounding is fine; just use the factors the question gives you.)\n\n### Model 4 — NPV with 5% inflation (the cash flows grow, the discount rate stays 10%)\nCF′_t = CF_t · (1.05)ᵗ.\n\n| Year | App A: CF′ | PV @10% | App B: CF′ (completed here) | PV @10% |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 21,000 | 19,090 | 15,750 | 14,317 |\n| 2 | 22,050 | 18,210 | 16,537.5 | 13,660 |\n| 3 | 23,152.5 | 17,390 | 17,364.4 | 13,041 |\n| 4 | 24,310.1 | 16,600 | 18,232.6 | 12,453 |\n| **Σ** | | **71,290** | | **53,471** |\n\n- **NPV_A = −80,000 + 71,290 = −8,710** ✓ (the notes round each PV to tens; −8,706 unrounded)\n- **NPV_B = −60,000 + 53,471 = −6,529.** The notes stop at the CF′ column (with a crossed-out first entry); this completes it.\n\nThe ranking does not change: B is still less negative.\n\n### App C — a positive-NPV project (notes p. 4)\nInvestment 100,000; CF = 25,000 · 35,000 · 40,000 · 45,000; r = 10%.\n- PVs: 22,725 · 28,910 · 30,040 · 30,735, so Σ = 112,410 and **NPV_C = −100,000 + 112,410 = +12,410** ✓. **Accept.**\n- **RR_C = average cash flow / investment = (145,000 / 4) / 100,000 = 36,250 / 100,000 = 36.25%** ✓. Here the cash flow is used as the profit.\n- Payback (not in the notes; uneven flows, so add them up year by year): 25,000 → 60,000 → **100,000 at the end of year 3**, so **PB_C = 3 years**.\n\n### App C with inflation — the nominal-rate method (notes p. 5)\nHere the 10% is a **real** rate and inflation is 5%, so the rate itself is converted:\n\n**r_nominal = (1 + r_real)(1 + i) − 1 = 1.10 × 1.05 − 1 = 0.155 = 15.5%**\n\n| Year | Nominal CF = CF·1.05ᵗ | PV factor @15.5% | PV |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 26,250 | 0.866 | 22,733 (the notes write 22,707: a small slip, since 26,250 × 0.866 = 22,733) |\n| 2 | 38,587.5 | 0.750 | 28,941 |\n| 3 | 46,305 | 0.649 | 30,052 |\n| 4 | 54,697.8 | 0.562 | 30,740 |\n\nΣ PV = 112,466, so **NPV ≈ +12,466** with these 3-decimal factors. The exact value is **+12,441**, which is the notes' final answer, so the notes used more precise factors.\n\n**Key insight: the NPV is the same as without inflation.** 12,441 is App C's exact real NPV, and 12,410 is the same number with 3-decimal factors. Inflating the cash flows **and** the discount rate cancels out, because 25,000 × 1.05 / 1.155 = 25,000 / 1.10.\n\n---\n\n## 3. ⚠ Three ways to handle inflation — know which one the question wants\n\n| Method | When the question says… | What you do | Effect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Tutor model 4**: inflate the cash flows | \"cash flows will grow with inflation of i\", discount rate r given (a market/nominal rate) | CF′_t = CF_t(1 + i)ᵗ, discount at r | NPV goes **up** (A: −16,620 → −8,710) |\n| **Tutor App C**: nominal rate | \"real rate r, inflation i\" | r_nom = (1 + r)(1 + i) − 1, discount the inflated CF′ at r_nom | NPV **unchanged** (C: 12,441 both ways) |\n| **Textbook M&S**: inflation in the rate | \"required rate k, inflation p\" (book example: k = 15%, p = 3%) | NPV = −A₀ + Σ F_t / (1 + k + p)ᵗ | NPV goes **down** (book: 18% rate gives +$1,939) |\n\n**For the midterm: follow the tutor**, since the tutor says this is on the exam and the formula given was CF′ = CF(1 + i)ᵗ. If the question gives a *real* rate, use the nominal-rate conversion, as in App C. Write the formula you are using as the first line of your answer; then the grader can follow you even if they expected another convention. (k + p is the book's shortcut for (1 + k)(1 + p) − 1; for 10% and 5% that is 15% vs 15.5%.)\n\n---\n\n## 4. Exam answer layout (the table the tutor uses)\n\n1. Write the formula, with a₀ negative.\n2. Write the PV factors 1/(1 + r)ᵗ to 3 decimals, unless they are given.\n3. Draw the table: **Year | CF | PV factor | PV**. Add an inflation column CF′ if needed.\n4. Sum the PV column, then NPV = a₀ + Σ PV.\n5. **State the decision**: accept/reject (NPV > 0?), and which project is better, with one reason.\n6. If payback and NPV disagree, say that NPV wins, because payback ignores the time value of money and all cash after payback.\n\n---\n\n**▶ Printable practice set with step-by-step solutions:** `study/practice/IE408 Financial Evaluation - Practice Set.pdf` (13 problems, 5 of them the textbook's own end-of-chapter problems; rebuild with `make_fe_practice.py`).\n\n## 5. Practice problems (answers below; computed with 3-decimal factors, exact values in brackets)\n\nDo P1, P2, P5 and P6 first: they cover PB, ARR and NPV, the tutor's priority. P3 and P4 are about inflation.\n\n**P1.** A project costs 120,000 and returns 30,000 a year for 6 years. The average annual profit is 22,000, and r = 12%. Find PB, ARR and NPV. Accept?\n\n**P2.** Cost 50,000; cash flows 10,000, 15,000, 20,000, 25,000 in years 1–4. Find the payback period.\n\n**P3.** Cost 40,000; CF = 12,000 a year for 4 years; r = 8%. (a) NPV. (b) NPV if the cash flows grow with 4% inflation (the tutor's method). Does the decision change?\n\n**P4.** The real rate is 6% and inflation is 3%. What nominal rate do you use?\n\n**P6 (the tutorial's situation).** Projects K and L both scored the same in the weighted and unweighted scoring models. K costs 90,000 and returns 30,000 a year for 5 years, with an average annual profit of 20,000. L costs 75,000 and returns 20,000 · 25,000 · 25,000 · 30,000 · 30,000, with an average annual profit of 16,000. r = 12%. Break the tie with PB, ARR and NPV.\n\n**P5.** Two projects each cost 50,000, with r = 10%. X returns 25,000 · 20,000 · 10,000 · 5,000; Y returns 10,000 · 15,000 · 25,000 · 30,000. Compare payback and NPV. Which one do you choose?\n\n### Answers\n**P1.** PB = 120,000 / 30,000 = **4 years**. ARR = 22,000 / 120,000 = **18.33%**. PV factors at 12%: 0.893, 0.797, 0.712, 0.636, 0.567, 0.507, with sum 4.112, so ΣPV = 123,360 and **NPV = +3,360** (exact +3,342). **Accept.**\n\n**P2.** Cumulative: 10,000 → 25,000 → 45,000 → 70,000. After 3 years, 5,000 is still missing out of year 4's 25,000, so **PB = 3 + 5,000/25,000 = 3.2 years**.\n\n**P3.** (a) Factors at 8%: 0.926, 0.857, 0.794, 0.735, so ΣPV = 39,744 and **NPV = −256** (exact −254.5). Reject. (b) CF′ = 12,480 · 12,979.2 · 13,498.4 · 14,038.3, so ΣPV = 43,716 and **NPV = +3,716**. Accept. **Yes, the decision flips.** This is why the inflation assumption matters.\n\n**P4.** (1.06)(1.03) − 1 = **9.18%** (the book's shortcut k + p gives 9%).\n\n**P5.**\n- **Payback:** X pays back in 25 + 20 = 45, then 5 of year 3's 10, so **PB_X = 2.5 years**. Y pays back in 10 + 15 + 25 = **exactly 3 years**.\n- **NPV:** NPV_X = 22,725 + 16,520 + 7,510 + 3,415 − 50,000 = **+170**. NPV_Y = 9,090 + 12,390 + 18,775 + 20,490 − 50,000 = **+10,745**.\n- **Choose Y.** X pays back sooner, but NPV accounts for every cash flow and its timing. Y is worth about 10,600 more today.\n\n**P6.**\n- **PB:** PB_K = 90,000 / 30,000 = **3 years**. L accumulates 20 → 45 → 70 thousand, then needs 5,000 of year 4's 30,000, so **PB_L = 3 + 5/30 = 3.17 years**. K is better.\n- **ARR:** ARR_K = 20,000 / 90,000 = **22.2%**; ARR_L = 16,000 / 75,000 = **21.3%**. K is better.\n- **NPV:** the factors at 12% are 0.893, 0.797, 0.712, 0.636, 0.567.\n  - NPV_K = 30,000 × 3.605 − 90,000 = 108,150 − 90,000 = **+18,150**.\n  - NPV_L = 17,860 + 19,925 + 17,800 + 19,080 + 17,010 − 75,000 = 91,675 − 75,000 = **+16,675**.\n  - Both are acceptable; K is higher.\n- **Decision: K.** It wins on all three models, so the tie from the scoring models is broken in K's favour.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Weak spots\n- Forgetting the **minus sign** on a₀, or adding the investment as a positive number.\n- Applying the PV factor of year t to the wrong row (year 1 gets 0.909, not 1).\n- Calling a negative-NPV project \"chosen\" without saying it should be rejected on the NPV rule.\n- Mixing the inflation methods. Write which one you use.\n- Payback with uneven flows: you must **accumulate**, not divide by the average.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"In the tutorial, why were financial models applied to App A and App B (\"App A = 19, B = 19\")?","a":"Both got the same score (19) in the weighted and unweighted scoring models, so the financial models (PB, ARR, NPV) were used to break the tie."},{"q":"Which four financial evaluation models did the tutorial cover, and which one did the tutor mark for the exam?","a":"Payback period, average rate of return, net present value (NPV) and NPV with inflation. NPV is certain to be on the midterm; the tutor advised practising the first three (PB, ARR, NPV) first."},{"q":"Payback period formula, and the payback for an 80,000 project returning 20,000 a year?","a":"PB = a₀ / annual cash inflow = 80,000 / 20,000 = 4 years."},{"q":"Average rate of return formula, and ARR for profit 15,000 on 60,000?","a":"ARR = average annual profit / average project investment = 15,000 / 60,000 = 25%. (The tutor uses the initial investment as the denominator.)"},{"q":"NPV formula with the tutor's symbols?","a":"NPV = a₀ + Σ_{t=1}^{n} CF_t / (1 + r)ᵗ, where a₀ is the initial investment entered as negative, CF_t the cash flow in year t, and r the discount rate."},{"q":"PV factors at 10% for years 1–4 (3 decimals)?","a":"0.909, 0.826, 0.751, 0.683 (sum 3.169)."},{"q":"NPV of App A (80,000; 20,000/yr; 4 yrs; 10%) and App B (60,000; 15,000/yr)?","a":"A: −80,000 + 63,380 = −16,620. B: −60,000 + 47,535 = −12,465."},{"q":"Both NPVs are negative. What do you write?","a":"By the NPV rule both should be rejected. If one must be chosen, B, because it loses less (−12,465 vs −16,620)."},{"q":"The tutor's inflation formula?","a":"CF′_t = CF_t · (1 + i)ᵗ, where i is the inflation rate; then discount CF′_t at r as usual."},{"q":"App A with 5% inflation, discounted at 10%: the cash flows and the NPV?","a":"21,000 · 22,050 · 23,152.5 · 24,310; PVs sum to 71,290; NPV = −8,710."},{"q":"Real rate 10% and inflation 5%: what nominal rate?","a":"(1 + r_real)(1 + i) − 1 = 1.10 × 1.05 − 1 = 15.5%."},{"q":"Why does App C have the same NPV with and without inflation in the nominal-rate method?","a":"The cash flows are inflated by (1.05)ᵗ and the rate by the same factor, (1.155)ᵗ = (1.10)ᵗ(1.05)ᵗ, so they cancel. NPV ≈ 12,441 either way."},{"q":"App C (100,000; 25k, 35k, 40k, 45k; 10%): NPV, ARR and payback?","a":"NPV = +12,410 (accept). ARR = 36,250 / 100,000 = 36.25%. Payback = 3 years (25 + 35 + 40 = 100)."},{"q":"How does the textbook (M&S) put inflation into NPV, and how does it differ from the tutor's way?","a":"The book uses F_t / (1 + k + p)ᵗ: inflation raises the discount rate, so NPV goes down. The tutor's model 4 inflates the cash flows at a fixed r, so NPV goes up. On the exam, follow the tutor and state the formula used."},{"q":"Payback with uneven cash flows 10k, 15k, 20k, 25k on a 50k investment?","a":"Accumulate: 25k after 2 years, 45k after 3, so 3 + 5/25 = 3.2 years."},{"q":"Payback and NPV rank two projects differently. Which one wins, and why?","a":"NPV. Payback ignores the time value of money and every cash flow after the payback point."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 fe"},{"id":"xp","title":"Exam Prep: Midterm 40% · Final 50%","slides":"2026/27 · priorities and gaps","md":"# IE408 — Exam Prep · 2026/27\n\n> **★ New 05.11.2026 — the tutor said financial evaluation is ON THE MIDTERM:** payback, average rate of return, NPV and NPV with inflation **NPV is certain; practise PB, ARR and NPV first** (inflation after). These models break ties when the weighted/unweighted scoring models give equal scores (tutorial: App A = App B = 19). → `fe-financial-evaluation.md` (worked example checked, 6 practice problems, inflation-method warning).\n\n_Built from the official syllabus (7 October 2026). The 2025/26 archive has its own exam-prep file; that\none is **previous-semester and a different instructor**, so this is the live document._\n\n## The structure, and what it demands\n\n| Component | Weight | When | Scope |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Mid-term** | **40%** | **Week 8** | **Chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8** |\n| **Final** | **50%** | end of semester | not stated — assume cumulative until told otherwise |\n| Homework ×5 | 10% | not stated | topics not stated |\n\nNo quiz. No project. No presentation. **Dates are not published** — get them in week 1.\n\n**For a 4.00 you need 95 overall.** With perfect homework the two exams must still average **94.4%**. The\narithmetic is in `00-course-map.md`; the consequence is that there is no component small enough to write\noff.\n\n## Priority one: the midterm — now fully digested\n\nThe midterm examines **chapters 1–5 and 8**. Chapters 1 to 5 are the managerial foundations taught in weeks\n1 to 5, and the 2025/26 archive has **almost nothing** on four of them. That gap is now closed: the\ntextbook arrived on 7 October 2026 and all six midterm chapters are digested in `study/book/`.\n\n> ### ⚠️ The editions renumber chapters 4, 5 and 6 — this changes what you revise\n>\n> The syllabus specifies the **6th edition**; the PDF now in the course folder is the **7th**. They permute\n> three chapters. The midterm's \"chapters 1–5 and 8\" means these, and the digests are numbered the\n> **6th-edition** way to match the syllabus:\n>\n> | Midterm chapter (6e) | Same thing in the 7e PDF | Digest |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | 1 Projects in Contemporary Organizations | ch. 1 | `book/B01` |\n> | 2 Strategic Management and Project Selection | ch. 2 | `book/B02` ★ |\n> | 3 The Project Manager | ch. 3 | `book/B03` |\n> | 4 **Project Organization** | **ch. 5** | `book/B04` |\n> | 5 **Project Planning** (the WBS lives here) | **ch. 6** | `book/B05` |\n> | 8 Scheduling | ch. 8 | `book/B08` ★ |\n>\n> **Conflict and Negotiation is 6e chapter 6 — outside the stated scope** — even though it is chapter 4 in\n> the PDF. Its digest is `book/B06`, kept for the final. **Confirm this mapping with Dr. Duraković**; it is\n> inferred from two independent sources for the 6th-edition contents, and it decides what you revise.\n\nAll six midterm chapters are now digested in `study/book/`, 166 flashcards in total.\n\n| Midterm chapter | Week | Digest | Archive help |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 — projects vs. operations, the life cycle | 1 | `B01`, 18 cards | A01 covers the process phases and iron triangle only |\n| **2 — selection models** ★ | 2 | `B02`, 30 cards | **none** — this is the archive's biggest gap |\n| 3 — the project manager | 3 | `B03`, 20 cards | none |\n| 4 — functional, pure project, matrix | 4 | `B04`, 22 cards | none |\n| **5 — project planning and the WBS** | 5 | `B05`, 28 cards | A07 mentions the WBS once, in passing |\n| **8 — PERT and CPM** ★ | 6–7 | `B08`, 30 cards | **A03, A04, A05** — strong and verified |\n\n**Project selection is calculable, and it is the midterm's hidden second calculation topic.** Chapter 2\ncarries payback period, net present value, internal rate of return, the profitability index and the\nweighted factor scoring model. Every formula and worked example in `B02` was recomputed during ingest.\n\n## Priority two: the techniques, which you already have verified\n\nWeeks 6, 7 and 9 to 13 are covered by the archive, and every worked number in it has been recomputed. One\ndataset exercises all of them.\n\n| This year's week | Topic | Archive chapter | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6–7 | PERT, network diagramming | A03, A04 | verified |\n| 6–7, 9 | Critical path method | **A05** | verified — the core asset |\n| 10 | Gantt charts, tracking | A06 | verified |\n| 11 | Schedule uncertainty, Z-scores | A10 | verified, with one rounding caveat |\n| 12 | Time-phased budgeting | A07 | verified — 216,000 KM table |\n| 13 | Crashing, cost–time trade-offs | A09 | verified, plus a derived second-crash case |\n\n**The one network to know cold** (full answers in `archive-2025/exam-prep.md`):\n\n- 8 activities, critical path **A → C → E → G → H**, duration **15 months**\n- slack: F = 6, B and D = 1, the rest 0\n- σ²_p = 3.11, σ_p = 1.76; P(≤16) = **71.57%**\n- first crash **A at 750 KM**; a second critical path then appears, and month two is cheapest from **H at 3,000 KM**\n- budget **216,000 KM**\n\n## Priority three: ProjectLibre\n\nNamed in **learning outcomes 2 and 5**, so it is examinable in some form even though the syllabus does not\nsay how. It is free and open source.\n\n1. Install it now, not in week 10.\n2. Enter the eight-activity network above with its predecessors and durations.\n3. Check that ProjectLibre's critical path matches the hand calculation — A, C, E, G, H at 15 months. If it\n   does, you have validated both the tool and your understanding at once.\n4. Produce the Gantt chart and a resource histogram from the same file.\n\n## The four calculation errors to drill out\n\nCarried from the archive, which verified each one against its worked example:\n\n| # | Error | Chapter |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **max** instead of **min** on the backward pass | A05 |\n| 2 | **(T₂ − T₁)** in the crash slope denominator, giving a negative slope | A09 |\n| 3 | Adding **non-critical** variances into σ²_p | A10 |\n| 4 | Not **recomputing the critical path** after a crash | A09 |\n\n## Logistics — unconfirmed, do not assume\n\nThe 2025/26 archive records pencils-only, a non-programmable scientific calculator, supplied Z-tables and a\nmultiple-choice pattern warning. **Those came from a different instructor.** Ask Dr. Duraković in week 1:\n\n- [ ] Pen or pencil?\n- [ ] Which calculator is allowed?\n- [ ] Are Z-tables supplied, or must they be memorised or brought?\n- [ ] Is the final cumulative?\n- [ ] Is ProjectLibre examined in a lab, in the homework, or not at all?\n\n## Revision budget\n\nThe university's own ECTS model allocates **24 hours for midterm revision and 32 for the final** — 56 hours,\nover a third of the course's 150. Blocking that time against the week-8 and end-of-semester dates once they\nare announced is the difference between an A and an A−.\n\n## Immediate actions\n\n1. **Email with `[IE408]` in the subject** to ask for the exam dates, the homework schedule, and — most\n   importantly — **whether the midterm scope follows the 6th-edition chapter numbers**. Office hours are\n   Tue 15:00–17:00, Wed 14:00–15:00 and Fri 15:00–17:00 in A F1.15 if you would rather ask in person.\n2. **Work `book/B02`** — the selection models are the midterm's hidden calculation topic and the one thing\n   nothing else in the system covers.\n3. **Drill the A05 network** until both passes are automatic; it serves weeks 6, 7, 9, 10, 11, 12 and 13,\n   and `book/B08` confirms the same formulas in the textbook's own notation.\n4. **Install ProjectLibre** and reproduce the archive network in it.\n5. **Read `book/B05`** for the WBS — week 5's topic, and the one drawable artefact in the pre-midterm half.\n","lesson":"","cards":[],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 xp"},{"id":"b01","title":"Book 1 · Projects in Contemporary Organizations ★","slides":"6e ch. 1 = 7e ch. 1 · week 1 · MIDTERM","md":"# B01 — Projects in Contemporary Organizations (ch. 1 in both the 6th and 7th editions)\n\n_The vocabulary chapter: what a project is, why organizations use projects instead of their normal functional\nmachinery, and the life-cycle frame the whole book hangs on. Read in week 1 alongside the syllabus, and examined in\nthe week-8 midterm (40% of the course, chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8)._\n\n> ### ⚠️ Edition mapping — read this once\n>\n> The syllabus specifies the **6th edition** and examines **chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8**. The PDF in the\n> course folder is the **7th edition**, and the two **permute chapters 4, 5 and 6**:\n>\n> | 6th ed. (what the syllabus means) | 7th ed. (what the PDF has) | On the midterm? |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | 1 Projects in Contemporary Organizations | 1 (same) | **yes** |\n> | 2 Strategic Management and Project Selection | 2 (same) | **yes** |\n> | 3 The Project Manager | 3 (same) | **yes** |\n> | 4 Project Organization | **5** The Project in the Organizational Structure | **yes** |\n> | 5 Project Planning | **6** Project Activity Planning | **yes** |\n> | 6 Conflict and Negotiation | **4** Negotiation and the Management of Conflict | **no** |\n> | 8 Scheduling | 8 (same) | **yes** |\n>\n> Files here are numbered by the **6th-edition** chapter, because that is what the syllabus cites.\n> Page references are **7th-edition printed pages**, so do not quote them at the professor.\n> **Confirm the scope with Dr. Duraković** — this mapping is inferred, and it decides what you revise.\n\n## How to use this chapter\n\nNothing in chapter 1 is calculated. Every point here is a definition, a list, or a comparison — which means the exam\nquestions it supports are the cheap ones: name the three forces, list the seven characteristics, draw the life\ncycle, distinguish a project from routine operations. These are points you either bank or throw away. Learn the\nlists verbatim.\n\n> **The single most important thing in this chapter for week 1 is the project-vs-operations\n> distinction** (the week-1 topic line is literally \"General introduction; project vs. operations\").\n> It is set out in full in the *Project vs. operations* section below.\n\n## 1. Why project management emerged — the three forces (pp. 2–3)\n\nProject management did not come out of business. The book is blunt about the direction of the transfer: business\nlearned it **from the military** — the US Navy's Polaris program, NASA's Apollo program, later the space shuttle and\nprecision-guided weapons (p. 2). Government went first; industry copied.\n\nThe chapter then names **three paramount societal forces** that made new management methods necessary:\n\n| # | Force | Why it forces projects |\n|---|-------|------------------------|\n| 1 | **Exponential expansion of human knowledge** | More academic disciplines now bear on any one product, so no individual holds the required knowledge — decisions must be made by **teams** |\n| 2 | **Growing demand for complex, sophisticated, customized goods and services** | Product design has to be built into production and distribution rather than bolted on |\n| 3 | **Evolution of worldwide competitive markets** for production and consumption | Cultural and environmental differences enter decisions about what, where, when and how to produce |\n\nWhy it matters: this is Material Review Question 1 (\"Name and briefly describe the societal forces that have\ncontributed to the need for project management\") and the first bullet of the chapter summary. All three converge on\nthe same conclusion — **work must be done by teams, and traditional organizational structures, geared to mass\nproduction of simpler goods, cannot coordinate them** (p. 2).\n\n⚠️ Order trap: the chapter opening lists the forces as knowledge → demand → worldwide markets (p. 2), but the\nend-of-chapter summary lists them as demand → knowledge → \"the global production–consumption environment\" (p. 22).\nSame three forces, two orderings and two wordings. Do not treat the numbering as meaningful.\n\nSecondary forces the chapter adds: **intense competition** between institutions, profit and not-for-profit,\nexpressed as management \"crusades\" — total quality control, supply chain management and above all **Six-Sigma**,\nitself run as projects — which puts the pressure on **time-to-market** (p. 2); a society that **assumes technology\ncan do anything** and ignores the economic and non-economic costs until a dramatic event (Chernobyl, Exxon Valdez,\nglobal warming) forces attention (pp. 2–3); and the fact that **projects keep getting bigger**, because \"the more we\ncan do, the more we try to do\" (p. 3).\n\n## 2. The three project objectives — and the disputed fourth (pp. 3–4)\n\nThe **direct project objectives** (also \"direct goals\") are:\n\n| Objective | Also called | Constraint in Figure 1-1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Performance** | scope, specifications | required performance |\n| **Time** | schedule | due date |\n| **Cost** | budget | budget limit |\n\nWhy it matters: this is the book's version of what the archived 2025/26 guide calls the **iron triangle** —\nidentical idea, different name. Figure 1-1 (p. 4) draws them as three axes with a target point inside; the\nimplication is that a **function relates them**, so you cannot move one without moving the others. The chapter\nstates the PM's primary task as managing exactly these **trade-offs** (p. 4).\n\nTwo refinements the book insists on. First, **time and cost are part of the outcome, not conditions on it** — a\nbuilding finished a year late or 20% over budget is \"quite a different outcome\" from the same structure delivered on\ntime and on budget (p. 3). Second, on **the fourth dimension, client expectations** (Darnell, 1997): Meredith &\nMantel hold that client expectations are **not a separate target** but an inherent part of the specifications, since\nseparating the two \"courts conflict\" between client and team; expectations that grow as the project proceeds are\n**scope creep**; and the conclusion is to fold the client's nebulous expectations into a single **total \"required\nperformance\"** objective (p. 3).\n\n⚠️ Note the tension to keep straight: the chapter body argues the fourth dimension should be absorbed into\nperformance, while the Directed Reading at the end of the chapter (Pinto & Kharbanda) argues the opposite — that\n**client satisfaction is a genuine fourth hurdle** replacing the old triple constraint (p. 28). An exam question\ncould be set from either side; cite both and say which source each comes from.\n\n### Direct vs ancillary goals\n\n| | Direct (project) goals | Ancillary (process) goals |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | Performance, time, cost; plus business impact and future opportunity | Improving the organization's PM competency and methods; individuals' increased managerial experience |\n| Stated? | Specified in the proposal | Mostly **unstated** |\n| Examples of the unstated kind | — | Health of the project team and of the rest of the organization; the project's **environment** — antipollution groups, trade unions, competitor firms |\n\nThe PM \"cannot burn out the team\" or wreck the functional departments to hit the direct objectives (p. 4). This\ndistinction is Material Review Question 11, which asks whether learning a new skill, or entering a new market, is a\ndirect or ancillary goal. By the book's reasoning: learning a skill = ancillary; entering a new market = direct,\nbecause **business impact and new opportunities are counted as direct goals**.\n\n**Shenhar, Levy & Dvir (1997)** found **four dimensions of project success** (p. 4; detailed in ch. 12): (1) project\nefficiency, (2) impact on the customer, (3) business impact on the organization, (4) opening new opportunities for\nthe future. Meredith & Mantel classify **all four as direct goals** — the first two obviously, the latter two as\nspecific objectives of the project. Chapters 1–11 nevertheless focus on the traditional three, because the newer\nobjectives \"did not replace\" time, cost and performance; they were **added**.\n\n## 3. The project manager and the professionalization of the field (pp. 4–6)\n\nThe PM's job: **integrate all aspects of the project**, make sure the right knowledge and resources are available\nwhen and where needed, and deliver the expected result on time and cost-effectively (p. 5).\n\n**Project Management Institute (PMI)**, founded **1969**, drives the professionalization of the field. Membership:\n**7,500 in 1990**, over **17,000** five years later, over **260,000 in more than 171 countries** by the end of\n**2007** (p. 5). Its outputs: the *Project Management Journal* and *PM Network*; the **PMBOK** (project management\nbody of knowledge) as the educational base; and three certificates — **PMP** (Project Management Professional),\n**PgMP** (Program Management Professional, for advanced program managers) and **CAPM** (Certified Associate in\nProject Management, lower education/experience bar).\n\nCausation matters here: growth in PMs and PMI membership was the **result, not the cause**, of growth in the number\nof projects (p. 6). Two named sources of that growth: the software industry, and senior management's need to control\na rising volume of non-routine activity in large organizations — which is how the **project-organized firm** appears\n(ch. 4). The job's standing problems: responsibility without full authority over resources or personnel; senior\nmanagement, client, team and public all speaking different languages with different objectives; constant\nfire-fighting; vendors who miss delivery dates. Against that: visibility, variety, prestige and money — but \"risk\nand conflict avoiders do not make happy project managers\" (p. 6). Project management is now a recognized **career\npath**, and large firms often give promising young managers a project \"tour of duty\" as a test (p. 14).\n\n## 4. Trends in project management (pp. 6–7)\n\nDriven by global markets, computing and telecommunications, and greater education — and by faster life cycles.\n\n| Trend | What it means | Cross-reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Achieving strategic goals** | Filter projects so objectives support the organization's strategy and mission; terminate those that do not and redirect their resources | ch. 2, esp. §2.7 — the **project portfolio process** |\n| **Achieving routine goals (\"projectizing\")** | Running ordinary departmental tasks as projects, with artificial deadlines and budgets, to borrow project discipline | §1.1 |\n| **Improving project effectiveness** | Formal **PMO** (Project Management Office); assessing PM **maturity**; **phase gates**, **earned value**, **critical ratios** | §2.1, §4.6, §5.1, §5.5, §10.3, §11.2–11.3 |\n| **Virtual projects** | Global teams across countries and time zones whose members may never physically meet | §4.3, §10.2 |\n| **Quasi-projects** | Performance/scope, deadline and/or budget undefined; led by IT/IS demand; handled with prototyping and phase-gating | §1.1 |\n\nThe danger in \"projectizing\", via Sandberg (2007, *Wall Street Journal*): if workers discover a deadline is\nartificial — they meet it and nobody cares, or they miss it with no penalty — **the credibility of every future\ndeadline and budget is destroyed**, \"much like the boy who cried wolf\" (p. 7).\n\nThe PMO also has the job of teaching PMs the organization's **ancillary goals**, which become goals of every project\n\"whether the project manager knows it or not\" (p. 7).\n\n## 5. Three revolutionary changes in managing organizations (p. 7)\n\n| # | Change |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Accelerating replacement of traditional **hierarchical** management by **consensual** management |\n| 2 | Adoption of the **systems approach** (systems engineering) — acting on one part of a system inevitably affects others |\n| 3 | Establishing **projects as the preferred way** to accomplish organizational goals |\n\nTwo claims built on these: using a hierarchical rather than consensual style on multi-organizational projects is \"a\nmajor generator of conflict\", and staffing a multidisciplinary project with people focused on their own discipline\nrather than on the project's problem does the same (p. 7).\n\nSupporting evidence: Thomas Hughes, *Rescuing Prometheus* (1998), on four projects that needed non-traditional\nmanagement, organization and problem-solving — **SAGE** air defense, the **Atlas** ICBM, the **Boston Central\nArtery/Tunnel** and **ARPANET** (p. 7). What separates these from the **Manhattan Project** is that Manhattan was\nthe sole full-time work of most participants, whereas MIT, the Pentagon, IBM, Bell Labs and RAND ran these later\nprojects *alongside* their normal work. Hughes's term for such projects is **transdisciplinary** (across\ndisciplines), which the authors prefer to \"interdisciplinary\" (between disciplines) (p. 8, footnote).\n\n## 6. The definition of a project, and the vocabulary hierarchy (§1.1, pp. 8–9)\n\n**PMI's definition**, quoted by the book: a project is \"A temporary endeavor undertaken to create a unique product\nor service\" (PMI, 2004, p. 5; quoted p. 8).\n\nThe authors' own broad definition: **a specific, finite task to be accomplished**. Scale and duration are not what\nmatter — \"what is relevant is that the project be seen as a unit\" (p. 8).\n\nHistory: the pyramids are arguable, Boulder Dam and Edison's light bulb are projects \"by any sensible definition\",\nbut **modern project management is usually said to have begun with the Manhattan Project** (p. 8). It spread from\nlarge R&D and weapons work and massive construction to smaller construction, car models and jet engines, then to\nservices, advertising campaigns, mergers, the non-profit sector, weddings and election campaigns — and most\nstrikingly to **software development**.\n\n**The terminology hierarchy** (mostly military in origin), largest to smallest:\n\n| Level | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| **Program** | An exceptionally large, long-range objective, broken down into a set of projects (glossary: a group of similar projects oriented toward a specific goal) |\n| **Project** | A specific, finite task, seen as a unit |\n| **Task** | A subset of a project, consisting of work packages |\n| **Work package** | A sub-element of a task at the lowest level of the **work breakdown structure**, used to assign costs and values |\n| **Work unit** | The components of a work package |\n\nWhy it matters: Material Review Question 7 asks exactly how projects, programs, tasks and work packages differ, and\nthe summary states the order outright: \"program, project, task, work package, work unit\" (p. 22). Expect to\nreproduce this chain. Be ready for the caveat too — **exceptions abound**: the Manhattan Project was really a\nprogram, while a \"task force\" was created to study a steel company's futures (p. 8). Note also that the **work\npackage / WBS** link is the hinge into week 5 (ch. 5).\n\n## 7. The seven characteristics of a project (pp. 9–11)\n\n| # | Characteristic | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Importance** | The most crucial attribute: important enough in senior management's eyes to justify a **special organizational unit outside the routine structure**. If the organization suspects it is not important, the project is \"generally doomed to fail\" |\n| 2 | **Performance** (specific end results) | A one-time activity with a **well-defined set of desired end results**, divisible into subtasks that need coordination and control of timing, precedence, cost and performance |\n| 3 | **Life cycle with a finite due date** | Slow beginning → buildup → peak → decline → **termination by a due date**. Some projects end by being phased into ongoing operations; like organic entities, they \"often resist termination\" |\n| 4 | **Interdependencies** | Projects compete with other projects for scarce resources (ch. 9), and **always** interact with the parent's ongoing operations. The pattern of interaction with functional departments is **changeable**, not regular |\n| 5 | **Uniqueness** | Every project has some unique elements; results are at least unique to this organization. Therefore projects **cannot be completely reduced to routine** |\n| 6 | **Resources** | Limited budgets for personnel and everything else — often **implied rather than detailed**, but strictly limited |\n| 7 | **Conflict** | The PM lives in a world of conflict: project vs. functional department, project vs. project, and conflict inside the team over resources and leadership. Some conflict is **helpful**, and the PM must tell the difference |\n\nWhy it matters: Material Review Question 4 asks for all seven with a brief description of each, and Class Discussion\nQuestion 23 asks **which attributes are always present and which are only frequently present** — note that the text\nsays projects *always* interact with the parent's ongoing operations, while inter-project interaction is merely\n\"common\".\n\nTwo details worth quoting back. **Symptoms that a project lacks the *importance* attribute** (p. 9): no mention of\nit by top management, assigning it to someone of low rank or stature, giving it to someone already overworked,\nfailing to monitor progress, failing to meet its resource needs. **The changeable interaction pattern** (p. 10):\nmarketing is involved at the beginning and end but not the middle, manufacturing throughout, finance at the\nbeginning, accounting (the controller) at the end and at reporting points.\n\n### The four parties-at-interest\n\n**Parties-at-interest** = **stakeholders**: the **client**, the **parent organization**, the **project team**, and\nthe **public** (pp. 10–11). The glossary's version is \"project team, client, senior management, and specific public\ninterest groups\" (p. 22). They define success and failure differently — the client wants changes, the parent\norganization wants profits that those changes may reduce — and individuals on projects are often **responsible to\ntwo bosses at once** with different priorities. \"Project management is no place for the timid.\"\n\n## 8. Project vs. operations — the week-1 distinction (p. 11)\n\nThe chapter asks whether, if those characteristics define a project, there are **non-projects**, and answers yes.\nIts examples of non-projects: a manufacturing line producing a flow of standard products; weekly employment reports;\npreparing school lunches; delivering mail; the flight of Delta-1288 from Dallas to Dulles; checking your e-mail.\n\n**The discriminator is not uniqueness — it is routine.** You could argue each of those activities is to some degree\nunique, \"but it is not their uniqueness that characterizes them. They are all routine. They are tasks that are\nperformed over and over again.\" Each project, by contrast, is a **one-time event**.\n\n| Dimension | **Project** | **Operations / routine functional work** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Frequency | One-time event | Performed over and over |\n| End point | Finite due date; terminated, or phased into operations | Ongoing, no planned end |\n| Output | Unique, at least to this organization; some customization always | Standard, repeated output |\n| Organization | Special unit **outside** the routine structure; temporary | Permanent functional department inside the hierarchy |\n| Staffing | **Cross-functional** team of representatives from the relevant departments, bringing different attitudes, time frames, learning and biases; members usually split time with their functional duties | Homogeneous backgrounds — finance people in finance, marketers in marketing |\n| Interaction pattern | Changeable, project-by-project | Regular and patterned between departments |\n| Manager's authority | Little formal authority; often cannot appraise, reward or punish team members; manages by **persuasion and influence** | Line authority through the hierarchy |\n| Relation to status quo | Represents change, so it **threatens** the status quo | Supports the status quo |\n| Risk and uncertainty | High — differences from past projects \"imbue every project with considerable risk\" | Low; predictable |\n| Management method | Project management; **routine managerial methods are inadequate** | Routine managerial methods |\n\nThe last row is the examinable sentence: \"Projects cannot be managed adequately by the managerial routines used for\nroutine work\" (p. 11). Note the borderline example the book deliberately picks — even building a section of\ninterstate highway is a project, because \"no two miles are alike\" and each demands adaptation to terrain and\nsubstructure. (The staffing, authority and status-quo rows come from the Directed Reading's section \"From Whence\nComes the Challenge?\", pp. 27–28, not from §1.1.)\n\n### Quasi-projects — the third category\n\nBetween projects and non-projects sit **quasi-projects**: \"Bill, would you look into this?\" \"Judy, we need to finish\nthis by Friday's meeting.\" There is no specific task identified, no specific budget and no specific deadline —\nperformance, schedule and budget are **implied** by the words \"this\", \"meet\" and \"we\" rather than delineated (p.\n11).\n\nCan project management methods be used on them? **Yes.** The recommended handling:\n\n1. Pin down performance, schedule and budget as precisely as possible, as early as possible — **without\n   antagonizing** the manager who assigned the work. Ask: is it needed soon? how accurate, thorough,\n   detailed?\n2. For the common information-systems case where **discovering the scope or requirements is itself the\n   work**, make that determination the project's **first milestone**, then review resources, budget,\n   deadline, capabilities and personnel against the newly discovered requirements.\n3. Alternatively, run it **cost-plus** and stop when the benefits no longer justify the cost.\n\nNote the asymmetry: a quasi-project is often \"completed\" by **de-scoping** the required performance to fit an\nartificial due date and budget (p. 7) — exactly the trade-off behaviour the three objectives predict. Material\nReview Question 12 asks for the characteristics of quasi-projects.\n\n## 9. Why project management? Advantages, disadvantages, limitations (§1.2, pp. 12–13)\n\n**Purpose.** A project exists to accomplish specific goals; it is **organized as a project** in order to **focus\nresponsibility and authority** for those goals on an individual or small group (p. 12).\n\nThe project form lets the manager (p. 12):\n\n1. Be responsive to the **client and the environment**;\n2. **Identify and correct problems early**;\n3. Make **timely trade-off decisions** between conflicting project goals;\n4. Ensure task managers do not optimize their own tasks at the expense of the whole — i.e. do not\n   **suboptimize**.\n\n| | Reported by users (p. 13) |\n|---|---|\n| **Advantages** — majority of users | Better **control**, better **customer relations** (Davis, 1974), probably higher **return on investment** (Ibbs et al., 1997) |\n| **Advantages** — significant proportion | Shorter development times, lower costs, higher quality and reliability, higher profit margins; also sharper orientation toward results, better interdepartmental coordination, higher worker morale |\n| **Disadvantages** — most organizations | Greater **organizational complexity**; increased likelihood that **organizational policy will be violated** (unsurprising, given the autonomy the PM needs) |\n| **Disadvantages** — a few firms | Higher costs, more management difficulties, low personnel utilization |\n\nThe one-line version to memorize: **\"the disadvantages of project management stem from exactly the same sources as\nits advantages\"** — they are the price paid for the advantages, and on balance the project form wins *if the work is\nappropriate for a project* (p. 13).\n\n**Limitations** (Material Review Question 3, p. 13):\n\n- Creating a project may itself be an **admission** that the parent organization and its managers cannot\n  achieve the outcome through the functional organization.\n- **Conflict** is a necessary side-effect.\n- The PM's **authority is inconsistent with the assigned responsibility**, so the PM depends on the\n  goodwill of functional managers for resources. Escalating to senior officials may win the immediate\n  argument but \"reflects poorly on the skills of the PM\" and can backfire long-term.\n\n**Against special pleading.** A near-universal tendency is to claim \"software (or construction, or R&D, or\nmarketing) projects are different, you can't expect us to schedule the way others do\". The book's philosophy rejects\nthis: the **similarities between all sorts of projects are far more pervasive than their differences** (p. 13).\nExpect this as a discussion question.\n\n## 10. The project life cycle (§1.3, pp. 14–17)\n\nThe conventional life cycle (Figure 1-3, p. 15) plots **% project completion against time** and is **S-shaped**,\nlabelled in three phrases worth memorizing:\n\n| Stage of the curve | Label in Figure 1-3 | What is happening |\n|---|---|---|\n| Beginning | **Slow start** | Start-up: a manager is selected, team and initial resources assembled, work program organized; minimal effort while the concept is developed and selected |\n| Middle | **Quick momentum** | Planning complete, real work underway, activity peaks |\n| End | **Slow finish** | Many parts must come together, and team members \"drag their feet\" and avoid the final steps |\n\nSo the pattern is **slow–rapid–slow**, and anyone who has watched a building go up has seen it (p. 14). The cause is\nthe changing level of resources across the stages.\n\n**Figure 1-4 (p. 15)** plots **level of effort** (person-hours or resources per unit time, or headcount) against\ntime in four phases: **(1) conception → (2) selection → (3) planning, scheduling, monitoring and control → (4)\nevaluation and termination.** Two relationships to know: progress and effort are strongly correlated, so the\n**derivative of the progress curve resembles the effort curve** (Cioffi, 2004); and because the effort curve is\n**non-symmetrical**, the progress curve generally is not either. There is no reason for the slowdown at the end to\nmirror the buildup at the start — some projects end abruptly, others \"not with a bang but a whimper\", and effort may\nnever reach zero if a **cadre group** is held for the next project, which rises \"phoenix-like\" from the old one's\nashes.\n\n⚠️ Terminology clash to be ready for: the glossary (p. 22) defines **life cycle** as **start-up → building →\nmaturing → termination**, four phases, which is *not* the same wording as Figure 1-4's conception / selection /\nplanning-and-control / evaluation-and-termination. Both are in this chapter. If asked for \"the stages\", give the\nFigure 1-4 set and mention the glossary's four-phase wording.\n\n**Which objective dominates when.** The conventional wisdom was: performance first (while planners find the methods\n— what the book calls the project's **technology**, \"the means for accomplishing a task\"), then cost during the\nhigh-activity period, then schedule at the end when the client demands delivery. **The book says this conventional\nwisdom turns out to be untrue: recent research indicates performance and schedule are more important than cost\nduring all stages** (p. 16). That reversal is a very likely exam question. A related hazard: once the \"how\" problems\nare solved, workers become preoccupied with improving performance **beyond the original specifications**, which\ndelays the schedule and raises cost.\n\n### Alternative life-cycle shapes (pp. 16–17)\n\nThe S-curve is \"seriously misleading\" for many projects. The horizontal axis can be **resources** instead of time\nwithout changing the function — the life cycle is really a **return-on-input** curve.\n\n| Shape | Example from the text |\n|---|---|\n| **Stairstep** | Progress toward a degree measured in credit hours — flat during each term, a step up at completion, a longer flat stretch over the summer |\n| **Flat line with a spike** | Passing a licensing exam — CPA, the bar, electrician or plumber certification |\n| **Concave, decreasing returns to scale** | Installing a multi-part new technology where the \"biggest bang for the buck\" parts are installed first |\n| **Inverse S** | Fast progress, a slowdown in the middle, speeding up again at the end |\n| **Exponential (\"goop to cake\")** | Figure 1-5: a cake is \"goop\" until the last few minutes of baking. Also some software projects, chemistry and chemical engineering projects, and the preparation of this book's manuscript |\n\nThe **goop-to-cake** case is the one to understand, because it reverses the economics:\n\n| | S-curve (Fig. 1-3) | Exponential curve (Fig. 1-5) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Near completion, extra input yields | Successively **smaller** increments — **diminishing marginal returns** | Successively **larger** increments — **increasing marginal returns** (bounded at 100%) |\n| Typical of | Projects whose progress accrues steadily | Outputs built from subunits that are useless alone and useful only assembled; chemical-type transformations |\n| % completion vs. cost / resource use | Closely correlated — which is the **basis of earned value** (ch. 10) | Little correlation between resources spent and progress in terms of final benefit |\n\nWhy it matters: the PM does **not** need to estimate the precise shape, but **must know which type applies**,\nbecause the distinction drives budgets and schedules (ch. 7) and decides whether earned value is meaningful at all\n(p. 17). Several end-of-chapter questions ask whether a given project (the Olympic Torch Relay, a bridge demolition,\na freeway) is S-shaped or \"the right half of a U\". The chapter adds that the life cycle can also be **viewed** as a\ncontrol system, a quality-control mechanism, a way of organizing risk management, or nested projects within larger\nprojects (p. 17).\n\n### Risk during the life cycle (pp. 17–18)\n\nFigures 1-6 and 1-7 make one point: **uncertainty starts wide and narrows as the project progresses.** Seen from\nproject start **t₀**, the band of uncertainty about final cost grows until it is \"quite wide\" by the estimated end\ndate; forecasts remade at **t₁** and then **t₂** cut it progressively, because **the more progress made, the less\nuncertainty about achieving the final goal**. Re-forecasting of performance, time and cost happens at **fixed\nintervals** or at **specific technological milestones**. The axes are interchangeable \"without significantly\naltering the shapes of the curves\" — relabel them and the same figure shows uncertainty about **schedule** at given\nlevels of expenditure.\n\nGlossary definitions to pair with this (p. 22): **risk** = \"the chance that project processes or outcomes will not\noccur as planned\"; **uncertainty** = having only partial or no information, often from ambiguity or complexity.\n\n## 11. How the book — and therefore the course — is organized (§1.4, pp. 18–22)\n\nThe book follows the project life cycle, from the creative idea to termination, in three parts:\n\n| Part | Theme | Chapters | Contents |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I | **Project initiation** | 2–5 | Ch. 2 PM **maturity**, project evaluation and selection, risk through simulation, proposals, and the eight-step **project portfolio process**; ch. 3 the project manager's roles, responsibilities, personal characteristics, multicultural operation; ch. 4 **negotiating for resources** and sources of interpersonal conflict; ch. 5 establishing the **project organization** and staffing the team |\n| II | **Project planning** | 6–9 | Ch. 6 activity planning and **phase-gate** systems; ch. 7 **budgeting**; ch. 8 **scheduling** — PERT, CPM, precedence diagramming; ch. 9 **resource allocation** — levelling for one project, allocation across several |\n| III | **Project execution** | 10–13 | Ch. 10 information requirements, monitoring, **PMIS** (examples use Microsoft Project); ch. 11 **control**; ch. 12 **audits and evaluation**, success and failure factors; ch. 13 forms of **termination** — shutdown, integration into the organization, extension into a new project |\n\nTwo asides. **Risk management is deliberately not given its own chapter** — because risk and uncertainty pervade\nproject life, its components (risk management planning, identification, assessment, quantification, response\ndevelopment, monitoring and control) are spread wherever relevant, with **risk identification** in ch. 5 (pp.\n21–22). And the old quantitative-vs-behavioural argument is settled thus: **you cannot manage a project without both\nmathematics and the science of human behaviour** (p. 20).\n\n> Relevance to IE408: the midterm's six chapters are, in the chapter-numbering above (7th ed.),\n> ch. 1, 2, 3, 5, 6 and 8 — the whole of **Part I: project initiation** except negotiation and conflict,\n> plus **activity planning and scheduling** from Part II. Weeks 9–14 then walk through the rest of\n> Parts II and III, which is what the final covers. See the edition-mapping box at the top.\n\n## 12. Directed Reading — \"Lessons for an Accidental Profession\" (Pinto & Kharbanda, 1995; pp. 26–34)\n\nNot part of §§1.1–1.4, but printed inside chapter 1 and listed under the week-1 reading, so treat it as examinable.\n\n**Why \"accidental profession\"** — two reasons given (p. 26):\n\n1. **Few formal or systematic programs exist for selecting and training project managers**, even in firms\n   that specialize in project work; training is ad hoc. Most PMs \"fall into their responsibilities by\n   happenstance rather than by calculation\".\n2. **Nobody grows up wanting to be a project manager** (Frame, 1987). It is neither a well-defined nor a\n   well-understood career path; \"the role is thrust upon people, rather than being sought\".\n\nThe consequence is a **\"fly or die\" mentality**: novices are handed a project with a time frame, a budget and a set\nof performance characteristics, and either master the job fast or fail, producing failed projects and lost money,\nmarkets and resources — the same mistake repeated company after company, \"as if experience and failure were the best\nteacher\".\n\nThe PM's position is captured in one line: an effective project manager is **\"the kingpin, but not the king\"**.\nLacking authority to appraise, reward or punish, the PM's style must be **persuasion and influence rather than\ncoercion and command** (p. 26).\n\n**The Vital Dozen** (Figure 1, pp. 28–29) — presented in no particular order, and the reading's first question asks\nyou to rank them and justify the ranking:\n\n| # | Guideline | The core of it |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Understand the **context** of project management | Projects exist outside the established hierarchy and **threaten the status quo because they represent change** |\n| 2 | Recognize project team **conflict as progress** | Conflict is a natural, unavoidable result of diverse backgrounds and a sign of healthy group maturation; analyse its nature before responding — avoidance, defusion or problem-solving — and never suppress it by reflex, or it festers and erupts worse later |\n| 3 | Understand **who the stakeholders are and what they want** | Assess principal stakeholders and their agendas early, including **\"intervenors\"** (Cleland, 1983) — external groups such as environmental activists who can wreck a project. Accept that you cannot please everyone; aim for satisfactory relations, not maximum happiness |\n| 4 | **Accept and use the political nature** of organizations | Resource decisions are made by bargaining; become adept at negotiation and influence |\n| 5 | **Lead from the front**; the view is better | Project management is \"leader intensive\"; vary the style to the situation rather than bending the situation to a preferred style; the worst leaders follow the **\"Mushroom Principle of Management\"** — keep subordinates in the dark and feed them manure |\n| 6 | Understand what **\"success\"** means | The traditional **triple constraint** is being replaced by a four-part model whose fourth hurdle is **client use and satisfaction**; rewarding the manager before transfer to the client \"is seriously jumping the gun\" |\n| 7 | Build and maintain a **cohesive team** | The PM plays motivator, coach, cheerleader, peacemaker, conflict resolver |\n| 8 | **Enthusiasm and despair are both infectious** | The leader is a billboard; the team takes its cue from the manager's attitude. Be an honest source of information rather than letting the rumour mill run |\n| 9 | **One look forward is worth two looks back** | Keep asking \"What if?\" — one large-scale study found the **leading determinant of project failure was the absence of troubleshooting mechanisms** |\n| 10 | **Remember what you are trying to do** | Keep the mission visible to the whole team, not just the PM; test it by asking members for their assessment of project status |\n| 11 | **Use time carefully or it will use you** | Keep a daily time log; kill agenda-less meetings and \"quick chats\" |\n| 12 | **Above all, plan, plan, plan** | \"Those who fail to plan are planning to fail\" — but there is a limit |\n\n**The three political types** (Figure 2, p. 30) — a clean table question:\n\n| | **Naive** | **Sensible** | **Sharks** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Underlying attitude | Politics is unpleasant | Politics is necessary | Politics is an opportunity |\n| Intent | Avoid at all costs | Further departmental goals | Self-serving and predatory |\n| Techniques | Tell it like it is | Network; expand connections; use the system to give and receive favours | Manipulate; use fraud and deceit when necessary |\n| Favourite tactics | None — the truth will win out | Negotiate, bargain | Bully; misuse information; cultivate and use \"friends\" and other contacts |\n\nThe argument is that **sharks and the naive are at equal disadvantage** — sharks because predatory tactics arouse\ndistrust, the naive because staying above the fray costs them the resources their project needs. The **politically\nsensible** middle ground uses politics as a basis for negotiation and bargaining, recognizing that any new project\nreshuffles the distribution of power and makes other managers nervous.\n\n**On planning, both directions** (guideline 12, pp. 31–32). Against rushing: \"steps that were poorly done are often\nsteps that must be redone\", and top management's pressure to show something is being done must be resisted. Against\nover-planning: **overly elaborate planning can be detrimental** — \"by the time an opportunity is fully investigated,\nit may no longer exist\"; only **28%** of surveyed entrepreneurs drew up a full-scale plan (Sweet, 1994); and the\nclosing line is the military slogan **\"No plan ever survives its first contact with the enemy.\"** Question 5 of the\nreading asks exactly where you draw that line. Gosselin's (1993) six time-control rules sit under guideline 11:\nrealistic estimates without overextending yourself, absolute clarity on what the boss or client requires, provision\nfor contingencies, revised estimates with options, clarity about which factors are fixed, and saying **\"Yes, and…\"\nrather than \"No, but…\"**.\n\n**Named cases.** A telephone company devalued the **\"skunkworks\"** label by applying it to 75+ projects reporting to\nthe CEO, wrecking its research allocation. A manufacturer that rewarded PMs only for time and budget, never for\nclient acceptance, got corner-cutting and poor quality. One multinational division gave untrained first-year\nmanagers a scheduling package and an emergency phone number — **fewer than 30%** of its new product efforts were\nprofitable. And the **\"Chunnel\"** ran roughly **15 months** late, missing the summer season, at a final cost of\n**£15 billion** against an original **£2.3 billion** estimate (O'Connor, 1993) — offered as the symptom of absent\ncontingency planning.\n\n**The twenty-first-century PM** (p. 33). The old image — decision maker, expert, boss, director — gives way to\n**leader, coach, facilitator**; Clarke (1993) adds natural salesperson, and the successful PM \"must manifest an\nobvious desire to see others succeed\". Turner (1993) predicts project-based management sweeping aside functional\nline management in favour of flat, flexible structures. The resolution of the technical-vs-human tension: **project\nmanagement is primarily a challenge in managing people.**\n\n## Overlap with the archived 2025/26 study guide\n\nThe archive is organized around techniques, this chapter around vocabulary, but two ideas collide:\n\n| Archive idea | This chapter's version |\n|---|---|\n| **Iron triangle** — scope, time, cost; change one and the other two move | The **three direct project objectives** — performance (scope), time, cost — related by a function, managed as **trade-offs** by the PM (Figure 1-1, p. 4). Same concept, different name; the archive's \"quality in the middle\" framing does not appear here, and Meredith & Mantel instead debate whether **client expectations** are a fourth dimension |\n| **Five process phases** — initiation, planning, execution, controlling, closing, with controlling as a loop | Meredith & Mantel do **not** use the five process groups in ch. 1. They use the **life-cycle curve** (slow start / quick momentum / slow finish) plus Figure 1-4's effort phases (conception, selection, planning-scheduling-monitoring-control, evaluation and termination), and the book's own three-part structure (initiation ch. 2–5, planning ch. 6–9, execution ch. 10–13) |\n\nPractical consequence: if a midterm question asks for \"the project life cycle\", answer from **this** chapter, not\nfrom the archive's PMBOK-style five process groups — the required textbook for IE408 is Meredith & Mantel, and the\narchive came from a different instructor in a different semester. Know both and label which is which.\n\n## Unclear or inconsistent in the source\n\n- ⚠️ **Figure 1-2 does not match its own text.** The text reports PMI membership \"over 260,000\" by the end\n  of 2007 (p. 5), but the figure's vertical axis is labelled \"PMI membership (1000)\" and tops out at 160,\n  with a horizontal axis ending at 2000. The figure appears to be older than the paragraph beside it. Quote\n  the **numbers in the text**, not the graph, and if asked about the \"pronounced bend\" in Figure 1-2\n  (Class Discussion Question 22), describe the shape — exponential growth — rather than specific values.\n- ⚠️ **Broken cross-reference.** The \"Achieving Strategic Goals\" trend says the project portfolio process\n  is described \"in Section 1.7\" (p. 6). Chapter 1 has only four sections; the portfolio process is in\n  **Chapter 2, §2.7**, which the same paragraph names correctly two lines earlier. Treat \"§1.7\" as a typo.\n- ⚠️ **Duplicated bullet in the demolition box.** The Demolition Plan list in the \"Demolishing San\n  Francisco's Bridges Safely\" box (p. 18) prints the same bullet — \"a Code of Safe Practice describing\n  personal protective equipment for the workers, as well as a maintenance plan for the equipment\" — twice.\n  The intended list is almost certainly five items, not six, and one of the two slots probably held a\n  different plan element that has been lost. Do not count the bullets in an answer.\n\n## Why this matters for the exam\n\n- **Lists are the whole chapter.** The most probable midterm items are: the **three societal forces**\n  (Material Review Q1), the **seven characteristics of a project** (Q4), the **three primary objectives**\n  (Q5), the **program → project → task → work package → work unit** hierarchy (Q7), and the\n  **advantages/disadvantages/limitations** of project management (Q3, Q6). Memorize them as lists with\n  one clause of explanation each; that is exactly the format the book's own review questions ask for.\n- **Project vs. operations is the week-1 topic, so expect it in some form.** Be able to define a project\n  (PMI's \"temporary endeavour… unique product or service\", plus the authors' \"specific, finite task\"),\n  give non-project examples, and state the discriminator correctly: **routine repetition, not\n  uniqueness**. Have the third category — **quasi-projects** — ready, with the first-milestone fix.\n- **Draw and label the life cycle.** The S-curve with \"slow start / quick momentum / slow finish\", the\n  effort curve beneath it, and the alternative **goop-to-cake** exponential curve with its reversed\n  marginal returns. Be ready to say which shape a described project would follow and why — and that the\n  PM must know the **type**, not the precise shape, because budgets, schedules and the validity of\n  **earned value** depend on it.\n- **Two counter-intuitive claims are prime \"explain why\" material**: performance and schedule outrank\n  cost at **all** stages, not just early (p. 16); and the disadvantages of project management come from\n  the **same sources** as its advantages (p. 13).\n- **Risk narrows, it does not vanish.** Expect a sketch-and-explain question on Figures 1-6/1-7: the\n  uncertainty band is widest at t₀ and tightens with each re-forecast, made at fixed intervals or at\n  technological milestones.\n- **The Directed Reading supplies the essay question.** Why project management is an \"accidental\n  profession\" (two reasons), the **four-part success model with client satisfaction**, and the\n  **naive / sensible / shark** table are the most answer-shaped items in it, and the reading's own\n  questions ask you to rank the Vital Dozen and to draw the line between too little and too much planning.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"How does PMI define a project?","a":"\"A temporary endeavor undertaken to create a unique product or service\" (PMI, 2004); Meredith & Mantel add their own broader definition — a specific, finite task to be accomplished, seen as a unit, regardless of scale or duration."},{"q":"Name the three societal forces that created the need for project management.","a":"(1) The exponential expansion of human knowledge; (2) the growing demand for complex, sophisticated, customized goods and services; (3) the evolution of worldwide competitive markets for producing and consuming goods and services. All three force work onto teams that traditional structures cannot coordinate."},{"q":"List the seven characteristics of a project.","a":"Importance; performance (a well-defined set of specific end results); a life cycle with a finite due date; complex interdependencies; uniqueness (some or all elements unique); limited resources; and an environment of conflict."},{"q":"What are the three direct project objectives, and what is the project manager's primary task in relation to them?","a":"Performance (scope/specifications), time (schedule) and cost (budget). Because a function relates them, the PM's primary task is to manage the trade-offs among them, plus the trade-off against ancillary goals."},{"q":"What distinguishes a project from a non-project (routine operations)?","a":"Non-projects are routine — tasks performed over and over (a production line, weekly reports, mail delivery, a scheduled airline flight); a project is a one-time event with a finite due date, unique elements, a temporary cross-functional unit outside the routine structure, and it cannot be managed by the routines used for routine work."},{"q":"Give the project terminology hierarchy from largest to smallest.","a":"Program → project → task → work package → work unit. A program is an exceptionally large, long-range objective broken into projects; a work package is the lowest level of the work breakdown structure, used to assign costs and values."},{"q":"What is a quasi-project, and how should a manager handle one?","a":"A project whose performance/scope, deadline and/or budget are only implied, not defined (\"look into this before Friday\"). Pin down performance, schedule and budget as early as possible without antagonizing the assigner; if discovering the requirements *is* the work, make that discovery the first milestone and then review resources against the new requirements; or run it cost-plus and stop when benefits no longer justify cost."},{"q":"Distinguish direct from ancillary project goals, with an example of each.","a":"Direct goals are specified objectives of the project — performance, time, cost, plus business impact and opening future opportunities (e.g. entering a new market). Ancillary goals are mostly unstated process goals — improving the organization's project management competency and methods, individuals gaining managerial experience (e.g. learning a new skill), and protecting the health of the team and the organization."},{"q":"Who are the four parties-at-interest (stakeholders) in a project, and why do they cause trouble?","a":"The client, the parent organization, the project team and the public. Each defines success and failure differently — the client wants changes, the parent organization wants profits those changes may reduce — and project staff are often responsible to two bosses with different priorities."},{"q":"Describe the conventional project life cycle in terms of percent completion over time.","a":"An S-curve: a slow start (start-up — manager selected, team and resources assembled, work program organized), quick momentum as the real work peaks, and a slow finish because many parts must come together and team members drag their feet; the project is then terminated by its due date or phased into ongoing operations."},{"q":"What are the four phases of the effort (level-of-resources) curve, Figure 1-4?","a":"Conception; selection; planning, scheduling, monitoring and control; evaluation and termination. Effort is minimal at the start, rises to a peak, and tapers off — and because it is non-symmetrical, the progress curve is non-symmetrical too."},{"q":"What is the \"goop to cake\" life cycle and why does it matter?","a":"An exponential progress curve (Figure 1-5) where almost nothing useful exists until the end — like a cake in the last minutes of baking. Typical of outputs assembled from subunits useless on their own, some software and chemical engineering projects. Unlike the S-curve, it shows increasing marginal returns near completion and resource spending correlates poorly with progress, so earned value is not meaningful for it."},{"q":"Which project objective was traditionally thought to dominate each stage of the life cycle, and what does the research actually say?","a":"Conventional wisdom: performance early, cost during high activity, schedule at the end when the client demands delivery. Meredith & Mantel report that this is untrue — recent research indicates performance and schedule are more important than cost during all stages."},{"q":"How does uncertainty behave over the life of a project?","a":"It is widest at the start: from t₀ the band of uncertainty about final cost grows until it is very wide by the estimated end date, and it narrows with each re-forecast (t₁, t₂) as the project progresses. New forecasts of performance, time and cost are made at fixed intervals or at specific technological milestones, and the axes can be swapped to show schedule uncertainty given levels of expenditure."},{"q":"State three advantages and three disadvantages of project management, and the one-line relationship between them.","a":"Advantages: better control, better customer relations, shorter development times (also lower costs, higher quality and reliability, higher profit margins, better interdepartmental coordination, higher morale). Disadvantages: greater organizational complexity, a higher likelihood that organizational policy will be violated, and low personnel utilization (some firms also report higher costs and more management difficulty). The disadvantages stem from exactly the same sources as the advantages — they are the price paid for them."},{"q":"What are the limitations of project management?","a":"Creating a project can be an admission that the functional organization and its managers cannot deliver the outcome; conflict is a necessary side-effect; and the PM's authority is inconsistent with the assigned responsibility, so the PM depends on functional managers' goodwill for resources — escalating to senior officials may win the moment but reflects poorly on the PM and can backfire."},{"q":"Why do Pinto & Kharbanda call project management the \"accidental profession\"?","a":"Two reasons: few formal or systematic programs exist for selecting and training project managers, so training is ad hoc and most PMs fall into the role by happenstance; and nobody grows up aspiring to be a project manager, since it is neither a well-defined nor a well-understood career path — the role is thrust upon people rather than sought."},{"q":"In the Directed Reading, what is the \"fourth hurdle\" for project success, and how do the three political types compare?","a":"The fourth hurdle beyond the traditional triple constraint (budget, schedule, performance) is client use and satisfaction — a project is only as good as it is used. The political types are the naive (politics is unpleasant, avoid it, the truth will win out), the sensible (politics is necessary; network, negotiate and bargain to further departmental goals) and sharks (politics is an opportunity; self-serving, manipulative, bullying). Sharks and the naive are equally disadvantaged; the sensible middle ground succeeds."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 b01"},{"id":"b02","title":"Book 2 · Strategic Management & Project Selection ★","slides":"6e ch. 2 = 7e ch. 2 · week 2 · MIDTERM · calculations","md":"# B02 — Strategic Management and Project Selection (Meredith & Mantel ch. 2) ★\n\n_Midterm material, and **the one pre-midterm chapter with real calculations in it**. Payback period, net\npresent value, internal rate of return, profitability index and the weighted factor scoring model all live\nhere. The 2025/26 archive has nothing on any of them, so this chapter is the biggest single gap it leaves._\n\n⚠️ **Edition note.** Taken from the **7th edition** PDF; the syllabus specifies the **6th**. **This chapter keeps the same number in both editions**, so nothing here is affected — but chapters **4, 5 and 6 are permuted** between the editions, which changes what the midterm covers. `study/exam-prep.md` has the full mapping.\nPage references are 7th-edition printed pages, so check one against your own copy before quoting it.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2 Why selection matters, and the two families of model\n\nEvery project consumes resources that no other project can then use, so selection is where strategy\nactually happens. The chapter splits all selection models into two families:\n\n| Family | Basis | When it is used |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nonnumeric** | judgement, necessity, politics | older, simpler; urgent or strategically obvious projects |\n| **Numeric** | money or scores | everything else; the majority of firms use profitability alone |\n\n**The single most quotable fact in the chapter:** a large majority of firms that use selection models use\n**profitability as the sole measure of acceptability**. Chapter 2 then spends its length explaining why that\nis inadequate.\n\n## 2.3 What makes a good selection model — the six criteria\n\nA likely short-answer question. The model should have:\n\n| # | Criterion | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Realism** | Reflects the firm's actual decision situation and objectives |\n| 2 | **Capability** | Sophisticated enough to handle the relevant factors — multiple periods, risk, internal and external conditions |\n| 3 | **Flexibility** | Gives valid results across the range of conditions the firm may meet, and can be adjusted |\n| 4 | **Ease of use** | Reasonably convenient, quick to run, understandable without special training |\n| 5 | **Cost** | Data-gathering and modelling cost low relative to the project's cost |\n| 6 | **Easy computerisation** | Information is easy to gather and store, and the model runs in standard software |\n\nMemory hook: **R-C-F-E-C-E**. The pattern is that criteria 1–3 are about the model being *right* and\ncriteria 4–6 are about it being *usable*. A model that is perfectly realistic but takes six months to run\nfails just as surely as a crude one.\n\n## 2.4 Nonnumeric models — the five types\n\nThese make no calculation at all. Know the names and one line each; they are easy marks.\n\n| Model | How the decision is made | Example from the book |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sacred cow** | A senior, powerful figure suggests it, often casually. The project is \"sacred\" and survives until it succeeds or that person personally kills it | \"If you have a chance, why don't you look into…\" |\n| **Operating necessity** | The project is required to keep the system running | A dike to protect a plant from a threatened flood |\n| **Competitive necessity** | Needed to maintain the firm's competitive position | XYZ Steel rebuilding its Chicago bar mill; business schools restructuring their MBA programmes |\n| **Product line extension** | Judged by how it fits the existing line — fills a gap, strengthens a weak link, extends in a desirable direction | New product added to a range |\n| **Comparative benefit** | Many non-comparable projects ranked by overall benefit to the firm, with no formal measure of \"benefit\" | Used widely, e.g. United Way funding decisions |\n\n> **The precedence rule, which is exactly the kind of detail an exam asks for:**\n> **operating necessity takes precedence over competitive necessity**, and both may bypass the careful\n> numeric analysis applied to less urgent projects.\n\n**The Q-sort** (Fig. 2-1) is the standard technique for comparative benefit: sort projects into good /\nfair / poor piles, split the oversized piles again until you have about eight ranked groups, and iterate.\n\n## 2.4 Numeric models — profit and profitability ★\n\nThis is the calculable section. Four models, each with its own formula.\n\n### Payback period\n\n> **Payback period = initial fixed investment ÷ annual net cash inflow**\n\nBook example (p. 46): a project costs **$100,000** and returns **$25,000** a year.\n\n> Payback = 100,000 / 25,000 = **4 years**\n\n**What it ignores, and why that is the exam point:**\n- It **ignores all cash flows beyond the payback period** entirely. A project paying back in 4 years and\n  then collapsing scores the same as one paying back in 4 years and running for 20.\n- It ignores the **time value of money**.\n- It serves as an **inadequate proxy for risk** — the logic being that the faster the money comes back, the\n  less exposure, which is true but crude.\n\n**The variant that catches people out.** In the PsychoCeramic Sciences example (p. 47) the payback is\ncomputed as\n\n> PB = 315,000 / (75,900 + 31,500) = **2.9 years**\n\nNote what is in the denominator: **average annual inflow plus annual depreciation**. Depreciation is a\nnon-cash charge, so it is added back to get the actual cash available. If a question gives you a\ndepreciation figure, it expects you to add it back.\n\n### Net present value (discounted cash flow)\n\n> **NPV = −A₀ + Σ_{t=1}^{n} F_t / (1 + k)ᵗ**\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| A₀ | initial cash investment (an outflow, so it enters negative) |\n| F_t | net cash flow in period t |\n| k | required rate of return — also called the **hurdle rate** or **cutoff rate** |\n| n | project life in periods |\n\n**With inflation**, where p_t is the predicted inflation rate in period t:\n\n> **NPV = −A₀ + Σ F_t / (1 + k + p_t)ᵗ**\n\n**Decision rule: accept if NPV > 0.**\n\n**Worked example (p. 46), verified.** $100,000 invested, $25,000 a year for 8 years, required return 15%,\ninflation 3%. The discount rate is therefore **0.15 + 0.03 = 0.18**:\n\n> NPV = −100,000 + Σ_{t=1}^{8} 25,000 / 1.18ᵗ = −100,000 + 101,939 = **+$1,939**\n\nPositive, so accept. I recomputed this during ingest and it is exactly right — the eight-year annuity\nfactor at 18% is 4.0776, and 25,000 × 4.0776 = 101,939.\n\n> ⚠️ **Inflation is added to the discount rate, not subtracted.** Writing (1 + k − p) is the classic\n> error, and with these numbers it would turn a marginal accept into a large one.\n\n**The sensitivity point the book makes about its own example:** the PsychoCeramic project is accepted at a\n15% hurdle rate but **would have been rejected at 14%**. NPV answers can flip on a single point of the\ndiscount rate, which is why the hurdle rate is a strategic choice and not an arithmetic detail.\n\n### Internal rate of return\n\n> **IRR is the discount rate k at which the present value of inflows equals the present value of outflows** —\n> equivalently, the k that makes NPV = 0.\n\nThe book writes it as A₀ + A₁/(1+k) + … + Aₙ/(1+k)ⁿ = R₁/(1+k) + … + Rₙ/(1+k)ⁿ, with A for outflows and R\nfor inflows, and notes that **k is found by trial and error** (p. 48). There is no closed form.\n\n**Decision rule: accept if IRR > the required rate of return.**\n\n⚠️ The book lists as a disadvantage that **IRR can produce multiple solutions** when the cash flow sign\nchanges more than once. That is a favourite exam detail.\n\n### Profitability index (benefit–cost ratio)\n\n> **Profitability index = NPV of all future expected cash flows ÷ initial cash investment**\n\n**Decision rule: accept if the ratio is greater than 1.0.** Some firms do not discount the flows when\ncomputing it, which makes it cruder still.\n\n### The advantages and disadvantages of all profitability models\n\nBoth lists are enumerable and therefore examinable.\n\n**Advantages (5):**\n1. The undiscounted models are simple to use and understand.\n2. They use readily available accounting data.\n3. Output is in terms familiar to business decision makers.\n4. Output is mostly on an absolute scale, allowing absolute go/no-go decisions.\n5. Some can be amended to account for project risk.\n\n**Disadvantages (8):**\n1. They ignore all nonmonetary factors except risk.\n2. Undiscounted models ignore the timing of cash flows and the time value of money.\n3. Discounted models are strongly **biased toward the short run**.\n4. Payback-type models ignore cash flows beyond the payback period.\n5. **IRR can produce multiple solutions.**\n6. All are sensitive to errors in the early-year input data.\n7. All discounting models are nonlinear, so the effect of an input error is not obvious.\n8. All depend on cash flow, yet how to define cash flow for project evaluation is not clear.\n\nThe deep criticism underneath the list: these models measure only what is denominated in money, and most of\nwhat makes a project strategically right or wrong is not.\n\n## 2.4 Numeric models — scoring ★\n\nScoring models exist to fix disadvantage 1: they let nonmonetary criteria into the decision.\n\n### Unweighted factor model\n\nList the criteria, score each project on each, add up. The book's Figure 2-2 sample form uses a simple\nqualifying checklist and totals the ticks (12 against 5 in its example).\n\nThe scales themselves must be defined. The book gives two contrasting five-point scales (p. 49):\n\n| Score | \"Estimated annual profits\" — objective | \"Quality of the final product\" — subjective |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5 | above $1,100,000 | significantly and visibly improved |\n| 4 | $750,001 – $1,100,000 | significantly improved, not visible to buyer |\n| 3 | $500,001 – $750,000 | not significantly changed |\n| 2 | $200,000 – $500,000 | significantly lowered, not visible to buyer |\n| 1 | less than $200,000 | significantly and visibly lowered |\n\nTwo things worth noticing. The profit bands are chosen so that **about 20% of candidate projects fall in\neach**, which is what makes a five-point scale discriminate rather than bunch. And the second scale scores\n**opinion**, not fact — the book says so explicitly. Mixing objective and subjective criteria in one total\nis normal here, and is also the model's main weakness.\n\n### Weighted factor scoring model — the formula to memorise\n\n> **S_i = Σ_{j=1}^{n} s_ij · w_j**\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| S_i | total score of project i |\n| s_ij | score of project i on criterion j |\n| w_j | weight of criterion j |\n\nEach project's score on each criterion is multiplied by that criterion's weight, and the products are\nsummed. Highest total wins.\n\n**Worked micro-example** (built during ingest; the book gives the formula without a compact numeric case):\n\n| Criterion | Weight w | Project A score | Project B score |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Profitability | 0.40 | 5 | 3 |\n| Fit with product line | 0.30 | 2 | 5 |\n| Technical risk (5 = low) | 0.20 | 3 | 4 |\n| Environmental impact | 0.10 | 4 | 4 |\n| **Weighted total** | **1.00** | **3.6** | **3.9** |\n\nA: 0.40(5) + 0.30(2) + 0.20(3) + 0.10(4) = 2.0 + 0.6 + 0.6 + 0.4 = **3.6**\nB: 0.40(3) + 0.30(5) + 0.20(4) + 0.10(4) = 1.2 + 1.5 + 0.8 + 0.4 = **3.9**\n\n**B wins, 3.9 to 3.6, even though A scores top marks on profitability and B only middling.** A's weakness\nis its poor fit with the product line, a criterion carrying 30% of the weight. That inversion is the whole\npoint of the model: a weighted score can reverse the ranking a pure profitability model would give, and an\nexam may well ask you to explain in one sentence why the less profitable project won.\n\n**Check your arithmetic by confirming the weights sum to 1.0.** If they do, the weighted total must land\ninside the score range (here 1 to 5); a total of 7 means an arithmetic slip.\n\n### Where the weights come from\n\n- **The Delphi technique** — the book calls it the most effective and most widely used. Developed by Brown\n  and Dalkey at RAND in the 1950s–60s. It converts subjective verbal judgements into numbers through\n  iterated anonymous rounds of expert estimates.\n- **The Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP)** — a similar and popular alternative based on pairwise\n  comparisons.\n\n### Advantages and limitations of scoring models\n\n**Advantages**, in the book's own framing: they allow multiple criteria including non-financial ones,\nthey are structurally simple, they are easy to adapt as conditions change, and **weighted models reflect\nthat some criteria matter more than others**.\n\n**Limitations:** the criteria are assumed independent, which is rarely true; the output is a **relative**\nmeasure, so it ranks projects against each other but gives no absolute go/no-go; and the whole structure\nrests on the weights, so — the book's sharpest line — if the weights are wrong the model \"rests on a faulty\nfoundation\".\n\n## 2.5 Analysis under uncertainty — risk\n\nSelection decisions use estimates, and estimates are uncertain. The chapter's response is to treat\nuncertainty explicitly rather than pretending a single-point estimate is a fact. The techniques it points\nat are **simulation** (notably Monte Carlo methods, which reappear in chapter 8 with Crystal Ball) and\n**sensitivity analysis** — varying one input and watching whether the decision changes. The\nPsychoCeramic 14%-versus-15% flip is a sensitivity analysis in miniature.\n\n## 2.6 The information base for selection\n\nA short but pointed section: every model above is only as good as the data fed into it, and the data are\nusually produced by people who want their project funded. Treat the information base as part of the model,\nnot as a neutral input.\n\n## 2.7 The Project Portfolio Process — eight steps\n\nThe PPP links project selection back to **strategy**, choosing a *set* of projects rather than judging each\none alone. The eight steps:\n\n| Step | Action |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | **Establish a project council** |\n| 2 | **Identify project categories and criteria** |\n| 3 | **Collect project data** |\n| 4 | **Assess resource availability** |\n| 5 | **Reduce the project and criteria set** |\n| 6 | **Prioritise the projects within categories** |\n| 7 | **Select the projects to be funded and held in reserve** |\n| 8 | **Implement the process** |\n\nThe logic to remember: steps 1–2 build the machinery, 3–4 gather the facts, 5–7 make the decision, and 8\nmakes it repeatable. **Step 4 is the one people forget** — a portfolio is constrained by resources, so a\nset of individually attractive projects can still be collectively impossible.\n\n## 2.8 Project proposals\n\nThe proposal is how a project enters the selection process. The chapter's practical point is that a\nproposal has to carry the technical approach, the implementation plan, the schedule, and the cost and\nbudget, because those are what the selection models consume.\n\n## Why this matters for the exam\n\n- **This is where the midterm's calculations are.** Chapters 1, 3, 4 and 5 are descriptive; chapter 2 and\n  chapter 8 are the two that can ask you to compute something. Expect payback, NPV or a weighted scoring\n  model.\n- **Know the five nonnumeric models by name** and the precedence rule between operating and competitive\n  necessity.\n- **Know the six criteria for a good model** (realism, capability, flexibility, ease of use, cost, easy\n  computerisation).\n- **Be able to state the decision rule for each numeric model**: payback — shorter is better, no absolute\n  rule; NPV — accept if positive; IRR — accept if above the required rate; profitability index — accept if\n  above 1.0.\n- **Expect a \"why is this model inadequate\" question.** The eight disadvantages are the answer bank.\n- **The weighted scoring formula S_i = Σ s_ij w_j is short enough to be asked for verbatim.**\n\n## Formula summary\n\n| Quantity | Formula | Accept when |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payback period | initial investment ÷ annual net cash inflow | shorter than the firm's cutoff |\n| Payback with depreciation | investment ÷ (average inflow + depreciation) | as above |\n| Net present value | −A₀ + Σ F_t/(1+k)ᵗ | NPV > 0 |\n| NPV with inflation | −A₀ + Σ F_t/(1+k+p_t)ᵗ | NPV > 0 |\n| Internal rate of return | the k making NPV = 0 (trial and error) | IRR > required rate |\n| Profitability index | NPV of future flows ÷ initial investment | ratio > 1.0 |\n| Weighted factor score | S_i = Σ_j s_ij · w_j | highest S_i |\n\n## Verification note\n\nBoth of the book's numeric examples were recomputed during ingest and both are correct: the payback of\n4 years, and the NPV of **+$1,939** at a combined 18% discount rate over eight years. The PsychoCeramic\npayback of 2.9 years also checks out at 315,000 ÷ 107,400. The weighted-scoring micro-example above is\nmine, not the book's, and its arithmetic is shown in full so it can be checked.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What are the two families of project selection model?","a":"Nonnumeric (judgement-based) and numeric (money or score based); nonnumeric models are older and simpler."},{"q":"What single measure do most firms use for project selection?","a":"Profitability, used as the sole measure of acceptability by a large majority of firms."},{"q":"Name the six criteria of a good selection model.","a":"Realism, capability, flexibility, ease of use, cost, easy computerisation."},{"q":"Name the five nonnumeric selection models.","a":"Sacred cow, operating necessity, competitive necessity, product line extension, comparative benefit."},{"q":"What is a sacred cow project?","a":"One suggested by a senior powerful figure, often casually; it survives until it succeeds or that person personally terminates it."},{"q":"Distinguish operating from competitive necessity.","a":"Operating necessity keeps the system running at all (a dike against a flood); competitive necessity maintains market position."},{"q":"Which takes precedence, operating or competitive necessity?","a":"Operating necessity — and both may bypass the numeric analysis used for less urgent projects."},{"q":"What is the comparative benefit model, and what technique implements it?","a":"Ranking many non-comparable projects by overall benefit with no formal measure; implemented with the Q-sort."},{"q":"State the payback period formula.","a":"Payback = initial fixed investment ÷ annual net cash inflow."},{"q":"A project costs $100,000 and returns $25,000 a year. Payback?","a":"Four years."},{"q":"Name three weaknesses of the payback period.","a":"It ignores all cash flows beyond payback, ignores the time value of money, and is only a crude proxy for risk."},{"q":"Why is depreciation added back when computing payback in the PsychoCeramic example?","a":"Depreciation is a non-cash charge, so adding it back gives the actual cash available: 315,000/(75,900+31,500) = 2.9 years."},{"q":"State the NPV formula and define its terms.","a":"NPV = −A₀ + Σ F_t/(1+k)ᵗ, where A₀ is the initial investment, F_t the net flow in period t, and k the required rate of return."},{"q":"How is inflation handled in the NPV formula?","a":"It is added to the discount rate: NPV = −A₀ + Σ F_t/(1+k+p_t)ᵗ."},{"q":"$100,000 invested, $25,000 a year for 8 years, k = 15%, inflation 3%. Give the NPV and the verdict.","a":"Discount at 18%; NPV = −100,000 + 101,939 = +$1,939, so accept."},{"q":"What are the other names for the required rate of return?","a":"The hurdle rate or the cutoff rate."},{"q":"Define the internal rate of return.","a":"The discount rate at which the present values of inflows and outflows are equal, i.e. the rate making NPV zero; found by trial and error."},{"q":"What is the known defect of the IRR model?","a":"It can produce multiple solutions when cash flows change sign more than once."},{"q":"State the profitability index and its decision rule.","a":"NPV of future expected cash flows ÷ initial investment; accept if greater than 1.0."},{"q":"Give three disadvantages of profitability models.","a":"They ignore all nonmonetary factors except risk, discounted versions are biased toward the short run, and all are sensitive to early-year input errors."},{"q":"Why do scoring models exist?","a":"To let nonmonetary and multiple criteria into the decision, which profitability models cannot do."},{"q":"State the weighted factor scoring formula and define its terms.","a":"S_i = Σ_j s_ij·w_j, where s_ij is project i's score on criterion j and w_j is that criterion's weight."},{"q":"Why are five-point scale bands chosen so each holds about 20% of projects?","a":"So the scale discriminates between candidates instead of bunching them all into one or two scores."},{"q":"What is the main structural weakness of a scoring model?","a":"It rests entirely on the weights, and it assumes the criteria are independent, which is rarely true."},{"q":"Is a scoring model's output absolute or relative?","a":"Relative — it ranks projects against each other but gives no absolute go/no-go decision."},{"q":"Which two techniques generate the weights, and who developed the first?","a":"The Delphi technique, developed by Brown and Dalkey at RAND in the 1950s–60s, and the Analytic Hierarchy Process."},{"q":"Name the eight steps of the Project Portfolio Process.","a":"Establish a project council; identify categories and criteria; collect project data; assess resource availability; reduce the project and criteria set; prioritise within categories; select projects to fund and reserve; implement the process."},{"q":"Which PPP step is most often forgotten, and why does it matter?","a":"Assessing resource availability — a set of individually attractive projects can be collectively impossible."},{"q":"What does the PsychoCeramic 14%-versus-15% comparison demonstrate?","a":"NPV decisions can flip on one point of the hurdle rate, so the rate is a strategic choice, not an arithmetic detail."},{"q":"What is the deeper criticism of all profitability models?","a":"They measure only what is denominated in money, while most of what makes a project strategically right or wrong is not."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 b02"},{"id":"b03","title":"Book 3 · The Project Manager ★","slides":"6e ch. 3 = 7e ch. 3 · week 3 · MIDTERM","md":"# B03 — The Project Manager (ch. 3 in both the 6th and 7th editions)\n\n*The role chapter: who the PM is, what the PM is answerable for, which demands are unique to the job, how a PM is selected, and what changes when the project crosses cultures. Printed pp. 107–160. Midterm material — the mid-term is 40% of IE408 and covers chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8.*\n\n> ### ⚠️ Edition mapping — read this once\n>\n> The syllabus specifies the **6th edition** and examines **chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8**. The PDF in the\n> course folder is the **7th edition**, and the two **permute chapters 4, 5 and 6**:\n>\n> | 6th ed. (what the syllabus means) | 7th ed. (what the PDF has) | On the midterm? |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | 1 Projects in Contemporary Organizations | 1 (same) | **yes** |\n> | 2 Strategic Management and Project Selection | 2 (same) | **yes** |\n> | 3 The Project Manager | 3 (same) | **yes** |\n> | 4 Project Organization | **5** The Project in the Organizational Structure | **yes** |\n> | 5 Project Planning | **6** Project Activity Planning | **yes** |\n> | 6 Conflict and Negotiation | **4** Negotiation and the Management of Conflict | **no** |\n> | 8 Scheduling | 8 (same) | **yes** |\n>\n> Files here are numbered by the **6th-edition** chapter, because that is what the syllabus cites.\n> Page references are **7th-edition printed pages**, so do not quote them at the professor.\n> **Confirm the scope with Dr. Duraković** — this mapping is inferred, and it decides what you revise.\n\n\n> ⚠️ **Label mismatch, read this once.** The IE408 week-3 entry is called *\"Project environment; organisation and stakeholders\"* with reading \"Chapter 3\". The chapter's actual title is **\"The Project Manager\"**. Organisational structure (functional / project / matrix) is **chapter 4 in the 6th edition** (chapter 5 in the 7th-edition PDF) — see B04, not here. Chapter 3 does cover *environment* — but in the narrow sense of §3.4–3.6: cultural, economic, political, legal and sociotechnical surroundings of a multicultural project. So revise this chapter as \"the PM role + cross-cultural environment\", and do not expect to find the organisation charts of a matrix here.\n\n**How this chapter is examined.** It is almost entirely descriptive — no formulas, no calculations. The examinable substance is therefore the **lists and the distinctions**: three responsibility areas, seven special demands, four selection skill categories, four/five team-member characteristics, four elements of culture, and the Posner tables. Memorise the lists with their counts; the distinctions (functional vs project manager, analytic vs systems, culture vs microculture, Type 1 vs Type 2) are what short-answer questions are built from.\n\n**PMBOK links the book makes:** chapters 3 and 4 map to **Human Resource Management** (knowledge area 6), and §3.2 is the book's first treatment of **Communications** (knowledge area 7) (p. 107, p. 114).\n\n---\n\n## Chapter frame (p. 107–108)\n\n- The PM must be appointed **before real progress is possible**, and is \"probably the major resource input to the project\" compared with team, capital and materials (p. 107).\n- Appointment timing: normally as soon as the project is funded, sometimes earlier (if the PM originated the project, start-up is simplified), occasionally late in the life cycle to replace a departing PM. The book's example: an agricultural products firm runs a senior scientist as PM until the technical problems are solved, then swaps in a marketing middle manager — and says the transition is difficult and results are sometimes unsatisfactory (p. 107).\n- **The PM's first set of tasks** (p. 107): preliminary budget and schedule; help select the team; get to know the client (internal or external); make sure facilities are available; secure early-life supplies; handle the routine start-up details. (The *initial project meeting* is deliberately deferred to ch. 5.)\n- **Two conditions decide the outcome and neither is fully under the PM's control** (pp. 107–108), though the PM can influence both early:\n  1. **Degree of top-management support** — strong, reasonably unqualified support greatly raises the chance of success (Pinto et al. 1989; Zimmerer et al. 1998).\n  2. **Orientation of team members** — **problem-oriented** rather than **discipline-oriented**. Hughes (1998) on SAGE and Atlas: teams \"polarized around problems rather than disciplines\" (p. 108).\n- The book's working assumptions: a large **functionally organised** parent firm running many projects alongside routine operations, with a technical component and an arms-length customer — deliberately the most demanding case. \"Technical component\" includes non-hardware methodology: a systems analysis, a statement of functional requirements, a due-diligence document (p. 108).\n\n---\n\n## 3.1 Project management and the project manager\n\n### Functional manager vs project manager — the core distinction (pp. 108–109)\n\n| | **Functional manager** | **Project manager** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Expertise | Specialist in the department's area | Generalist across many functional areas |\n| Mental method | **Analytic approach** — break the whole into parts | **Systems approach** — understand the part through the whole |\n| Primary skill | **Analysis** | **Synthesis** |\n| Role toward people | Direct **technical supervisor** | **Facilitator** |\n| Decides | How it will be done, who does it, what resources go to the task | Organising, staffing, budgeting, directing, planning, controlling the *project* |\n\nThe book's image: the PM starts as a technical specialist and \"must now metamorphose from technical caterpillar into generalist butterfly\" (p. 108).\n\n- A **system** = a set of interrelated components that accepts inputs and produces outputs **in a purposeful manner** (p. 108). \"Purposeful\" restricts attention to systems involving humans; inputs/outputs imply a **boundary** separating system from **environment**; the interrelationships define the **structure**.\n- The analytic method is not wrong, merely **inadequate** for a complex system — dissecting a frog gives only partial understanding of the frog (p. 109). Pascal, quoted by van Gigch: impossible to know the parts without the whole, or the whole without the parts (p. 109).\n- Both managers need technical knowledge, but of different kinds: the functional manager in the **technology of the process**, the PM in the **science of project management** *plus* some technical competence in the project work. The book notes researchers **disagree on how much** technical knowledge a PM needs (p. 109) — a safe exam phrasing is \"reasonable understanding, not expert depth\".\n- **Three questions of synthesis** facing the PM (p. 109): *what* needs to be done, *when* must it be done, and *how* are the required resources to be obtained.\n- **Separation of powers** (pp. 109–110): functional managers often pick the people, may design the technology, and influence resource deployment — yet carry little or no direct responsibility for results. The PM \"manages\" the project. The arrangement is a permanent source of discomfort; the PM must not let the functional manager usurp project control, and the functional manager must not let the PM take over technical decisions or personnel assignment.\n- **Micromanagement** (p. 110): a senior manager withdraws the PM's delegated powers and runs the project. It kills creativity and initiative, frustrates everyone and ensures mediocre performance or failure. The authors' blunt advice: a micromanaged PM should **request a transfer**.\n- The opposite pole is a **collegial** relationship — minimal conflict, cooperation as norm, nobody fussing about credit, high likelihood of success. But: **collegiality without talent still fails**, \"even if the project team smiles a lot while failing\" (p. 110).\n\n### ★ The PM's three areas of responsibility (pp. 110–111)\n\n*Why it matters: this is the single most quotable list in the chapter and matches review question 3 (\"Discuss the PM's responsibilities toward the project team members\").*\n\n| # | Responsible **to** | What that means concretely |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **The parent organisation** | Proper conservation of resources; timely and accurate project communications; careful, competent management of the project. Keep senior management fully informed of status, cost, timing and prospects; warn of likely future problems and of over-budget / late risk, with remedies. **Above all: never let senior management be surprised.** |\n| 2 | **The project and the client** | Preserve the integrity of the project against the conflicting demands of all parties with legitimate interests; sort understanding from misunderstanding, balance rivalries, cater to the client — while still delivering on time, within budget, to specification. |\n| 3 | **The project team** | Dictated by the project's **finite** nature and the team's specialisation: be concerned with team members' futures after the project. If the PM does not help with the transition back to functional homes or on to new projects, workers start protecting their careers instead of finishing the project (→ ch. 13, termination). |\n\n- **Tweed coat management** (p. 111) — the discredited notion that highly educated researchers need a \"special type\" of managing. In the book's example a scientist/PM was shielded from schedules and budgets, which an outsider kept; those projects \"invariably far exceeded budget and schedule\". The two hidden assumptions (all researchers wear tweed; more education means fewer street smarts) have **no supporting evidence**. Scientists respond to a caring, supportive style and can keep reasonable records.\n\n### PM career paths (pp. 111–113)\n\n- Many projects are too small to need a full-time manager, so quite a few PMs run several at once. Small, short projects are an **excellent training ground** for new PMs and a scaled-down version of running a whole organisation — hence projects are a growth environment for future executives (review question 1).\n- Procter & Gamble example: management trainees are given project responsibility (e.g. guiding a new cosmetic through toxicity testing) and learn planning, follow-through, negotiation and political sensitivity (p. 112).\n- Honest caveat (p. 112): if project management sounds orderly and rational, the authors apologise — the one-word descriptor is **\"messy\"** (Kotter 1982 on how unstructured real general managers are). Precisely because of that, good planning and organising skills matter more, \"or the chaos becomes unmanageable\".\n- A typical path climbs by project size: tooling manager (small U) → project engineer (larger V) → manufacturing manager (large W) → deputy PM (large X) → PM (small Y) → PM (large Z) (p. 112).\n- Multiple career paths to the top are \"more talked about than acted on\"; the authors know of no specific path taking PMs to CEO, with few exceptions — **Eli Lilly**, whose new-drug projects run 8–12 years, built one (p. 112, footnote). In many firms PM experience is nonetheless a desirable or mandatory rung, because the PM demonstrates goal achievement **without de jure authority** in near-chaotic conditions (p. 114).\n- **Practice box — AT&T** (pp. 113–114). After deregulation AT&T turned project coordination (low status, a temporary stepping-stone *out* of PM work) into a profession, creating a National Project Management organisation. Career ladder: **Trainee** (6 months) → **Cost Analysis/Schedule Engineer** (6–18 months) → **Site Manager** (6–12 months) → **Small Project Manager** ($1–3M) → **Project Manager** ($3–25M) → **Program Manager** (multiyear, >$25M). Skills sought from the Leadership Continuity Plan: interpersonal leadership; oral and written communication; a presidential big-picture perspective; political sensitivity; delegating, problem-solver orientation; optimistic can-do attitude; planner mentality; **kaizen** (continuous improvement) spirit; administrative in-charge credibility. Note the cultural change required: replace admiration for **heroic rescues** with admiration for competent work from the start.\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 Special demands on the project manager\n\n### ★ The seven special demands (pp. 114–126)\n\n*Why it matters: the chapter's §3.2 subheadings and the chapter summary (p. 143) are two wordings of the same list. Learn the seven, and know both labels for #5 and #6.*\n\n| # | Demand (§3.2 heading) | Summary wording (p. 143) | Core content |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Acquiring Adequate Resources** | Acquiring adequate physical resources | Initial budgets are frequently insufficient |\n| 2 | **Acquiring and Motivating Personnel** | Acquiring and motivating personnel | People must be \"borrowed\" from functional departments |\n| 3 | **Dealing with Obstacles** | Dealing with obstacles | A unique project guarantees a series of crises |\n| 4 | **Making Project Goal Trade-offs** | Making goal trade-offs | Cost / schedule / performance, plus project-vs-firm-vs-career |\n| 5 | **Failure and the Risk and Fear of Failure** | Maintaining a balanced outlook in the team | Mood swings between elation and despair must be damped |\n| 6 | **Breadth of Communication** | Communicating with all parties | Constant selling, reselling and explaining |\n| 7 | **Negotiation** | Negotiating | Underpins all six above; whole of ch. 4 |\n\n**1. Acquiring adequate resources (pp. 114–116).** Budgets fall short because of proposers' natural optimism, purposeful understatement to win funding, or genuine uncertainty (no point buying a centrifuge now if the right type is known only in nine months). The good PM knows the **resource trade-offs**: a skilled machinist can work with unsophisticated machinery where a beginner cannot; subcontracting substitutes for missing programmers but costs instruction time and may cause delays.\n  - **The estimate-discounting phenomenon** (Gagnon 1982; Gagnon et al. 1987; pp. 115–116): the person doing the task sometimes **over**estimates time and cost; the immediate supervisor discounts that pessimism and may **under**estimate; each successive level up the hierarchy lowers the numbers again. Recognising it does not prevent it.\n  - Resources are perceived as strictly limited, so acquisition becomes a **win–lose** game, and the winners are often the managers with political connections — hence the value of a **champion** or **sponsor** high in the parent organisation (p. 116). Cautionary case: the Chicago Irish-potato-famine monument ($1M, four storeys, committee formed 1994) stalled when the church sponsor who championed it moved city in 1999 — the risk of long multiyear efforts.\n  - **Practice box — Katrina** (p. 115): Melvin Wilson, a marketing manager at 1,250-employee Mississippi Power, became \"Director of Storm Logistics\" overnight, charged with restoring power to 195,000 customers in 12 days; both storm centres were lost, 11,000 repairmen from 24 states and Canada arrived, needing housing, 5,000 trucks, 140,000 gallons of fuel a day, 8,000 tetanus shots. Delivered in 12 days. The point: resource acquisition and logistics, far beyond prior experience.\n\n**2. Acquiring and motivating personnel (pp. 116–118).** Most people must be **borrowed** from functional departments, so the PM negotiates twice — with the functional manager, then with the individuals. The PM wants exactly the two types the functional manager most wants to keep: people with **scarce necessary skills** and **top producers**. The functional manager may additionally be jealous of a more glamorous project. The functional manager retains control of **evaluation, salary and promotion** for loaned staff, so the PM can mostly only offer the **challenge of the work itself** — which Herzberg (1968) argues is often enough. Souder (1974) showed R&D lab output is **not** correlated with the level of freedom in the lab; individuals have unique needs for freedom vs direction. Motivation problems are milder on routine, repeated projects (construction, maintenance) where the PM has de facto influence over pay and promotion. NASA anecdote: \"We don't motivate them, but, boy, are we careful about whom we select.\"\n\n### ★ Characteristics of effective project team members (pp. 117–118)\n\n*Why it matters: review question 5 asks for these directly, and only the first is normally considered during selection.*\n\n| # | Characteristic | Why the book includes it |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **High-quality technical skills** | Solve most technical problems in-house; minor glitches arrive at inconvenient times and must be fixed fast, not queued behind the functional department's own work |\n| 2 | **Political sensitivity** | Senior project members, not just the PM, must protect the delicate balance of power between projects and functional units; the politically inept upset it |\n| 3 | **Strong problem orientation** | Pill (1971): multidisciplinary projects succeed far more often with problem-oriented than discipline-oriented members; discipline-oriented people ignore whatever lies outside their training |\n| 4 | **Strong goal orientation** | Projects punish focus on activity rather than results; workflow is uneven (60-hour weeks, then lulls) — \"clock watchers\" will not succeed |\n| 5 | **High self-esteem** | So members do not hide failures or risks. \"Never let the boss be surprised\"; egos strong enough to share credit and blame, to be held accountable, and to keep ethical standards under uncertainty. Shooting the messenger stops all bad news from below |\n\n> ⚠️ **Discrepancy in the book.** The §3.2 text enumerates **five** characteristics (above). The chapter summary (p. 143) lists only **four**, dropping *strong goal orientation*. If an exam question says \"the four characteristics\", give the summary's four; if it says \"name the characteristics\", give all five and note that goal orientation is sometimes folded into problem orientation.\n\n**3. Dealing with obstacles (pp. 118–119).** Uniqueness means crises. **Scope creep** — changes in required project performance — is named here as a typical one. Better planning means fewer crises, but no planning can anticipate everything: \"the successful PM is a fire fighter by avocation.\"\n  - Early fires are about **resources**: each level of the hierarchy pares the budget/schedule, and each cut must be translated back into technical plans (shorter tests, cut supplier lead times). The obvious \"cure\" of **padding** the budget is unethical, a bad idea, and creates worse problems than it solves (p. 119, → ch. 7).\n  - Experience is the asset: the PM learns from own projects, the wise PM also from others' war stories, which become early-warning signals and a bank of pretested remedies. Fire-fighting should be **organised** so fires are detected early and assigned to specialists.\n  - Warning about **\"adrenalin junkies\"**: someone addicted to the excitement of crises may be an **arsonist** — watch them (p. 119).\n  - Late-project obstacles cluster into two: (a) last-minute schedule and technical changes — handle them \"the best you can\" and be ready to scramble; (b) uncertainty about what happens to team members at the end — and here the key is **open communication**, including emotions, worries and anxieties, not only facts.\n\n**4. Making project goal trade-offs (pp. 119–121).** Four nested layers of trade-off:\n  1. Among the project's own goals — **cost, time, performance** (plus ancillary goals).\n  2. Between **progress and process** — technical advance vs smooth running of the team. Near the end, members may have to do work they are not trained for or dislike (copying, collating the final report); their reaction is a good reading of **morale**, and another argument for problem-oriented people.\n  3. **Between several projects** when the PM runs more than one — avoid the appearance of favouritism; the authors recommend staggering the projects' life cycles so they do not demand the same constrained resources at the same time.\n  4. Between the **project's goals and the firm's goals**, and finally between project, firm and the **PM's own career**.\n  - **The ethics trap of enthusiasm** (p. 121): enthusiasm is a prime requirement for success, yet it can lead the PM to (1) overstate the project's benefits, (2) understate probable completion costs, (3) ignore technical difficulties in reaching required performance, and (4) make trade-offs biased toward the project and against the parent organisation — and to take unjustified risks.\n\n**Table 3-1 — relative importance of objectives by life-cycle stage (p. 120).** Conventional wisdom said performance dominates at the start, cost in the middle, schedule at the end. Kalu (1993) and Kloppenborg et al. (1990) showed that is **not** true:\n\n| Life-cycle stage | Cost | Schedule | Performance | Reading |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Formation** | 1 | 1 | 1 | No significant difference — design to meet all client-set goals; every objective is vulnerable |\n| **Buildup** | 3 | 1 | 2 | **Schedule dominates** (scheduling commitments are made here) > performance > cost |\n| **Main** | 3 | 1 | 1 | Schedule and performance roughly tied, both well above cost (cost's importance rises a little vs buildup) |\n| **Phaseout** | 3 | 2 | 1 | **Performance** > schedule > cost |\n\n*Note: 1 = high importance. Source in the book: Kloppenborg et al. 1990, p. 78.* Cost is never the top-ranked objective after formation — review question 19 asks exactly why cost drops right after formation.\nWorked illustration of over-delivering to be safe (p. 120): a client required query answers within **3 seconds 95%** of the time; the firm designed to **1.5 seconds 50%** of the time, and by meeting that stricter internal standard met the client's.\n\n**5. Failure and the risk and fear of failure (pp. 121–122).** What looks like failure at one moment may look like success later. Split projects by how well they are understood:\n\n| | **Type 1 project** | **Type 2 project** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nature | Well-understood, routine (e.g. routine construction) | Poorly understood; large uncertainty about what should be done |\n| When trouble appears | Looks simple at the start; problems arrive **later** in the life cycle, when they are hardest to absorb | Many difficulties **early** — \"planning problems\" |\n| Root cause | Not organised to handle unexpected crises and deviations, and/or lacking the technical expertise to do so | Failure to **define the mission** carefully, and sometimes to get client acceptance of the mission |\n| Consequence | Commonly late and over budget — but that is rarely why they fail | Bad schedule/plan, wrong people, inability to handle later crises; looks technical but is a requirements/specification failure |\n\n  - Contingency allowances are usually built into Type 1 budgets, but late problems cannot be absorbed without slack — \"change orders are always received after the final design is set in concrete\" (p. 121).\n  - The **psychic** consequence is the PM's real job here: technical snags send waves of pessimism and optimism through the staff, and both extremes hurt. Blind optimism (\"everything will turn out all right\") is bad; despair (\"why try when we are destined to fail?\") is worse. Setting slack large enough for Murphy's law but small enough not to arouse suspicion in cost-conscious senior management is equally delicate (p. 122).\n\n**6. Breadth of communication (pp. 122–125).** Most of the PM's time goes on communicating (Mintzberg 1973): constant **selling, reselling and explaining** the project to outsiders, top management, functional departments, clients, other parties-at-interest and the team. The PM is the project's liaison with the outside world while also solving lab problems, handling field crises, cajoling subcontractors and reducing interpersonal conflict — possibly all in one day. Four fundamental issues sit underneath:\n  1. **Know why the project exists** — fully understand its intent and have a clear definition of how success or failure will be judged, or trade-offs will drift toward goals top management never intended.\n  2. **Accept that failure happens** — competent managers are rarely ruined by one failure, but repeated failure reads as incompetence; in general do not volunteer to take over a project with a high probability of failure.\n  3. **Secure top-management support** — weak support clouds the project's future and makes an R&D project more likely to be terminated (Green 1995). The lukewarm-VP example: going to the CEO to override a vice-president is very risky either way, so the PM's job description should include \"construction and maintenance of alliances with the leaders of functional areas\".\n  4. **Build and maintain a solid information network** — customer complaints, department-head criticism, who is favourable, vendor price changes, a looming supplier strike. Too little information blinds the PM to an incipient crisis; **too much desensitises** the PM to early warnings.\n  - And finally, **flexibility** in as many ways, with as many people and activities as possible: every decision narrows future decisions, but failing to decide stops the project — itself a trade-off. The PM still needs the support of non-involved middle and upper-middle management (p. 125).\n  - **Practice box — the Wreckmaster** (pp. 123–125). After a 10-car derailment on the Lexington Line (14th St–Union Square, 28 August 1991), the NYCTA appointed a **\"Wreckmaster\"** (Larry Gamache, general superintendent of track operations — 24 years' service, prior field supervision of derailments, and a three-year subway reconstruction project delivered early and under budget). Eight phases: **1 respond to injury · 2 secure the area · 3 initiate command facilities · 4 remove debris · 5 remove damaged equipment · 6 facility repair · 7 test · 8 clean-up** (phases 1–3 run concurrently). He built a mental flow chart of predecessors and concurrent tasks, set team captains per phase, ran a command centre and held regular meetings for all parties — i.e. precedence logic plus breadth of communication. Target was Tuesday rush hour after the holiday weekend; trains ran late Monday evening.\n\n**7. Negotiation (p. 125).** Every one of the six demands above — resources, personnel, obstacles, trade-offs, fear of failure, communication patterns — depends directly on negotiation skill. \"There is almost no aspect of the PM's job that does not depend directly on this skill.\" Ch. 4 is devoted to it.\n\n---\n\n## 3.3 Selecting the project manager\n\nSelection of the PM is \"one of the two or three most important decisions concerning the project\" (p. 126).\n\n### ★ The eight commonly-sought attributes — the \"not right\" list (p. 126)\n\n*Why it matters: the book sets this list up only to knock it down, which makes it a perfect trick question. These reasons \"are not so much wrong as they are 'not right' \" — they miss the key criterion.*\n\n1. A strong technical background\n2. A hard-nosed manager\n3. A mature individual\n4. Someone who is currently available\n5. Someone on good terms with senior executives\n6. A person who can keep the project team happy\n7. One who has worked in several different departments\n8. A person who can walk on (or part) the waters\n\n> **The key criterion: the best PM is the one who can get the job done.** Hard workers are easy to find; what is rare is the individual focused on completing a difficult job — a **\"closer\"**. Of all desirable characteristics, this **drive to complete the task** is the most important (p. 126). The same point is repeated in the summary (p. 143) and in the class-project instruction (p. 147): pick the one who can get the job done, **not** the one who is merely available — and do **not** appoint co-PMs unless they have a history of working well together, because then nobody knows who is responsible for what.\n\n### ★ The four categories of required skills = the selection criteria (pp. 126–129)\n\n*Why it matters: review question 2 is literally \"Name the categories of skills that should be considered in the selection of a project manager.\" Note the book's rule that it is not enough to possess these — they must also be **perceived** by others. \"The fact and the perception are both important.\"*\n\n| # | Skill category | What it consists of |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Credibility** | **Technical credibility** + **administrative credibility** (see below) |\n| 2 | **Sensitivity** | Political antennae; sensing interpersonal conflict; technical sensors for hidden failure |\n| 3 | **Leadership, ethics and management style** | Interpersonal influence toward goals; a strong sense of ethics; a style adapted to the project |\n| 4 | **Ability to handle stress** | Four identified causes, below |\n\n**1a. Technical credibility (pp. 126–127).** The PM must be *perceived* by client, senior executives, functional departments and team as knowing enough to direct the project. Reasonable technical competence is associated with success and is seen by team members as a positive leadership characteristic (Ford et al. 1992; Zimmerer et al. 1998). \"Technical\" explicitly includes accounting, law, psychology, anthropology, religion, history, playwriting, Greek — not just hard science. The PM need **not** out-argue the experts; the PM needs a reasonable understanding of the base technologies, the ability to explain project technology **upward** to senior management, to interpret client and senior-management technical needs **downward** to the team, and to hear the team's problems well enough to address or escalate them.\n\n**1b. Administrative credibility (p. 127).** Four responsibilities performed \"with apparently effortless skill\": to client and senior management — keep the project on schedule and within cost and make reports accurate and timely (the book notes this can put the PM in an ethically awkward position); to the team — have material, equipment and labour available when and where needed; to all parties — represent each one's interests to the others, since the PM is \"the person in the middle\"; and to the project — make the tough trade-off decisions, and be seen as having the mature judgement and courage to do so consistently.\n\n**2. Sensitivity (p. 127).** Three sensors:\n  - *Political* — a working set of political antennae (called \"mandatory attributes\" earlier, p. 123).\n  - *Interpersonal* — successful PMs are **not conflict avoiders**: they sense conflict early, then confront and deal with it before it escalates into inter- and intradepartmental warfare. They must persuade people to cooperate irrespective of personal likes and dislikes.\n  - *Technical* — sense when things are being \"swept under the rug\". Note the irony the book points out: the team was deliberately staffed with task-oriented people, and such people may be unable to tolerate **their own** failures (though they tolerate others') and so hide them.\n\n**3. Leadership, ethics and management style (pp. 127–129).**\n  - **Leadership** (Tannenbaum et al. 1957): \"interpersonal influence, exercised in situations and directed through the communication process, toward the attainment of a specified goal or goals.\"\n  - Add **enthusiasm, optimism, energy, tenacity, courage and personal maturity**. Leadership is hard to define in advance and is mostly recognised after the fact. The PM should capitalise on strengths and cover weaknesses, know when to take over and when to \"give the team its head\", when to punish and when to reward, when to communicate and when to stay silent — and above all **know how to get others to share commitment to the project**, which the summary (p. 143) defines as leadership.\n  - **Ethics.** Nixon's (1987) list of relatively common ethical missteps in business (p. 128) — a tidy enumerable list:\n\n| # | Misstep | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **\"Wired\" bids and contracts** | The winner has been predetermined |\n| 2 | **\"Buy-in\"** | Bidding low intending to cut corners or force later contract changes |\n| 3 | **Kickbacks** | — |\n| 4 | **\"Covering\" for team members** | Group cohesiveness protecting wrongdoing |\n| 5 | **Taking \"shortcuts\"** | To meet deadlines or budgets |\n| 6 | **Using marginal materials** | Substandard inputs |\n| 7 | **Compromising on safety** | — |\n| 8 | **Violating standards** | — |\n| 9 | **Consultant loyalties** | e.g. auditors: to employer, to client, or to the public? |\n\n  - A public-sector PM easily gets entangled in pollution, public safety, plant location, use of public lands. The **PMI Code of Ethics** for project managers was created at the 1982 PMI symposium (Ireland et al.) and updated/approved in 1989, 1995 and 2006; the 2006 version is roughly **eight times** the length of earlier ones and is free at PMI.org. Its focus is behaviour producing a high **trust** level among PM, team, senior management, client and other stakeholders; the authors single out the \"**Honesty**\" section to be read three times. An **\"ethics audit\"**, recommended for nonprofits (Schaefer et al. 1998), is recommended by the authors for any firm.\n  - **Management style.** Little has been written about the *proper* style for a PM; an informal brand of **participative management** is generally preferred. **Shenhar (1998)** instead argues style should be **adapted to the project type**, on two dimensions (pp. 128–129):\n\n| Dimension | Levels | Style progression |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Technological uncertainty** | low tech → medium tech → high tech → very high tech | firm, rigid and formal → moderately firm → moderately flexible → highly flexible |\n| **System complexity** | assembly → system → array | in-house informal → formal main/subcontractor relationship → remote and highly formal |\n\n  Managerial practices (e.g. use of PM tools) also differ significantly across both dimensions. Note the recurring self-serving claim the book rejects: every profession believes its problems are uniquely difficult and therefore needs **less** managerial control.\n\n**4. Ability to handle stress (p. 129).** The book's observation is that project stress mirrors the parent organisation's: hectic firms produce hectic projects. **Four major causes of stress associated with managing projects:**\n\n| # | Cause | The book's remedy |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | The PM never develops a reasonably **consistent set of procedures and techniques** | This is what the whole textbook is for |\n| 2 | **Too much on their plates** | Include yourself as a **resource** when planning; PM software flags a resource used beyond capacity, giving evidence to discuss workload with senior management |\n| 3 | A **high need to achieve that is consistently frustrated** | Slevin (1989): stress arises when demands exceed the ability to cope, worst in high-achievement-need people. It is axiomatic that senior managers give the toughest projects — the ones most likely to carry unsolvable problems — to their best PMs. \"The cure is obvious, except to the senior managers who continue the practice\" |\n| 4 | The **parent organisation is in the throes of major change** | Restructuring and downsizing make worry about one's future a common condition; beyond the book's scope |\n\n---\n\n## 3.4 Problems of cultural differences\n\n- **It is not geography that matters.** Multicultural problems arise between countries, between **industries**, between firms in the same region, and even between **divisions of one firm**. International projects make the effect biggest and most visible, but it exists any time different organisations — including different parts of one organisation — must work together (pp. 129–130).\n- And it is not only culture: the **environments** differ too — **economic, political, legal and sociotechnical** (p. 129, repeated in the summary, p. 143). Memorise those four as the \"project environment\" answer to review question 10.\n- If multiple cultures and environments are present, **more than one organisation** is involved, which alone complicates the PM's standing task of reducing conflict among the parties-at-interest / stakeholders — **project team, client, senior management and the public**. With different nations, industries and firms involved, the conflicts are \"greater by an order of magnitude\", and even plain communication becomes almost impossibly complex (p. 130). Reference example: Hughes (1998) on the Boston Central Artery/Tunnel, chapter \"Coping with Complexity\".\n\n### ★ The four elements of culture (p. 130)\n\n*Why it matters: review question 9 asks you to describe each of the four. **Culture** = the entire way of life of a group of people.*\n\n| # | Element | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Technology** | Tools used; material things produced and used; how food is prepared; skills; **attitudes toward work** — all aspects of material life |\n| 2 | **Institutions** | The structure of the society: organisation of government, nature of the family, organisation *and* content of religion, division of labour, economic system, system of education, how voluntary associations form and persist |\n| 3 | **Language** | Always unique because it develops to meet that culture's needs; translation is rarely precise because words carry **connotative** as well as **denotative** meaning (the book's example: \"apple\" denotes a fruit but connotes health, bribery, New York, a colour, a computer, a dance, favouritism) |\n| 4 | **Arts / aesthetic values** | Dictate what is found beautiful and satisfying; if communication is the glue binding a culture, **art is the most efficient means of communicating**; a society's \"style\" comes from here |\n\n### Culture and the project (pp. 130–131)\n\n- **Perception of time** is the most obvious effect. In the US and several Western industrial nations time is valued as a resource (\"time is money\"); Latin American views differ — in South America being half an hour late to a project meeting is being \"on time\", while in **Japan lateness causes loss of face**. In some cultures **quality of the work** outranks on-time delivery. The result is a common perception that US managers are \"impatient\".\n- **Staffing philosophy** differs. In Latin America the **compadre** system (networks of extended family) leads managers to prefer relatives and friends. US managers see this as a major source of inefficiency — but the book says there is scant evidence for that: one private study found differences between US and Latin American chemical firms were *smaller* than differences between US chemical firms and US clothing manufacturers (p. 131). ⚠️ The study is described only as \"one private study\", with no citation.\n- The near-universal outside view is that US managers \"understand everything about technology and nothing about people\" — the desire to get down to business, where Asian, Middle Eastern, Latin American and southern European cultures value **\"getting to know you\"** as the precursor to trust. In many cultures the manager is expected to take a personal interest in subordinates' lives, which contradicts the usual US advice to stay out of employees' personal affairs.\n- **Multiculturalism may not travel** (WSJ box, p. 131): Grove and Hallowell, studying AT&T workplaces abroad, found \"the values that give impetus to diversity issues here don't necessarily exist abroad\" — other societies may treat ethnic difference as an appropriate basis for assigning workplace roles. Their advice to American managers abroad: **value equality without judging cultural norms**.\n\n### Microcultures and the project (pp. 131–132)\n\n- **Microculture** = the \"corporate culture\" of an organisation, or even of a project — the term the authors use to distinguish it from national or regional culture. Microcultures vary industry to industry and firm to firm just as cultures vary nation to nation: sales techniques normal in the wholesale automobile industry would cause outrage and lawsuits in business machines.\n- **Promises mean different things in different microcultures** — nobody takes seriously the promised completion date of a software application, a home-remodelling contractor's finish date, or an author's manuscript deadline. This matters more for a PM than for any other manager, because the PM depends on commitments from people who owe the project little allegiance, have little cause for loyalty, and over whom the PM has little or no **de jure authority**. So **the PM must know whose promises can be relied on and whose cannot.**\n- The PM cannot even assume shared acceptance of **accountability** (Dodson 1998), of **empowerment**, or of a **customer-oriented view of quality** (de Macedo-Soares et al. 1995). Hauptman et al. (1996), on 50 transnational projects, found product-development team accomplishment depended on how well they handled **two-way communication and problem solving** and their **willingness to deal with ambiguous and uncertain information** — and that handling cultural differences in these areas was critical to success. Levinson et al. (1995) describe steps enabling **interorganizational learning** in international alliances.\n- **Practice box — Energo** (pp. 132–133). A several-hundred-million-dollar project between a state-run Middle East client and a state-run European contractor stalled on cultural difference (religion, role of women, power distance between managers and workers, management style), worsened by an isolated desert site, poor communication, harsh conditions and a legal/political environment changing daily. The two separate organisational systems created an almost impenetrable **interface/boundary**, so they deliberately **integrated the two systems into one unified system** — plan drawn up, environmental impacts recognised, overall organisation restructured, integration designed, then implemented. Exhibit 1's integrative actions ran across physical appearance (war-room offices, joint offices, daily site tours with the counterpart PM), myths and stories (\"we are one team with two sides\"; separate the person from the position and stick to the problem), ceremonies (frequent meetings at all levels, celebrate each key event, social gatherings, group visits to historical sites) and management style (plan/organise/control **with** your counterparts, no finger-pointing for wrong decisions, execute decisions quickly). Neither side's staff could give up its own perspective at first; the PMs persisted with a lot of management-by-walking-around and integration gathered speed. At termination, external bargaining agents could not settle final payment — so the PMs were brought back and allowed to close the project their own way, continuing the integration process.\n\n---\n\n## 3.5 Impact of institutional environments\n\n*The book marks §3.5 and §3.6 as shaded sections that \"can be skipped without loss of continuity\" (p. 133 footnote). ⚠️ Treat that as the book's view, not the instructor's — do not skip them for IE408 unless Dr. Duraković says so.*\n\nIn general systems theory, a system's **environment** is everything outside it that receives its outputs or delivers its inputs; a culture's institutions are part of the environment of every project (p. 133).\n\n| Environment | What the PM faces |\n|---|---|\n| **Socioeconomic** (pp. 133–134) | The need to interact with governments is **lower in the US than almost anywhere**, regardless of who controls whom; on international projects expect bureaucracy at local, regional and national levels. Foreign governments' intentions are rarely evil — they protect local citizens from \"invading companies\", protect national treasures, maximise national employment, require some profit reinvestment, enforce safety. Some rules come from ancient tradition (no alcohol in Islamic nations, no pork products in Israel; and the \"A-OK\" hand sign should be avoided in several South American countries, though that is custom, not law). **The PM's job description should include acquiring a working knowledge of the host country's culture**, and the project should honour host-country norms where possible. The uncomfortable consequence: some cultures will not give a **female** PM the same respect as a male PM, leaving senior management choosing between its own anti-discrimination policy and a markedly higher risk of failure — the same problem arises with a Jewish PM in an Arab country or an Armenian PM in Turkey. |\n| **Legal** (pp. 134–135) | The US is by far the **most litigious** society — not more disagreements, but less recourse to courts elsewhere and therefore **more recourse to negotiation**. Martin (1993) concludes that failing to understand the culture where negotiation takes place puts the ignorant party at severe disadvantage, and the same holds for **microcultures**. **Trust** matters (Gogal et al. 1988), which is why the **compadre** system works as a substitute for the detailed explicit contracts the US uses with arms-length suppliers. Each nation's laws are a product of its history, because the conflicts law regularises reflect its culture. Collaboration between competitors has grown (SEMATECH; the Automotive Composites Consortium of Chrysler, Ford and GM — both enabled by the **National Cooperative Research Act of 1984**; Japan had more than 60 research consortia between 1961 and 1983, some with over 40 members), and transnationally (Airbus Industries — British-French-German-Spanish; CFM International — GE + Snecma; International Aero Engines — Pratt & Whitney, Rolls Royce, Japan Aero Engines, MTU, Fiat). **Why international projects exist in such numbers:** some resource the project needs is not readily available in the host nation — most commonly **technological knowledge** (second common reason, per footnote: entry into a previously closed market). Firms invited in for proprietary knowledge have found afterwards that it was no longer proprietary; patent laws and attitudes to the sanctity of patents differ by nation. **NAFTA** made all areas of technology patentable among the US, Canada and Mexico and was the **first international agreement to protect trade secrets** in addition to copyrights and patents (Chopra 1993). Bottom line: \"there is no substitute for qualified legal assistance.\" |\n| **The business cycle** (pp. 135–136) | Cycles in developed countries rise and fall together but never match precisely — different depth, different timing, sometimes a country skips a downturn. So local perceptions of prosperity vs recession differ, which changes attitudes to investment and employment, the project's **risk**, and even notions of a proper project timeframe. In high unemployment, nations erect **institutional barriers**: mandated delays, failure to approve investments, refusal to allow repatriation of earnings, \"inability\" to locate scarce human or capital resources, official foot-dragging on permissions, lack of capital equipment. Nations practise **commercial reciprocity** much as firms do, so a PM earns valuable goodwill by **buying from host-country vendors and employing qualified nationals** — sometimes hiring nationals is a condition imposed by the host organisation. |\n| **Technological** (pp. 136–139) | Not strictly institutional, but decisive: success often depends on planning the project to be **compatible with the technology available in the host nation** (Graham et al. 1988). **Ackoff's story** (p. 139): men carried pails of water across a road; Ackoff suggested a pipe and a gate operated by one person to save labour cost; his government host asked, \"And how will the men we replace support their families?\" The lesson: a nation's technology is largely a function of the **relative cost (supply) of the factors of production**, modified by tradition, policy and law. Modern easing: email, internet, conference calls, videoconferencing have greatly reduced communication problems for **virtual** projects (ch. 4) — and multicultural projects are virtual by definition — but technology does not relieve the PM of cultural sensitivity. Dodson: \"Project management is ultimately **expectation management**\", requiring negotiation skills that eclipse quantitative, \"metrical\" skills; for at least **three-quarters of the world's population relationship comes above all else — above time, above budget, above specification**. |\n\n- **Practice box — Brazil during unstable times** (pp. 137–138). Rio de Janeiro built a combined school and carnival stadium: 70,000 Samba seats, 200,000 total capacity, 4,000 students the rest of the year; $15M cost against $10M a year spent on temporary festival facilities, so it paid back quickly — in **4½ months**, to be ready for Lent. Four environmental obstacles and the responses: **political uncertainty** (a new governor wanting quick results; new governments freeze assets and prices, change taxes, revalue currency, so the economy adopts a \"wait and see\" attitude); **rampant inflation at 25% per month** — indexing systems were needed just to judge whether a price was reasonable, and a second **\"escalation\"** invoice was issued for the interval between first invoice and payment (itself subject to inflation if payment was late); **governmental bureaucracy** — defeated by special government concession granting simplified bidding and purchasing plus case-by-case expediting through the highest state and local officials; **local contractor politics** — local contractors refused the \"impossible\" deadline, so two out-of-state contractors were engaged, after which the locals reconsidered and took about 30% of the work. The project finished on time, within budget and to high quality; extreme public pressure and participant commitment helped, and the very short timespan itself contributed to success.\n\n## 3.6 Multicultural communications and managerial behaviour\n\n- **Language cannot be overstated** (p. 139). Almost every writer advises learning the host nation's language. The PM usually need not be **fluent** — a skilled translator handles precise communication — but speaking the language even haltingly pleases the hosts.\n- Language is a composite of words, signs, symbols, movements and positions of the body, pictures, sounds, equations and objects. *How* we send and receive is part of the message (\"the media are a part of the message\", paraphrasing McLuhan), and even source and destination can change meaning (the book's example: \"I'll give you a ring\" at the end of a date).\n\n### Structure and style of communications (pp. 139–140)\n\nThe steel-training case: young Indian engineers, fluent in English, were trained as first-line supervisors in Cleveland and Pittsburgh mills on American in-plant communication methods that depended heavily on **written** memoranda — and only months later did an outsider point out that just **17% of workers in an Indian steel mill could read**. Why did the engineers not say so? Because in Asiatic nations teachers and senior officials are held in such high regard that questioning or correcting them is almost unthinkable. A second cultural clash: the engineers felt it was beneath them to pick up a shovel on the blast-furnace floor, and only an on-site demonstration by a very senior American executive persuaded them to continue without resentment.\n\n- **The direction of information flow is the examinable distinction** (p. 140). In the US, **delegation** is the preferred style, authority is diffused, and information moves **up** to the manager from the delegatees. Where authority is **highly centralised**, it becomes the PM's own responsibility to **seek out** information (Smith et al. 1993). This collides with the book's fundamental tenet \"never let the boss be surprised\": the manager of an international project **cannot count on being voluntarily informed** of problems by subordinates.\n- China (Gogal et al. 1988; Graham et al. 1988): management is authoritarian, and the need to negotiate — largely with the state — is at least as important as anywhere else. Multicultural projects will **extend**, not reduce, the role of negotiation.\n\n### Managerial and personal behaviour (pp. 140–142)\n\nGrinbergs et al. (1993) compared Swiss and American managers/engineers of comparable age, education and salary on software projects:\n\n| | **Swiss** | **American** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Formality | \"Much more formal\" with each other | More collegial |\n| Planning | Long-term strategy much more important | Thorough planning and long-term strategy **not** absolute prerequisites to starting a project |\n| Change / risk | More resistant to change, more risk averse | More willing to experiment and innovate |\n| Bureaucracy | More accepting of it | — |\n| Quality / ethic | More focused on quality, stronger work ethic | Shorter time horizon, communicated more openly |\n\n- Formal/informal address modes carry the same difference: German *Sie*/*du*, French *vous*/*tu* — an American using *du* to a German counterpart will be understood but may sound rude.\n- In Asian nations an American PM should criticise indigenous subordinates with great care: **loss of face** is a serious problem. In communist states such as China the **pseudo-egalitarianism** (so called because the actual style is highly authoritarian — the authors invoke *Animal Farm*) may make criticism completely unacceptable.\n- **The more structured a country's social system, the less direct managerial communication is.** Highly structured social classes make participative management hard, because the more educated, higher-class manager's authority is assumed to be denigrated by it. In North America senior managers interact with first-line supervisors and with blue- and white-collar workers, and communication flows easily across functional lines; elsewhere it is indirect and follows the **lines of authority on the organisation chart**. The authors note you do not have to leave the US to see this: many US firms are quite authoritarian with a wide social gulf between manager and worker.\n\n### ★ Dinsmore et al.'s factors needing special consideration on a multicultural project (pp. 141–142)\n\n*Why it matters: review question 13 says \"Identify the five multicultural factors requiring special consideration.\"*\n\n| # | Factor | Status in the text |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Importance of **language and culture** | \"already noted\" |\n| 2 | Need to deal with the **politics and politicians** of the host nation | \"already noted\" |\n| 3 | The PM may have to use **indigenous staff members** | \"already noted\" |\n| 4 | Possibility of **input supply and technology problems** | \"already noted\" |\n| 5 | Need to **obey local laws and customs** | \"already noted\" |\n| +1 | Additional **risk factors**: kidnapping, disease, faulty medical care | \"two other matters\" |\n| +2 | The **\"expatriate way of life\"** — providing for the physical and psychological needs of people living in a \"strange land with different customs and way of life\" | \"two other matters\" |\n\n> ⚠️ **Enumeration is messy in the source.** The book says Dinsmore et al. \"list five factors\", then gives five items in a parenthetical as ones \"already noted\", then adds **two more** matters — so the printed passage effectively contains seven. The safe answer: give the five above as the five factors, and mention the two additional matters separately. Also note the book's own balance: in many countries project workers face **less** crime risk than in the US and easier access to medical care.\n\n- **Do not \"go native\"** (p. 142): an Austrian economist quoted by the authors — American managers who \"come over here and wear lederhosen and funny hats are laughable. No one takes them seriously.\"\n\n### Final comments on multicultural projects (p. 142)\n\n- Do not take on an international project without preparation in the host nation's **culture and language**. Lack of preparation causes **cultural shock** → frustration → withdrawal; a no-win situation. If the organisation has no internal resources for the preparation, hire outside consultants with real knowledge and teaching skill. The book's parenthetical warning: an existing employee **who merely happens to be of the right nationality is not a suitable training resource**. Language lessons are mandatory even if they do not reach technical vocabulary.\n- **Psychosocial needs matter** (Pinto et al. 1991): it is important that team members enjoy working with each other and perceive the project as a valuable way to spend their time. This is **doubly important** for multicultural projects and expatriate members, who are away from home and depend on national cohorts for psychosocial needs — so the project becomes a critical source of psychological and social payoff, and a PM with a strong tendency to focus only on **task** outcomes must deliberately make sure these other needs are met.\n- Encouraging close: people invariably judge other cultures by their own, so working comfortably in another culture takes great effort — but most Americans **underestimate** their own ability. Speaking several languages does not by itself imply sensitivity to another culture (glance at the Balkans or the Middle East). If a PM from Toronto can manage a project in Quebec and one from Boston in Albuquerque, multicultural management is **do-able**.\n\n## Glossary terms worth memorising verbatim (pp. 143–144)\n\n| Term | Book definition |\n|---|---|\n| **Analytic approach** | Breaking problems into their constituent parts to understand the parts better and thereby solve the problem |\n| **Systems approach** | A wide-ranging, synthesising method for addressing problems that considers multiple and interacting relationships; contrasted with the analytic approach |\n| **Culture** | The way of life of any group of people |\n| **Microculture** | The \"corporate culture\" within the organisation, or even the project |\n| **Environment** | Everything outside the system that delivers inputs or receives outputs from the system |\n| **Champion** | A person who spearheads an idea or action and \"sells\" it throughout the organisation |\n| **Facilitator** | A person who helps people overcome problems, either with technical issues or with other people |\n| **Trade-off** | Allowing one aspect to get worse in return for another aspect getting better |\n| **Tweed coat management** | The concept that highly educated people such as engineers require a special type of management |\n| **Functional** | One of the standard organisation disciplines such as finance, marketing, accounting or operations |\n\n## Case: The National Jazz Hall of Fame (pp. 149–156) — what to take from it\n\nA PM (Mr Robert Rutland, a UVA history professor) **stumbles into a public project by accident**: his real goal was saving Charlottesville's Paramount Theatre, and the project **evolved** into founding the National Jazz Hall of Fame. Examinable themes, all drawn from §3.1–3.4:\n\n- **Acquiring resources is the central difficulty.** Philanthropic organisations refused grants because no board member had experience with such a project; the NEA and NEH only consider organisations operating at least two years. Saving the Paramount needed at least $600,000, far beyond reach. At the end of the case there is **under $2,500 in the bank**.\n- **Competition / rival stakeholders:** the Harlem YMCA + CBS Records hall of fame, Dizzy Gillespie's proposed International Jazz Hall of Fame in Kansas City, the defunct New York Jazz Museum and the New Orleans museum (both failed for lack of funds), and the Tulane and Rutgers archives (which do not call themselves halls of fame). *Billboard*'s warning: splitting the jazz community's energies across too many efforts for the same goal is what has happened before.\n- **Benchmark:** the Country Music Hall of Fame (founded 1967, Nashville, cooperative fundraising by city, artists and sponsors; >500,000 visitors in 1983; $2.1M budget funded **85% admissions, 10% gift-shop and mail sales, 5% donations**; \"Friends of Country Music\" = 2,000+ people at $25/year).\n- **The consultant's three recommendations:** (1) direct-mail campaign to the 100,000-name Smithsonian jazz list, fronted by an appeal from a jazz great, costing $25,000–30,000 and breaking even at a **2% response** with $25 average gifts; (2) appoint a **full-time executive director** with the surplus, to run fundraising, establish the performance centre and museum, and coordinate the collection; (3) promote at strategic tourist locations — 50,000 tourists at $3.00 each would fund operations. Survey evidence: national survey of 1,300, **12.7% returned (165)**; respondents skewed older, male, graduate-educated, professional, >$50k income, 75% give $200+/year to nonprofits; **swing most popular**, then Dixieland; most-wanted service = **performance centre / concert hall**; a newsletter rated relatively unimportant; weighted average willingness to give **$23.40/year**. Tourist survey (~100 interviews at the Western Virginia Visitors Center): ~70% like jazz, >60% would visit, suggested admission **$3.50**.\n- **Critical success elements the consultant named:** professionalism in all services and communications, an executive director with prior fundraising **and** direct-mail experience plus love of jazz and administrative skill and creativity, frequent communication with Founding Sponsors, and greater local prominence to secure city officials', merchants' and community cooperation.\n- The case questions are pure chapter-3 drill: what is the project and has it stayed the same; who are the stakeholders including competitors; which of the chapter's PM skills matter most here; what **credibility** does Rutland have and is he a leader; which cultures are relevant and what is the project environment.\n\n## Directed Reading: \"What It Takes to Be a Good Project Manager\" (B. Z. Posner, pp. 156–160)\n\n**The debate it resolves.** One school prescribes a set of **personal characteristics** (aggressiveness, confidence, poise, decisiveness, resolution, entrepreneurship, toughness, integrity, versatility, multidisciplinarity, quick thinking). **Roman** objects that it would take an extraordinary individual to have them all, and proposes instead identifying the **critical problems** PMs face and selecting someone who can handle them. **Badaway** counters that PMs' primary problems are not technical at all — managers fail because they **lack organisation and management skills**. Posner's finding: the two views are **not contradictory but two sides of the same coin**.\n\n**The survey.** Questionnaires at a nationwide series of PM seminars, voluntary and confidential; **N = 287** (189 men, 98 women), ages 22–60 (mean 37.4, s.d. 8.3). The typical respondent: a 37-year-old male with nine people reporting to him, running a small-to-moderate project in a **matrix** structure. 56% were the formal project manager; immediate group size 2 to over 100 (median 8.9); 59% worked primarily on small projects; **more than 63% in a matrix structure**. Two open-ended questions (order randomised), analysed by **content analysis**: what causes you problems, and what makes an \"above average\" PM. Result: ~**900** problem statements and ~**1,400** skill statements.\n\n### ★ Table 1 — Project management problems (p. 157)\n\n*Percentages = share of PMs whose response fell in that cluster. Why it matters: this is the chapter's explicit \"sources of conflict / sources of trouble\" list, and review questions ask you to separate problems of **project setup** from problems of **PM skill**.*\n\n| Rank | Problem | % |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Resources inadequate** | 69 |\n| 2 | **Meeting (\"unrealistic\") deadlines** | 67 |\n| 3 | **Unclear goals / direction** | 63 |\n| 4 | **Team members uncommitted** | 59 |\n| 5 | **Insufficient planning** | 56 |\n| 6 | **Breakdown of communications** | 54 |\n| 7 | **Changes in goals and resources** | 42 |\n| 8 | **Conflicts between departments or functions** | 35 |\n\n**The causal chain Posner draws** — this is the real answer, not the ranking: senior management **fails to be clear about project objectives** → poor planning (lack of clear goals and effective planning were named by **more than 60%**) → mistaken resource allocations and unrealistic schedules → people assigned to the project do not commit energetically. So inadequate resources is a *symptom*, not the root cause. Communication breakdowns were called \"inevitable\" — products of ambiguity plus the difficulty of coordinating diverse perspectives and personalities — so the PM's challenge is to **handle** them as they arise rather than predict and control them beforehand. Communication problems and conflict were constantly linked by respondents (\"conflicts between departments end up as major communication hassles\"), and interdepartmental conflict traces back to poor goal-setting and planning. Managing change was often labelled **\"Murphy's Law\"**. Decisively: **fewer than one in ten** PMs named a *technological* factor as a significant source of problems. Hitchcock's (1967) study of first-line R&D/engineering supervisors found the parallel trio: insufficient definition of policy from the top downward, how to define the goal of a problem, and budgeting and manpower assignments.\n\n### ★ Table 2 — Project manager skills (p. 158)\n\n*Why it matters: this is the empirical counterpart to §3.3's four categories, and the one place where the chapter gives numbers.*\n\n| Rank | Skill area | % | Components named |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Communication skills** | 84 | Listening, persuading |\n| 2 | **Organizational skills** | 75 | Planning, goal-setting, analysing |\n| 3 | **Team building skills** | 72 | Empathy, motivation, esprit de corps |\n| 4 | **Leadership skills** | 68 | Sets an example, energetic, vision (big picture), delegates, positive |\n| 5 | **Coping skills** | 59 | Flexibility, creativity, patience, persistence |\n| 6 | **Technological skills** | 46 | Experience, project knowledge |\n\nPoints the text makes about individual entries: being **persuasive / able to sell one's ideas** and **good listening** are the two halves of communication (\"good project managers manage not by the seat of their pants but by the soles of their feet\"); within organisational skills the **ability to prioritise** — \"stays on track\", \"keeps the project goals in perspective\" — is significant; good PMs are good problem solvers, but what really separates them is **problem finding** — spotting issues before they become problems, which buys degrees of freedom; team building rests on **empathetic relationships** (\"the best project managers use a lot of 'we' statements\"), helped by clear objectives and breaking the project into parts so members see their interdependencies; leadership is \"the ability to see the forest through the trees\"; coping matters because change is the only constant, and how well a PM handles stress — \"grace under pressure\" — significantly affects success or failure; and **technological expertise was often felt to be detrimental**, because it decreased flexibility and willingness to consider alternatives. PMs need only be versed enough in the technology to **ask the right questions** — \"you've got to be able to know when people are blowing smoke at you.\"\n\n### Table 3 — skills matched to problems (pp. 159–160)\n\n| Skill | Problem(s) it addresses |\n|---|---|\n| **Communication** | Breakdowns in communications |\n| **Organizational** | Insufficient planning; resources inadequate |\n| **Team building** | Team members uncommitted; weak inter-unit integration |\n| **Leadership** | Unclear goals / direction; interpersonal conflicts |\n| **Coping** | Handling changes |\n| **Technological** | Meeting (\"unrealistic\") deadlines |\n\nPosner concedes the one-to-one matching **oversimplifies** the dynamic nature of project management but says it has inherent logical appeal; he explicitly declines to argue which comes first — either PMs need the skills to handle the problems, or the problems demand the skills. **The study's headline conclusion:** \"the primary problems of project managers are not technical, but human.\" Improving technological capability helps only insofar as it improves the PM's ability to communicate, be organised, build teams, lead and deal comfortably with change. The challenge for technical people moving into management is to recognise and develop their **interpersonal** skills.\n\n> ⚠️ **Note two labelling quirks before you quote Table 3.** It pairs team building with \"weak inter-unit integration\" and leadership with \"interpersonal conflicts\", neither of which is a Table 1 heading (Table 1 has \"conflicts between departments or functions\"); and the skill rankings deliberately do **not** line up with the problem rankings — reading question 2 asks exactly why the top skill is not matched to the top problem.\n\n## Why this matters for the exam\n\n- **Expect a \"list and explain\" question.** The highest-probability targets are the **three areas of responsibility** (parent organisation / project and client / project team), the **seven special demands**, the **four selection skill categories**, the **four or five effective-team-member characteristics**, and the **four elements of culture**. Learn the counts — they are how such questions are phrased.\n- **Expect the functional-vs-project-manager contrast**, in table form or as \"state four differences\". The compact answer: generalist not specialist, synthesis not analysis, systems approach not analytic approach, facilitator not direct technical supervisor — and note that the functional manager may still choose the technology and the people while carrying no responsibility for results.\n- **Expect a selection question with a trap.** \"Which attribute matters most when selecting a PM?\" The answer is **the one who can get the job done** — a *closer*, driven to complete a difficult task — not a strong technical background and definitely not \"whoever is available\". Pair that with the point that the four skills must be **perceived** by others, not merely possessed.\n- **Expect Table 3-1 as a mini data-reading item.** Know that formation ranks all three objectives equally, buildup is **schedule**-dominated, main is schedule-and-performance, phaseout is **performance**-dominated, and cost is never first after formation — plus the fact that this **overturned** the conventional performance-then-cost-then-schedule story.\n- **Expect one culture/environment question**, most likely either the four elements of culture, or the four environments (economic, political, legal, sociotechnical), or **culture vs microculture vs multiculture**. The reusable thesis sentence: what matters is not national borders but differences in **cultures and environments**, which also arise between industries, firms and divisions of one firm.\n- **Expect the Posner reading to be mined for its one-line conclusion** — the primary problems of project managers are **human, not technical** (fewer than one in ten named a technological factor), and the differentiator is **problem finding**, not problem solving. If percentages are asked for, the anchors are communication 84% and resources inadequate 69%.\n- **Have the two \"prime law\" phrases ready verbatim**, because they are cheap marks and reusable in essay answers: **\"Never let the boss (senior management) be surprised\"** and **\"the best PM is the one who can get the job done.\"**\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"How does a project manager differ from a functional manager in expertise, method and role?","a":"The PM is a generalist, a synthesiser using the systems approach, and a facilitator; the functional manager is a specialist, an analyst using the analytic approach, and a direct technical supervisor (pp. 108–109)."},{"q":"Define a \"system\" as Meredith & Mantel do, and say why the word \"purposeful\" matters.","a":"A set of interrelated components that accepts inputs and produces outputs in a purposeful manner; \"purposeful\" restricts attention to systems involving humans, since machines are not purposeful (p. 108)."},{"q":"What is micromanagement in a project, and what is the authors' advice to its victim?","a":"A senior manager withdraws the PM's delegated powers and runs the project, killing creativity and ensuring mediocrity or failure; the authors do not know how to cure it and advise the PM to request a transfer (p. 110)."},{"q":"Name the PM's three areas of responsibility.","a":"To the parent organisation (conserve resources, timely accurate communication, competent management), to the project and the client (preserve project integrity against conflicting demands while meeting time, budget and specification), and to the project team (help members' transition out at the project's finite end) (pp. 110–111)."},{"q":"What is \"tweed coat management\" and what went wrong with it?","a":"The idea that highly educated researchers need special managing; a scientist/PM was shielded from schedules and budgets kept by an outsider, and such projects invariably far exceeded budget and schedule (p. 111)."},{"q":"List the seven special demands on the project manager.","a":"Acquiring adequate resources; acquiring and motivating personnel; dealing with obstacles; making project goal trade-offs; failure and the risk/fear of failure (maintaining a balanced outlook); breadth of communication; negotiation (pp. 114–126, 143)."},{"q":"Describe the estimate-discounting phenomenon Gagnon identified.","a":"The person doing the task sometimes overestimates time and cost; the immediate supervisor discounts that pessimism and may underestimate; each higher level lowers the estimates again, and recognising the pattern does not prevent it (pp. 115–116)."},{"q":"Name the five characteristics of effective project team members, and say which the book's summary omits.","a":"High-quality technical skills, political sensitivity, strong problem orientation, strong goal orientation and high self-esteem; the summary on p. 143 lists only four, dropping strong goal orientation (pp. 117–118)."},{"q":"Why does the book prefer problem-oriented to discipline-oriented team members?","a":"Pill (1971) showed multidisciplinary projects succeed far more often with problem-oriented members, because discipline-oriented people view the problem only through their own field and ignore everything outside their training (p. 118)."},{"q":"According to Table 3-1, which objective dominates at each life-cycle stage?","a":"Formation — cost, schedule and performance equally important; buildup — schedule, then performance, then cost; main — schedule and performance tied above cost; phaseout — performance, then schedule, then cost (p. 120)."},{"q":"What four unethical temptations can a PM's enthusiasm for the project produce?","a":"Overstating the project's benefits, understating probable completion costs, ignoring technical difficulties in reaching required performance, and making trade-offs biased toward the project and against the parent organisation (p. 121)."},{"q":"Contrast Type 1 and Type 2 projects and the reason each fails.","a":"Type 1 are well-understood routine projects whose problems appear late and which fail from not being organised to handle unexpected crises or lacking technical expertise; Type 2 are poorly understood, with early planning problems rooted in failure to define the mission and specifications (pp. 121–122)."},{"q":"What is the single most important criterion for selecting a project manager?","a":"The one who can get the job done — a \"closer\" with a drive to complete a difficult task; hard workers are easy to find, and criteria such as a strong technical background or current availability miss the point (p. 126)."},{"q":"Name the four categories of skills used to select a PM, and the extra condition the book attaches.","a":"Credibility (technical and administrative), sensitivity, leadership/ethics/management style, and ability to handle stress — and it is not enough to possess them, they must also be perceived by others (pp. 126–129)."},{"q":"What does technical credibility actually require of a PM?","a":"A reasonable understanding of the base technologies — not expert depth — plus the ability to explain project technology to senior management, interpret client and management technical needs to the team, and understand the team's problems well enough to address or escalate them (pp. 126–127)."},{"q":"What are the four major causes of stress associated with managing projects?","a":"No consistent set of procedures and techniques; too much on the PM's plate; a high need to achieve that is consistently frustrated (senior managers give the toughest projects to their best PMs); and major change in the parent organisation (p. 129)."},{"q":"Name the four elements common to all cultures.","a":"Technology (tools, material things, skills, attitudes toward work), institutions (government, family, religion, division of labour, economic system, education, voluntary associations), language, and arts or aesthetic values (p. 130)."},{"q":"What are the four aspects of the project environment the chapter identifies?","a":"Economic, political, legal and sociotechnical — and the chapter stresses that these environmental differences, not national boundaries, are what complicate multicultural projects (pp. 129, 143)."},{"q":"Distinguish culture, microculture and the PM consequence of microcultural diversity.","a":"Culture is the whole way of life of a group; a microculture is the corporate culture of a firm or even a project; because promises and accountability mean different things in different microcultures, the PM — who has little de jure authority over those who make them — must know whose promises can be relied on (pp. 130–132)."},{"q":"What is the headline conclusion of Posner's directed reading, and the top entry in each of his two tables?","a":"The primary problems of project managers are human, not technical (fewer than one in ten named a technological factor); the top problem is inadequate resources (69%) and the top skill is communication (84%), with problem *finding* distinguishing superior PMs (pp. 157–160)."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 b03"},{"id":"b04","title":"Book 4 · Project Organization ★","slides":"6e ch. 4 = 7e ch. 5 · week 4 · MIDTERM","md":"# B04 — Project Organization (6th ed. ch. 4 = 7th ed. ch. 5, \"The Project in the Organizational Structure\")\n\n*How a project is attached to its parent firm — functional, pure project, matrix or mixed — what each form costs you, how senior management chooses between them, and how the project team, risk group and project office are staffed. **Midterm material** (the midterm is 40% of the course, sits in week 8 and covers chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8).*\n\n**Printed pages covered:** 189–235 (body 189–221; summary/glossary 221–223; questions 223–225; Oilwell Cable case 226–229; Directed Reading on virtual projects 229–235). Page citations are **7th-edition** printed pages.\n\n---\n\n> ### ⚠️ Edition mapping — read this once\n>\n> The syllabus specifies the **6th edition** (scope: chapters **1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8**); the PDF in the course folder is the **7th**, and the two **permute chapters 4, 5 and 6**. Chapters 1–3 and 8 are identical in both. The rest:\n>\n> | 6th ed. (what the syllabus means) | 7th ed. (what the PDF has) | On the midterm? |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | **4** Project Organization | **5** The Project in the Organizational Structure ← *this file* | **yes** |\n> | **5** Project Planning | **6** Project Activity Planning (contains the WBS, §6.4) | **yes** |\n> | **6** Conflict and Negotiation | **4** Negotiation and the Management of Conflict | **no** |\n>\n> Page references here are **7th-edition printed pages**, so do not quote them at the professor. ⚠️ This mapping is **inferred** and it decides what you revise — **confirm the scope with Dr. Duraković.**\n\n\n## ⚠️ Scope warning — ask the instructor before you revise\n\nThe IE408 weekly schedule labels **week 5** as *\"Scope; work breakdown structure (WBS)\"* with the reading given as **\"Ch. 5\"**. **This chapter is not about scope or the WBS.**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| What this chapter is | **The Project in the Organizational Structure** — functional / pure project / matrix / mixed forms, how to choose one, risk management + the project office, the project team, human factors |\n| What it says about the **WBS** | Almost nothing: two passing mentions (p. 214 and the glossary on p. 223), **both of which say \"see Chapter 6\"** |\n| Where the WBS actually lives | **7th ed. chapter 6, §6.4** — which is **6th ed. chapter 5**, the edition the syllabus requires |\n| Stated midterm scope | chapters **1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8** |\n\nThe edition permutation above is the likely explanation: under **6th-edition** numbering, \"Ch. 5\" = *Project Planning* (scope and the WBS), which matches the week-5 label, and **this** chapter is 6th-ed **chapter 4**, also inside the stated scope. On that reading both are examinable and nothing is wrong.\n\n> ⚠️ **But that mapping is inferred, not stated anywhere in the syllabus, and it decides what you revise. Email Dr. Duraković (subject line `[IE408] ...`) and ask explicitly: (a) is the WBS examinable on the midterm, and (b) does \"chapter 5\" mean the 6th-edition chapter 5?** Until he answers, treat **both** this chapter and the WBS chapter as examinable — that is the only safe reading, and the WBS is also the entry point to chapter 8, which is in scope either way.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter framing — why organisational form is a question at all\n\nA firm that succeeds grows, and growth forces an organisational structure whose organising principle is **specialisation** (p. 189). The structure persists as long as it is sufficient for the work; when it starts to *obstruct* the work, pressure to reorganise builds — and the new structure is still specialised, just specialised differently. Common bases: function, product line, geography, production process, customer type, subsidiary, time, vertical/horizontal integration — and large firms often use several at different levels (p. 189).\n\nThe newer form is the **project organization**, also sold as **\"enterprise project management\"**, \"managing organizations by projects\" or the \"project-oriented firm\" (p. 189) — \"project management practices and tools across an enterprise\" (Levine, 1998). Its origin is probably the software industry, which long decomposed big applications into small projects and integrated them afterwards. Many firms now run routine business the traditional way and run **anything that represents a change** as a project — the book's hospital example keeps ordinary departments while supporting several dozen projects (p. 189).\n\n### Four reasons project-oriented organisations grew fast (p. 190) ★\n\n| # | Driver | Why it breaks the traditional structure |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Speed and market responsiveness** | Passing a product from function to function is no longer competitive; first-to-market is a powerful advantage. Also products tailored per client (different cosmetics mix per drugstore). |\n| 2 | **Diverse specialised inputs** | Each new product/service needs a different mix of specialities; ad hoc teams that form, deliver and disband are the norm. |\n| 3 | **Rapid technological expansion destabilises structures** | Mergers, downsizing, spin-offs, new channels all need system-wide response, and no traditional mechanism handles change at that scale — project organisation can. |\n| 4 | **Senior managers lack confidence in their control** | The hospital CEO became project-oriented because she had no way to understand, measure or control anything non-routine. Converting non-routine work into projects forces accountability, planning, integration and routine progress reporting. |\n\n**Transition cost:** moving a non-project firm to a fully project-oriented one is \"extraordinarily difficult\" and, when all goes well, **rarely takes less than three years** (p. 190). Kotter (1997) lists **eight steps** for leading fundamental change, most requiring active top-management leadership.\n\n### The three organisational decisions every project forces (p. 190) ★\n\n1. How to **tie the project to the parent firm** (this is the chapter's main subject).\n2. How to **organise the project itself** (§5.7, continued in ch. 6).\n3. How to organise **activities common to several projects** (→ the risk management group and the project office, §5.6).\n\n**Who decides:** it is rare for the PM to have much influence over the project–parent interface; the choice is made by **senior management**, and the PM is rarely even asked for input (pp. 190, 201). But the PM's work is strongly shaped by it, so the PM must understand it. Experienced PMs do visibly mould the project's organisation to their own notions — one team member described life on two *matrix* projects under different PMs as completely different (p. 190).\n\n---\n\n## 5.1 The project as part of the functional organization\n\nGive the project a \"home\" inside one of the firm's **functional divisions**. The book's chart is the **University of Cincinnati** (Figure 5-1, pp. 191–192): an MS in Project Management would sit under the Senior VP & Provost → dean of Business and/or Engineering, run by a senior operations-management faculty member; a new parking garage or a campus LAN would fall under the VP for Business Affairs.\n\n**Placement rule (p. 191):** assign the project to the functional unit that has **the most interest in its success**, or that can be **most helpful in implementing it**. More than one plausible parent often exists (the MPM could equally sit under Graduate Studies).\n\n### Advantages (pp. 191–192)\n\n| # | Advantage | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Maximum flexibility in staff use** | The right division is the administrative base for the relevant experts; they are assigned temporarily, contribute, and go straight back to normal work. |\n| 2 | **Individual experts serve many projects** | A broad technical base lets people be switched between projects with relative ease. |\n| 3 | **Specialists are grouped, so knowledge is shared** | The project gets whatever technical knowledge sits in the group — a source of creative, synergistic technical solutions. |\n| 4 | **Continuity** | **Technological** continuity when individuals leave the project or the firm, plus **procedural, administrative and policy** continuity from staying inside one division. |\n| 5 | **The normal career path is preserved** | The project may bring glory, but the functional field is the person's professional home and the focus of their growth and advancement. |\n\n### Disadvantages (p. 192) — the book's verdict: these outweigh the advantages (p. 221)\n\n| # | Disadvantage | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **The client is not the focus** | The functional unit's own work normally takes precedence over the project, hence over the client. |\n| 2 | **Function-oriented, not problem-oriented** | The division orients to activities particular to its function; a successful project must be problem-oriented. |\n| 3 | **Responsibility is not pinpointed** | Occasionally *no one* has full responsibility: the PM is accountable for some parts, someone else for others → lack of coordination and chaos. |\n| 4 | **Slow, arduous response to the client** | Often several layers of management between project and client. |\n| 5 | **Suboptimisation** | Issues inside the functional home's interest area get careful treatment; issues outside it get \"short shrift, if not totally ignored\". |\n| 6 | **Weak motivation** | The project is off the mainstream of interest; team members may read it as a \"professional detour\". |\n| 7 | **No holistic approach** | A jet transport or a hospital emergency room cannot be well designed except as a totality; no division can avoid focusing on its own interests, and cross-divisional communication is \"slow and difficult at best\". |\n\n> **PM in Practice — Prevost Car, Quebec (pp. 192–193):** the VP of production had to add **31% capacity in five months**. A PM consulting firm ran a five-day scoping meeting (their PMs + a value engineering expert + Prevost's seven foremen), producing a **$10 million, 60,000 ft² expansion** plan with a further 20% option. Top management approved after two days' study; the project finished **on time and on budget**, the follow-on 20% likewise. Success \"infected\" the firm: the next initiative (reducing workplace injuries) was also run as a project, and soon everything was. The VP: \"it's a question of finding what couldn't be better managed by project.\"\n\n---\n\n## 5.2 Pure project organization\n\nThe opposite extreme (Figure 5-2, pp. 193–194). The project is **separated from the parent system** and becomes a **self-contained unit** with its own technical staff and administration, tied to the parent only by \"the tenuous strands of periodic progress reports and oversight\" (p. 193). Parents vary: some prescribe administrative, financial, personnel and control procedures in detail; others grant near-total freedom subject to final accountability.\n\n### Advantages (p. 194)\n\n| # | Advantage | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **PM has full line authority** | The PM reports to a senior executive but is \"like the CEO of a firm dedicated to carrying out the project\". |\n| 2 | **Whole workforce reports to the PM** | No functional division heads whose permission must be sought or advice heeded on technological decisions. |\n| 3 | **Shortened communication lines** | The functional structure is bypassed; the PM talks directly to senior corporate management → faster communication, fewer failures. |\n| 4 | **A permanent cadre of experts** | With several successive similar projects, deep skill pools form and can themselves attract customers — Lockheed's **\"Skunk Works\"** (name from the *Li'l Abner* comic strip) is the example. |\n| 5 | **High commitment and motivation** | A strong separate identity breeds commitment and the task orientation of ch. 3. |\n| 6 | **Swift decisions** | Centralised authority lets the project react rapidly to client and senior-management needs. |\n| 7 | **Unity of command** | Each subordinate has one and only one boss; quality of life improves. |\n| 8 | **Structurally simple and flexible** | Easy to understand and implement. |\n| 9 | **Supports a holistic (systems) approach** | Optimising subsystems instead of the total project is often a major cause of technical failure. |\n\n### Disadvantages (pp. 194–195)\n\n| # | Disadvantage | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Duplication of effort → expensive** | Each project tends to be fully staffed; \"personnel managers come in integers, not fractions\", and staff is usually not shared across projects. |\n| 2 | **Stockpiling of people and equipment** | To guarantee access, the PM hires critical skills **when they are available rather than when needed**, and keeps them longer than needed \"just in case\". (1 + 2 = this form is very expensive.) |\n| 3 | **Technical obsolescence of staff** | Team members go deep in the project's technology but **fall behind in their other technical expertise**; the functional division is the repository of technical lore and is not readily accessible to them. Serious when the project is \"high technology\". |\n| 4 | **Inconsistent policies and procedures** | In the sheltered project environment, administrative corner-cutting is common and easily justified; \"they don't understand our problems\" excuses ignoring headquarters. |\n| 5 | **Projectitis** | The project takes on a life of its own; strong **we–they divisiveness** distorts relations with the parent; friendly rivalry becomes bitter competition and political infighting between projects. |\n| 6 | **\"Life after the project\"** | Uncertainty at termination: layoffs? low-prestige work? skills gone rusty? the team broken up? |\n\n**Glossary (p. 223): projectitis** = \"a social phenomenon, inappropriately intense loyalty to the project.\"\n\n---\n\n## 5.3 The matrix organization\n\nThe matrix was developed to **couple the advantages of the pure project with the desirable features of the functional form, while avoiding some disadvantages of each** (p. 195). Formally: **a pure project organization overlaid on the functional divisions of the parent firm** (Figure 5-3, p. 196). In Figure 5-3, PM1 reports to a program manager and draws 3 people from manufacturing, 1½ from marketing, ½ each from finance and personnel, 4 from R&D.\n\n### ★ The authority split — the single most quotable sentence in the chapter (p. 196)\n\n> The **PM controls *when* and *what*** these people will do; the **functional managers control *who*** will be assigned to the project and **what technology** will be used.\n\nRestated in the disadvantages (p. 199): in matrix projects **the PM controls administrative decisions and the functional heads control technological decisions.**\n\n### Reporting line (p. 196)\n\nThere is **no single executive to whom PMs generally report**. If the project is part of a program, the PM reports to the **program manager**; otherwise it is common to report to the functional manager with the keenest interest (e.g. PMs of Office of Naval Research mathematics projects reporting to the ONR section head for Mathematical Sciences); in small firms with few projects, directly to a senior executive.\n\n### The matrix variants (pp. 195–197) ★ near-certain exam content\n\nThe book's own names are **\"project\" or \"strong\"**, **\"balanced\"**, and **\"functional\" or \"weak\"**. The primary difference between them is **the relative power/decision authority of the project manager versus the functional manager** (p. 195). In practice there is an \"almost infinite variety\" of forms between the extremes.\n\n| Variant | Book's other names | Resembles | What functional units supply | PM's situation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Strong matrix** | \"project\" matrix | Pure project | **People** — individuals assigned to the project full- or part-time | Near-full control of the work; project is not stand-alone but is not separated from the parent either |\n| **Balanced matrix** | — | Between the two | A mixture; frequently-needed groups stay functional and take project work **\"much like outside contractors\"** (toxicology in cosmetics, QA in multiproduct manufacturing, computer graphics in publishing) | Control over the work is **diminished**, but the project gets immediate access to the group's expertise and the group keeps its technological integrity. The book's dry aside: the balanced matrix is \"typically anything but balanced\" |\n| **Weak matrix** | \"functional\" matrix | Functional form | **Capacity**, not people — the department adds the project's job to its normal workload | A project may have **only one full-time person: the PM**, whose primary task is to *coordinate* activities carried out by functional departments. Priority may be set by senior management or negotiated; the group's internal charges may be negotiated; the task can even be subcontracted outside |\n\n**Discriminator to memorise:** *strong = the functional unit lends **individuals**; weak = the functional unit lends **capacity** (deliverables).* The book states this explicitly in §5.7 (p. 214) and in matrix advantage 7 (p. 198): some functional units might furnish people while others supply only capacity.\n\n⚠️ **Terminology note.** The terms **\"lightweight\"** and **\"heavyweight\"** appear in this chapter **only** in end-of-chapter question 38/39 (p. 224: \"consider the applicability of a *lightweight* team structure… a *heavyweight* or *balanced* structure\"), never in the body. If Dr. Duraković uses them, the mapping is lightweight ≡ weak/functional matrix and heavyweight ≡ strong/project matrix. Do not claim the book defines them.\n\n### Why the matrix appeared: the systems approach (pp. 196–197)\n\nHigh-technology firms had to integrate several functional specialities across a *set* of projects and wanted to **time-share expertise** between them, and the projects' technical needs demanded a **systems approach**. The old sequential route — R&D → engineering (reworks it) → manufacturing (reworks it again for existing machinery) — took enormous time and the emergent project could bear \"scant resemblance to the original specifications\". Worse, separately designed subsystems (a jet engine, a weapon system, an airframe) rarely performed as conceived **because the parts were not designed as a unified system**.\n\nThe **systems approach** does not require one firm to make everything; it requires **one organisation to take responsibility for the integrity of the project design** — that the parts are compatible and the combination functions as expected. (The book's aside: \"integrity\" and \"integration\" share a root.) Functional housing was too constraining; pure project was workable but expensive through duplication of scarce technical talent; the matrix, letting the PM draw **temporarily** on all relevant functions, was \"a way out of the dilemma\".\n\n> **Evidence (p. 197):** Chrysler's **LH sedans** (also the PT Cruiser and the Viper) were designed by **concurrent / simultaneous engineering** — marketing, engineering, manufacturing, design, QA and others working together from the outset. Besides designs widely rated \"outstanding\", it cut the design-to-street process by about **18 months**. The value of the saved time comes from (1) less design labour and overhead and (2) earlier sales and return on investment.\n> Glossary (p. 222): **concurrent/simultaneous engineering** = originally a design team containing both design and manufacturing engineers, now expanded to include quality control, purchasing and other relevant areas.\n\nA warning attached to the matrix (p. 197): **discipline-oriented** team members (ch. 3) tend to become ardent supporters of their functional areas, sometimes to the project's detriment (de Laat 1994; Kalu 1993). The resulting power struggles tax the PM's conflict-reduction skills.\n\n### Advantages (p. 198)\n\n| # | Advantage | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **The project is the point of emphasis** | One person, the PM, is responsible for delivering on time, within cost, to specification — shared with the pure project form. |\n| 2 | **Access to the whole technology reservoir** | The overlay draws labour and talent temporarily from all functional divisions; with several projects, those talents serve all of them, **sharply reducing the duplication** of the pure project form. |\n| 3 | **Less end-of-project anxiety** | Members attach to the project but also feel close to their functional \"home\". |\n| 4 | **Rapid, flexible response** | As fast and flexible as a pure project toward the client, *and* toward demands from inside the parent firm — a nested project must adapt to the parent or it will not survive. |\n| 5 | **Consistency with parent policy** | Access to representatives of the parent's administrative units preserves policies, practices and procedures, which fosters **project credibility** with the parent's administration — \"commonly undervalued\". |\n| 6 | **Company-wide balance of resources** | With several simultaneous projects, staffing and scheduling can **optimise total system performance** across the different time/cost/performance targets rather than serve one project at the others' expense. |\n| 7 | **Wide range of adaptable forms** | Strong/weak (people vs capacity) and every mixture — great flexibility within one basic structure, always subject to the parent's needs, abilities and desires. |\n\n### Disadvantages (pp. 198–199) — the book's framing: **all of them involve conflict**, mostly between functional and project managers\n\n| # | Disadvantage | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Power is delicately balanced → doubt about who is in charge** | Functional form: the division holds decision power. Pure project: the PM does. Matrix: power is balanced, often delicately. Doubt makes the work suffer; if the project is successful and visible, infighting for credit; if it fails, \"even more brutal\" infighting to avoid blame. |\n| 2 | **The project set must be managed as a set** | Balancing time/cost/performance across projects is a tough monitoring job, and moving resources between projects **fosters political infighting among PMs**, each caring more about their own project than about organisation-wide optimisation. |\n| 3 | **Shutting a project down is almost as hard as in a pure project** | In **strong** matrices especially: projects have individual identities and \"resist death\" — **projectitis is still a serious disease** in matrix organisations. |\n| 4 | **Split authority is complex in practice; negotiation becomes decisive** | Administrative decisions (PM) vs technological decisions (functional heads) is simple to write and hard to operate. The PM's ability to negotiate everything from resources to technical assistance to delivery dates is a key success contributor — **\"success is doubtful for a PM without strong negotiating skills.\"** |\n| 5 | **Violates unity of command** | Project workers have **at least two bosses** (functional head + PM); split loyalties and confusion are unavoidable. The book paraphrases Plato on democracy: matrix management \"is a charming form of management, full of variety and disorder.\" |\n\n### Virtual projects (pp. 199–200) — introduced inside §5.3, because they are usually matrix-structured\n\n**Virtual projects** are those in which the team's work **crosses time, space, organizational, or cultural boundaries** (p. 199) — different time periods, geographically dispersed, different organisations, different cultures. Their rise was enabled by the Internet and other communication technologies, and such teams are usually organised in **some matrix-type structure** rather than functional or pure project. Kalu (1993) defines **virtual positions** as task processes whose performance requires \"composite membership\" in both project and functional organisations — typical in complex organisations, creating overlapping, shared responsibility for execution. Some authors (Adams et al., 1997) restrict \"virtual\" to **geographic dispersion** only.\n\n**Gratton's (2007) six rules for geographically dispersed virtual teams (pp. 199–200):** (1) use them only for **challenging, interesting** projects that are also meaningful to the company; (2) **solicit volunteers** — they are more enthusiastic and dedicated; (3) include **a few members who already know each other**, and make **one in every six or seven a \"boundary spanner\"** with many outside contacts; (4) create an **online resource** where members learn about each other (especially how they prefer to work), collaborate and brainstorm; (5) encourage **frequent communication but not social gatherings** (those happen naturally anyway); (6) split the work into **geographically independent modules** so a delay in one location does not stall another.\n\n---\n\n## ★ The three forms side by side (synthesis of 5.1–5.3)\n\nThe comparison a single exam question is most likely to ask for. Every cell comes from the advantage/disadvantage lists above.\n\n| Dimension | **Functional** | **Pure project** | **Matrix** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Where the project lives** | Inside one functional division — the one most interested in its success or most able to help | A self-contained unit **separated** from the parent, linked only by progress reports and oversight | A project organisation **overlaid** on the functional divisions |\n| **PM's authority** | Weakest — sometimes **no one** holds full responsibility for the project | **Full line authority**; \"like the CEO of a firm dedicated to the project\" | **Shared and delicately balanced**: PM owns *when/what* + administrative decisions; functional heads own *who* / *what technology* + technical decisions |\n| **Who the workers report to** | Their functional superiors | The **PM only** — unity of command holds | **At least two bosses** — unity of command is violated |\n| **Where staff come from** | The host division's own experts, assigned temporarily | A **dedicated full-time** workforce held by the project | Functional divisions lend **people** (strong matrix) or **capacity/deliverables** (weak matrix) |\n| **Cost** | **Lowest** — experts are shared and return to normal work | **Highest** — full staffing per project duplicates everything, plus stockpiling of people and equipment | **Low-ish** — access to the whole technology reservoir, \"sharply reducing the duplication\" of pure project |\n| **Client focus / response speed** | **Poor and slow** — the division's own work takes precedence; several management layers to the client | **Fast** — centralised authority, short lines to senior management | **As fast and flexible as pure project**, and also responsive to the parent firm |\n| **Technical depth and currency** | **Best** — the division is the repository of technical lore and the normal career path | Deep in the project's technology, but staff **fall behind in their other expertise** | **Good** — people keep a functional \"home\" and the project reaches every division's expertise |\n| **Holistic / systems view** | **Not facilitated** — suboptimisation; slow cross-divisional sharing | **Supported** by the structure | Supported, **plus** company-wide balancing of resources across several projects |\n| **Consistency with parent policy** | **High** — procedural, administrative and policy continuity | **Low** — administrative corner-cutting; \"they don't understand our problems\" | **High** — access to the parent's administrative units preserves policy and builds project credibility |\n| **Motivation / identity** | **Weak** — off the mainstream; may look like a \"professional detour\" | **Very high** — strong separate identity, but → **projectitis** and fear of life after the project | High, with **less end-of-project anxiety** (members also feel close to the functional home); projectitis still serious in strong matrices |\n| **Characteristic failure mode** | Suboptimisation and nobody accountable | Cost, duplication, projectitis, technical obsolescence | **Conflict** — all five of its disadvantages involve conflict, mostly PM vs functional manager |\n| **Choose it when** | The focus is **in-depth application of one technology**, or heavy capital investment of a type the function normally uses | Many **similar** projects (construction), or a one-time unique task needing tight control that fits no single function | Inputs from **several functions** + sophisticated technology, but specialists **not needed full-time** — especially when projects **share experts** |\n\n---\n\n## 5.4 Mixed organizational systems\n\n**Divisionalisation** splits a large monolithic organisation into smaller, more flexible units, capturing some advantages of small specialised units while keeping some advantages of size (p. 200).\n\n| Mixed / divisional base | How projects sit in it (pp. 200–201) |\n|---|---|\n| **By product** | Each product project is a relatively autonomous, integrated element; engineering, finance etc. are dedicated to the product. **Software** projects are typical — they come in clusters belonging to one overall system, and pursuing them as a group keeps them compatible and raises the chance they finish together. Lawn-furniture example: plastic and aluminium divisions, a new style becomes a project in its division — and if a new product mixes plastic *and* aluminium, the **pure project** form forestalls interdivisional turf battles. |\n| **By territory** | Attractive to national organisations that are physically/geographically spread and whose products have geographic uniqueness (ladies' garments). |\n| **By customer division** | Used when projects reflect a paramount interest in different *types* of customer and customer preferences outweigh territory or product — consumer vs manufacturer, civilian vs military. |\n| **By process division** | Common in manufacturing: a project on new metal-removal methods based in the machining division, including several people from the R&D lab → effectively a **combination of functional and matrix** forms. |\n| **\"Mixed\" form** (Figure 5-4, p. 201) | Pure functional and pure project coexisting in one firm. Rarely as pure as drawn, but not uncommon. The usual practice: **spin off large, successful, long-run projects as subsidiaries or independent operations**, and nurture young, unstable, small projects under an existing division, then wean them to pure projects with their own identity, and finally allow a **venture team** — or for a larger project a **venture firm** — inside the parent. Example: **Texas Instruments' Speak and Spell**, developed by one of its employees. |\n| **Staff organization** (Figure 5-5, p. 201) | An apparently standard functional organisation **plus a staff office that administers all projects**. This frees the functional groups of administrative problems while using their technical talent. In a large specialty chemical firm it worked so well that the staff office became the nucleus of a **whole division whose sole purpose is administering projects** — the equivalent of a **\"project office\"** (→ §5.6). |\n\n**Danger of hybridisation (p. 200):** flexibility is the gain — the firm adapts its structure to special problems. But **dissimilar groupings inside the same accountability centre encourage overlap, duplication and friction** through incompatible interests → again, conflict between functional and project managers.\n\n**The staff form vs the matrix (p. 201):** in many ways indistinguishable, but the staff form is typically used for **small, short-run projects** where a full matrix is not justified. It shares several matrix advantages and disadvantages, but **project life is so short that projectitis is rarely contracted**. If the number or size of projects grows, it naturally evolves into a formal matrix.\n\n**The book's bottom line (p. 201):** **most firms adopt the matrix as the basic method of housing projects**, adding occasional pure, functional and hybrid projects where those have special advantages. The matrix's managerial difficulties \"are more than offset by their relatively low cost and by their ability to get access to broad technical support.\"\n\n---\n\n## 5.5 Choosing an organizational form\n\nAddressed to **senior management, not the PM** (p. 201). Even experienced practitioners find the choice hard to explain: it is determined by the situation but is **partly intuitive**, with few accepted design principles and **no step-by-step procedure**. All you can do is weigh the nature of the project, the characteristics and pros/cons of each option, and the **cultural preferences of the parent organisation**, and make the best compromise (p. 202).\n\n### ★ The matching criteria — form by situation (p. 202)\n\n| Choose… | When… |\n|---|---|\n| **Functional** | The major focus must be the **in-depth application of a technology** rather than minimising cost, meeting a specific schedule or responding speedily to change. Also when the project needs **large capital investment in equipment or buildings of a type the function normally uses**. |\n| **Pure project** | The firm runs **a large number of similar projects** (e.g. construction). Also for **one-time, highly specific, unique tasks requiring careful control** that do not suit a single functional area — e.g. developing a new product line. |\n| **Matrix** | The project needs **integration of inputs from several functional areas** and **reasonably sophisticated technology**, but **does not need all the technical specialists full-time** — then the matrix is \"the only satisfactory solution\". Especially true when **several projects must share technical experts**. Caveat: matrices are complex and a difficult challenge for the PM. |\n\n### The six-step selection procedure (p. 202; restated in the summary, pp. 221–222)\n\n1. **Define the project** with a statement of objective(s) identifying the major outcomes desired.\n2. Determine the **key tasks** for each objective and **locate the units in the parent organisation** that are the functional \"homes\" for such tasks.\n3. **Arrange the key tasks by sequence** and decompose them into **work packages** (summary wording: group them into logical work steps).\n4. Determine **which organisational units** must carry out the work packages and **which units must work particularly closely with which others**.\n5. List **special characteristics or assumptions**: level of technology needed, probable length and size, potential problems with the individuals likely to be assigned, possible **political problems between functions**, and the firm's **previous experience** with different ways of organising projects.\n6. **Choose a structure** in light of the above, with full cognisance of the pros and cons of each form.\n\nAdditional matters to weigh (p. 202): the individuals or small groups who will do the work and **their personalities**, the technology, the client(s), the **political relationships of the functional units**, the **culture of the parent organisation**, and environmental factors inside and outside the parent.\n\n> **PM in Practice — Trinatronic, Inc. (pp. 203–204) — a worked application of the six steps.** Objective: design, build and market a portable computer to a given spec, retailing at **≤ $1,500**. Thirteen key tasks (A–M) were mapped to organisational units (specs → Marketing + R&D; hardware design → R&D; production engineering → Engineering/Manufacturing; small-run manufacture and quality/reliability tests → Manufacturing + QA; operating systems and applications software → Software Products; all testing → QA; documentation and service system → Technical Writing sections; marketing program and demonstrations → Marketing). These fall into **four categories of work**: (1) design/build/test hardware, (2) design/write/test software, (3) set up production and service/repair systems with spares and manuals, (4) design the marketing effort with demonstrations, brochures and manuals — needing groups to design, to test, to engineer production, to design marketing, to prepare documents, \"and, lest we forget, a group to administer all the above groups\". That spans **at least three major divisions and perhaps half a dozen departments**.\n> **Decision and reasoning:** the design is within the state of the art but **hitting the ≤ $1,500 price requires an advance** in it; the project will run **18–24 months** and be the firm's most expensive ever. Therefore: **functional is inappropriate** (too much interaction between major divisions for one function to be a comfortable home); pure project or matrix are both feasible; **choose the simpler pure project** if the cost of the extra personnel is not too high. ★ The two switches that would flip the answer to **matrix**: (a) if the highly qualified scientific professionals were needed only **part-time**, or (b) if this were **only one of several such projects drawing on a common staff base**.\n\n---\n\n## 5.6 Two special cases — risk management and the project office\n\nThe chapter has tacitly assumed the project has, or can get, sufficient skill, knowledge and resources (p. 204). Acquiring technical resources mainly depends on the PM's **negotiating skill** (ch. 4). Two problems remain even then:\n\n1. **No project in history has ever been completed precisely as planned** — \"uncertainty is a way of life for PMs.\" → **risk management**.\n2. Executing a project is a complex managerial task needing planning, budgeting, scheduling and control tools a neophyte PM may not know, plus contractual, administrative and reporting duties required by law, the client and the project's organisational home. → the **project management office**.\n\n### Risk management (pp. 204–205)\n\nPMBOK® Guide 3rd ed. (2004): risk management is **\"the systematic process of identifying, analyzing, and responding to project risk\"**, consisting of six subprocesses — to which the authors add a seventh.\n\n| # | Subprocess | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Risk Management Planning** | Deciding how to approach and plan the project's risk management activities. |\n| 2 | **Risk Identification** | Determining which risks might affect the project and documenting their characteristics. |\n| 3 | **Qualitative Risk Analysis** | Qualitative analysis of risks and conditions to **prioritise** their impacts on project objectives. |\n| 4 | **Quantitative Risk Analysis** | Estimating **probability and consequences** of risks and the implications for project objectives. |\n| 5 | **Risk Response Planning** | Developing procedures and techniques to **enhance opportunities and reduce threats** to objectives. |\n| 6 | **Risk Monitoring and Control** | Monitoring residual risks, identifying new risks, executing risk-reduction plans, evaluating their effectiveness **throughout the life cycle**. |\n| **7** | **Create and Maintain a Risk Management Data Bank** ← *the authors' addition, not PMBOK's* | A **permanent record** of identified risks, the methods used to mitigate or resolve them, and the results of all risk management activities (p. 206). |\n\n**The two meanings of \"risk\" (footnote, p. 205):** one is the **probability that an event will occur** (decision theory, ch. 2 §2.5); the other is **danger or threat**. Context decides which is meant.\n\n**The authors' two criticisms of real-world practice (p. 206):**\n- **Subprocess 7 is almost invariably ignored** — \"if the risk management system has no memory, the task of risk identification will be horrendous\", and relying on individuals' recollections is itself risky.\n- Risk identification routinely (1) **fails to consider the project's external environment** and (2) **focuses on misfortune, overlooking the risk of positive things happening**.\n\n**Contents of the risk data bank (p. 206):** all **environments** that may impact the project; all **assumptions** in the preliminary plan that may be a source of risk; all **risks identified**, with estimated impacts and probabilities; the full list of **categories and key words** used for risks, assumptions and environments; details of every qualitative and quantitative estimate **with the method used**; minutes of all group meetings including every mitigating action — **including the decision to ignore a risk**; and the **actual outcomes** plus the results of the actions taken. Two conditions make it worth anything (pp. 206–207): it must be **available to anyone** doing risk management for the organisation, and the **multiple key words and categories** matter because the information must reach managers of widely varied disciplines. Otherwise every team starts from scratch and risk management becomes expensive, slow and ineffective. The literature rarely mentions **system memory** — exceptions: Royer (2000) and the latest PMBOK.\n\n#### FMEA — Failure Mode and Effect Analysis (p. 207) ★ the only calculable technique in the chapter\n\nFMEA (Stamatis, 2003) is **the application of a scoring model like those used for project selection in ch. 2**, in six steps:\n\n1. **List possible ways the project might fail.**\n2. Score **severity (S)** of each failure's consequences on a **10-point scale**: 1 = \"no effect\", 10 = \"very severe\".\n3. For each cause of failure, score the **likelihood (L)** of occurrence, 1–10: 1 = \"remote\", 10 = \"almost certain\".\n4. Score the **ability to detect (D)** a failure associated with each cause, 1–10: **1 = detection almost certain** with normal monitoring/control systems, **10 = practically certain the failure will *not* be detected** in time to avoid or mitigate it.\n5. Compute the **Risk Priority Number: RPN = S × L × D**.\n6. Consider ways to **reduce S, L and D** for each cause with a significantly high RPN.\n\n⚠️ The source text renders step 5 as \"RPN  S  L  D\" — the operators were lost in extraction. It is the standard FMEA product **S × L × D** (range 1–1000), consistent with \"a scoring model\". If Dr. Duraković writes it differently, follow him.\n\n**Note the direction of the D scale**: *high* D means *hard to detect*, so all three scores push RPN the same way — bigger is worse.\n\n#### An added note on risk identification (p. 207)\n\nRisks depend on the project's technology **and on the many environments the project exists in** — \"environment\" = anything outside the system (the project) that can affect or be affected by it. The **manner in which the risk management *process* is conducted** also depends on those environments: e.g. a strong corporate **cost-cutting** culture changes how risk managers identify and handle risks in personnel and resource allocation. Typical published practice considers only the project's internal environment (technical and interpersonal risks, occasionally negative market risks); Jiang et al. (2001) is a thoughtful IT-risk model that still **ignores competitors, the IT market, user industries and the legal environment**.\n\n#### Organizing for risk management (pp. 207–208)\n\n- No single risk management unit can serve all projects of a firm, because projects differ in technology, markets, regulators and stakeholders. **In general a unique risk management group is formed for each project** — though a family of similar routine projects (e.g. machine repair and maintenance of similar size and scope) may share one group.\n- **Membership depends on the project.** For a **new product**: (1) a specialist in the product's science/technology; (2) a market specialist (market size, sales, cannibalisation of the firm's other products, state of competition); (3) a manufacturing specialist (can we or our subcontractor make it at acceptable quality?); (4) a purchasing specialist (**supply-chain** risks); (5) a **product safety expert** for product liability, plus a public relations professional and an attorney for safety claims; (6) a **patent attorney** for infringement risk; (7) someone (perhaps a program manager) who knows the firm's **complete project set** and can spot projects that might contribute to or profit from this one; (8) the same or another person who can foresee **personnel, resource or scheduling conflicts**; (9) a **government relations expert** on licences and approvals. Some of these attend only the risk-identification and response-planning sessions. The group may use the **window-of-opportunity** idea from ch. 2 to estimate market risk.\n- For a **process-improvement** project instead: **industrial engineers** (can current production schedules be maintained while a new machine is installed?), **financial experts** (glitches in estimated cash flows), an **air and water pollution specialist**, and **potential users of the process**.\n- **Timing: \"It is never too early in the life of a project to begin managing risk.\"** A sensible selection decision is impossible without knowing the risks, so the **risk management plan and initial risk identification must be done before the project is formally selected** — therefore the risk management group is formed **as soon as a potential project is identified**.\n- Early risks are loosely defined and mostly **external** (state of technology, business conditions in relevant industries); the usual response is to **track the pertinent environments** and estimate the chance the project survives various conditions. Risks of project technology, schedule, budget and resource allocation only take shape in the **planning** stage. (Developing the risk management plan itself: ch. 6.)\n- Because risk analysis uses techniques many PMs are not trained in, **some organisations put risk specialists in a project office** and have them staff each project's risk management activities.\n\n#### Dealing with disaster (pp. 208–209)\n\nNormal risks are handled by an **expected-value cost/benefit** approach: estimated loss (or gain) × probability of occurrence, compared with the **expected cost of mitigating or preventing** it. **Invest in prevention when the reduction in expected loss exceeds the cost of mitigation or prevention.**\n\nThe hard case is **catastrophic, potentially ruinous loss at very low probability** — a run on a bank, a strike at a critical supplier's plant, a flood that shuts a construction project, a terrorist attack on a building, the discovery that a substance in a complex drug may be toxic. Here the cost may be massive but the probability so small that the **expected cost of the disaster is less than that of many smaller, far more probable risks** — which is exactly why expected value alone misleads. The book recommends Sheffi's *The Resilient Enterprise* (2005) on how firms cope with catastrophes affecting facilities, supply chains and customer bases.\n\n> **PM in Practice — M5 East Tunnel, Sydney (p. 205):** built under strict budget and schedule requirements that \"may have been excessive\". An **inexpensive computer system with a high failure rate** makes the security cameras fail often, forcing operators to close the tunnel because they cannot react to an accident, fire or excessive pollution. Built for **70,000 vehicles/day**, it now carries **100,000**, so any glitch snarls traffic immediately. A managerial risk analysis **including the risk of overuse** might have mandated more reliable computers once the costs of failure were counted.\n\n### The Project Management Office (PMO) (pp. 209–212)\n\nWith the move toward \"management by projects\", an organisational entity is needed to help manage the multiplying projects: the **Project Management Office (PMO)**, a.k.a. the **Project Office**, **Program Management Office**, **Project Support Office** (p. 209). Forms vary and so does reporting level, from low in the organisation to the very top.\n\n**Traits of the best PMOs (Baker, 2007; p. 209):** run like the best businesses (a **business plan**, focused, emphasis on **results**); **strong executive support**; **future-oriented learning organisations**; and offering **the best project leadership in the organisation**.\n\n**Glossary (p. 223):** a PMO is \"an office to deal with multiple projects and charged with improving the **project management maturity** and expertise of the organization, as well as increasing the **success rate** of projects.\" Compare the glossary's **war room** (p. 223): \"a project office where the latest detail on project progress is available\", possibly also a source of technical assistance.\n\n#### Why firms start a PMO — Block et al. (2001) survey (p. 210)\n\n| Why they started a PMO | Share | What the PMO actually does | Share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Establish **consistent PM standards and methods**; and **initiated by senior management direction** | almost **⅔** | Established/maintained **standard project processes** | **78%** |\n| **Eliminate project delays** and **correct poor project planning** | about **half** | Offered **consulting help** on projects | **64%** |\n| Improve project performance, eliminate **cost overruns** | just under **40%** | Offered **training and mentoring** | **58%** |\n| Reduce **customer dissatisfaction** | about **¼** | **Project tracking** (≈half) · **portfolio management** (somewhat less) · kept a **stable of PMs** | **28%** |\n\n#### Purposes (p. 210)\n\nA major contribution is establishing **project administration procedures** for selecting, initialising, planning, budgeting and scheduling projects, and serving as a **repository** for reports on the performance of the planning, budgeting, scheduling and resource-allocation processes. PMO files also hold reports on risk management, project audits, evaluations and histories.\n\nTwo **unarticulated overarching purposes**: (1) ensuring the firm's **project portfolio supports organisational goals and strategy** (ch. 2) — the PMO is then \"the critical tie between strategic management and the project managers\"; (2) the **gradual assimilation of good project management practice into the whole organisation**, moving it from a functional to a project-based form **in culture as well as structure**.\n\n> ★ **The boundary rule (p. 210):** the PMO's role is that of an **enabler/facilitator of projects, not the doer of projects**. Top management must not let the PMO usurp the technical aspects — scheduling, budgeting etc. — which are **the project manager's responsibility**. When the PMO does get involved in project management tasks, it should be to **facilitate liaison with top management**, not to do the team's work.\n\n#### Tasks of the PMO (Block, 1999; pp. 210–211) ★ review question 11\n\nGrouped, the ~19 listed tasks fall into five jobs:\n\n| Job | Tasks |\n|---|---|\n| **Process** | Establish and enforce good PM processes (bidding, risk analysis, project selection, progress reports, executing contracts, selecting software); **assess and improve the organisation's project management maturity**; develop and improve an **enterprise project management system** |\n| **People** | Offer **training** and help PMs get **certified**; identify, develop and **mentor** PMs and keep a stable of candidates; offer **consulting** to PMs; help with **administrative details** such as status reports; serve as a **\"home\"** where PMs communicate with each other and with PMO staff; **champion** project management excellence |\n| **Portfolio and risk** | Establish a process for **estimating and evaluating risk**; determine whether a new project **\"fits\"** the changing organisation; identify **downstream market/organisational changes** and their impact (still relevant? scope change? cost effects?); review and manage the **project risk portfolio**, including **limiting the number of active projects**, **reining in runaway projects** and managing potential disasters |\n| **Oversight** | Conduct **reviews and audits**, particularly **early** in each life cycle, reporting progress against organisational goals |\n| **Infrastructure** | Maintain and store **project archives**; establish a **project resource database** and manage the resource pool; **collect and disseminate** techniques from evaluations; **assist in project termination** |\n\n**Phasing (p. 211):** *short term* (first few months) — assess current practices, perhaps evaluate the progress of each project. *Midterm* — start standardising processes and procedures, help individual projects with e.g. risk analysis and administrative details, perhaps initiate a strategic **portfolio analysis**. *Long term* (after about a year) — assemble a **resource database**, train PMs, conduct **audits**, consult on individual projects.\n\n**Reality check (p. 211):** it would be rare for a PMO to handle all of the above. Many **control the Project Selection Process** and manage a few related matters (records and archives, risk management, training new PMs). The consultant quoted says the PMO's major role is to create and facilitate the **project selection methodology** supporting upper management's decisions — evaluating proposed initiatives against company goals, estimating costs, proving the proposed **ROI** — then shifting to checking whether the funded project is meeting its objectives. Many PMOs give little or no direct support during execution but conduct **several major evaluations of all portfolio projects, during the life cycle and after the fact**, to see whether they achieved what was claimed at selection.\n\n#### Forms and reporting level (pp. 211–212)\n\n| Level of ambition | What the PMO does |\n|---|---|\n| **Limited** | An **information centre**: reports on project progress, assesses the organisation's project maturity |\n| **Next level** | Establishes PM procedures and practices, promulgates **lessons learned**, creates a **risk analysis database**, helps PMs with administrative/managerial matters, possibly basic training |\n| **Upper level** | Establishes a **resource database**, monitors **interproject dependencies**, manages the **portfolio** to attain organisational goals, **audits and prioritises** individual projects, establishes an **enterprise project management system** |\n\nBy **reporting level**: to test a PMO before organisation-wide approval, top management may place it in a **functional department** (IT, Engineering), where its main job is helping that department's PMs with their individual projects. At **business level** it takes on more responsibility for good practice and may offer basic training. At **higher levels** its responsibilities broaden and become **less tactical and more strategic**. ★ The book calls the bottom-up pilot **\"a risky way to implement a PMO\"** if the goal is organisation-wide project capability: failing to rescue one troubled engineering project says nothing about the PMO's value in performing the many other tasks.\n\n#### Implementing the PMO (p. 212)\n\n- **Treat the PMO itself as a project** and apply good project management procedures to it.\n- Do not initiate it until it has the **full commitment of top management**, and give it a **senior management sponsor/champion** determined to see it through.\n- One route: a **pilot program** in an area under the champion's responsibility → assess it, correct mistakes, **publicise the benefits** to the rest of the organisation. Benefits increase progressively with the PMO's reach.\n- **Liu et al. (2007):** PMOs have a significantly positive impact on projects with **high task uncertainty**, and that positive impact **decreases as task uncertainty diminishes**.\n- **Why PMOs fail (Tennant, 2001):** the executives who establish them **often do not understand project management practices themselves**, so they hold unrealistic expectations — e.g. temporary help for a project in trouble, or cost reductions on ongoing projects. **\"The PMO is not a quick fix for saving projects that are failing\"**; its primary objective is to improve project management processes over the long run. PMOs also cannot correct **upper-management failures**: inappropriate project goals, insufficient project support, inadequate resource availability. A recent trend is **outsourcing the PMO functions** themselves — the authors wonder whether that signals impending trouble or wise recognition of upper management's limits.\n\n> **PM in Practice — TSA coordination center (p. 210):** 3 months and $20 million to build a **13,500 ft²** centre coordinating up to **300 tradespeople** working simultaneously. A strong PMO was crucial: it **accelerated procurement and approval, halving times in some cases**, and engaged a team leader, a master scheduler, a master financial manager, a procurement specialist, a civil engineer and other specialists. Finished in **97 days, on budget**, and won an award from the National Association of Industrial and Office Properties.\n\n---\n\n## 5.7 The project team\n\nDifferent projects have vastly different staff needs. The book's concrete example is a **fairly large software engineering project**; the titles would change for a construction or medical research project but **the roles played would be similar** (p. 212).\n\n### The six key project-office roles (p. 213; Figure 5-6, p. 215)\n\n| Role | Responsibilities |\n|---|---|\n| **Systems architect** | The basic **product design and development**: functional analysis, specifications, drawings, cost estimates, documentation. |\n| **Development engineer** | **Efficient production** of the product or process the architect designed: manufacturing engineering, design and production of code, **unit testing**, production scheduling, other production tasks. |\n| **Test engineer** | **Installation, testing and support** of the product/process once its engineering is complete. |\n| **Contract administrator** | All **official paperwork**: standards compliance (including quality/reliability), customer (engineering) changes, billings, questions, complaints, legal aspects, costs, and negotiation of other matters related to the contract authorising the project. Not uncommonly also **project historian and archivist**. |\n| **Project controller** | **Daily account** of budgets, cost variances, labour charges, project supplies, capital equipment status; regular reports; close touch with both the PM and the **company controller**. Can serve as historian if the administrator does not. |\n| **Support services manager** | Product support, **subcontractors**, data processing, purchasing, contract negotiation, general management support. |\n\n★ **Of these, the two that must report directly to the PM are the systems architect and the project controller** (p. 213) — because they control two of the project's three main goals, **technical performance** and **budget**. (**The PM is usually in personal control of the schedule.**) On a large project all six could work out of the project office and report directly to the PM.\n\n⚠️ **Inconsistency in the book.** The end-of-chapter summary (p. 222) says the direct reports are \"the **project engineer** and project controller\", and lists the larger team as \"project engineer, manufacturing engineer, **field manager**, contract administrator, project controller, support service manager\" — the 6th-edition titles. §5.7 (p. 213) uses **systems architect / development engineer / test engineer**. Same roles, renamed for the software example; the summary was not updated. If the exam uses the old names, read *project engineer* ≈ *systems architect*. The summary also asserts that **\"every project should have a project office, even if it must be shared with another project\"** (p. 222) — a claim the body of §5.7 does not repeat.\n\n### Staffing the team (pp. 213–214)\n\n1. The PM works from a **forecast of personnel needs over the project life cycle**.\n2. A **work breakdown structure (WBS)** is prepared to determine the exact nature of the tasks required (described and illustrated in **chapter 6**).\n3. **Skill requirements** for those tasks are assessed and **like skills aggregated** to determine workforce needs.\n4. The **functional departments** are then contacted to locate individuals who meet those needs.\n\n⚠️ A warning worth remembering (p. 214): developing the WBS **often requires consulting senior programmers and testers**, and the PM must understand, plan for and closely monitor the **effect of those consultations on current projects** — senior people are commonly pulled off their own work to handle planning crises arising elsewhere in the WBS.\n\n**Subcontracting (p. 214)** is adopted when appropriately skilled personnel are unavailable or cannot be located, when subcontractors deliver at lower cost, or when special equipment is not available in-house. The need to subcontract **grows as firms downsize**. Glossary (p. 223): **subcontract** = \"subletting tasks out to smaller contractors.\"\n\n**The double negotiation (p. 214):** many firms insist on using **\"local\" resources** when available, for better control of resource usage and quality. So the PM typically must negotiate with **both the department head and the employee** — \"selling\" the employee on the challenge and excitement of the project, and convincing the department head that lending the employee serves the department head's own interest.\n\n### ★ Who should report directly to the PM (or to the PM's deputy, often the project architect) (p. 214)\n\n1. **Senior project team members** who will have a **long-term relationship** with the project.\n2. Those with whom the PM will require **continuous or close communication**.\n3. Those with **rare skills necessary to project success**.\n\nThe PM must rely on **reason** to persuade a department head to lend such people: the head, who sees the project as a more or less glamorous source of prestige the department cannot share, \"has little natural motivation to be cooperative.\" **Project success depends on the PM's political skill as much as on the team's technical skill** (p. 214).\n\n### The drift toward weak matrices (pp. 214–215)\n\nWeaker matrices have become more and more frequent: asked how many people report directly to them, PMs answering **\"None!\"** is not uncommon. The authors' view of the **most common structure of all**: a PM, **one or two key skilled contributors** who may be full-time, and a wide variety of **services or capacity** supplied by functional groups. Typical of R&D projects inside larger programs — in a **pharmaceutical** project, one or two senior scientists and lab technicians are assigned, while toxicity testing, efficacy testing and writing the product insert arrive as **deliverables from functional units** rather than as people.\n\nConsequence (p. 215): the PM bargains for fewer individuals **but negotiating skill is just as critical**. Weak-matrix success typically depends on the **few technical specialists assigned directly to the project**, so the PM's ability to negotiate both for those technicians and for **timely delivery of services from functional departments** is a key determinant of success.\n\n> **PM in Practice — Lucasfilm (p. 215):** the *Star Wars* creators need quick-moving, flexible, **multidisciplinary teams of insiders and freelance outsiders** for short-lived, rapidly forming and dissolving projects — standard project management approaches are **too slow** for this environment. Lucasfilm moved all its disparate units into a **$350 million single-roofed complex** so they can interact as the moment's tasks require; managers are **\"rootless\"**, sweeping through office suites depending on the talents needed next. As performance pressure with tighter schedules and budgets increases, the authors suggest this may be the future of project organisation and leadership.\n\n---\n\n## 5.8 Human factors and the project team\n\nThe frame (p. 216): meeting schedule and cost without compromising performance **looks** technical but is \"a technical problem with a human dimension\", and **the principles and practices of good general management still apply**.\n\n### 1. Perfectionism (p. 216)\n\n**Project professionals tend to be perfectionists.** Out of pride of workmanship they keep improving — and therefore **changing** — the product, which makes meeting goals almost impossible, because **changes cause delays**. Two responses: the PM must **continually stress the importance of meeting due dates**, and should establish a **technical change procedure at the beginning of the project** to control the incidence and frequency of change. ★ The authors' caution: it would **not be wise to assume everyone will automatically follow** such a procedure. (More in chs. 6 and 11.)\n\n### 2. Motivation (pp. 216–217)\n\nThe PM often has **little control over economic rewards and promotions** — certainly true when the matrix is weak — but can still motivate.\n\n- **Herzberg (1968)**, studying what motivates technical employees (engineers, scientists, project professionals), holds that the motivators are **recognition, achievement, the work itself, responsibility, advancement, and the chance to learn new skills**. It is the PM's responsibility to **structure project work so as to emphasise these factors**.\n- The authors' own device: **judicious \"thank you\" notes** from the PM to functional managers who supplied capable, committed people or effective capacity — **with copies to the individuals and to the functional manager's boss**. Crucially, **do not write them for mediocre or poor performance**; an occasional **\"non-thank you\" note** may be in order.\n- **Participative management** (Argyris, Likert, McGregor, 1950s–60s): the worker or team should play a significant role in deciding **what means** are used to meet desired ends and in finding better ways of doing things. **Management By Objectives (MBO)** was the early mechanism — it let the worker take responsibility for the design and performance of a task under controlled conditions. Glossary (p. 222): MBO = a 1960s approach encouraging managers to give subordinates more freedom in determining **how** to achieve task objectives.\n- Later programs without some of MBO's problems: **Six Sigma, Employee Involvement (EI)** a.k.a. **continuous improvement teams (CIT)**, and **Total Quality Management (TQM)**; plus the rise of **self-directed teams (SDT / SDWT)** and **self-managed teams (SMT)**. They differ somewhat in structure and in the decision authority and autonomy exercised, but all aim at improving worker performance, production methods and product quality.\n\n#### Six advantages of empowerment for project teams (p. 217) ★\n\n1. It **harnesses the team's ability to manipulate tasks** so project objectives are met; the team is encouraged to find better ways.\n2. **Professionals do not like being micromanaged** — participative management gives them a goal and lets them design their own methods (usually within some constraints on their authority).\n3. Members **know they are responsible and accountable** for achieving the deliverables.\n4. There is a good chance of **synergistic solutions** from team interaction.\n5. Members get **timely feedback** on their performance.\n6. The PM gains **a tool for evaluating the team's performance**.\n\nAll six increase motivation. But the success of SDWTs and all other teams **ultimately depends on a clear statement of what the team must accomplish, when, and at what cost**: senior management must \"make the effort to clearly delineate project goals, responsibilities, and authority\" (Ford et al., 1992; Nelson, 1998). And **giving a project to a team does not supersede the need for competent project management skills** (p. 217).\n\n### 3. Team building (pp. 217–218)\n\nChapter 6's **action plan / WBS** — concepts \"more or less borrowed from MBO\" — is a detailed planning and scheduling technique built by the PM **together with the team**. The resulting document is **both a plan and a control mechanism**, and because the process is participative and makes members accountable for their own parts, it **motivates them and clearly denotes the degree to which they are mutually dependent**. The authors say this last outcome is **not well recognised in the team-building literature**. Glossary (p. 222): **action plan** = a detailed plan of what needs to be done and when.\n\n> ★ **The core claim (p. 218):** bringing people together — even in the same organisation, contributing to the same objectives — **does not mean they will behave like a team**. Organising the work so that **members are mutually dependent and recognise it** produces a strong impetus to form a team. \"Project success will be associated with teamwork, and project failure will surely result if the group does not work as a team.\" If many members are also **problem oriented** (ch. 3 §3.2), the likelihood rises further.\n\n- **Tippet et al. (1995):** companies are generally doing **a poor job of team building**; weak areas are **lack of effective rewards**, **inadequate individual and team performance feedback mechanisms**, and **inadequate individual and team goal-setting**.\n- **Dewhurst (1998):** a group of individuals working **independently** is a **\"Name-Only-Team\" (NOT)**, for which the \"work group math\" is **2 + 2 = 3 or less**. ⚠️ The source renders this as \"2  2  3 or less\" — operators lost in extraction; the sense is unambiguously that a NOT delivers *less than* the sum of its parts.\n- Recommended reading: **Lencioni (2002)**, *The Five Dysfunctions of a Team*, \"a leadership fable\" — the authors' first choice if you read only one work on teams. Also Carlisle's (1976) classic \"MacGregor\" on the power of delegation, the inspiration for *The One Minute Manager*.\n\n### 4. Conflict (pp. 218–221)\n\nConflict between team members, and between team members and outsiders **including the client**, \"seems to be the natural state of existence for projects\". The authors' strong feeling: **the PM who cannot manage conflict is doomed to failure**. **Negotiation is the PM's primary tool** for resolving it — but conflict **may be a highly creative force** in a project team when controlled by an astute PM (p. 218).\n\n**Source of most project conflict (p. 218):** individuals viewing the project **through the eyes of their own discipline or department** (de Laat 1994; Hughes 1998; Pelled et al. 1994). Such people are **not problem oriented** and are rarely effective team members. The infighting they cause is **perceived by most team members as \"political\"**, and **if the PM lets project decisions be dictated by that infighting, the project is apt to fail**.\n\n**Handling it (p. 221):** conflict can be handled several ways, but one thing is certain — **\"conflict avoiders do not make successful project managers.\"** Compromise is occasionally helpful; **most often gently confronting the conflict is the method of choice**. ★ The key to resolution rests on **the manager's ability to transform a win–lose situation into win–win**.\n\n#### Table 5-1 — major conflict source by life-cycle phase, with recommendations (Thamhain & Wilemon, 1975; p. 219) ★\n\n| Phase | Conflict source | Recommendations |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Project formation** | **Priorities** | Clearly defined plans; joint decision making and/or consultation with affected parties; stress the project's importance to organisational goals |\n| | **Procedures** | Develop detailed administrative operating procedures; secure approval from key administrators; develop a statement of understanding or **charter** |\n| | **Schedules** | Develop schedule commitments **in advance of actual project commencement**; forecast other departments' priorities and their possible impact |\n| **Buildup** | **Priorities** | Effective feedback to support areas on forecast plans and needs via **status review sessions** |\n| | **Schedules** | Schedule **work breakdown packages** (project subunits) **in cooperation with functional groups** |\n| | **Procedures** | **Contingency planning** on key administrative issues |\n| **Main program** | **Schedules** | Continually monitor work in progress; communicate results to affected parties; forecast problems and consider alternatives; identify trouble spots needing closer surveillance |\n| | **Technical** | Early resolution of technical problems; communicate schedule and budget restraints to technical personnel; emphasise adequate early technical testing; facilitate early agreement on final designs |\n| | **Labor** | Forecast and communicate **staffing requirements early**; establish staffing requirements and priorities with functional and staff groups |\n| **Phaseout** | **Schedules** | Close schedule monitoring; consider **reallocating available staff** to critical areas prone to slippage; prompt resolution of technical issues affecting schedules |\n| | **Personality and labor** | Develop plans for **reallocation of people on completion**; maintain harmonious relationships with team and support groups; **try to loosen up the high-stress environment** |\n\n#### Table 5-2 — number of conflicts during a sample project (p. 219)\n\n| Source of conflict | Start | Early | Main | Late |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Project priorities | 27 | **35** | 24 | 16 |\n| Administrative procedures | 26 | 27 | 15 | 9 |\n| Technical trade-offs | 18 | 26 | **31** | 11 |\n| Staffing | 21 | 25 | 25 | 17 |\n| Support cost estimates | 20 | 13 | 15 | 11 |\n| **Schedules** | 25 | 29 | **36** | **30** |\n| Personalities | 16 | 19 | 15 | 17 |\n\n**Narrative of the pattern (pp. 218–219, 221–222; Figure 5-7, p. 220, plots conflict *intensity* over the life cycle):** at **formation**, priorities, procedures and schedules have roughly **equal** potential as the focus of conflict. In **buildup**, **priorities** become significantly more important than any other factor (procedures are by now almost entirely established). In the **main program**, priorities are finally settled, so **schedules become the biggest cause of trouble**, followed by **technical disagreements**, with adequate support also a concern. At **finish/phaseout**, **meeting the schedule** is critical, but **interpersonal tensions easily ignored early suddenly erupt** in the last hectic weeks, worsened by **worry about reassignment**. Summary compression (p. 222): **priorities and policies at first; schedule and technical problems in the main phase; schedule and personal issues near termination.**\n\n✅ **Table 5-2 verified.** The PDF dumps this table with each row's label *after* its four numbers, so it had\nto be reconstructed. All 28 values were re-checked against the source during ingest and the mapping above is\ncorrect. The FMEA risk priority number, which the PDF renders as `RPN  S  L  D` with the operators stripped,\nis **RPN = S × L × D** — consistent with the book's own step 6, \"reduce the S, L and D for each cause with a\nsignificantly high RPN\".\n\n⚠️ The three exhibits use three different sets of phase names — Table 5-2 \"Start / Early / Main / Late\", Table 5-1 \"Project formation / Buildup / Main program / Phaseout\", Figure 5-7 \"at the project formation / early program phases / during the main program / toward the end\". Treat them as the same four phases. Table 5-2's own numbers put the **priorities** peak in *Early* (35) and the **schedules** peak in *Main* (36), matching the narrative.\n\n> **PM in Practice — Sasol Benfield column repair, South Africa (pp. 220–221):** a fire in a carbonate regeneration column meant the damaged part of a **19 ft wide × 231 ft long** column had to be cut out and replaced, with only **40 days** allowed. Ground rules: the project is **schedule-driven, not cost-driven**; **no float anywhere**; **always plan to reduce scheduled times, not meet them**; **resources are not a limitation**; **continuous communication across all levels**; **safety not compromised**; **quality not compromised**. Plus deliberate team measures: a **higher premium on team than individual performance**; attention to the \"soft\" side (transport, accommodation, food, avoiding excessive overtime, matching each member's preferred communication form); a **communication board updated twice daily** showing progress and **time saved with the name of the person who achieved it**; **twice-daily shift-change meetings** and **twice-daily planning meetings** detailing the next two days' work. Result: people proposed ideas to save **five minutes**; the project finished in **25 days — 15 early — saving over $21 million of an $85 million budget** (≈ 40% off the schedule, 25% off the cost).\n\n---\n\n## Case and Directed Reading (pp. 226–235)\n\n**Case — Oilwell Cable Company, Inc. (pp. 226–229).** A Lawrence, Kansas maker of wire/cable for submersible oil-well pumps, run largely by **eleven employee teams** (six production, plus management, resource, technical, administrative, maintenance) that set their own work and vacation schedules and job functions, handle grievances and even reprimand members. Key context: in a **process** firm direct labour is ~5% of product cost and indirect labour another 5%, versus ~30% in a job shop — so **employee commitment, not wage control, is the competitive lever**. In the 1990 downturn the teams chose the method (**layoffs**) and revised management's recommended list, taking **three full days** against normal 1.5-hour weekly meetings. Absenteeism 0.7%; two voluntary departures since 1988; **$1,000/person/year** training spend. Operations manager **Norm St. Laurent** wants to add **microprocessors** to rubber mixing equipment (cutting production time ~1% and scrap from 1% to 0.5%, displacing nobody) and must decide whether to route it through the rubber-mixing team first — an earlier, simpler change made without the team's knowledge \"wound up causing considerable trouble\". ★ The chapter-5 hooks: **question 3** — if the production team approves and implements the conversion, **what form of project organization is that?**; **question 4** — would a **PMO** make sense at this size, or is there a better arrangement?; question 5 is a small **NPV** calculation ($25,000 microprocessors + $5,000 installation, 10% margin); question 6 spots an inconsistency between Norm's recollection of 1985–89 productivity and Exhibit 2.\n\n**Directed Reading — \"The Virtual Project: Managing Tomorrow's Team Today\" (Adams & Adams, 1997; pp. 229–235).** Classical team-building research assumed **bureaucratic conditions** — shared workplace, frequent contact, people who know each other and expect to keep working together. Kast & Rosenzweig define teamwork as \"small groups of three to fifteen people that meet face-to-face\", and PMBOK lists **collocation** among its five team-development tools: **both ignore the virtual project.** A **virtual project** (\"distributed team\") is one whose participants are dispersed enough that they **seldom if ever meet face-to-face** — the distance need not be great, since colleagues in one industrial complex whose schedules never allow a meeting also qualify. Across time zones direct communication is severely limited and costs rise, so **electronic systems must carry the burden of team development**.\n\n**Kostner's six issues → the article's remedies (sidebar, p. 231):**\n\n| Issue (problem) | Remedy |\n|---|---|\n| **Developing trust** — *the greatest challenge for the remote PM*; irregular communication, low familiarity, \"us vs them\" | Provide and use a **variety of communication alternatives**; communicate electronically except where signatures are required; give **all** members the project management software |\n| **Developing group identity** — fewer shared experiences, no cohesion, roles not understood | Regular teleconferences; manage the agenda so a variety of participants are involved; **logos, mottoes, creative humour**; stay in contact between meetings; **exclude no one** |\n| **Sharing information** — no informal lunch/coffee discussion, no common transmission system | Use technology for extra sharing (phones, pagers, faxes, e-mail, Internet); **distribute all key reports to all members**; one **central access point** (project web page, LAN account) |\n| **Developing clear structures** — uncertain roles, clashing cultures, no defined decision process | **Standard meeting formats**; define goals, objectives, problems and concerns at the **kickoff** and reiterate often; have participants describe problems and **evaluate risks as a group** |\n| **Formation of \"cliques\"** — members bond with whoever is co-located, breeding antagonism | The PM **cannot prevent them but can manage them**: identify and track them, form **subcommittees drawing from different cliques**, create chances to mix |\n| **Understanding information** — each member's information is **inconsistent and incomplete**, seen from a different perspective (\"inequities of information\") | Ask members to **explain their viewpoints** and describe planned actions with impacts on others; use **different levels of information for different participants** |\n\n**The four technologies (pp. 232–233):** **Internet/e-mail** — best for detailed status data that phone or video cannot carry, and cheaper than fax at volume; ★ firms that restrict e-mail to \"official\" business and strip out jokes destroy exactly the **informal traffic that substitutes for personal contact**. **The pager** — numbers published with the team roster, so members are released from irrelevant agenda items and **paged in** when needed (set up at the kickoff, with a **911 code** for emergencies). **Teleconferencing** — audio only, so speakers must identify themselves; designed for two people, so flow gets hard as participants are added; the PM **cannot manage the results of communication, only the process**; publish a detailed agenda **2–3 days ahead** (purpose, day-date-time, call-in number, expected duration, chair, items, key participants per item), cap the call at about **1.5 hours**, group the agenda into *due by now* and *due before next meeting*, carry a **\"deliverables\"** topic naming owners, and put **open discussion last** so project-critical beats individual-critical; raise **major schedule changes at the beginning**. **Videoconferencing** — adds body language and facial expression, \"in many cases transferring more meaning than the actual words\", and **full team participation in building the initial WBS and project plan at the kickoff is crucial to commitment**, so make the kickoff videoconference-accessible; but it needs pre-arranged sites, is expensive, and bandwidth delay plus camera self-consciousness make it \"stiff and stilted\".\n\n**Closing claim (pp. 233–234):** basic team-building knowledge is still essential, but the **methods** have changed, and fluency in all current electronic communication techniques is **\"rapidly becoming a mandatory skill\"** for anyone involved in virtual projects.\n\n---\n\n## Why this matters for the exam\n\n- ★ **The three-form comparison is the single most likely question.** Review question 2 is literally \"identify the advantages and disadvantages of the **matrix** form\", and question 7 is \"advantages and disadvantages of housing a project in a **functional** form\". Be able to produce **5 functional advantages / 7 functional disadvantages, 9 pure-project advantages / 6 disadvantages, 7 matrix advantages / 5 disadvantages** — or at least four or five of each, with the mechanism, not just the label.\n- ★ **\"Name the four basic types of project organization and give one characteristic, advantage and disadvantage of each\"** (review question 3) — the four are **functional, pure project, matrix, mixed**. Do not forget *mixed*; it is easy to drop because §5.4 has no advantage/disadvantage list of its own.\n- ★ **A scenario question asking you to choose a form and justify it** (Hydrobuck incident, p. 224; Trinatronic; \"how would you organize a project to develop a complex new color fax–copy machine, versus a simpler disk drive?\", question 17). Answer with the p. 202 matching criteria *plus* the Trinatronic switches: part-time specialists or several projects sharing a staff base ⇒ **matrix**; many similar projects, or a unique task needing tight control across functions ⇒ **pure project**; depth of one technology or heavy function-specific capital ⇒ **functional**. Then name one disadvantage you are accepting.\n- **The matrix authority split and unity of command.** \"The PM controls *when* and *what*; the functional manager controls *who* and *what technology*\" — plus administrative vs technological decisions, two bosses, and the weak-vs-strong discriminator (**capacity vs people**).\n- **Conflict over the life cycle** (review question 9, discussion question 16, Tables 5-1/5-2, Figure 5-7). Memorise the sequence **priorities/procedures → priorities → schedules + technical → schedules + personality/reassignment**, and be able to give one Thamhain recommendation per phase.\n- **Risk management and the PMO** (review questions 5, 10, 11; discussion questions 18, 23). The **six PMBOK subprocesses plus the authors' seventh (the risk data bank)**, the **FMEA six steps with RPN = S × L × D**, the PMO's **enabler-not-doer** boundary, and the Tennant point that a **PMO is not a quick fix for failing projects**.\n- Smaller but cheap marks: **program manager vs project manager** (question 1), **the systems architect's duties** (question 8), **who reports directly to the PM** (the three criteria), **projectitis**, **suboptimisation**, **concurrent engineering**, and **three ways of dealing with conflict — plus the explicit \"no, it need not be zero-sum: turn win–lose into win–win\"** (question 6).\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What are the three organisational decisions that arise the moment any project is initiated?","a":"How to tie the project to the parent firm; how to organise the project itself; how to organise activities common to other projects (p. 190)."},{"q":"Where should a project be placed if it is housed in a functional organisation?","a":"In the functional unit with the greatest interest in the project's success, or the one that can be most helpful in implementing it (p. 191)."},{"q":"Give three advantages of housing a project in a functional division.","a":"Maximum flexibility in staff use (experts assigned temporarily then returned); one expert can serve many projects; specialists grouped together share knowledge; plus technological/procedural continuity and preservation of the normal career path (pp. 191–192)."},{"q":"Give four disadvantages of the functional form.","a":"The client is not the focus; the division is function- rather than problem-oriented; responsibility is often not pinpointed; response to the client is slow through layers of management; suboptimisation of issues outside the division's interests; weak motivation; no holistic design (p. 192)."},{"q":"Give four advantages of the pure project form.","a":"The PM has full line authority; everyone reports to the PM; communication lines are short (straight to senior management); a permanent cadre of experts can form (Lockheed's \"Skunk Works\"); high commitment; swift decisions; unity of command; structural simplicity; and support for a holistic/systems approach (p. 194)."},{"q":"Why is the pure project form expensive?","a":"Each project tends to be fully staffed, so support functions are duplicated (\"personnel managers come in integers, not fractions\"), and PMs stockpile people and equipment — hiring critical skills when available rather than when needed and keeping them longer than needed (pp. 194–195)."},{"q":"What is projectitis?","a":"Inappropriately intense loyalty to the project — a strong \"we–they\" divisiveness that distorts relations with the parent organisation and turns rivalry into bitter political infighting; it also brings worry about \"life after the project ends\" (pp. 195, 223)."},{"q":"Define the matrix organization.","a":"A pure project organization overlaid on the functional divisions of the parent firm, combining the two extremes so as to gain advantages of each and avoid some disadvantages of both (p. 195)."},{"q":"In a matrix, what does the project manager control and what do the functional managers control?","a":"The PM controls *when* and *what* the assigned people do (administrative decisions); the functional managers control *who* is assigned and *what technology* is used (technological decisions) (pp. 196, 199)."},{"q":"Distinguish strong, balanced and weak matrices.","a":"Strong (\"project\") matrix most resembles a pure project and functional units supply **individuals**; weak (\"functional\") matrix most resembles the functional form and units supply **capacity** — a project may have only one full-time person, the PM, who coordinates work done inside departments; the balanced matrix lies between, with frequently needed groups taking project work like outside contractors. The distinguishing variable is the relative power of PM versus functional manager (pp. 195–197)."},{"q":"Give four disadvantages of the matrix form.","a":"Delicately balanced power creates doubt over who is in charge (and infighting over credit or blame); the project set must be monitored as a set and resource shifting breeds infighting among PMs; projects still resist death (projectitis) in strong matrices; the split of administrative/technological authority is complex so the PM must be a strong negotiator; and it violates unity of command — workers have at least two bosses (pp. 198–199)."},{"q":"When is the functional form the right choice, and when the pure project form?","a":"Functional when the focus is in-depth application of a technology rather than cost, schedule or speed of response, or when large capital investment in equipment/buildings of a type the function normally uses is needed. Pure project when the firm runs many similar projects (e.g. construction), or for one-time, highly specific, unique tasks needing careful control that do not fit one function (p. 202)."},{"q":"When is the matrix \"the only satisfactory solution\"?","a":"When the project needs integration of inputs from several functional areas and reasonably sophisticated technology, but does not need all the technical specialists full-time — especially when several projects must share technical experts (p. 202)."},{"q":"List the six steps for choosing an organisational form for a project.","a":"(1) Define the project with an objective statement naming major outcomes; (2) determine key tasks per objective and locate their functional homes; (3) sequence the key tasks and decompose them into work packages; (4) determine which units must do which packages and which must work closely together; (5) list special characteristics and assumptions (technology level, length and size, personnel and political problems, the firm's past experience); (6) choose a structure with full cognisance of each form's pros and cons (p. 202)."},{"q":"What are the six PMBOK risk management subprocesses, and what seventh do Meredith & Mantel add?","a":"Risk management planning; risk identification; qualitative risk analysis; quantitative risk analysis; risk response planning; risk monitoring and control — plus the authors' seventh, create and maintain a **risk management data bank** as a permanent record of risks, mitigation methods and results (pp. 204–206)."},{"q":"State the six steps of FMEA and the RPN formula.","a":"(1) List ways the project might fail; (2) score severity S 1–10 (1 = no effect, 10 = very severe); (3) score likelihood L 1–10 (1 = remote, 10 = almost certain); (4) score detectability D 1–10 (1 = detection almost certain, 10 = practically certain it will not be detected in time); (5) compute **RPN = S × L × D**; (6) find ways to reduce S, L and D for causes with significantly high RPN (p. 207)."},{"q":"What is the PMO's role relative to the project manager's?","a":"The PMO is an **enabler/facilitator** of projects, not the doer; top management must not let it usurp technical work such as scheduling and budgeting, which is the PM's responsibility. When it does touch project tasks it should be to facilitate liaison with top management (p. 210)."},{"q":"Name six tasks a Project Management Office commonly performs.","a":"Establish and enforce PM processes (bidding, risk analysis, selection, progress reports, contracts, software); assess and improve project management maturity; train and certify PMs; identify, develop and mentor PMs; consult and help with administrative details; manage the project risk portfolio and rein in runaway projects; conduct reviews and audits; maintain archives and a resource database; assist in project termination (pp. 210–211)."},{"q":"Who on a project team should report directly to the PM?","a":"The systems architect and the project controller (controlling technical performance and budget, while the PM personally controls the schedule), plus senior members with a long-term relationship to the project, those needing continuous or close communication with the PM, and those with rare skills necessary to project success (pp. 213–214)."},{"q":"According to Herzberg, what motivates technical project professionals when the PM controls no rewards or promotions?","a":"Recognition, achievement, the work itself, responsibility, advancement, and the chance to learn new skills — so the PM must structure project work to emphasise these (p. 216)."},{"q":"How does the dominant source of project conflict change over the life cycle?","a":"At formation, priorities, procedures and schedules are roughly equal; in buildup, **priorities** dominate; in the main program, **schedules** dominate followed by technical disagreements; at phaseout, **meeting the schedule** plus erupting personality tensions and worry about reassignment (Tables 5-1/5-2, pp. 218–219)."},{"q":"How should a PM handle conflict, per the chapter?","a":"Not by avoiding it — \"conflict avoiders do not make successful project managers.\" Negotiation is the primary tool; compromise sometimes helps, but gently confronting the conflict is usually the method of choice, and the key is transforming a win–lose situation into win–win. Controlled conflict can even be creative (pp. 218, 221)."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 b04"},{"id":"b05","title":"Book 5 · Project Planning & the WBS ★","slides":"6e ch. 5 = 7e ch. 6 · week 5 · MIDTERM","md":"# B05 — Project Planning (6th ed. ch. 5 = 7th ed. ch. 6, \"Project Activity Planning\")\n\n_Midterm material, week 5 — \"Scope; work breakdown structure\". **This is the chapter that nearly got\nmissed.** The 6th and 7th editions number their middle chapters differently, and in the 7th-edition PDF\nthis material sits at chapter 6, outside the stated midterm range. It is squarely inside it in the 6th\nedition the syllabus specifies._\n\n> ### ⚠️ The edition problem, and why this chapter exists\n>\n> The syllabus specifies the **6th edition** and says the midterm covers **chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8**.\n> The PDF in the course folder is the **7th edition**, and the two editions **permute chapters 4, 5 and 6**:\n>\n> | 6th ed. (what the syllabus means) | 7th ed. (what the PDF has) |\n> |---|---|\n> | 4 — Project Organization | **5** — The Project in the Organizational Structure |\n> | **5 — Project Planning** | **6** — Project Activity Planning ← *this chapter* |\n> | 6 — Conflict and Negotiation | **4** — Negotiation and the Management of Conflict |\n>\n> Chapters 1, 2, 3, 7, 8 and onward keep their numbers in both. So the midterm's \"chapters 1–5 and 8\" maps\n> to **7th-edition chapters 1, 2, 3, 5, 6 and 8**. Read this chapter; do **not** assume 7th-edition\n> chapter 4 (conflict and negotiation) is examined — on the 6th-edition numbering it is chapter 6, outside\n> the stated range. **Confirm with Dr. Duraković**, because this is the kind of thing that costs real marks.\n>\n> Page references below are to the **7th-edition PDF**.\n\n---\n\n## 6.1 The project plan — nine elements\n\nThe chapter's most examinable list. A project plan contains:\n\n| # | Element | What goes in it |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Overview** | Short summary of the objectives and scope, for senior management |\n| 2 | **Objectives or scope** | The detailed statement of goals |\n| 3 | **General approach** | Both the **managerial** and the **technical** approach |\n| 4 | **Contractual aspects** | Complete list of agreements and commitments — the chapter calls this a *critical* section |\n| 5 | **Schedules** | The various schedules and all **milestone events** |\n| 6 | **Resources** | Two aspects: the **budget**, and cost monitoring and control |\n| 7 | **Personnel** | Expected personnel requirements, including skills and timing |\n| 8 | **Risk management plans** | Potential problems **and** potential opportunities |\n| 9 | **Evaluation methods** | The standards and procedures the project will be judged against |\n\n**The two details most likely to be tested.** Element 3 covers **both** managerial and technical approach,\nnot just technical. Element 8 covers **opportunities as well as problems** — the same positive-risk idea\nthe archive's risk chapter makes much of.\n\n## Three documents that are easy to confuse\n\nThe chapter's own closing review question asks you to contrast them, so expect it.\n\n| Document | What it is | Focus |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Project plan** | The overall nine-element document above | The whole project, strategy to evaluation |\n| **Action plan** | A listing of the tasks required to complete the project | **Schedule, resources, personnel** per task |\n| **Work breakdown structure** | A usually **hierarchical** view of the activities in the action plan | Decomposition into work packages |\n\nThe relationship: the **action plan** supplies the task list; the **WBS** is a hierarchical way of viewing\nit; the **project plan** is the document that contains both plus everything else.\n\n## Hierarchical planning and the \"even planning process\"\n\nThe method the chapter uses to generate the detail: start from the objectives in the project plan, break\neach into the activities needed to achieve it, then break those activities down again, and keep going. Each\nactivity has an **outcome (event)** associated with it.\n\nIt is called the **even planning process** because the decomposition is carried to a **consistent level of\ndetail across all branches** — you do not plan one part of the project in fine detail while another stays\nvague. Why it helps: an unevenly planned project hides its risk in the under-planned branches, and\nestimates built from mismatched levels of detail cannot be compared or aggregated.\n\n## 6.2 Systems integration — three objectives\n\n**Systems integration** (also called systems engineering or concurrent engineering) means integrating the\ntechnical disciplines to meet the customer's objectives, and integrating the project into the customer's\nsystem. It has three objectives (p. 246):\n\n| # | Objective | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Performance** | What the system *does* — design, reliability, quality, maintainability, repairability. Subject to **over**design as well as underdesign; must fall inside the client's design parameters |\n| 2 | **Effectiveness** | Designing the components to achieve the desired performance |\n| 3 | **Cost** | Cost treated as a **design parameter**, with trade-offs between design, production and material cost |\n\n**The three rules under \"effectiveness\"** — a clean, quotable list:\n\n1. Require **no** component performance specification unless it is necessary to meet a system requirement.\n2. **Every** component requirement should be traceable to one or more system requirements.\n3. Design components for **system** performance, not subsystem performance.\n\nThe chapter then observes drily that clients and project teams violate all three routinely — tolerances far\ntighter than any system requirement, superfluous \"bells and whistles\", and off-the-shelf parts that do not\nwork with the rest. The causes it gives are distrust between buyer and seller, overspecifying \"to be sure\",\nand assuming \"this part will do just as well\".\n\n**Value engineering** (or value analysis) is named as the technique for examining these cost trade-offs —\ndescribed as the consistent and thorough use of cost/effectiveness analysis.\n\n⚠️ **The risk balance**, worth a sentence in an essay: if systems integration takes a **risky** approach the\nproject may be delayed; if it is **too conservative** the project forgoes enhanced capability or better\neconomics. A good design also avoids locking the project into a rigid solution with no adaptability.\n\n## 6.3 The action plan\n\nA listing of the activities required to complete the project, focused on the schedule, resources and\npersonnel for each. The chapter is explicit that **its form is not sacrosanct** — it will differ between\norganisations and even between projects in the same organisation, and not every element needs to appear.\n\nThe structural idea to carry forward: each activity has **predecessors**, a **duration** and an\n**outcome**. That is precisely the input the network diagram in chapter 8 consumes, which is why planning\ncomes before scheduling and never the other way round.\n\n## 6.4 The work breakdown structure ★\n\n> The **WBS** is a hierarchical decomposition of the project into successively finer levels of detail,\n> ending in **work packages** that can each be individually planned, budgeted, scheduled, monitored and\n> controlled.\n\n**The definitional sentence worth memorising** (p. 261): the WBS **is not a document**. No single version\nsuits all needs; any given WBS is one of many possible documents, and its uses are limited only by the\nneeds of the project and the imagination of the project manager.\n\n### What a WBS is used for\n\nWriting specifications for a work package, establishing **account numbers**, specifying hardware and\nsoftware, identifying resource needs, and serving as the basis for **cost estimates** (ch. 7) and\n**task duration estimates** (ch. 8).\n\n### The levels\n\nFigure 6-6 shows a conference project numbered through four levels, 0 to 3, with account numbers attached:\n\n```\nLevel 0   Conference                       (10.00.00)\nLevel 1   Location  Facilities  Entertainment  Sessions  Staffing\n                    (12.00.00)\nLevel 2   Equipment  Food  Building\n                     (12.02.00)\nLevel 3   Coffee breaks (12.02.01)  Breakfasts (12.02.02)  Lunches (12.02.03)\n          Dinners (12.02.04)  Drinks (12.02.05)\n```\n\nThe **account-number scheme mirrors the hierarchy**, which is what lets costs roll up automatically from\nwork package to project total.\n\n### The seven steps for designing and using a WBS\n\nThe chapter presents these as general steps, noting that for small projects some may be skipped, combined\nor handled less formally.\n\n| Step | Action |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | **List the task breakdown in successively finer levels** until every meaningful task or work package can be individually planned, budgeted, scheduled, monitored and controlled |\n| 2 | **For each work package, identify the relevant data** — vendors, durations, equipment, materials, specifications — and list the responsible people and organisations. Build a **linear responsibility chart** |\n| 3 | **Review the work package information with those responsible** to verify accuracy, then aggregate upward through each level of the hierarchy to a project summary |\n| 4 | **Aggregate the total project budget** from four elements (below) |\n| 5 | **Aggregate schedule information and milestones** into a **projected baseline schedule** |\n| 6 | **During execution, compare actual against planned resource use** at every level to identify problems early and harden the final cost estimate |\n| 7 | **Compare actual against scheduled progress** at every level, and move resources to tasks behind schedule |\n\n**The warning attached to step 1**, which is the chapter's sharpest line: if the set of work package\ndescriptions is not complete and properly arranged, **it is highly unlikely the project can be completed on\ntime, on budget and to specification**.\n\n### The four elements of the total project budget (step 4) ★\n\nAn enumerable list, so an easy exam target:\n\n| # | Element | Contents |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Direct budgets from each task** | The task-by-task costs built up from the WBS |\n| 2 | **Indirect cost budget** | General and administrative overhead (G&A), marketing costs, potential penalty charges, other costs not attributable to a particular task |\n| 3 | **Contingency reserve** | For unexpected emergencies |\n| 4 | **Residual** | The profit from the project — which may, on occasion, be **intentionally negative** |\n\n⚠️ **The point the chapter flags forward to chapter 7:** the budget used for **pricing or profit\ncalculation should not be the same budget the PM uses to control the project**. Two different purposes,\ntwo different documents.\n\n### The projected baseline schedule (step 5)\n\nIntegrates all the schedules relevant to the various parts of the project. It is comprehensive and may\ninclude contractual commitments, key interfaces and sequencing, milestone events and progress reports, plus\na **time contingency reserve** for unforeseeable delays.\n\nNote the symmetry: a **cost** contingency reserve in the budget, a **time** contingency reserve in the\nschedule.\n\n## 6.4 The linear responsibility chart\n\nAlso called a **responsibility matrix**. A grid of activities against people or organisational units,\nshowing who is responsible for what.\n\n**What it gives the project manager:**\n- who is responsible for each task;\n- **critical interfaces between units** that will need special managerial coordination;\n- who must **approve** what;\n- who must **report** to whom.\n\nFor a simple project it can be simplified; the chapter also shows a **verbal responsibility chart** used by\none firm to reorganise its distribution system, which ran to a 30-page document covering 116 major\nactivities.\n\n**The related document:** an **organisational breakdown structure (OBS)** displays the organisational units\nresponsible for each WBS element. The WBS and OBS are **not** identical — one WBS section may be the\nresponsibility of several departments, while several sections may all belong to one. The OBS lets a\ndepartment manager see their total responsibility across a project.\n\n## 6.5 Interface coordination and integration management\n\nThe objective of **interface management** is to manage the points where the work of different groups meets,\nsince that is where coordination fails. **Multifunctional teaming** is the chapter's named mechanism for\nachieving systems integration, and it is also valuable for assessing and mitigating risk, particularly in\nanticipating crises.\n\n## Why this matters for the exam\n\n- **This chapter is the \"Scope; WBS\" week**, and the WBS is the one structured, drawable artefact in the\n  pre-midterm half of the course. Expect to build or interpret one.\n- **The nine elements of a project plan** is the most quotable list in the chapter.\n- **The four budget elements** and **the seven WBS steps** are both enumerable and therefore examinable.\n- **\"Contrast the project plan, the action plan and the WBS\"** is literally one of the chapter's own review\n  questions. Have a one-line answer for each.\n- **The linear responsibility chart** has a short, memorable list of four uses.\n- **Systems integration's three objectives** (performance, effectiveness, cost) with the three effectiveness\n  rules underneath.\n- **The WBS is not a document** — a definitional point that makes a good true/false or short-answer trap.\n\n## Verification note\n\nNo numeric worked examples appear in this chapter, so nothing required recomputation. The edition mapping\nat the top was checked against two independent sources for the 6th-edition contents and against the 7th\nedition's own table of contents; chapters 4, 5 and 6 are confirmed to be permuted between the editions,\nwhile chapters 1, 2, 3, 7 and 8 keep their numbers.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Name the nine elements of a project plan.","a":"Overview, objectives or scope, general approach, contractual aspects, schedules, resources, personnel, risk management plans, evaluation methods."},{"q":"What two things does the \"general approach\" element cover?","a":"Both the managerial and the technical approach."},{"q":"What does the risk management element cover besides problems?","a":"Potential opportunities as well as potential problems."},{"q":"What are the two aspects of the \"resources\" element?","a":"The budget, and cost monitoring and control."},{"q":"Contrast the project plan, the action plan and the WBS.","a":"The project plan is the whole nine-element document; the action plan lists the tasks with their schedule, resources and personnel; the WBS is a hierarchical view of those activities broken into work packages."},{"q":"What is the \"even planning process\"?","a":"Hierarchical decomposition carried to a consistent level of detail across all branches, so no part of the project is planned finely while another stays vague."},{"q":"Why does uneven planning cause problems?","a":"Risk hides in the under-planned branches, and estimates built at mismatched levels of detail cannot be compared or aggregated."},{"q":"What is systems integration also called?","a":"Systems engineering or concurrent engineering."},{"q":"Name the three objectives of systems integration.","a":"Performance, effectiveness and cost."},{"q":"What does \"performance\" include as a systems integration objective?","a":"What the system does — design, reliability, quality, maintainability and repairability; it is subject to overdesign as well as underdesign."},{"q":"State the three rules for designing components effectively.","a":"Require no component specification unless needed for a system requirement; make every component requirement traceable to a system requirement; design for system rather than subsystem performance."},{"q":"What is value engineering?","a":"Value analysis — the consistent and thorough use of cost/effectiveness analysis to examine cost trade-offs."},{"q":"What happens if systems integration is too risky, or too conservative?","a":"Too risky delays the project; too conservative forgoes enhanced capability and better project economics."},{"q":"Define the work breakdown structure.","a":"A hierarchical decomposition of the project into finer levels, ending in work packages that can each be individually planned, budgeted, scheduled, monitored and controlled."},{"q":"Why does the book say the WBS \"is not a document\"?","a":"Because no single version suits all needs — any given WBS is only one of many possible documents for the project."},{"q":"Name four uses of a WBS.","a":"Writing work package specifications, establishing account numbers, identifying resource needs, and providing the basis for cost and duration estimates."},{"q":"What does the WBS account-numbering scheme achieve?","a":"It mirrors the hierarchy, so costs roll up automatically from work package to project total."},{"q":"What is the consequence of an incomplete set of work package descriptions?","a":"It is highly unlikely the project can be completed on time, on budget and to specification."},{"q":"Name the four elements of the total project budget.","a":"Direct budgets from each task, an indirect cost budget (G&A, marketing, penalties), a contingency reserve for emergencies, and the residual or profit."},{"q":"What may the residual element of the budget occasionally be?","a":"Intentionally negative."},{"q":"Why should the pricing budget differ from the control budget?","a":"They serve different purposes — one calculates price and profit, the other is what the PM uses to control the project."},{"q":"What is the projected baseline schedule, and what may it include?","a":"An integration of all the project's schedules, possibly including contractual commitments, key interfaces and sequencing, milestones, progress reports and a time contingency reserve."},{"q":"What two kinds of contingency reserve appear in this chapter?","a":"A cost contingency reserve in the budget and a time contingency reserve in the schedule."},{"q":"What is a linear responsibility chart also called, and what does it show?","a":"A responsibility matrix; it shows who is responsible for each activity, the critical interfaces needing coordination, who approves what, and who reports to whom."},{"q":"What is an organisational breakdown structure, and how does it relate to the WBS?","a":"It displays the organisational units responsible for each WBS element; the two are not identical, since one WBS section may involve several departments and several sections may belong to one."},{"q":"What is the objective of interface management?","a":"To manage the points where the work of different groups meets, which is where coordination typically fails."},{"q":"What mechanism achieves systems integration, and what else is it good for?","a":"Multifunctional teaming, which also helps assess and mitigate risk and anticipate crises."},{"q":"Which 7th-edition chapter corresponds to 6th-edition chapter 5?","a":"Chapter 6, \"Project Activity Planning\" — the editions permute chapters 4, 5 and 6."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 b05"},{"id":"b06","title":"Book 6 · Conflict & Negotiation","slides":"6e ch. 6 = 7e ch. 4 · NOT on the midterm","md":"# B06 — Conflict and Negotiation (6th ed. ch. 6 = 7th ed. ch. 4, \"Negotiation and the Management of Conflict\")\n\n*Why projects generate conflict, how conflict shifts shape across the project life cycle, and the negotiating tools — partnering, the project charter, principled negotiation — a PM uses to settle it.*\n\n> ### ⚠️ On the 6th-edition numbering the syllabus uses, this is **chapter 6 and NOT on the midterm**.\n>\n> It is chapter 4 only in the 7th-edition PDF. The stated midterm scope is 6th-edition chapters 1–5 and 8.\n> Read it for the final and for general competence, but **do not spend scarce pre-midterm hours here** —\n> spend them on B04 (project organization) and B05 (project planning), which are the real chapters 4 and 5.\n> Section 4.2's material on the **project charter** is the exception: the course's week-2 label names the\n> charter explicitly, so that part may well be examined even though the chapter is not.\n\n---\n\n> ### ⚠️ Edition mapping — read this once\n>\n> The syllabus specifies the **6th edition** and examines **chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8**. The PDF in the\n> course folder is the **7th edition**, and the two **permute chapters 4, 5 and 6**:\n>\n> | 6th ed. (what the syllabus means) | 7th ed. (what the PDF has) | On the midterm? |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | 1 Projects in Contemporary Organizations | 1 (same) | **yes** |\n> | 2 Strategic Management and Project Selection | 2 (same) | **yes** |\n> | 3 The Project Manager | 3 (same) | **yes** |\n> | 4 Project Organization | **5** The Project in the Organizational Structure | **yes** |\n> | 5 Project Planning | **6** Project Activity Planning | **yes** |\n> | 6 Conflict and Negotiation | **4** Negotiation and the Management of Conflict | **no** |\n> | 8 Scheduling | 8 (same) | **yes** |\n>\n> Files here are numbered by the **6th-edition** chapter, because that is what the syllabus cites.\n> Page references are **7th-edition printed pages**, so do not quote them at the professor.\n> **Confirm the scope with Dr. Duraković** — this mapping is inferred, and it decides what you revise.\n\n\n> ⚠️ **The weekly label does not match the chapter.** The syllabus calls week 4 *\"Project planning, selection and quality\"* and assigns *Chapter 4*. But Chapter 4 of Meredith & Mantel is **\"Negotiation and the Management of Conflict\"** — there is no planning, selection or quality content in it. **Project selection is Chapter 2 material** (selection models, numeric/non-numeric criteria, the project portfolio); planning is Chapter 6 in this book, which is *not* on the midterm list. Treat the week-4 label as the instructor's own topic ordering and revise **the chapter, not the label** — the midterm reading list says \"chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8\", and this is what chapter 4 contains.\n\n> ⚠️ **Edition note.** The syllabus requires the **6th edition**; the page numbers cited here `(p. nnn)` are the **7th edition** printed pages 161–188. The section numbering (4.1–4.4) and all the content below are stable between the two, but do not quote page numbers at the professor.\n\n---\n\n## Why this chapter sits where it does\n\n- Conflict arises because people on the same project have **different ideas about how to reach the project's objectives** — and which way is \"one best\" is surrounded by **uncertainty**. So the chapter is also a **risk-management** chapter: negotiation is how the PM manages the risk that uncertainty creates (p. 161).\n- Working definition the book borrows from Thomas (1976): conflict is the process that begins when one party perceives the other **\"has frustrated, or is about to frustrate, some concern of his\"** (p. 161).\n- A conflict is **resolved** when every party's frustration has dropped low enough that none contemplates any present or future action against another (p. 161).\n- The chapter concentrates on **goal conflicts** — one individual or group pursuing goals different from another's — not on conflicts of belief or feeling (p. 161).\n- Conflict is not purely destructive: debate over the technical approach often produces a **collaborative solution better than any originally proposed**, and conflict educates groups about each other's objectives, which is a *precondition* for win-win negotiation (p. 162).\n- Explicitly **not** a how-to-negotiate primer, and it barely covers buyer–vendor negotiation (usually done by purchasing, not the PM) (p. 161).\n\n> ⚠️ Two cross-reference slips in the book itself: p. 162 points at \"Section 4.3\" for win-win negotiation, but win-win is actually in **4.4**; and p. 170 says \"In Chapter 4 we discussed some of the human factors…\", which must mean **Chapter 3** (The Project Manager). Don't let these confuse you in a closed-book exam.\n\n---\n\n## 4.1 The Nature of Negotiation\n\nTwo definitions the book quotes, neither of which it finds satisfying (p. 164):\n\n| Source | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Wall (1985) | the process by which two or more parties seek **\"an acceptable rate of exchange for items they own or control\"** |\n| Cohen (1980) | a field of knowledge focused on **\"gaining the favor of people from whom we want things\"** |\n\nThe book's point is that no single definition covers everything we call negotiation; synonyms run from \"mediate\" and \"arbitrate\" to \"dicker\" and \"haggle\" (p. 164).\n\n**The critical distinction — opponents vs. allies** (p. 164):\n\n| | Conflicts with outsiders | Conflicts inside the organization |\n|---|---|---|\n| Typical subject | property rights, contractual obligations | also looks like rights and obligations… |\n| How parties see each other | **opponents** | **allies** — members of an alliance with common interests |\n| Right objective | best deal | best outcome **for the parent organization** |\n\nSupporting concepts:\n\n- **Lateral relations** — communications across lines of equivalent authority, letting decisions be made horizontally rather than up and down the hierarchy. Organizations use them to integrate units with different goals; integrating units with different goals is precisely what *produces* conflict (p. 164).\n- **Pareto-optimal solution** (Raiffa, 1982) — a solution such that **no party can be made better off without making another party worse off by the same amount or more**. This is the stated general objective of intraproject negotiation (p. 164).\n- **Why win-lose is wrong inside a project:** the PM will negotiate with the same stakeholders many times in the future. Win a win-lose fight and from then on you face **a determined adversary who seeks to defeat you** (p. 164).\n- **Ethics:** a client asking for a software function often cannot tell whether it is a trivial task or a major job, because it lacks the technical competence. The contractor can therefore inflate the price of a trivial task, or understate a big one to win the contract and raise the cost later. The book's rule: **each party must be honest even when no further work between them is expected** (p. 164).\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 Partnering, Chartering, and Scope Change\n\n> **Give this section extra weight.** The syllabus's **week 2** is labelled *\"Project initiation; charter and plan development\"* — so the **project charter** is named twice in this course, once in week 2 and again (through chapter 4) in week 4. The charter and scope-change material is therefore the most likely chapter-4 content to be examined, and the most likely to be assumed knowledge in a week-2-flavoured question.\n\n### The three situations that demand the PM's highest negotiating skill (p. 165)\n\n| # | Situation | Why it is hard |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Use of **subcontractors** | probably accounts for **more litigation than all other aspects of the project combined** |\n| 2 | Using input from **two or more functional units** to design and develop the project's mission | the authors' experience: one of the two most common and troublesome issues PMs report |\n| 3 | Managing **changes ordered in deliverables and/or priorities after the project is underway** | the other of the two most common and troublesome issues |\n\n*Why it matters:* a one-line exam question (\"name the three project situations requiring the greatest negotiating skill\") maps straight onto this list, and item 1 carries the memorable litigation claim.\n\n### Partnering\n\n**Partnering** (Cowen et al., 1992) — *\"a method of transforming contractual relationships into a cohesive, cooperative project team with a single set of goals and established procedures for resolving disputes in a timely and effective manner.\"* (p. 165)\n\nThe default relationship it is meant to replace is **adversarial**: the parent organization wants the deliverable at the lowest cost as soon as possible; the subcontractor wants to produce it at the highest profit with the least effort. Mutual suspicion follows, and the two parties will almost always have significantly different ideas about the exact nature of the deliverable (p. 165).\n\n**Reasons firms partner, beyond avoiding litigation** (Beecham et al., 1998) (p. 165):\n\n| Reason |\n|---|\n| Diversification of technical risk |\n| Avoidance of capital investment |\n| Reducing political risk on multinational projects |\n| Shortening the duration of the project |\n| Pooling of complementary knowledge |\n\n**The elements of partnering — Cowen et al.'s multistep process** (p. 165). *Why it matters: this is a numbered, three-phase, four-sub-point structure — exactly the shape of a \"describe the partnering process\" exam question.*\n\n| Phase | Steps |\n|---|---|\n| **1. Set up** | parent firm commits to partnering → selects subcontractors who will also commit → **joint team-building exercises** → develop a **charter** for the project |\n| **2. Implement** (a four-part agreement) | (1) **joint evaluation** of project progress; (2) an agreed **method for resolving problems/disagreements**; (3) acceptance of a goal of **continuous improvement (TQM)** for the joint project; (4) **continuous support from senior management of both parties** |\n| **3. Close** | commit to a **joint review of \"project execution\"** when the project is complete |\n\nEach step must be accompanied by **nonadversarial negotiation**, and the whole concept rests on an assumption of **mutual trust** (p. 165). The same steps and assumptions apply when the \"partners\" are different divisions or departments of **one** parent organization (p. 165).\n\nPartnering also scales to **multiparty consortia** (p. 166):\n\n| Example | Members |\n|---|---|\n| Automotive Composites Consortium (1989) | Chrysler, Ford, General Motors — plastic-processing technologies for car manufacture |\n| Airbus Industrie | British, French, Spanish, German firms — each **subsidised by its own government** |\n| CFM International | USA and France |\n| International Aero Engines | USA and UK |\n\nThe Automotive Composites Consortium was exempted from US anti-trust prosecution by the **National Cooperative Research Act of 1984**, passed expressly to allow cooperation among competitors (p. 166).\n\n**Partnering's limits:** inexperienced firms underrate its difficulty; it needs strong and *continuous* senior-management support from every participant; and above all it requires **open and honest communication** — the hardest requirement of all (p. 166).\n\n### Chartering\n\n**Project charter** — *a written agreement between the PM, senior management, and the functional managers who are committing resources and/or people to a specific project* (p. 166).\n\n| Property | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Form | varies widely; \"may take many different forms\" |\n| Typical contents | expected **deliverables**, often with **schedules, budgets and resource commitments** |\n| What it attests | that senior management of all relevant organizations, the functional managers and the PM are **\"on the same page\"** about what, when and at what cost |\n| Implied obligation | no party will change the agreement **unilaterally**, or at least not without prior consultation |\n| Reality check | **\"Most projects do not have charters\"** — which the authors give as one reason most projects are not completed on specification, on time and on budget |\n\n*Why it matters: the \"what is a project charter / who signs it / what does it contain\" question is almost free marks, and the parties (PM + senior management + functional managers) are the part students forget.*\n\n**A signed-off plan can *be* the charter:** when individuals responsible for tasks sign off on their action-plan commitments, and senior management has signed off on the overall mission statement, and **all parties recognize it as a charter**, that set of action plans constitutes a project charter (p. 166).\n\n**A less specific charter — the Cowen et al. partnering-team commitment** (p. 167):\n\n| Commitment |\n|---|\n| Meet **design intent** |\n| Complete the contract **without need for litigation** |\n| Finish the project **on schedule** — via timely resolution of issues and managing a **joint schedule** |\n| Keep **cost growth to less than 2 percent** |\n\nEven these loose terms presuppose prior agreement on the design intent, the schedule and the costs (p. 167).\n\n### Scope change (what question papers usually call \"scope creep\")\n\n> ⚠️ Terminology: the chapter says **\"scope change\"** throughout — the phrase *\"scope creep\"* does not appear in it. If the exam uses \"scope creep\", answer with this chapter's causes-and-responses structure; the mechanism is the same (uncontrolled accumulation of changes to the expected performance of the project).\n\nFraming: changing the scope — the performance expected of a project — is a major project-management issue and **constitutes the second PMBOK knowledge area** (p. 167). No matter how carefully a project is planned, **it is almost certain to be changed before completion**; no matter how carefully defined at the start, scope is subject to considerable uncertainty (p. 167).\n\n**The three basic causes of change — and the risk response to each.** *Why it matters: this is the single most quotable table in the chapter. A \"state the three causes of scope change and how each is managed\" question is a near-certainty, and the three-row cause→response pairing is what earns full marks.*\n\n| # | Cause | Underlying factor | Book's examples | Risk response (p. 168) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Planners erred** — wrong assessment of how to achieve the end, or wrong choice of goal | **technological uncertainty** | a building foundation redesigned because the preliminary geological study missed a weakness in the ground; an R&D project altered by metallurgical test results; a cheaper/faster innovation discovered mid-project | mitigate by **careful analysis of the technologies involved**, including **technological forecasting** |\n| 2 | **Client/user or team learns more** about the deliverable or its setting | **increase in user or team knowledge / sophistication** | software extended because the user thinks of new uses; physicians requesting laminar air-flow control in an ICU for infection-prone patients | manage by **improving up-front communication with the client** and **establishing a formal process to handle change** |\n| 3 | **Mandate** — a change in the project's *environment*, outside the PM's control | external rule change | a new law; a regulatory unit's new policy; a trade association's new standard; the user's parent organization applying a new purchasing criterion; a state-mandated pollution-control system | **largely unpredictable** — \"managed\" only by building **flexibility into the budget and schedule** |\n\nMandates may also affect **priorities only** — e.g. moving an important customer to the head of the queue for a scarce resource (p. 167).\n\n**Consequences of change, and why negotiating it is hard:**\n\n- Whatever the change, **specifications must be altered and the schedule and budget recalculated**, so new agreements between the parties-at-interest must be negotiated (p. 168).\n- These negotiations are difficult because **most stakeholders have a strong interest in the status quo**. If a change benefits the client but raises cost, the producer tries to capture part of the client's benefit as added charges; the client resists. Again the target is a Pareto-optimal solution (p. 168).\n- **Change by mandate additionally reshuffles other projects' priorities.** The PM suddenly loses access to key resources, contributors miss meetings and slip promised dates. The usual PM response is anger or discouragement — **neither is appropriate** (p. 168).\n\n**Priorities as they actually exist** (p. 168) — the authors' finding after talking to PMs and senior managers, no matter how ornate the formal priority procedure:\n\n| Level | What it is | Tie-break rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **High priority** — the set of projects currently being supported | within the set, precedence goes to the **earliest due date** |\n| 2 | **Lower priority** — \"we would like to do these when we have time and money\" | — |\n| 3 | **Urgent / mandates** — must be done immediately | **everything else is delayed** to meet the mandate |\n\nMethods for settling priority conflicts do exist (selection models that rank projects by value; senior management setting interproject priorities; the PM setting intraproject task priorities by schedule, task difficulty and resource availability) — but they are \"all too often irrelevant\" because both project and functional managers **optimize their individual interests** rather than the whole organization (p. 171).\n\n**Sober conclusion:** charters and partnering would help, and outsourcing is growing rapidly, but **the use of charters and partnering is growing slowly**. Senior managers and functional managers resist firm commitments in the face of an uncertain future; clients, aware of their own ignorance about whether the output will meet their needs, are cautious too; and in a litigious society any scheme asking parties to forgo lawsuits is viewed with suspicion. Many organizations \"preach\" team building, TQM and employee involvement without practising them — for every participative manager, a dozen micromanagers; for every team player, a dozen \"blame placers\". **\"The era of project charters and partnering is approaching, but it is not yet here.\"** (p. 168)\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 Conflict and the Project Life Cycle\n\nPurpose of the section: by knowing **which conflicts belong to which stage**, the PM can diagnose a conflict faster and more accurately, and so avoid escalating it by handling it ineffectually (p. 169).\n\n### The two four-stage life-cycle models (p. 169)\n\n| Thamhain & Wilemon (1975a) | Adams & Barndt (1983) | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| Project **formation** | **conceptualization** | senior management tentatively, sometimes unofficially, approves preliminary planning; often preceded by unofficial \"bootleg\" feasibility work; initial planning, basic objectives adopted, project may be \"scoped out\" |\n| Project **buildup** | **planning** | detailed planning, budgeting, scheduling, aggregation of resources |\n| **Main program** | **execution** | the lion's share of the actual work |\n| **Phase-out** | **termination** | work completed, product turned over to client/user; disposition of the project's assets and personnel; may include preparing the next related project |\n\nThe book treats the two models as not significantly different.\n\n### The three categories of conflict (p. 170)\n\n*Why it matters: this is the chapter's core taxonomy, it is review question 7, and the whole life-cycle mapping is built on it.*\n\n| # | Category | What it is | What falls into it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Differing goals / expectations** | groups working on the project have different goals and expectations about the project, its costs and rewards, its relative importance and its timing | **schedules**, intra- and interproject **priorities**, **cost estimates**, **staff time / labor** |\n| 2 | **Uncertainty about the locus of authority** | uncertainty over who may decide resource allocation, administrative procedure, communication, technological choice | **technical** choices, **administrative procedures** |\n| 3 | **Interpersonal conflict** | humans are an integral part of every project, and some personalities will clash | **personality** conflicts |\n\nMechanisms behind each:\n\n1. Day-to-day project work is carried out by **many different units with different objectives and technical judgments**. At base: **the PM's concern is the project; the functional manager's primary interest is the daily operation of the function** (p. 170).\n2. The textbook rule — in a matrix, the **functional manager** controls *who* works on the project and makes technical decisions, while the **project manager** controls the **schedule and flow of work** — is correct but \"rarely clear\" amid the day's countless little crises (p. 170).\n3. In conflicts between the project and the client, or between senior management and the project, **the PM personifies the project** and so is generally a party to the conflict (p. 170).\n\n**Seven conflict types** are measured in the underlying Thamhain & Wilemon study: **schedules, priorities, labor (staff requirements), technical opinions, (administrative) procedures, cost, personality** (p. 169). Their **\"intensity\"** was estimated as *conflict frequency × a measure of conflict magnitude*, adjusted for the proportion of PMs reporting that type (Figure 4-1, p. 170).\n\n> ⚠️ Figure 4-1 plots conflict intensity (axis 0 to 0.4) against program life for each of the seven types plus \"average total conflict\", but the extracted text preserves only the legend, not the per-type values. Use the stage-by-stage statements below rather than trying to recall numbers off the figure.\n\n### Table 4-1 — Project conflicts by category and parties-at-interest (p. 171)\n\n*Why it matters: review question 1 is literally \"review and justify the placement of the seven types of conflict into the nine cells of Table 4-1\", and question 11 asks you to reallocate them. Learn the grid, and be ready to argue a placement.*\n\n| Parties-at-interest | Goals | Authority | Interpersonal |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Project team** | Schedules, Priorities | Technical | Personality |\n| **Client** | Schedules, Priorities | Technical | — |\n| **Functional and senior management** | Schedules, Priorities, Labor, Cost | Technical, Administrative | Personality |\n\nThe **three traditional sources** (parties) of conflict are therefore: **the project team itself, the client, and functional and senior management** — to which the authors add **the problem/discipline orientation** of the people working on the project (p. 180).\n\n> ⚠️ In the extracted text the functional/senior-management row prints \"Labor Cost\" on one line under *Goals*; I have read that as two separate goal-type conflicts (Labor and Cost), which matches the summary's list of conflict types. Verify against the printed table if you can — this is the one cell assignment I am not certain of.\n\nExamples of how goal conflict is generated (p. 171):\n\n- Senior management sometimes **arbitrarily fixes all three parameters** — time, cost and performance — and then assumes the PM can hit all of them. **Underestimation of cost and time is the natural consequence** (see ch. 7 on budgeting), which produces PM-vs-senior-management conflict.\n- The PM then passes the stringent estimates down to **functional managers**, who complain they cannot meet them → more conflict. The net effect is that **failure is built into the job** of managing the project.\n- Functional managers may also disagree with the PM on the project's **priority**, on assigning a **specifically named individual**, or on the **applicability of a technical approach**.\n- The **client's** priorities and schedule — inside or outside client — may differ radically from both senior management's and the team's; and the **team** has its own views on schedule and staffing levels.\n- These goal-type conflicts occur **in all stages** but are **particularly serious in the early stages** (p. 171).\n\nOn authority: **\"Locus-of-authority conflicts are endemic to matrix-organized projects.\"** Senior management wants the efficiency of matrix management while keeping the managerial comforts of a traditional hierarchy — \"a sure source of conflict\". PMs often argue over scheduling and resource priorities with functional managers who **outrank them by several levels**, and the constant PM complaint is *\"I have to take the responsibility, but I have no authority at all.\"* (pp. 171–172)\n\nOn people problems: most personality clashes on the project team actually come from **differences in technical approach or problem-solving philosophy**. A personality conflict can *cause* a technical conflict — and **any** type of conflict can look, at first blush, like a personality clash (p. 172).\n\n**What partnering and charters do about all this:** neither stops conflicts arising, but they **sharply lower conflict intensity**, provide a framework for resolving conflict, and let the PM and functional managers take positions that support the **total organization** rather than suboptimizing the project or the function (p. 172).\n\n### Conflict by life-cycle stage\n\n*Why it matters: the prompt-shaped exam question. Review question 3 is \"identify the types of likely conflicts during the project buildup, main program and phase-out stages\", and the summary bullets single out two facts: **total conflict peaks at buildup**, and **schedule conflict dominates main program and phase-out**.*\n\n| Stage | Dominant conflicts | Root cause at this stage | Key facts to quote |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Formation** | confusion over setting up a project inside a matrix; **goal/priority** conflicts | almost nothing about the project or its governance has been decided; even the technical objectives are understood only in the most general sense | goal-type conflicts occur in all stages but are **particularly serious here** (p. 171); conflict avoidance at this stage \"is inviting disaster in the next\" (p. 172) |\n| **Buildup** | **priorities, schedules, administrative procedures** (carried over from formation, per Thamhain), plus rising **technical** conflict | the PM must extract commitments from functional managers already under pressure; plans move from concept to detail, so technical choices get decided | **\"The total level of conflict is at its highest in this transition period.\"** Strong matrix → PM seeks a commitment of **people**; weak matrix → PM seeks a commitment of **work** (p. 173) |\n| **Main program** | **schedules** (still the major source) and **technical** conflict | a late activity delays every dependent task; **catching up is far harder than falling behind** and demands extra resources the PM may not have. Technically, the project's **interfaces** multiply rapidly as the project grows, and so does the probability of problems at them | managing the schedule *is* managing **time/cost/performance trade-offs** — but adding cost or scaling down capability are trade-offs the PM avoids if any viable alternative exists; trade-off freedom is constrained by **contract, company policy and ethics** (pp. 173–174) |\n| **Phase-out** | **schedules** (major source) and **personality** conflict (second-ranked, per Thamhain 1975b) | slippage from the main program is felt here; firm deadlines make the environment \"hectic\". Personality conflict comes from the pressure to finish plus **anxiety about leaving the project** (reassignment or return to a functional unit) | **technical problems are comparatively rare** — most are solved or bypassed and interfaces are working. Cost overruns, if not outrageous, are **tolerated but remembered**. The authors add two observed conflicts: over **distribution of the project's capital equipment and supplies**, and **between the project phasing out and projects just starting** that need its scarce-talent people (p. 174) |\n\n**The four fundamental issues of the formation stage** (p. 172) — *why it matters: review question 2 asks you to discuss each, and the summary lists them as the \"critical issues to handle in the project formation stages\".*\n\n| # | Issue | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Specify the **technical objectives** precisely enough for the buildup stage's detailed planning | |\n| 2 | Obtain **commitment of resources** from senior management and functional managers | |\n| 3 | Set **and communicate** the project's **priority** relative to the organization's other projects | the authors insist on doing this as early as possible: it will not save the project from a mandate, but it is an **important political signal** to functional managers about precedence in resource conflicts |\n| 4 | Establish the project's **organizational structure** far enough for the **WBS** and a **linear responsibility chart** (or equivalent) to be prepared next stage | |\n\nThese four are **necessary but not sufficient**. Three of them — delimiting the technical objectives, getting management commitment, and setting relative priority — must be resolved **whatever organizational form** is chosen; but the form chosen determines **how** the conflicts get handled: the **stronger the matrix** (pure project at the limit), the more authoritative the PM's role; the **weaker the matrix** (functional organization at the limit), the more authority sits with functional managers. **Lack of clarity about the relative power of PM vs. functional managers is a major component of all conflicts over technical decisions, resource allocation and scheduling** (p. 172).\n\nUnderneath the four issues sit the practical questions: which functional areas are needed, at what level of involvement, how resource/facility conflicts with other projects *and with the functions' routine work* get settled, who may decide technical/scheduling/personnel/cost questions, and — most important — **how changes in the parent organization's priorities will be communicated to everyone** (p. 172).\n\n**The authors' addition to Thamhain's taxonomy:** defining conflict as goals / authority / interpersonal **excludes** conflict between **discipline-oriented and problem-oriented** team members, which the authors consider frequent and important (and which drives much of the planning conflict in ch. 6). Their evidence is an interview from Pelled et al. (1994) on multifunctional design teams, in which one member says of another that he \"will do whatever he thinks is right to get his [own] job done\" regardless of the company (p. 174).\n\n**Bottom line of 4.3:** conflict is an **inherent characteristic of projects**; the ability to reduce and resolve it in ways that support the project's goals is a prime requisite for PM success; the primary tool is **negotiation**; and **the method of handling conflict established in the formation stage sets the pattern for the entire project** — so the PM's negotiating *style* is critical (p. 174).\n\n### The two case vignettes in 4.3\n\n| Case | Lesson |\n|---|---|\n| **Selling new area codes (BellSouth, South Carolina, code 864)** | the project team's hardest task was not technical but \"selling\" customers a product they absolutely did not want, with no time to involve the public in the decision. Measures: regional advertising, promotional brochures, bill inserts, a contracted inbound telemarketing firm, briefing all customer-contact employees, and a \"South Carolina Area Code Assistance Hotline\". Went live 1 May 1996 smoothly — customers had no choice, but could have made it far harder (pp. 162–163) |\n| **Montreal Archipel Dam consensus feasibility study** | the Secretariat rejected the recommended **central authority + arbitration** design in favour of a **consensus** approach with a **veto right for each of ten government departments**. Result: a **leadership gap** — nobody identified incompatible objectives, decision rules or common priorities; vetoes were abused; the study ran 1–2 years long; and it recommended postponing the dam to 2015 with only **$35 million** on recreation, *less than the cost of the feasibility study itself*. Three proposed causes: fear of litigation between departments, difficulty comparing positive and negative impacts for lack of decision rules, and long delays/sacrifices from the failure of consensus. Moral: **\"Attempting to avoid conflict through mandated consensus simply defeats the purpose\"** — consensus is desirable, but leadership cannot be abandoned (pp. 175–176) |\n\n---\n\n## 4.4 Some Requirements and Principles of Negotiation\n\nThe key insight: **few project conflicts are about whether a task will be done or a deliverable produced.** They are about the **precise design** of the deliverable and **how, by whom, when and at what cost** it will be achieved. Hence **the work of the project will be done** — and if negotiations break down so that work stops, **everyone loses** (p. 176).\n\n### The three requirements of project negotiation (p. 176)\n\n*Why it matters: review question 4 asks exactly this, and the summary bullet restates all three.*\n\n| # | Requirement | Reasoning the book gives |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | The conflict must be **settled without irreparable harm to the project's objectives** | the work must continue; a breakdown that halts work loses for all parties |\n| 2 | The method must **allow and foster honesty** between the negotiators | the divorce-attorneys analogy: spouses (or a rug peddler and a tourist) may use unethical tactics and profit, but attorneys practising in the same community know they must negotiate again, and **any behaviour that breeds mistrust makes future negotiation extremely difficult**. The peddler expects no further contact, so conscience is his only governor |\n| 3 | The solution must satisfy the **individual's own needs, the other parties' needs, and the parent organization's needs** — i.e. a **win-win** solution | parties-at-interest to a project are **allies, not enemies or competitors**; \"negotiating to a win-win solution is the key to conflict resolution in project management\" |\n\n### The four points of principled negotiation (Fisher & Ury, 1983) (p. 177)\n\n*Why it matters: review questions 5 and 8 both target this list; it is the most likely single item to be asked verbatim from chapter 4. \"Principled negotiation\" = win-win, developed in the Harvard Negotiation Project.*\n\n| # | Principle | What it means in a project |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Separate the people from the problem** | conflicting parties are emotional, perceive things differently, and confuse emotion with objective fact, so they attack each other rather than the problem. Define the **substantive problem** carefully, so everyone works on it rather than on each other |\n| 2 | **Focus on interests, not positions** | **positional bargaining** example: PM — \"I need this subassembly by November 15\"; functional manager — \"we might be able to deliver it by February 1\". That is haggling; ego gets invested in the position and the real concerns never surface, leaving both sides feeling they lost something. Opening with **\"Let's talk about the schedule for this subassembly\"** is enough to avoid it |\n| 3 | **Before trying to reach agreement, invent options for mutual gain** | parties enter knowing the outcome they want and are therefore blind and uncreative. Once the substantive problem is spelled out, deliberately generate a **wide variety** of possible solutions or solution elements that advance mutual interests; success at this **positively reinforces** win-win behaviour. Cohen's illustration: he wanted the mountains, she wanted the shore — the creative win-win sent them both to **Lake Tahoe** |\n| 4 | **Insist on using objective criteria** | find standards — **market value, expert opinion, law, company policy** — to judge the quality of an outcome, so the negotiation stops being a contest of wills or an exercise in stubbornness. Example: if a functional manager wants an expensive test process, the PM may legitimately ask whether that process is **required to meet the specified quality standards** |\n\n**Positional negotiation** defined (glossary, p. 180): *stating immediate wants on the assumption that the environment is static.* The two quoted positions — \"I won't pay more than \\$250,000 for that property\" and \"we might be able to deliver it by February 1\" — assume respectively that the bidder's forecast of property values is accurate and that the group's workload or material supply will not change (p. 177).\n\n**The practical technique for getting from positions to interests:** when the other party states a position, **ask \"Why?\"** (p. 178). The worked example: \"the test requested for May 15 cannot be run until June 2\" may simply mean the test supplies arrive on June 2 — and if the PM can source supplies elsewhere, the test runs on time. **If the negotiation stays a debate on positions, the PM never finds that out.**\n\nThe chapter also lists tactical questions every beginning negotiator faces but which it does not answer (p. 178): what to do when the other side plays win-lose; when they seat you with a bright light in your eyes; when they refuse to negotiate to put you under time pressure; how to settle apparently purely technical disputes; how to handle threats; what to do when a functional manager goes **over your head to your boss**; and how to deal with someone you suspect dislikes you.\n\nNote for completeness: at the time of writing, **negotiation had not yet found its way into the PMBOK** despite growing professional recognition (p. 178).\n\n### The Quad Sensor vignette (pp. 178–179)\n\nDave Dogers (PM, **weak matrix**) needed Jeff Gamm, the container group's top designer, for a shipping-container design; Gamm's manager Tab Baturi had agreed, then quietly substituted the new graduate McCutcheon because mandated changes from the VP Marketing had swamped Gamm. Instead of escalating, Dogers **asked both men how things were going** (interests, not positions), discovered Baturi's real problem was lost designers and changed priorities, and **lent him two designers** (Setter, and Levy via a favour owed by Anderson). He then **split the task**: Gamm on the hard container design, McCutcheon on production setup and test design, trained by Dogers' own people — so Baturi also got his new man trained.\n\nReading of it for the exam: a textbook **interest-based, win-win, non-positional** negotiation; the conflict categories on display are **goals/priorities** (mandated changes reshuffling work) and **authority** (a weak matrix, where the PM cannot command people). ⚠️ The ethical sting in the tail is deliberate: Baturi says he will **\"bury the added charge somewhere\"** and add it to the VP's account — class discussion question 9 asks you to judge exactly that, so do not present it as good practice.\n\n---\n\n## Directed Reading — Burke, \"Methods of Resolving Interpersonal Conflict\" (pp. 183–188)\n\nDesign: managers on a university management course each described a time they felt **especially GOOD** and a time they felt **especially BAD** about how an interpersonal conflict was resolved. **53 effective + 53 ineffective** descriptions from **57 individuals**, ~70% response rate. Descriptions were coded into the **five Blake & Mouton (1964) methods** (p. 184).\n\n**The five methods of conflict resolution** (p. 184). *Why it matters: this five-way list is the classic exam item from the reading, and it sits directly alongside the chapter's win-win material — confrontation-problem solving is the reading's name for principled negotiation.*\n\n| Method | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Withdrawing** | easier to refrain than to retreat from an argument; silence is golden — \"see no evil, hear no evil, speak no evil\" |\n| **Smoothing** | play down the differences and emphasize common interests; divisive or hurtful issues go undiscussed |\n| **Compromising** | splitting the difference, bargaining, searching for an intermediate position — **no one loses but no one wins** |\n| **Forcing** | a **win-lose** situation; participants are antagonists and competitors, not collaborators; fixed positions, polarization; creates a victor and a vanquished |\n| **Confrontation–Problem Solving** | **open exchange of information** about the conflict as each party sees it, and a working through of differences to a solution **optimal to both** — **both can win** |\n\n**Table 1 — methods associated with effective vs. ineffective resolution** (p. 184; `*` = difference significant at the .05 level):\n\n| Method | Effective (N = 53) | % | Ineffective (N = 53) | % |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Withdrawal | 0 | 0.0\\* | 5 | 9.4\\* |\n| Smoothing | 0 | 0.0 | 1 | 1.9 |\n| Compromise | 6 | 11.3 | 3 | 5.7 |\n| **Forcing** | 13 | **24.5\\*** | 42 | **79.2\\*** |\n| **Confrontation–problem solving** | 31 | **58.5\\*** | 0 | **0.0\\*** |\n| Other (unresolved / undeterminable / irrelevant) | 3 | 5.7 | 2 | 3.8 |\n\nThree significant differences: far **more Confrontation** in the effective examples (58.5% vs 0.0%), far **less Forcing** (24.5% vs 79.2%), and **less Withdrawing** (0.0% vs 9.4%) (p. 184).\n\nThe key twist on Forcing: of the 13 \"effective\" Forcing cases, **11 were written by the party who benefited** — Forcing was judged effective **by the victor and not by the vanquished**; whether outsiders would objectively call it effective remains unresearched (p. 184). This cuts against Lawrence & Lorsch, who found Forcing an effective **back-up** to Confrontation from the organization's standpoint; Burke's earlier study found use of the two methods **negatively correlated** — managers high on one tend to be low on the other.\n\n**Cost of failure:** **19 of 53 (26%)** ineffective cases involved a **termination or change of employment**, in two patterns — the employee quits because the problem was never resolved (Forcing is associated with voluntary termination), or the visible \"problem employee\" is dismissed instead of the conflict being resolved.\n\n**The 13 characteristics of Confrontation** (p. 187) condense into four clusters — the reading's own question 4 asks you to condense them, so practise this:\n\n| Cluster | Characteristics |\n|---|---|\n| **Stake and belief** | both parties have a **vested interest in the outcome**; both **believe collaboration can produce a better solution** |\n| **Where the problem is located** | the conflict is **in the relationship**, not in each person separately — so it must be **defined by those who have the relationship**, and solutions must be **generated by those who must make them work** |\n| **How the problem is worked** | the goal is to **solve the problem, not accommodate different points of view** (causes of doubt and misunderstanding are identified, alternatives explored and tested); parties are **problem-minded not solution-minded**, with **\"fluid\" rather than \"fixed\" positions**; both sides accept that **each position has potential strengths and weaknesses** and rarely is one completely right; the conflict is viewed **objectively, not personally**; and one recognises the limits of arguing one's own case while downgrading the opponent's — it drives **increased polarization** and **selective listening for weaknesses** instead of listening to understand |\n| **Attitude and interpersonal work** | **understand the conflict from the other's point of view** and accept their legitimate needs fully; **examine your own hostilities and antagonisms** first; **understand the less effective methods** (win-lose, bargaining); offer **\"face-saving\"** situations so changing one's view is not read as capitulation; and **minimize status differences, defensiveness and other barriers** |\n\nTwo themes run through the list — **attitudes** and **skills**. Maier's research found the **leader's attitude toward disagreement** to be a critical variable: the same person with different ideas is either a \"problem employee and troublemaker\" or an \"innovator\" depending on it. The **interpersonal** skills build a *helping relationship* (mutual trust and respect, candid communication, awareness of others' needs); the **problem-solving** skills are locating and stating the problem, seeking alternatives, exploring and testing them, and selecting the best. The reading's conclusion matches the chapter's: **differences and disagreements need not produce dissatisfaction — they can produce innovation and creativity** (pp. 187–188).\n\n---\n\n## Glossary (p. 180) — memorise these verbatim-ish\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| **Interfaces** | the boundaries between departments or functions |\n| **Lateral relations** | communications across lines of equivalent authority |\n| **Pareto-optimal solution** | a solution such that no party can be made better off without making another party worse off by the same amount or more |\n| **Positional negotiation** | stating immediate wants on the assumption that the environment is static |\n| **Principled negotiation** | a process of negotiation that aims to achieve a win-win result |\n| **Parties-at-interest** | those who have a vested interest in the outcome of the negotiations |\n| **Win-win** | when both parties are better off in the outcome |\n\n---\n\n## Why this matters for the exam\n\n- **The four points of principled negotiation and the three requirements of project negotiation are the two most likely short-answer items** in the whole chapter — they are review questions 4, 5 and 8, and the chapter summary repeats both. Learn them as named lists, in order.\n- **Expect a structured question on conflict across the life cycle**: \"which conflicts dominate each stage?\" Two facts carry the marks — **total conflict peaks in the buildup stage**, and **schedule conflict is the leading source in both the main program and phase-out** stages, with **personality second in phase-out** and **technical conflict rare** there.\n- **Table 4-1 (seven conflict types in nine cells) is an explicit review question twice over** — once to justify the standard placement, once to reallocate it with your own reasoning. Be able to draw the 3×3 grid and defend at least two cells.\n- **Charter questions are double-weighted in this course** because week 2 is \"project initiation; charter and plan development\". Be fluent on: who signs a charter (**PM + senior management + functional managers**), what it typically contains (deliverables, schedules, budgets, resource commitments), the no-unilateral-change implication, and the claim that **most projects have no charter** — which the authors link to most projects missing specification, time and budget.\n- **Scope change is near-certain**, most likely as \"state the three causes and how each is managed\". Answer in three cause→response pairs: planner error/technological uncertainty → technology analysis and forecasting; growing user/team knowledge → up-front client communication plus a **formal change process**; mandate → unpredictable, so build **flexibility into budget and schedule**. Add that mandates also reshuffle **other projects' priorities**.\n- **The directed reading is fair game**: the **five Blake & Mouton methods** and the headline finding that **Confrontation–Problem Solving accounted for ~58.5% of effective resolutions and 0% of ineffective ones, while Forcing accounted for 79.2% of ineffective ones**. The nuance worth a bonus mark: Forcing looked \"effective\" only to the **winner**.\n- **Definitions are cheap marks**: Pareto-optimal, positional vs. principled negotiation, interfaces, lateral relations, parties-at-interest.\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Thomas's (1976) definition of conflict, as the chapter uses it?","a":"The process that begins when one party perceives that the other has frustrated, or is about to frustrate, some concern of his."},{"q":"What is a Pareto-optimal solution?","a":"A solution such that no party can be made better off without making another party worse off by the same amount or more."},{"q":"What are the three project situations demanding the PM's highest negotiating skill?","a":"Use of subcontractors (which causes more litigation than all other aspects combined); using input from two or more functional units to design and develop the project's mission; and managing changes ordered in deliverables or priorities after the project is under way."},{"q":"Define partnering (Cowen et al., 1992).","a":"A method of transforming contractual relationships into a cohesive, cooperative project team with a single set of goals and established procedures for resolving disputes in a timely and effective manner."},{"q":"What is a project charter, and who are its parties?","a":"A written agreement between the project manager, senior management, and the functional managers committing resources or people to the project; it typically details expected deliverables with schedules, budgets and resource commitments, and implies no party will change it unilaterally."},{"q":"What do the authors say about how common charters are, and what do they infer from it?","a":"Most projects do not have charters, which they give as one reason most projects are not completed on specification, on time and on budget."},{"q":"The three basic causes of scope change?","a":"(1) Planners erred about how to achieve the end or about the goal itself, rooted in technological uncertainty; (2) the client/user or project team learns more about the deliverable or its setting, i.e. increased user or team knowledge; (3) a mandate — an uncontrollable change in the project's environment such as a new law, regulation or standard."},{"q":"How is each cause of scope change managed?","a":"Technological uncertainty by careful analysis of the technologies involved including technological forecasting; increased user knowledge by better up-front communication with the client plus a formal change process; mandates are largely unpredictable, so only flexibility built into budget and schedule helps."},{"q":"The three categories of conflict, and which conflict types belong to each?","a":"Differing goals/expectations (schedules, priorities, cost estimates, labor/staff time); uncertainty about the locus of authority (technical and administrative procedure decisions); and interpersonal conflict (personality clashes)."},{"q":"The three traditional parties-at-interest (sources of conflict) in Table 4-1, plus the authors' addition?","a":"The project team, the client, and functional and senior management — to which the authors add conflict from the problem versus discipline orientation of people on the project."},{"q":"At which life-cycle stage is total conflict highest, and why?","a":"The buildup stage — the PM is extracting commitments of people (strong matrix) or work (weak matrix) from already-pressured functional managers while plans move from concept to detail, so priority, schedule, procedure and technical conflicts pile up in one transition."},{"q":"Which conflicts dominate the main program and phase-out stages?","a":"Schedules dominate both; technical conflict over multiplying project interfaces is also frequent and serious in the main program, while in phase-out technical problems are rare and personality conflict is second-ranked."},{"q":"The four fundamental issues of the project formation stage?","a":"Specifying the technical objectives precisely enough for detailed planning; obtaining resource commitment from senior and functional management; setting and communicating the project's priority relative to other projects; and establishing the project's organizational structure enough to prepare a WBS and linear responsibility chart."},{"q":"The three requirements of project negotiation?","a":"The conflict must be settled without irreparable harm to the project's objectives; the method must allow and foster honesty between negotiators; and the solution must satisfy the individual's needs, the other parties' needs and the parent organization's needs — i.e. win-win."},{"q":"The four points of principled negotiation (Fisher & Ury, 1983)?","a":"Separate the people from the problem; focus on interests, not positions; before seeking agreement, invent options for mutual gain; and insist on using objective criteria such as market value, expert opinion, law or company policy."},{"q":"Define positional negotiation and give the chapter's cure for it.","a":"Stating immediate wants on the assumption that the environment is static (e.g. \"we might be able to deliver it by February 1\"); the cure is to ask \"Why?\" when a position is stated, so the underlying interest — such as late delivery of test supplies — becomes visible and solvable."},{"q":"The five Blake & Mouton methods of conflict resolution in Burke's reading?","a":"Withdrawing, Smoothing, Compromising, Forcing, and Confrontation–Problem Solving."},{"q":"Burke's headline results for Confrontation and Forcing?","a":"Confrontation–Problem Solving was 58.5% of the 53 effective resolutions and 0% of the 53 ineffective ones; Forcing was 79.2% of ineffective resolutions and only 24.5% of effective ones — and in 11 of those 13 effective Forcing cases the writer was the winner."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 b06"},{"id":"b08","title":"Book 8 · Scheduling: PERT & CPM ★","slides":"6e ch. 8 = 7e ch. 8 · weeks 6–7 · MIDTERM · calculations","md":"# B08 — Scheduling: PERT and CPM (Meredith & Mantel ch. 8) ★\n\n_Midterm material, weeks 6–7, and the **calculable half of the midterm** alongside chapter 2. This is also\nthe one chapter where the 2025/26 archive is already strong — `archive-2025/03`, `04` and `05` cover the\nsame ground. Read both: the archive drills the mechanics, this chapter supplies the book's own notation,\nits worked example and two ideas the archive does not have._\n\n⚠️ **Edition note.** Taken from the **7th edition** PDF; the syllabus specifies the **6th**. **This chapter keeps the same number in both editions**, so nothing here is affected — but chapters **4, 5 and 6 are permuted** between the editions, which changes what the midterm covers. `study/exam-prep.md` has the full mapping.\n\n---\n\n## 8.1 Why schedule at all\n\nThe book's case for a network schedule, condensed. A network:\n\n- shows the **interdependence** of activities explicitly;\n- shows the **expected completion date**;\n- identifies the **critical activities** that will delay the whole project if they slip;\n- identifies activities with **slack** that can be delayed without harm;\n- determines the dates on which activities **may** start and **must** start;\n- and, given three-point estimates, allows an estimate of the **probability** of finishing by a given date.\n\nThat last point is what separates PERT from a plain bar chart, and it is section 8.2's payoff.\n\n## 8.2 The two conventions, and the terminology trap\n\n> **PERT = ADM = AOA.** Activities are **arrows**; nodes are events.\n> **CPM = PDM = AON.** Activities are **nodes**; arrows are dependencies.\n\n| Older name | PMI's name | Layout | Activity is a… |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **PERT** | **ADM** — Arrow Diagram Method | **AOA** activity-on-arrow | arrow |\n| **CPM** | **PDM** — Precedence Diagram Method | **AON** activity-on-node | node |\n\nThis three-way naming is a likely exam question precisely because it is confusing. The book adopts **AON\nfor most of the chapter**, which is also what commercial software uses.\n\n**The practical differences (p. 341):**\n\n| | AON | AOA |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dummy activities | **not required** | **required** in some logic patterns |\n| Events | must be added as zero-duration activities or milestones | **clearly identified** as nodes |\n| Drawing | easier | slightly harder |\n| Software | used by most PC-based packages | less common |\n\nA **dummy activity** consumes no time and no resources; its sole purpose is to indicate a technological\nrelationship. It appears when, say, activities a, b and c must precede d but only a and b must precede e.\n\n⚠️ **Note the historical split the book preserves:** PERT came from probabilistic, research-type projects\nand carries the three-time estimate; CPM came from construction and originally used single-time estimates\nwith a cost trade-off. In modern practice the two have merged, which is why \"PERT/CPM\" is written as one\nthing.\n\n## 8.2 Three-point estimates ★\n\n> **T_E = (a + 4m + b) / 6**\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| a | optimistic time |\n| m | most likely time — **the mode** |\n| b | pessimistic time |\n\n**Why Beta and not normal.** The book is explicit: the Beta distribution is used because it is **highly\nflexible in form** and can accommodate the extreme cases where **a = m** or **b = m**. A normal\ndistribution cannot — it is symmetric by construction.\n\nT_E is an estimate of the **mean**, computed as a weighted average with weights **1-4-1**.\n\nThe range about m may be **symmetric** (m − a = b − m) or **strongly asymmetric**, and both occur in the\nbook's own table.\n\n> **Variance:** σ² = ((b − a)/6)²\n> **Standard deviation:** σ = √σ² = (b − a)/6\n\nThe justification given is that **the standard deviation of a Beta distribution is approximately one-sixth\nof its range**. That is an approximation, not an identity, and the book says so.\n\n**Two scholarly caveats the book itself raises**, worth a sentence in an essay answer:\n\n1. **Sasieni (1986)** pointed out that the (a + 4m + b)/6 formula cannot be derived from the Beta\n   distribution without assumptions that are not obviously reasonable, and wondered where it came from.\n   Littlefield et al. later traced it to a US Navy paper that states those assumptions.\n2. **The Parkinson's law criticism (Williams, 1995):** actual activity times are rarely below the mode and\n   often above it, because work expands to fill the time allotted. Problems make an activity take longer;\n   nothing makes it take less. This explains the right skew.\n\nA third criticism: once activity times are set they become **targets**, so PERT's success may come from the\n*process* of estimating rather than from the estimates — a comparison the book draws with Management by\nObjectives.\n\n## 8.2 Forward and backward pass\n\nIdentical to `archive-2025/05-critical-path-method.md`, which drills the mechanics with a verified\neight-activity network. In brief:\n\n- **ES** = 0 for starting activities, otherwise **max(EF of all predecessors)** — an activity cannot begin\n  until every path leading to it is complete.\n- **EF** = ES + T_E\n- **LF** = project duration for the final activity, otherwise **min(LS of all successors)**\n- **LS** = LF − T_E\n- **Slack** = LS − ES = LF − EF\n\nThe **critical path** is the chain of zero-slack activities; its length is the project duration.\n\n## The book's worked example — verified\n\nTen activities, a to j. From Table 8-2 (p. 347):\n\n| Activity | T_E | σ² | σ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| a | 20 | 4 | 2 |\n| b | 20 | 0 | 0 |\n| c | 10 | 4 | 2 |\n| **d** | **15** | **25** | **5** |\n| e | 10 | 4 | 2 |\n| f | 14 | 4 | 2 |\n| g | 4 | 0 | 0 |\n| h | 11 | 5.4 | 2.32 |\n| i | 18 | 28.4 | 5.33 |\n| j | 8 | 4 | 2 |\n\nNote that **b and g have zero variance** — their a, m and b estimates are identical, so their durations are\ncertain. Activity **i has the largest variance at 28.4**, and **d the second largest at 25**.\n\n> **Critical path: a → d → j. Duration m = 20 + 15 + 8 = 43 days.**\n> **Critical path variance = 4 + 25 + 4 = 33 days². σ = √33 = 5.745 days.**\n\n## 8.2 Probability of meeting a deadline ★\n\n> **Z = (D − μ) / √(σ²_μ)**\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| D | the **desired** project completion time |\n| μ | the **critical time** — sum of T_E along the critical path |\n| σ²_μ | the **variance of the critical path** — sum of the variances of its activities |\n| Z | the standard normal deviate |\n\nThis is the archive's Z = (T_s − T_E)/σ_p in the book's own notation. Same formula, different letters.\n\n⚠️ **Only critical path variances are summed.** Non-critical activities contribute nothing, however\nuncertain they are. Activity i has the largest variance in the whole project (28.4) and does not enter the\ncalculation at all.\n\n### Forward: what are the chances of finishing in 50 days?\n\n> Z = (50 − 43) / 5.745 = **1.22**\n> Table 8-5 → row 1.2, column .02 → **0.8888**\n> **Almost 89%**\n\n### Backward: what deadline gives 95% confidence?\n\nLook up 0.95 in the table → **Z = 1.645**. Then solve for D:\n\n> D = μ + Z·σ = 43 + 5.745(1.645) = 43 + 9.45 = **52.45 days**\n\nRearranging the Z formula to find a date rather than a probability is the standard second half of a\nquestion. The book notes its interpolation is approximate because the table values are not strictly linear.\n\n### The near-critical path problem — the idea the archive does not have\n\nThis is the most sophisticated point in the chapter and a strong candidate for a discriminating exam\nquestion.\n\nThe path **c → h → i** is **not** critical: it runs 10 + 11 + 18 = **39 days** against the critical path's\n43. But its variance is **4 + 5.4 + 28.4 = 37.8**, which is *larger* than the critical path's 33, giving\nσ = 6.15.\n\n| Path | Duration | Variance | σ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| a-d-j (critical) | **43** | 33 | 5.745 |\n| c-h-i (near-critical) | 39 | **37.8** | 6.15 |\n\nNow ask the same questions of it:\n\n> P(c-h-i within 50 days): Z = (50 − 39)/6.15 = **1.79**\n> P(c-h-i within 43 days): Z = (43 − 39)/6.15 = **0.65** → probability **0.74**\n\nSo there is a **26% chance that the non-critical path c-h-i overruns 43 days** and delays the project.\nMeanwhile the critical path itself has only a 50% chance of finishing within its own mean of 43 days,\nbecause 43 *is* its mean.\n\n> **Assuming the two paths are independent, the probability that both finish within 43 days is the\n> product: (0.50)(0.74) = 0.37.**\n\n**The lesson, and it is a big one:** the probability of finishing on the critical path's expected date is\n**not** the probability of finishing the project. A high-variance near-critical path can easily become the\nreal constraint. The practical response is to watch paths with large variance even when they have slack.\n\n⚠️ Note the book's own caution: the independence assumption is questionable when paths share activities,\nwhich is why section 8.3 turns to **simulation** (Monte Carlo, using Crystal Ball) for a better answer.\n\n### Reading the Z table\n\nThe table is cumulative: the value is the **area to the left** of Z.\n\n- Area to the **left** of Z = 1.34 → 0.9099\n- Area to the **right** → 1 − 0.9099 = **0.0901**\n- Area between the **mean and Z** → 0.9099 − 0.5 = **0.4099**\n\n**As D approaches μ, Z approaches zero, and the chance of on-time completion approaches 50–50.** The\nmanagerial implication the book draws is blunt: promising the expected completion date is promising a coin\nflip.\n\n## 8.3 Simulation\n\nWhere the analytic Z-score approach breaks down — multiple near-critical paths, shared activities,\nnon-independent risks — the chapter turns to **Monte Carlo simulation**, sampling each activity duration\nfrom its distribution thousands of times and reading the distribution of project durations off the results.\nThe book uses Crystal Ball. The conceptual point for an exam: simulation answers the *same* question the\nZ-score answers, but without needing the critical path to be the only path that matters.\n\n## 8.4 Using these tools\n\nThe closing section's practical warning is that a network is a model, and its output is only as good as the\nestimates and the precedence logic put into it. Garbage precedence produces a confidently wrong critical\npath.\n\n## Why this matters for the exam\n\n- **This chapter plus chapter 2 are where the midterm's calculations live.** Expect T_E, a forward and\n  backward pass, slack, a critical path, and a Z-score lookup.\n- **The PERT/ADM/AOA versus CPM/PDM/AON naming** is a cheap theory question — learn all six terms.\n- **Know why Beta and not normal:** flexibility of form, and it handles a = m or b = m.\n- **Know that only critical path variances are summed.** The book's own example makes the trap vivid, since\n  the highest-variance activity in the project is not on the critical path.\n- **The near-critical path result (0.50 × 0.74 = 0.37)** is the most distinctive thing in the chapter and\n  the likeliest \"explain this\" question.\n- **Be ready to run the Z formula backwards** to find a date for a given confidence level.\n\n## Formula summary\n\n| Quantity | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Expected activity time | **T_E = (a + 4m + b)/6** |\n| Activity variance | **σ² = ((b − a)/6)²** |\n| Activity standard deviation | **σ = (b − a)/6** |\n| Forward pass | **ES = max(EF of predecessors)**, **EF = ES + T_E** |\n| Backward pass | **LF = min(LS of successors)**, **LS = LF − T_E** |\n| Slack | **LS − ES = LF − EF** |\n| Critical path variance | **σ²_μ = Σ σ² over critical activities only** |\n| Probability of a deadline | **Z = (D − μ)/√(σ²_μ)** |\n| Date for a confidence level | **D = μ + Z·√(σ²_μ)** |\n\n## Verification note\n\nEvery number in the chapter's worked example was recomputed during ingest and all are correct: the critical\npath a-d-j at 43 days, variance 33, σ = 5.7446 (printed 5.745); Z = 1.2185 → 1.22 → 0.8888; the 95%\ndeadline at 43 + 9.45 = 52.45 days; and the near-critical path c-h-i at 39 days, variance 37.8, σ = 6.148,\ngiving Z values of 1.79 and 0.65 and the joint probability 0.50 × 0.74 = 0.37.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Give the six things a network schedule shows.","a":"Activity interdependence, the expected completion date, the critical activities, the activities with slack, the dates activities may and must start, and the probability of meeting a deadline."},{"q":"Match PERT and CPM to ADM, PDM, AOA and AON.","a":"PERT = ADM = AOA (activities on arrows); CPM = PDM = AON (activities on nodes)."},{"q":"Which convention needs dummy activities, and which does the book mostly use?","a":"AOA needs them; the book and most software use AON."},{"q":"What is a dummy activity?","a":"A connector with no time and no resources whose sole purpose is to indicate a technological relationship."},{"q":"How are events represented in an AON network?","a":"They must be added as zero-duration activities or milestones; AOA identifies them naturally as nodes."},{"q":"State the expected activity time formula and name the weights.","a":"T_E = (a + 4m + b)/6 — a weighted average with weights 1-4-1."},{"q":"What does m represent statistically?","a":"The mode, the most likely time."},{"q":"Why is the Beta distribution used rather than the normal?","a":"It is highly flexible in form and can handle the extremes where a = m or b = m, which a symmetric normal cannot."},{"q":"State the activity variance and standard deviation formulas.","a":"σ² = ((b − a)/6)² and σ = (b − a)/6."},{"q":"What assumption underlies σ = (b − a)/6?","a":"That the standard deviation of a Beta distribution is approximately one-sixth of its range — an approximation, not an identity."},{"q":"What did Sasieni point out about the T_E formula?","a":"That it cannot be derived from the Beta distribution without assumptions that are not obviously reasonable; it was later traced to a US Navy paper."},{"q":"What is the Parkinson's law criticism of PERT estimates?","a":"Actual times are rarely below the mode and often above it, because work expands to fill the allotted time — which explains the right skew."},{"q":"Why might PERT's success come from the process rather than the estimates?","a":"Because set activity times become targets, like Management by Objectives, and so act as self-fulfilling prophecies."},{"q":"State the forward and backward pass rules.","a":"ES = max(EF of predecessors) and EF = ES + T_E; LF = min(LS of successors) and LS = LF − T_E."},{"q":"In the book's example, what is the critical path and its duration?","a":"a → d → j, 20 + 15 + 8 = 43 days."},{"q":"What is the variance and standard deviation of that critical path?","a":"4 + 25 + 4 = 33 days², so σ = √33 = 5.745 days."},{"q":"State the deadline probability formula and define its terms.","a":"Z = (D − μ)/√(σ²_μ), where D is the desired completion time, μ the critical path duration, and σ²_μ the critical path variance."},{"q":"Which activities' variances enter the project variance?","a":"Only those on the critical path, however uncertain the others are."},{"q":"In the book's example, which activity has the largest variance, and does it count?","a":"Activity i at 28.4, and it does not count — it is not on the critical path."},{"q":"What is the probability of finishing the book's example in 50 days?","a":"Z = (50 − 43)/5.745 = 1.22, giving 0.8888, almost 89%."},{"q":"What deadline gives 95% confidence in that example?","a":"Z = 1.645, so D = 43 + 5.745(1.645) = 52.45 days."},{"q":"How do you rearrange the Z formula to find a date?","a":"D = μ + Z·√(σ²_μ)."},{"q":"What is the near-critical path in the book's example and why does it matter?","a":"c-h-i, 39 days with variance 37.8 — larger variance than the critical path, so it can still overrun and delay the project."},{"q":"What is the chance c-h-i exceeds 43 days?","a":"Z = (43 − 39)/6.15 = 0.65 giving 0.74, so a 26% chance of overrunning."},{"q":"What is the probability both paths finish within 43 days, and what does it show?","a":"0.50 × 0.74 = 0.37 assuming independence — the critical path's on-time probability is not the project's."},{"q":"Why is the critical path only 50% likely to finish within 43 days?","a":"Because 43 is its mean, and as D approaches μ, Z approaches zero and the odds approach 50–50."},{"q":"What is the managerial implication of that?","a":"Promising the expected completion date is promising a coin flip."},{"q":"How do you read the area to the right of a Z value from the table?","a":"Subtract the table value from 1; for Z = 1.34 that is 1 − 0.9099 = 0.0901."},{"q":"How do you find the area between the mean and a Z value?","a":"Subtract 0.5 from the table value; for Z = 1.34 that is 0.9099 − 0.5 = 0.4099."},{"q":"When does the chapter turn to simulation instead of the Z-score?","a":"When the independence assumption fails, for example with multiple near-critical paths sharing activities — Monte Carlo then samples each duration repeatedly."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2026","tag":"26 b08"},{"id":"a00","title":"Course Map & What Is Still Unknown","slides":"2025/26 · provenance, topic sequence, exam rules","md":"# IE408 — Project Management (Course Map) · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n> ### ⚠️ SUPERSEDED — the real 2026/27 syllabus has been read.\n>\n> **`study/00-course-map.md` is the live course map. Start there.** This file is kept as the record of what\n> the 2025/26 run looked like.\n>\n> The official syllabus arrived on 7 October 2026 and three things turned out to be different:\n> **a different instructor** (Benjamin Duraković), **a different assessment structure** (final 50%,\n> midterm 40%, five homeworks 10% — no quizzes and no project), and **a different topic order**.\n>\n> Consequently **every \"the instructor says\" note below is unsourced for this year** — the pencils-only\n> rule, the supplied Z-tables, the multiple-choice warning and the weeks 8–15 exam scope all came from\n> somebody else's course.\n>\n> **`study/archive-vs-2026.md` maps every chapter here onto this year's weeks** and lists what is missing.\n> The short version: this archive covers the post-midterm half very well and the pre-midterm half barely\n> at all.\n\n## What the source actually is\n\nA single written study guide, structured week by week (2 through 15), with formulas, worked examples and\nASCII diagrams. There are no lecture slides, no syllabus page and no grading table in it.\n\nEverything it did not know has since been answered by the official syllabus, and in most cases the answer\ndiffers from what the guide implied:\n\n| Question the guide left open | The 2026/27 answer |\n|---|---|\n| Grading breakdown | **Final 50%, midterm 40%, 5 homeworks 10%** |\n| Quiz dates | **There are no quizzes** |\n| Project or homework component | **Five homeworks, 10% total. No project.** |\n| Textbook | **Meredith & Mantel, 6th ed.**, plus PMBOK 4th ed. and ProjectLibre |\n| Instructor | **Assoc. Prof. Dr. Benjamin Duraković** |\n| Midterm scope | **Chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8**, in week 8 |\n\nWhat the guide still provides, and provides well, is **the technique content and its worked examples** —\nall of which were recomputed and verified during ingest.\n\n## Topic sequence (weeks 2–15)\n\n| Week | Topic | Digest |\n|------|-------|--------|\n| 2 | Process framework, iron triangle, scope and innovation | `01-process-framework-and-scope.md` |\n| 3 | Quality as inverse variance; the eight dimensions | `02-quality-and-variance.md` |\n| 4 | PERT three-point estimating; Beta vs triangular | `03-pert-estimation.md` |\n| 5 | Network diagramming, AON vs AOA, dummy activities | `04-network-diagramming.md` |\n| 6–7 | Critical Path Method, forward/backward pass, slack | `05-critical-path-method.md` ★ |\n| 8 | Gantt charts and progress tracking | `06-gantt-and-tracking.md` |\n| 9 | Resource allocation and time-phased budgeting | `07-resources-and-budgeting.md` |\n| 10 | Risk management, qualitative vs quantitative | `08-risk-management.md` |\n| 11, 13 | Crashing, least-cost method, overtime economics | `09-crashing.md` ★ |\n| 12 | Schedule uncertainty, project variance, Z-scores | `10-schedule-uncertainty.md` ★ |\n| 14–15 | Integration, exam rules, instructor tips | `11-synthesis-and-exam-rules.md` |\n\n★ = the three calculation chapters. Everything the guide calls a \"practical problem\" is built from these.\n\n## What the instructor reportedly said about the exam\n\nRecorded in the week-15 notes. Carried forward because it is the most actionable thing in the whole guide,\nbut **confirm it this year before relying on it**:\n\n- **Scope:** the final focuses on **weeks 8–15**, while still assuming the network fundamentals from 5–7.\n- **Structure:** theory questions (definitions, Gantt vs network, risk strategies, quality/variance) plus\n  **one or two full practical problems** — build the diagram, run both passes, find slack and the critical\n  path, pick a crashing candidate, do a Z-score lookup.\n- **Pencils and erasers only. Pens are forbidden** — network passes need correcting mid-exam, and pen makes\n  a mess that loses marks.\n- **A non-programmable scientific calculator is required**; phones and smart devices are not allowed.\n- **Z-tables are provided** with the paper, so they do not need memorising.\n- Multiple-choice answer keys have followed deliberate patterns in past years, and per-question time limits\n  may be shortened.\n\n## How the whole course fits together\n\n```\nPERT (a, m, b)  ──►  T_E and σ² per activity\n                          │\n                          ▼\n                 AON network diagram\n                          │\n                          ▼\n        forward pass ES/EF  ──►  backward pass LS/LF  ──►  slack, critical path\n                          │                                        │\n          ┌───────────────┴───────────────┐                        │\n          ▼                               ▼                        ▼\n   Gantt chart + tracking        time-phased budget         σ_p and Z-score\n          │                                                        │\n          └──────────────────►  crashing: least-cost  ◄────────────┘\n```\n\nEvery exam problem is a walk through some part of that chain, and the chain only runs in one direction.\nIf the forward pass is wrong, everything downstream is wrong, which is why chapter 05 is the one to\nover-practise.\n\n## Status of this archive\n\n- Source: classmate's study guide, weeks 2–15, 2025/26 run.\n- All numeric worked examples in the guide were **recomputed and verified** during ingest. See each\n  chapter's \"verification\" note. One genuine discrepancy was found, in the week-12 Z-score homework —\n  `10-schedule-uncertainty.md` explains it.\n- Superseded 7 October 2026 by the official syllabus. The live material is in `study/` — `00-course-map.md`,\n  `exam-prep.md` and `archive-vs-2026.md`.\n","lesson":"","cards":[],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 00"},{"id":"a01","title":"Process Framework, Iron Triangle & Scope","slides":"2025/26 · week 2","md":"# 01 — Process Framework, the Iron Triangle and Scope (Week 2) · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n_Definitional chapter. Nothing here is calculated, which makes it pure theory-question material._\n\n## The five process phases\n\nA project moves through five process groups. The first four are sequential; the fifth runs alongside.\n\n| # | Phase | What happens |\n|---|-------|--------------|\n| 1 | **Initiation** | Establish eligibility, high-level goals, the foundational scope |\n| 2 | **Planning** | Baseline schedule, resource allocation, cost estimates, network diagram, risk plan |\n| 3 | **Execution** | Mobilise people and physical resources, perform the scheduled activities |\n| 4 | **Controlling** | Monitor against the baseline, track Gantt completion, manage change, audit |\n| 5 | **Closing** | Formal stakeholder acceptance, handover of deliverables, lessons learned |\n\n```\n  Initiation ──► Planning ──► Execution ──► Closing\n                     ▲            │\n                     └ Controlling┘\n```\n\n**The shape is the point.** Controlling is not a step between execution and closing; it is a loop that\ncompares execution back against the plan and feeds corrections in. Draw it as a loop, not as a box in the\nrow, if asked to sketch the life cycle.\n\n**Which phase owns which tool** — a question the guide flags as likely:\n\n| Tool | Phase |\n|------|-------|\n| Network diagram / CPM | Planning |\n| Gantt chart | Execution and Controlling |\n| Risk register | Planning, revisited in Controlling |\n| Lessons learned | Closing |\n\n## The iron triangle\n\nThree mutually dependent constraints bound every project:\n\n- **Scope** — the output, deliverables and boundaries of the work.\n- **Time** — the schedule baseline and duration needed to run all activities.\n- **Cost** — money, people, equipment, materials.\n\n> **Change any one and the other two move.** That single sentence is the whole concept, and it is the\n> justification for everything in the crashing chapter: buying time costs money, which is the triangle\n> in action.\n\nQuality is sometimes drawn in the middle as the thing the three constraints jointly determine, which\nconnects this chapter to the next one.\n\n## Scope management and innovation\n\nWork done inside a defined scope produces two kinds of result:\n\n1. **Core scope achievements** — the planned innovations, aimed at the target business requirement and the\n   existing core market.\n2. **Accidental inventions** — discoveries made tangentially during the work that fall *outside* the core\n   business scope.\n\n```\n                        R&D / research phase\n                                │\n            ┌───────────────────┴───────────────────┐\n            ▼                                       ▼\n   core scope achievement                  accidental innovation\n     (the intended goal)                   (unintended, tangential)\n            │                                       │\n            ▼                                       ▼\n   target business and                   new or non-core external\n      core market                                 market\n                                                    │\n                                        ┌───────────┴───────────┐\n                                        ▼                       ▼\n                             commercial licensing        rights transfer\n```\n\n## Open versus closed innovation\n\n- **Core inventions** go straight to the organisation's existing target market, through its own marketing\n  and distribution channels. This is the closed path, and it works because the firm already has the\n  competence and the infrastructure.\n- **Tangential discoveries** usually land outside the firm's competence. The **open innovation** answer is\n  to **license or transfer** the intellectual property to a third party that already has the manufacturing\n  and distribution network to exploit it in that secondary market.\n\n**Connection worth remembering:** this is the same logic as the *Share* response to a positive risk in\nchapter 08. A discovery you cannot exploit yourself is an opportunity you hand to someone who can. If an\nexam question mentions an accidental invention outside the core market, both \"open innovation / licensing\"\nand \"share the opportunity\" are correct framings.\n\n## Verification note\n\nNothing in this chapter is numeric, so nothing needed checking.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Name the five project management process phases in order.","a":"Initiation, planning, execution, controlling, closing."},{"q":"Which phase is not purely sequential, and why?","a":"Controlling — it loops alongside execution, comparing progress against the baseline and feeding corrections back."},{"q":"What belongs to the initiation phase?","a":"Project eligibility, high-level goals and the foundational scope."},{"q":"Name four planning-phase outputs.","a":"Baseline schedule, resource allocation, cost estimates, network diagram (and the risk management plan)."},{"q":"What happens in closing?","a":"Formal stakeholder acceptance, handover of deliverables, documented lessons learned."},{"q":"Name the three constraints of the iron triangle.","a":"Scope, time (schedule), cost (budget and resources)."},{"q":"State the central property of the iron triangle.","a":"The three are interdependent — changing any one inevitably affects the other two."},{"q":"Which phase does a network diagram belong to, and which does a Gantt chart belong to?","a":"The network diagram is a planning tool; the Gantt chart is for execution and controlling."},{"q":"Distinguish a core scope achievement from an accidental invention.","a":"The core achievement is the planned innovation serving the existing target market; the accidental invention arises tangentially and falls outside the core business scope."},{"q":"Under open innovation, what do you do with a discovery outside your core competence?","a":"License or transfer the intellectual property to a third party able to commercialise it in that secondary market."},{"q":"Why are core inventions handled internally?","a":"The organisation already has the target market, the marketing and the distribution channels for them."},{"q":"Which risk response does open-innovation licensing resemble?","a":"Share — partnering with an entity better equipped to capture an opportunity's benefit."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 01"},{"id":"a02","title":"Quality as Inverse Variance","slides":"2025/26 · week 3","md":"# 02 — Quality as Inverse Variance (Week 3) · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n_Short chapter, but it carries the idea that makes the whole statistics half of the course make sense:\n**consistency is quality**. Chapter 10 is the same idea applied to a schedule._\n\n## The definition\n\nQuality is an inverse function of variance:\n\n> **Quality ∝ 1 / σ²**\n\n| Variance | Behaviour | Quality |\n|----------|-----------|---------|\n| **High σ²** | Wide dispersion, unstable, unpredictable | **Low** |\n| **Low σ²** | Tightly clustered outputs, predictable | **High** |\n\nThe claim is deliberately counterintuitive: quality here is **not** about being good, it is about being\n**predictable**. A process that is consistently mediocre scores higher on this definition than one that\nalternates between excellent and terrible.\n\n## The \"Ben\" analogy\n\nThe instructor's own illustration, using a hypothetical classmate:\n\n- **High variance.** Ben is hostile in the morning, delighted to see you by the afternoon, and different\n  again next year. The spread between his states is enormous, so you can never prepare. The relationship\n  is **low quality**.\n- **Low variance.** Ben is consistently stern and reserved — never warm, but never surprising. You can\n  prepare and adjust. Dispersion is near zero, so the relationship is **high quality**.\n\nThe analogy is doing real work: it shows that the *level* of the mean is irrelevant to this measure. Only\nthe **spread** counts. A permanently grumpy Ben is high quality; an unpredictable Ben is not.\n\n## The eight dimensions of quality\n\n| # | Dimension | Description | How it is measured |\n|---|-----------|-------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | **Performance** | Primary operating characteristics | Output speed, capacity, functional execution |\n| 2 | **Features** | Secondary characteristics supplementing basic function | Presence of secondary functions or accessories |\n| 3 | **Reliability** | Probability of not failing within a given time | **MTBF** (mean time between failures); failure rate |\n| 4 | **Durability** | Product life and resistance to wear and stress | Operating lifespan before replacement is required |\n| 5 | **Serviceability** | Ease, speed and competence of repair | **MTTR** (mean time to repair); parts availability |\n| 6 | **Conformance** | Degree to which the product meets set standards | Compliance rate against technical specification |\n| 7 | **Aesthetics** | Visual, tactile or auditory appeal | Sensory evaluation and design fit |\n| 8 | **Perceived quality** | Subjective judgement from reputation or advertising | Brand trust, market perception over time |\n\n**The two that are easy to confuse:**\n\n- **Reliability vs durability.** Reliability asks *how often does it break* (MTBF); durability asks *how\n  long does it last in total before you replace it*. A light bulb can be reliable (never flickers) and\n  not durable (burns out in a year).\n- **Conformance vs performance.** Conformance is about meeting the **specification**; performance is about\n  the specification's **level**. A product can conform perfectly to a modest spec.\n\n**The two subjective ones** are aesthetics and perceived quality. Perceived quality is the only dimension\nthat does not measure the product at all — it measures the customer's belief about the product.\n\n**Memory hook:** the first six are properties of the object (what it does, what else it does, how often it\nfails, how long it lasts, how easily it is fixed, whether it matches spec); the last two are properties of\nthe observer.\n\n## Verification note\n\nNothing numeric here. The one formula, Quality ∝ 1/σ², is a stated proportionality rather than a\ncalculation, and the variance formula it refers to is derived in chapter 03.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"State the mathematical definition of quality used in this course.","a":"Quality is inversely proportional to variance: Quality ∝ 1/σ²."},{"q":"Does high variance mean high or low quality, and why?","a":"Low quality — wide dispersion means instability and unpredictability."},{"q":"Why can a consistently mediocre process count as high quality here?","a":"The definition measures spread, not level; predictability is what is being rewarded."},{"q":"In the Ben analogy, which Ben is high quality?","a":"The consistently stern one — near-zero dispersion makes him predictable, so others can prepare."},{"q":"Name all eight dimensions of quality.","a":"Performance, features, reliability, durability, serviceability, conformance, aesthetics, perceived quality."},{"q":"Which dimension is measured by MTBF?","a":"Reliability — the probability a unit does not fail within a specified time."},{"q":"Which dimension is measured by MTTR?","a":"Serviceability — the ease, speed and competence of repair."},{"q":"Distinguish reliability from durability.","a":"Reliability is how often it fails; durability is total operating life before replacement."},{"q":"Distinguish conformance from performance.","a":"Conformance is meeting the stated specification; performance is the level of the primary operating characteristics."},{"q":"What is \"features\" as a quality dimension?","a":"Secondary characteristics that supplement the basic functioning of the product."},{"q":"Which dimension does not measure the product itself?","a":"Perceived quality — it measures brand reputation and customer belief."},{"q":"Which two dimensions are subjective?","a":"Aesthetics and perceived quality."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 02"},{"id":"a03","title":"PERT Three-Point Estimating ★","slides":"2025/26 · week 4","md":"# 03 — PERT Three-Point Estimating (Week 4) · ARCHIVE 2025/26 ★\n\n_The first calculation chapter. Everything downstream — the network, the critical path, the Z-score —\nstarts from the two numbers produced here: **T_E** and **σ²**._\n\n## Why three points\n\nA single duration estimate hides the uncertainty in it. PERT (Program Evaluation and Review Technique)\nasks for three, and weights them using a Beta distribution.\n\n| Estimate | Symbol | Meaning | Probability |\n|----------|--------|---------|-------------|\n| **Optimistic** | a | Duration if nothing goes wrong at all | P → 0 |\n| **Most likely** | m | Duration under normal conditions, including the common delays | P = maximum (the **mode**) |\n| **Pessimistic** | b | Duration if every obstacle and delay materialises | P → 0 |\n\nBoth a and b are near-impossible edge cases. Only **m** is the peak of the distribution.\n\n```\n  probability\n    density\n       ▲            mode (m)\n       │               ███\n       │             ███████\n       │          █████████████\n       │       ███████████████████   T_E\n       │     ███████████████████████  │\n       └──────┬──────────┬────────────┬──────────► time\n              a          m            b\n```\n\nThe distribution is **asymmetric** — m is not generally halfway between a and b, because delays stretch\nthe right tail further than good luck shortens the left one.\n\n## The formulas\n\n**Expected duration (Beta weighting):**\n\n> **T_E = (a + 4m + b) / 6**\n\nThe most likely value carries four of the six weight units; the two extremes carry one each.\n\n**Activity variance:**\n\n> **σ² = ((b − a) / 6)²**\n\n**Activity standard deviation:**\n\n> **σ = (b − a) / 6**\n\n**Note on the sign.** ((b − a)/6)² = ((a − b)/6)², because squaring removes the sign. You cannot produce a\nnegative variance by subtracting in the wrong order — but you *can* produce a negative standard deviation\nif you take the square root carelessly, so keep b − a in that order.\n\n**What the variance formula tells you:** it depends **only on the spread b − a**. The most likely value m\ndoes not appear. Two activities with wildly different T_E can have identical variance, and an activity\nwith a = b has **zero** variance no matter what m is.\n\n## Beta versus triangular distribution\n\n| | Beta | Triangular |\n|---|---|---|\n| Formula | T_E = (a + 4m + b) / 6 | E = (a + m + b) / 3 |\n| Weighting | m weighted ×4 | all three weighted equally |\n| Typical use | **Activity time estimates**, risk modelling | **Budget and cost estimates** |\n| Why | The mode is well-informed, so trust it | Tail data is poorly defined, so do not pretend to weight it |\n\nThe distinction is a likely theory question. The short version: **time uses Beta, cost uses triangular**,\nbecause for durations you have a defensible most-likely value and for costs you often do not.\n\n## Worked micro-example\n\nFor an activity with a = 2, m = 4, b = 12:\n\n| Quantity | Working | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| T_E | (2 + 4×4 + 12) / 6 = 30 / 6 | **5.00** |\n| σ | (12 − 2) / 6 = 10 / 6 | **1.67** |\n| σ² | 1.67² | **2.78** |\n| Triangular E | (2 + 4 + 12) / 3 = 18 / 3 | **6.00** |\n\nNotice T_E = 5.00 sits **below** the triangular 6.00. Beta pulls the estimate toward the mode; the\ntriangular average is dragged up by the long pessimistic tail. Expect that direction whenever b − m is\nlarger than m − a.\n\n## The degenerate case, and why it matters later\n\nIf **a = m = b**, then T_E = a and **σ² = 0**. The guide's main network example (chapter 05) is written\nthis way — every activity has a = m = b, so every duration is certain.\n\n⚠️ That makes the chapter-05 table useless for practising variance, and it is the source of a real\ninconsistency with the week-12 Z-score example, which uses non-zero variances for the same activity\nletters. Chapter 10 explains it in full. Be aware that the two examples are **not** the same data set.\n\n## Verification note\n\nT_E and σ formulas restated as given. The micro-example above was computed during ingest and is new — the\nsource guide gives no worked PERT numbers of its own, only the formulas, which is itself worth noticing:\nevery a/m/b triple in the guide is degenerate.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What do the three PERT estimates represent?","a":"Optimistic a (nothing goes wrong), most likely m (normal conditions with common delays), pessimistic b (every obstacle occurs)."},{"q":"Which of a, m, b has the highest probability?","a":"m, the most likely estimate — it is the mode. Both a and b have probability tending to zero."},{"q":"State the PERT expected-duration formula.","a":"T_E = (a + 4m + b) / 6."},{"q":"How is the weight distributed in the Beta formula?","a":"Six units total — four on the most likely value, one each on the optimistic and pessimistic."},{"q":"State the activity variance formula.","a":"σ² = ((b − a) / 6)²."},{"q":"State the activity standard deviation formula.","a":"σ = (b − a) / 6."},{"q":"Does m appear in the variance formula?","a":"No — variance depends only on the spread b − a."},{"q":"Can you get a negative variance by subtracting in the wrong order?","a":"No — squaring removes the sign, so ((b−a)/6)² = ((a−b)/6)²."},{"q":"What is the variance of an activity with a = b?","a":"Zero — the duration is certain regardless of m."},{"q":"State the triangular distribution formula.","a":"E = (a + m + b) / 3, a simple arithmetic average."},{"q":"When is triangular used instead of Beta, and why?","a":"For budget and cost estimates, where precise tail-weighting data is not available to justify weighting the mode."},{"q":"Which distribution is used for activity durations?","a":"Beta, via T_E = (a + 4m + b) / 6."},{"q":"For a = 2, m = 4, b = 12, give T_E and σ.","a":"T_E = 30/6 = 5.00; σ = 10/6 = 1.67, so σ² = 2.78."},{"q":"Why is the PERT distribution asymmetric?","a":"The most likely value is not generally midway between a and b — delays stretch the pessimistic tail further than good luck shortens the optimistic one."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 03"},{"id":"a04","title":"Network Diagramming: AON vs AOA","slides":"2025/26 · week 5","md":"# 04 — Network Diagramming: AON and AOA (Week 5) · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n_How to draw the picture that chapter 05 then calculates on. Drawing it wrong is the most common way to\nlose a practical problem, because every later number inherits the mistake._\n\n## The two conventions\n\n**Activity-on-Node (AON)** — activities live in the boxes, arrows carry only the dependency.\n\n```\n   ┌────────────────┐      ┌────────────────┐\n   │   Activity A   │─────►│   Activity B   │\n   │ (duration = 2) │      │ (duration = 3) │\n   └────────────────┘      └────────────────┘\n```\n\n**Activity-on-Arrow (AOA)** — activities live on the arrows, nodes are start/end milestone events.\n\n```\n   (1) ──── Activity A (2) ────► (2) ──── Activity B (3) ────► (3)\n```\n\n| | AON | AOA |\n|---|---|---|\n| Activity shown as | a node (box) | an arrow |\n| Node means | the activity | an event (a moment in time) |\n| Arrow means | a dependency | the activity |\n| Dummy activities needed | **no** | **yes, sometimes** |\n| Status | **preferred in modern practice** | older convention |\n\nThe CPM node format in chapter 05 is an AON box, so AON is the one to default to unless a question\nexplicitly asks for AOA.\n\n## Structural rules for AOA diagrams\n\n1. **Dummy activities.** Drawn as a **dashed arrow**, with **zero duration and zero cost**. They exist\n   purely to keep the logic correct — specifically to stop two activities sharing the same start *and*\n   end node, which would make them indistinguishable, and to express a dependency that would otherwise\n   force a wrong one.\n2. **Node numbering.** Number sequentially left to right, so that every arrow runs from a lower number to\n   a higher one (**i < j**).\n3. **No crossing lines.** Arrange arrows so they do not intersect.\n4. **One start, one end.** Every network originates at a single start node and terminates at a single end\n   node.\n\n**Why dummies have zero duration:** they are not work. If a dummy had duration it would lengthen the path\nthrough it and change the project duration, which would mean the diagram's notation was altering the\nproject — the thing a notation must never do.\n\nRule 4 matters more than it looks. If several activities have no predecessors they all start at the one\nstart node, and if several have no successors they all converge on the one end node. A dangling activity\nthat terminates nowhere is a diagram error, and in the forward/backward pass it produces an activity whose\nlatest finish is undefined.\n\n## Reading a predecessor table into a diagram\n\nThe input to every practical problem is a predecessor table. The mechanical procedure:\n\n1. Any activity with **no predecessor** connects to the start node.\n2. Any activity **not named as a predecessor of anything** connects to the end node.\n3. For each row, draw an arrow from each listed predecessor into that activity.\n\nApplied to the course's standard example (full table in chapter 05):\n\n| Activity | Predecessors |\n|---|---|\n| A | none → from start |\n| B | none → from start |\n| C | A |\n| D | A, B |\n| E | C |\n| F | C |\n| G | D, E |\n| H | F, G → to end |\n\nA and B start it; H alone ends it. C has one predecessor, D and G have two each, H has two.\n\n```\n        ┌──► C ──┬──► E ──┐\n   A ───┤        │        ├──► G ──┐\n        └──► D ◄─┘        │        ├──► H\n   B ────────┘  └─────────┘        │\n                  F ───────────────┘\n```\n\n(The clean version is drawn in chapter 05 with all the pass numbers attached.)\n\n## Critical path notation\n\nFrom the week-7 notes, for presenting a finished diagram:\n\n- The **critical path** must be highlighted — **red**, or **double/dashed lines** (`====>`).\n- Non-critical paths use ordinary solid black arrows.\n- The highlighted path must run continuously from the start node to the end node. A critical path with a\n  gap in it is a calculation error, since by definition it is an unbroken chain of zero-slack activities.\n\n## What the diagram is for\n\nThe week-7 notes make the presentational case: a network diagram is a **single-slide synthesis** of a\nproject that may contain months of work and pages of arithmetic. One picture carries\n\n- every dependency,\n- the early and late schedules (ES, EF, LS, LF),\n- the slack available on each non-critical activity,\n- the total project duration,\n- and the critical path that management must protect.\n\nThat is the honest answer to \"why bother drawing it\" in a theory question.\n\n## Verification note\n\nThe predecessor mapping above was checked against the chapter-05 table; A and B are the only activities\nwith no predecessors and H is the only one that is nobody's predecessor, so the single-start/single-end\nrule is satisfied without adding artificial nodes.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"In AON, what do nodes and arrows represent?","a":"Nodes are activities; arrows are dependency relationships only."},{"q":"In AOA, what do nodes and arrows represent?","a":"Arrows are activities; nodes are start/end milestone events."},{"q":"Which convention is preferred in modern project management?","a":"Activity-on-Node."},{"q":"What is a dummy activity and how is it drawn?","a":"A logic-only connector with zero duration and zero cost, drawn as a dashed arrow."},{"q":"Why must a dummy have zero duration?","a":"It is not work — any duration would lengthen the path through it and change the project duration."},{"q":"What is the purpose of a dummy activity?","a":"To preserve correct logic, in particular to stop two activities sharing identical start and end nodes."},{"q":"Which convention needs dummy activities?","a":"AOA only; AON expresses the same logic with arrows alone."},{"q":"State the node-numbering rule for AOA.","a":"Number sequentially left to right so every arrow runs from a lower to a higher number (i < j)."},{"q":"How many start and end nodes may a network have?","a":"Exactly one of each."},{"q":"Which activities connect to the start node?","a":"Every activity with no predecessors."},{"q":"Which activities connect to the end node?","a":"Every activity that is not listed as a predecessor of any other."},{"q":"How must the critical path be marked on a presented diagram?","a":"In red or with double/dashed lines, running continuously from start node to end node."},{"q":"Name five things a finished network diagram communicates on one slide.","a":"Dependencies, early and late schedules, slack, total project duration, and the critical path."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 04"},{"id":"a05","title":"Critical Path Method ★","slides":"2025/26 · weeks 6–7","md":"# 05 — Critical Path Method: Forward and Backward Pass (Weeks 6–7) · ARCHIVE 2025/26 ★\n\n_The core of the course. Every practical exam problem runs through this chapter, and everything after it\ninherits these numbers. The worked example below was recomputed from the predecessor table during ingest\nand **every value in the source guide's table is correct**._\n\n## The AON node format\n\n```\n┌──────────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────┐\n│  ES          │   duration T_E   │          EF  │\n├──────────────┴──────────────────┴──────────────┤\n│              Activity name (ID)                │\n├──────────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────┤\n│  LS          │    slack  SL     │          LF  │\n└──────────────┴──────────────────┴──────────────┘\n```\n\nTop row is the **early** schedule, bottom row the **late** schedule, and the middle of the bottom row is\nthe slack between them. Draw this box before filling anything in; most lost marks come from mixing up\nwhich corner holds which number.\n\n## The two passes\n\n### Forward pass — left to right, gives ES and EF\n\n- **ES** for an activity with no predecessors = **0**\n- **ES** otherwise = **max(EF of all immediate predecessors)**\n- **EF = ES + T_E**\n\n**Use max.** An activity cannot start until *every* predecessor has finished, so the latest of them governs.\n\n### Backward pass — right to left, gives LF and LS\n\n- **LF** for the final activity = **total project duration**\n- **LF** otherwise = **min(LS of all immediate successors)**\n- **LS = LF − T_E**\n\n**Use min.** The activity must finish early enough not to delay *any* successor, so the earliest of them governs.\n\n> **The single most common error:** using max on the backward pass. Forward takes the max, backward takes\n> the min. If a backward pass gives an activity more slack than feels right, this is usually why.\n\n### Slack\n\n> **Slack = LS − ES = LF − EF**\n\nBoth forms must agree. If they do not, one of the passes is wrong — a free self-check worth doing on\nevery activity.\n\n### The critical path\n\nThe chain of activities with **slack = 0**, running unbroken from start to end. Its length is the total\nproject duration. Every project has at least one; it may have several in parallel, which matters enormously\nin chapter 09.\n\n## Worked example — the course's standard network\n\n### Input\n\n| Activity | Predecessors | a | m | b | T_E = (a+4m+b)/6 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | none | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 |\n| B | none | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 |\n| C | A | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 |\n| D | A, B | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 |\n| E | C | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 |\n| F | C | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 |\n| G | D, E | 5 | 5 | 5 | 5 |\n| H | F, G | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 |\n\n⚠️ Every triple has **a = m = b**, so each T_E is just that number and **every variance is zero**. This\ntable is for practising the passes, not the statistics. Chapter 10's Z-score example uses different, non-zero\nvariances for the same letters — the two are **not** the same data set.\n\n### Full schedule\n\n| Activity | T_E | Pred. | ES | EF | LS | LF | Slack | Critical? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 2 | none | 0 | 2 | 0 | 2 | **0** | **YES** |\n| B | 3 | none | 0 | 3 | 1 | 4 | 1 | no |\n| C | 2 | A | 2 | 4 | 2 | 4 | **0** | **YES** |\n| D | 4 | A, B | 3 | 7 | 4 | 8 | 1 | no |\n| E | 4 | C | 4 | 8 | 4 | 8 | **0** | **YES** |\n| F | 3 | C | 4 | 7 | 10 | 13 | 6 | no |\n| G | 5 | D, E | 8 | 13 | 8 | 13 | **0** | **YES** |\n| H | 2 | F, G | 13 | 15 | 13 | 15 | **0** | **YES** |\n\n- **Critical path: A → C → E → G → H**\n- **Total duration: 2 + 2 + 4 + 5 + 2 = 15 months**\n\n### How the interesting cells arise\n\n- **D's ES = 3, not 2.** D waits on both A (EF 2) and B (EF 3). max(2, 3) = 3.\n- **G's ES = 8, not 7.** G waits on D (EF 7) and E (EF 8). max(7, 8) = 8. E is the binding one, which is\n  why E is critical and D is not.\n- **H's ES = 13.** H waits on F (EF 7) and G (EF 13). max(7, 13) = 13.\n- **C's LF = 4, not 13.** C feeds both E (LS 4) and F (LS 10). min(4, 10) = 4. E is the tighter successor.\n  Taking the max here would wrongly give C six months of slack.\n- **A's LF = 2.** A feeds C (LS 2) and D (LS 4). min(2, 4) = 2.\n- **F's slack = 6.** F finishes at 7 but is not needed until 13, because G takes five months on the parallel\n  route. F is the slackest activity in the network and the last one you would ever spend money crashing.\n\n### The diagram\n\n```\n            A(2)                C(2)                E(4)\n         ES0  EF2            ES2  EF4            ES4  EF8\n         LS0  LF2            LS2  LF4            LS4  LF8\n          SL 0  ══════════════ SL 0 ══════════════ SL 0\n            ║                   ║  ╲                 ║\n            ║                   ║   ╲                ║\n            ║                   ║    ╲               ║\n            ╚═══╗               ║     ▼              ║\n                ▼               ║    F(3)            ║\n              D(4)              ║  ES4  EF7          ║\n           ES3  EF7             ║  LS10 LF13         ║\n           LS4  LF8             ║   SL 6             ║\n            SL 1                ║     │              ║\n              │                 │     │              ║\n            B(3)                      │              ║\n         ES0  EF3                     │              ║\n         LS1  LF4                     │              ║\n          SL 1                        │              ║\n              └──────────┐            │        ╔═════╝\n                         ▼            │        ▼\n                       G(5) ◄═════════╪════════╝\n                    ES8  EF13         │\n                    LS8  LF13         │\n                     SL 0             │\n                         ║            │\n                         ╚════► H(2) ◄┘\n                            ES13 EF15\n                            LS13 LF15\n                             SL 0          END = 15\n```\n\n`══` marks the critical path. B and D form a slack-1 route into G; F is a slack-6 dead end into H.\n\n## Checks to run before you move on\n\n1. Every activity's **LS − ES equals LF − EF**.\n2. The **critical path has no gaps** from start to end.\n3. The **sum of critical durations equals the project duration** (2+2+4+5+2 = 15 ✓).\n4. **Every activity has slack ≥ 0.** A negative slack means the backward pass started from the wrong total.\n\n## Verification note\n\nThe full table was recomputed from the predecessor list during ingest: forward pass, backward pass and both\nslack formulas. All 40 values in the source guide match. The critical path and the 15-month duration are\nconfirmed.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What are the four schedule values computed for each activity?","a":"Earliest start ES, earliest finish EF, latest start LS, latest finish LF."},{"q":"State the forward-pass rules.","a":"ES = 0 for starting activities, otherwise ES = max(EF of immediate predecessors); EF = ES + T_E."},{"q":"State the backward-pass rules.","a":"LF = project duration for the final activity, otherwise LF = min(LS of immediate successors); LS = LF − T_E."},{"q":"Why max on the forward pass but min on the backward pass?","a":"An activity cannot start until every predecessor is done (latest governs), and must finish early enough not to delay any successor (earliest governs)."},{"q":"Give both formulas for slack.","a":"Slack = LS − ES = LF − EF; the two must agree, which is a built-in check."},{"q":"Define the critical path.","a":"The unbroken chain of zero-slack activities from start to end; its length is the total project duration."},{"q":"In the standard example, what is the critical path and the project duration?","a":"A → C → E → G → H, total 15 months."},{"q":"Why is D's ES = 3 rather than 2?","a":"D follows both A (EF 2) and B (EF 3), and the forward pass takes the maximum."},{"q":"Why is G's ES = 8?","a":"G follows D (EF 7) and E (EF 8); max(7, 8) = 8, so E is the binding predecessor."},{"q":"Why is C's LF = 4 and not 13?","a":"C precedes E (LS 4) and F (LS 10); the backward pass takes the minimum, so the tighter successor E governs."},{"q":"What is F's slack and why is it so large?","a":"6 months — F finishes at 7 but is not needed until 13, because the parallel route through G takes five months."},{"q":"How much slack do B and D each have?","a":"One month each; they form a slack-1 route into G."},{"q":"What does a negative slack indicate?","a":"An error — usually a backward pass started from the wrong project total."},{"q":"Name three checks on a finished CPM table.","a":"Both slack formulas agree; the critical path is unbroken start to end; the critical durations sum to the project duration."},{"q":"Can a project have more than one critical path?","a":"Yes — parallel critical paths are possible, and they change how crashing must be done."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 05"},{"id":"a06","title":"Gantt Charts & Progress Tracking","slides":"2025/26 · week 8","md":"# 06 — Gantt Charts and Progress Tracking (Week 8) · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n_The instructor's exam scope reportedly starts here. The comparison table below is the most likely theory\nquestion in the whole course._\n\n## Network diagram versus Gantt chart\n\nThey answer different questions, which is why a project needs both.\n\n```\nNETWORK (structural dependency view)        GANTT (chronological timeline view)\n   A ──► C ──► E ──► G ──► H                Act │M1 M2 M3 M4 … M13 M14 M15\n    ╲           ▲                            A  │██\n     ╲         ╱                             B  │███┈\n      ──► D ───                              C  │   ██\n                                             D  │   ████┈\n                                             E  │      ████\n                                             F  │      ███┈┈┈┈┈┈┈\n                                             G  │           █████\n                                             H  │                 ██\n                                        legend: █ work   ┈ slack\n```\n\n| | **Network diagram (CPM)** | **Gantt chart** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purpose | Mathematical analysis: slack, dependency logic, critical path | Execution tracking, schedule visualisation, time-phased budgeting |\n| Phase | **Planning** | **Execution and controlling** |\n| Dependencies | Explicit, the primary focus | Secondary — shown with arrows between bar ends |\n| Progress | Static; does not show partial completion well | Dynamic; tracks percentage completion inside the bars |\n| Milestones | Event nodes | **Black diamonds ◆** |\n| Time axis | None — position carries no date meaning | **Yes** — horizontal axis is the calendar |\n\n**The one-line distinction:** the network tells you **what depends on what**; the Gantt tells you **what\nhappens when**. The network has no time axis at all, which is why it cannot show progress, and the Gantt\nshows dependency only as an afterthought, which is why it cannot compute slack.\n\n**Slack is visible on a Gantt chart** as the gap between where a bar ends and where its successor begins —\ndrawn above as `┈`. F carries six months of it, matching the chapter-05 table.\n\n## Progress tracking: the \"filling a bottle\" method\n\nDuring the controlling phase, each activity bar is shaded in proportion to the work completed.\n\n```\nActivity F progress:\n┌────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  completed work  40%   │          remaining work  60%          │\n│  ████████████████████  │  ·································  │\n└────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nA bar shaded 40% means 40% of that activity's work is done and 60% remains. The metaphor is deliberate:\nthe bar fills linearly like liquid in a bottle.\n\n**The assumption worth stating in an exam:** this treats progress as **linear in time**, so 40% of the\nduration elapsed is read as 40% of the work done. That is an approximation. It is adequate for tracking and\nfor time-phased budgeting, and it is what makes the budget baseline in chapter 07 possible, but it is not\ntrue of every activity.\n\n## Milestones\n\nMarked with a **black diamond ◆**. A milestone has **zero duration** — it is a moment, not a span, so it\ngets a symbol rather than a bar. Typical milestones are approvals, handovers and phase gates.\n\nNote the parallel with chapter 04's dummy activity: both carry zero duration, but a dummy exists for\n*logic* in an AOA network, while a milestone exists for *visibility* on a Gantt chart. They are not the\nsame thing and an exam could reasonably test the difference.\n\n## What the Gantt chart feeds\n\nThe Gantt chart is the bridge between the schedule and the money. Once activities sit on a calendar, the\ncosts attached to them also sit on a calendar, which produces the time-phased budget baseline of chapter\n07. That is why the order in the course is network → Gantt → budget and never the reverse.\n\n## Verification note\n\nThe slack windows shown in the Gantt sketch (B and D one month each, F six months) were checked against the\nverified chapter-05 table and agree.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What is the primary purpose of a network diagram versus a Gantt chart?","a":"The network is for mathematical analysis — slack, dependencies, critical path; the Gantt is for execution tracking and schedule visualisation."},{"q":"Which project phase does each belong to?","a":"The network diagram to planning; the Gantt chart to execution and controlling."},{"q":"Give the one-line distinction between the two.","a":"The network shows what depends on what; the Gantt shows what happens when."},{"q":"Why can a network diagram not show progress?","a":"It has no time axis — node position carries no date meaning."},{"q":"How are dependencies shown on a Gantt chart?","a":"Secondarily, with arrows connecting the ends of bars."},{"q":"How are milestones represented on a Gantt chart?","a":"By black diamonds ◆."},{"q":"What is the duration of a milestone?","a":"Zero — it is a moment in time, so it gets a symbol rather than a bar."},{"q":"Distinguish a milestone from a dummy activity.","a":"Both have zero duration, but a dummy preserves logic in an AOA network while a milestone marks visibility on a Gantt chart."},{"q":"Describe the \"filling a bottle\" tracking method.","a":"The activity bar is shaded in proportion to work completed — a bar 40% shaded means 40% done, 60% remaining."},{"q":"What assumption does bar shading make?","a":"That progress is linear in time, so elapsed duration is proportional to work completed."},{"q":"How does slack appear on a Gantt chart?","a":"As the gap between a bar's end and the start of its successor."},{"q":"Why must the Gantt chart come before the budget baseline?","a":"Costs can only be spread across a calendar once the activities themselves sit on one."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 06"},{"id":"a07","title":"Resources & Time-Phased Budgeting ★","slides":"2025/26 · week 9","md":"# 07 — Resource Allocation and Time-Phased Budgeting (Week 9) · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n_How a schedule becomes a cash-flow plan. The master table below was recomputed during ingest and every\none of its 15 cumulative figures is correct._\n\n## The cost baseline chain\n\n```\n   project scope\n        │\n        ▼\n   work breakdown structure (WBS)\n        │\n        ▼\n   activity schedule (Gantt chart)\n        │\n        ▼\n   resource assignment (labour, materials, rates)\n        │\n        ▼\n   time-phased budget baseline (the S-curve)\n```\n\nEach step is strictly dependent on the one above it. You cannot price what you have not scheduled, and you\ncannot schedule what you have not decomposed.\n\n1. **Scope definition** — identify all required deliverables.\n2. **Resource quantification** — map people, materials and machinery to individual activities.\n3. **Cost rate application** — multiply quantities by unit prices or monthly labour rates.\n4. **Time integration** — align each cost with the schedule (using ES or LS) to produce a monthly baseline.\n\n**Why ES or LS is a real choice.** Scheduling a cost at an activity's *earliest* start spends the money as\nsoon as possible; scheduling at the *latest* start defers it. For activities with slack the two give\ngenuinely different cash-flow curves, so the baseline depends on which convention you adopt. For critical\nactivities, ES = LS, so there is no choice to make.\n\n**The S-curve.** Plotting the cumulative column produces a shallow-steep-shallow S: slow spending during\nmobilisation, rapid spending through the main construction, tapering at closeout. In the table below the\nmid-project months M9 and M12 each cost 32,000 KM, against 1,000 KM in M1 and 2,000 KM in M15 — that\ncontrast *is* the S.\n\n## Internal labour versus external contracts\n\n| | **Internal labour** | **External subcontract** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Basis | Headcount × monthly wage rate | Lump sum or milestone payment |\n| Example rates | 1,000 KM/month per technician; 2,000 KM/month per engineer | 5,000 KM architectural fee on document completion |\n| Part-time | Fractional allocation: 0.5 of a worker = 1,000 KM/month | n/a |\n| Cash-flow shape | Smooth, spread over the activity's duration | A spike in a single month |\n\nThe fractional-allocation example repays attention: 0.5 of a worker costing 1,000 KM/month implies a\n2,000 KM/month engineer at half time, not a technician.\n\n## Unit prices used in the worked example\n\n| Material | Unit price | Worked quantity |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bricks | 5 KM per piece | 100 pieces = 500 KM |\n| Rebar steel | 3 KM per kg | 2,000 kg = 6,000 KM |\n| Concrete | 200 KM per m³ | 50 m³ = 10,000 KM |\n\nEach is a plain quantity × rate multiplication. Expect an exam to supply its own rates and quantities and\nask for the monthly total.\n\n## The time-phased cash-flow master table\n\n| Month | Activity / resource | Labour cost | Material cost | Monthly total | Cumulative |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | A (ext. architect) + B (admin) | 1,000 | 0 | 1,000 | **1,000** |\n| M2 | A (5k lump) + B (1k admin) | 6,000 | 0 | 6,000 | **7,000** |\n| M3 | B (admin) + C (taxes) | 1,000 | 500 | 1,500 | **8,500** |\n| M4 | C (permit) + D (prep) | 2,000 | 20,000 | 22,000 | **30,500** |\n| M5 | D (site prep) | 10,000 | 300 | 10,300 | **40,800** |\n| M6 | D (procurement, bricks) | 2,000 | 20,000 | 22,000 | **62,800** |\n| M7 | D (concrete and rebar) | 2,000 | 10,000 | 12,000 | **74,800** |\n| M8 | E (frame construction) | 10,000 | 200 | 10,200 | **85,000** |\n| M9 | E + F (framing and finish) | 12,000 | 20,000 | 32,000 | **117,000** |\n| M10 | E + F | 12,000 | 10,000 | 22,000 | **139,000** |\n| M11 | G (masonry) | 10,000 | 12,000 | 22,000 | **161,000** |\n| M12 | G (masonry) | 20,000 | 12,000 | 32,000 | **193,000** |\n| M13 | G (masonry) | 10,000 | 4,000 | 14,000 | **207,000** |\n| M14 | H (final clean and handover) | 5,000 | 2,000 | 7,000 | **214,000** |\n| M15 | H (final closeout) | 2,000 | 0 | 2,000 | **216,000** |\n\n**Total project budget: 216,000 KM over 15 months.**\n\n### Reading the table\n\n- The **15 months match the project duration** from chapter 05, and the activity letters follow the same\n  network. The budget is the chapter-05 schedule with money attached.\n- **M2 spikes to 6,000 KM** because the architect's 5,000 KM lump sum lands in a single month on top of\n  1,000 KM of admin. That is the external-contract cash-flow shape in action.\n- **Materials dominate the middle.** M4, M6 and M9 are each 20,000 KM of materials against 2,000 to 12,000\n  KM of labour. Procurement, not wages, drives the peak.\n- **M12 is the most expensive month** at 32,000 KM, tied with M9.\n- **Spending tapers at the end**: 7,000 then 2,000 KM, because closeout is labour-light.\n\n### The two exam skills\n\n1. **Monthly total = labour + materials.** Every row above satisfies it.\n2. **Cumulative = previous cumulative + this month's total.** All 15 rows satisfy it.\n\nThose two columns are the whole exercise. An exam will give you activity costs and a schedule and ask for\nthe time-phased table, and the marks are in getting the running total right.\n\n## Verification note\n\nRecomputed during ingest. Every monthly total equals labour + materials, and the full cumulative column\nreproduces exactly, ending at 216,000 KM. No discrepancies.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"List the five steps from scope to cost baseline.","a":"Scope → work breakdown structure → activity schedule (Gantt) → resource assignment → time-phased budget baseline."},{"q":"What are the four stages of building the baseline?","a":"Define scope, quantify resources, apply cost rates, integrate with time."},{"q":"Why does the choice of ES or LS matter for the budget?","a":"For activities with slack it shifts when money is spent; for critical activities ES = LS, so no choice exists."},{"q":"What shape does the cumulative budget curve take, and why?","a":"An S-curve — slow during mobilisation, steep through main construction, tapering at closeout."},{"q":"How is internal labour costed?","a":"Headcount times monthly wage rate, with fractional allocations for part-time work."},{"q":"What are the example monthly rates?","a":"1,000 KM per technician, 2,000 KM per engineer; 0.5 allocation of an engineer is 1,000 KM/month."},{"q":"How is external subcontracted work costed?","a":"As lump sums or milestone payments, for example a 5,000 KM architectural fee paid on document completion."},{"q":"How do internal and external costs differ in cash-flow shape?","a":"Internal labour spreads smoothly across the duration; an external lump sum spikes in a single month."},{"q":"Give the three unit prices used in the example.","a":"Bricks 5 KM/piece, rebar 3 KM/kg, concrete 200 KM/m³."},{"q":"What is 2,000 kg of rebar and 50 m³ of concrete worth?","a":"6,000 KM and 10,000 KM respectively."},{"q":"What is the total budget and duration of the worked project?","a":"216,000 KM over 15 months."},{"q":"How is each month's cumulative figure obtained?","a":"Previous cumulative plus this month's total, where the monthly total is labour plus materials."},{"q":"Why does M2 cost 6,000 KM?","a":"The architect's 5,000 KM lump sum falls in that month on top of 1,000 KM of admin labour."},{"q":"What drives the mid-project cost peak?","a":"Materials — M4, M6 and M9 each carry 20,000 KM of materials, far above the labour in those months."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 07"},{"id":"a08","title":"Risk Management","slides":"2025/26 · week 10","md":"# 08 — Risk Management (Week 10) · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n_Mostly theory, and the response strategies are a near-certain exam question. The symmetry between threat\nand opportunity responses is the thing to hold on to._\n\n## Definition\n\n> A **risk** is an uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, has a **positive or negative** impact\n> on project objectives.\n\nTwo halves of that definition carry marks. It is **uncertain** — a certainty is not a risk, it is a\nconstraint. And it can be **positive**: an opportunity is as much a risk as a threat. Most students only\nremember the negative half.\n\n## The response strategies\n\n```\n                      uncertain event\n                            │\n          ┌─────────────────┴─────────────────┐\n          ▼                                   ▼\n   negative outcome                    positive outcome\n       (THREAT)                         (OPPORTUNITY)\n          │                                   │\n  ┌───────┼───────┬────────┐          ┌───────┼────────┐\n  ▼       ▼       ▼        ▼          ▼       ▼        ▼\nAvoid  Transfer Mitigate Accept      Take  Enhance   Share\n```\n\n### Threats — four responses\n\n| Strategy | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Avoid** | Change the plan so the risk condition cannot arise | Leave 15 minutes early so missing the bus is impossible |\n| **Transfer** | Shift the financial or operational impact to a third party | Insurance, warranties, fixed-price subcontracts |\n| **Mitigate** | Act early to reduce the probability or the impact | Install flood barriers; call a taxi after a 5-minute delay |\n| **Accept** | Acknowledge it and absorb the impact if it happens | Used when no response is cost-effective |\n\n**Avoid versus mitigate** is the distinction most often tested. Avoid **eliminates** the risk — afterwards\nthe probability is zero. Mitigate **reduces** it — the risk still exists, just smaller. In the bus example,\nleaving early avoids; calling a taxi once already delayed mitigates, because the delay has already happened\nand you are limiting its consequence.\n\n**Transfer does not reduce the risk.** The event is just as likely; somebody else now pays for it. That is\nwhy insurance is transfer and not mitigation.\n\n### The insurance boundary\n\nA specific point from the guide, worth keeping because it is concrete:\n\nInsurers readily cover **individual assets** (buildings, equipment) and **routine liabilities** (auto,\nhealth). They generally **refuse** systematic, catastrophic or unquantifiable events — widespread crop\nflooding, wildfires, hurricanes, force majeure generally.\n\nThe reason is that transfer only works when the insurer can pool many independent risks. A hurricane\ndamages every policyholder in the region at once, so there is no pool to spread it across. **Transfer\nfails for correlated risks**, which is why those are retained, mitigated, or avoided instead.\n\n### Opportunities — three responses\n\n| Strategy | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Take** (exploit) | Commit resources to make sure the opportunity is realised | Commercialise a new technology discovered during R&D |\n| **Enhance** | Increase the probability or the positive impact | Add resource to make the favourable outcome more likely |\n| **Share** | Partner with someone better placed to capture the benefit | License an accidental patent to a firm with manufacturing and distribution |\n\n**The mirror:** take ↔ avoid (both make an outcome certain), enhance ↔ mitigate (both change probability\nand impact, in opposite directions), share ↔ transfer (both involve a third party). Accept is the one with\nno opposite — it is used for both, since you can also simply accept an opportunity and take it if it\narrives.\n\n**Share connects straight back to chapter 01.** Licensing an accidental invention to a firm with the right\ninfrastructure is both the *share* response and the *open innovation* model. Same action, two vocabularies.\n\n## Qualitative versus quantitative analysis\n\n| | **Qualitative** | **Quantitative** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data type | Categorical / ordinal | Numerical / monetary |\n| Output | High / medium / low; red / yellow / green | Days, KM, euros |\n| Tools | **Risk breakdown structure (RBS)**, **probability and impact matrix** | **Probability trees** (expected monetary value), **data envelopment / analogue benchmarking** |\n| Purpose | Prioritise and screen | Measure the actual impact |\n\n- **Risk breakdown structure** — a hierarchical decomposition of the *sources* of risk, built the same way\n  a work breakdown structure decomposes work.\n- **Probability and impact matrix** — scores each risk on likelihood and severity and ranks the results.\n- **Probability trees** — decision trees that compute **expected monetary value**: multiply each outcome by\n  its probability and sum.\n- **Data envelopment / analogue method** — benchmarking against statistical data from comparable past\n  projects.\n\nThe practical sequence is qualitative **first** to screen a long list down, then quantitative on the few\nthat survive, because quantitative analysis is expensive and only worth doing on risks that matter.\n\n## Verification note\n\nNo numeric examples appear in the source for this week, so nothing required recomputation. Note that the\nguide's numbering of the qualitative/quantitative tool lists is garbled in the original (restarting at 1\nmid-list); the content is reproduced above in a corrected structure.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Define a risk.","a":"An uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, has a positive or negative impact on project objectives."},{"q":"What two features of that definition are most often forgotten?","a":"That it must be uncertain, and that it may be positive as well as negative."},{"q":"Name the four threat-response strategies.","a":"Avoid, transfer, mitigate, accept."},{"q":"Name the three opportunity-response strategies.","a":"Take (exploit), enhance, share."},{"q":"Distinguish avoid from mitigate.","a":"Avoid changes the plan so the risk cannot occur at all; mitigate reduces its probability or impact while the risk still exists."},{"q":"What does transfer actually achieve?","a":"It shifts the financial or operational impact to a third party; the event remains just as likely."},{"q":"Give three examples of transfer.","a":"Insurance, warranties, fixed-price subcontracts."},{"q":"Which risks will insurers generally not cover, and why?","a":"Systematic catastrophic events such as widespread flooding, wildfires and hurricanes — correlated risks hit every policyholder at once, so there is no pool to spread them across."},{"q":"When is accept the right response?","a":"When no cost-effective response exists, so the impact is simply absorbed."},{"q":"Give the bus example for avoid versus mitigate.","a":"Leaving 15 minutes early avoids missing the bus; calling a taxi after a 5-minute delay mitigates the consequence."},{"q":"What does the share strategy involve?","a":"Partnering with an external entity better equipped to capture the opportunity, for example licensing a patent to a firm with distribution."},{"q":"How do the threat and opportunity strategies mirror each other?","a":"Take ↔ avoid, enhance ↔ mitigate, share ↔ transfer; accept has no opposite."},{"q":"What data does qualitative analysis use, and what does it produce?","a":"Categorical or ordinal data, producing high/medium/low or red/yellow/green rankings."},{"q":"Name the two qualitative tools.","a":"The risk breakdown structure and the probability and impact matrix."},{"q":"Name the two quantitative tools.","a":"Probability trees for expected monetary value, and data envelopment / analogue benchmarking."},{"q":"What is a risk breakdown structure?","a":"A hierarchical decomposition of the sources of risk, built like a WBS but for risks."},{"q":"Why run qualitative analysis before quantitative?","a":"Quantitative analysis is expensive, so the qualitative screen narrows the list to the risks worth measuring."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 08"},{"id":"a09","title":"Crashing & the Least-Cost Method ★","slides":"2025/26 · weeks 11 & 13","md":"# 09 — Crashing and the Least-Cost Method (Weeks 11 and 13) · ARCHIVE 2025/26 ★\n\n_The second big calculation chapter, and the one with the famous trap. All eight crash slopes in the\nsource table were recomputed during ingest and every one is correct._\n\n## What crashing is\n\nShortening the project by throwing extra resources at it — overtime, extra crews, more equipment. It is\nthe iron triangle from chapter 01 in action:\n\n> **Shorter time ⟹ higher direct cost.**\n\n```\n  direct\n   cost\n     ▲\n  C₂ ┤ - - - - - - - - - - - ● crash point (T₂, C₂)\n     │                      ╱\n     │                     ╱  ← slope = cost per unit of time saved\n     │                    ╱\n  C₁ ┤ - - - - - - - - - ● normal point (T₁, C₁)\n     │                   │\n     └───────────────────┴──────────────► time\n     0                  T₂              T₁\n```\n\nNote the axis direction: moving **left** along the time axis (shorter) means moving **up** the cost axis.\n\n## The crash cost slope\n\n> **Slope = (Crash cost − Normal cost) / (Normal time − Crash time) = (C₂ − C₁) / (T₁ − T₂)**\n\nThe units are money per unit of time, for example KM per month. It is the price of buying one month.\n\n**Watch the denominator order.** Cost is new minus old, but time is **old minus new**. They are deliberately\nopposite, because crashing raises cost and lowers time, and this ordering keeps the slope positive. Writing\n(T₂ − T₁) gives a negative slope and will cost you the question.\n\n## The three rules\n\n1. **Crash only critical path activities.** Shortening a non-critical activity consumes its slack and\n   changes nothing about the project duration. In the chapter-05 network, crashing F (six months of slack)\n   buys literally nothing.\n2. **Respect technical and legal constraints.** Some activities cannot be crashed at any price:\n   - concrete curing needs a mandatory 3-to-6-week chemical hydration period;\n   - bread baking needs its fixed 30-minute thermal process;\n   - a legal complaint window has a statutory 15-day wait.\n   Money does not accelerate chemistry or statute.\n3. **Respect maximum crash limits.** No activity can go below its technical crash time T₂. The maximum\n   saving available from one activity is **T₁ − T₂**.\n\n## The least-cost method\n\nAmong the eligible critical activities, crash the one with the **lowest slope** first. Then **recompute the\ncritical path**, because crashing can make a previously non-critical path become critical, after which the\ncheapest next move may be somewhere else entirely.\n\n## The parallel critical path trap\n\nThis is the point of the week-11 material.\n\n```\n        ┌──► activity A (slope 2,000 KM) ──┐\n        │                                  │\n  start ┤                                  ├──► node D (slope 3,000 KM) ──► end\n        │                                  │\n        └──► activity B (slope 1,800 KM) ──┘\n```\n\nTwo parallel critical paths. Crashing A alone does **not** shorten the project, because the path through B\nis still its original length. To gain one unit of time you must either shorten **both** parallel paths or\nshorten something **common** to both.\n\n| Option | Action | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Crash A **and** B together | 2,000 + 1,800 = **3,800 KM** |\n| 2 | Crash the shared node D | **3,000 KM** |\n\n**Decision: option 2**, at 3,000 KM, because 3,000 < 3,800.\n\nThe lesson: with parallel critical paths, the naive \"pick the lowest slope\" rule fails. B has the lowest\nindividual slope at 1,800 KM, but crashing B alone achieves nothing. A **shared** activity on a higher slope\ncan be cheaper than a pair of parallel ones on lower slopes, because you pay once instead of twice.\n\n## The worked crashing table\n\n| Activity | Critical? | Normal T₁ | Crash T₂ | Max crash | Normal C₁ | Crash C₂ | **Slope** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | **YES** | 2 | 1 | 1 | 22,000 | 22,750 | **750** |\n| B | no | 3 | 2 | 1 | 30,000 | 34,000 | 4,000 |\n| C | **YES** | 2 | 1 | 1 | 36,000 | 37,000 | **1,000** |\n| D | no | 4 | 1 | 3 | 48,000 | 97,000 | 16,333 |\n| E | **YES** | 4 | 2 | 2 | 56,000 | 58,000 | **1,000** |\n| F | no | 3 | 1 | 2 | 30,000 | 49,000 | 9,500 |\n| G | **YES** | 5 | 4 | 1 | 80,000 | 84,500 | **4,500** |\n| H | **YES** | 2 | 1 | 1 | 16,000 | 19,000 | **3,000** |\n\nSlopes in KM per month. The critical activities are A, C, E, G, H — the same critical path as chapter 05.\n\n**First crash: activity A, at 750 KM per month**, the lowest slope on the critical path, saving one month.\n\n### What the table teaches\n\n- **The cheapest activity overall is not always eligible.** All of A's competitors for lowest slope are\n  critical too here, but note that B at 4,000 and D at 16,333 are non-critical and therefore irrelevant no\n  matter what they cost.\n- **E offers the most time.** It can give two months where most others give one. At 1,000 KM/month that is\n  2,000 KM for two months — often the better buy than A's single month, if more than one month is needed.\n- **A is exhausted after one month.** Its max crash is 1, so a second month must come from somewhere else —\n  but *not* simply from the next-cheapest critical activity, as the worked continuation below shows.\n- **Crashing in sequence:** recompute the critical path after **every** crash. B and D sit on a slack-1\n  route that becomes critical immediately, which changes the answer for the second month.\n\n> ⚠️ **The recompute step is where marks are lost.** After crashing A by one month, the A→C→E→G→H path is\n> 14 months while the B→D→G→H path is 3+4+5+2 = 14 months. **They are now equal, so the network has two\n> critical paths** — and from that point the parallel-path trap above applies.\n\n### Crashing the second month — the trap in the real network\n\nThis is not in the source guide, but it follows directly from the verified table and is the most likely\nway an exam would extend the problem. The network has four paths in total:\n\n| Path | Normal | After A −1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| A→C→E→G→H | **15** | **14** ← critical |\n| B→D→G→H | 14 | **14** ← now critical too |\n| A→D→G→H | 13 | 12 |\n| A→C→F→H | 9 | 8 |\n\nWith two critical paths, sort the still-crashable activities by where they sit:\n\n- **Shared by both paths:** G (4,500) and H (3,000)\n- **Only on A→C→E→G→H:** C (1,000) and E (1,000)\n- **Only on B→D→G→H:** B (4,000) and D (16,333)\n\n| Option | Action | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Crash one shared activity | **H** | **3,000 KM** |\n| Crash one from each path | E (1,000) + B (4,000) | 5,000 KM |\n\n**Buy the second month from H, at 3,000 KM.** Note how badly the naive rule misleads here: C and E are the\ncheapest activities left at 1,000 KM each, and **both are useless on their own**, because neither touches\nthe B→D→G→H path. Paying 3,000 once beats paying 1,000 and 4,000 separately.\n\n## Overtime economics (week 13)\n\nHow the extra resource is actually bought:\n\n- **Human capital scaling** — add specialised personnel, or add workers to shifts.\n- **Machinery mobilisation** — extra excavators, mixers or tools to run tasks in parallel.\n\n**Overtime rates:**\n\n- A regular shift is **8 hours** (for example 08:30 to 17:00).\n- Work beyond 8 hours attracts a premium of typically **30% to 50% above the regular hourly wage**.\n- Some institutional settings (university faculty, for instance) use **monthly hour targets** of around\n  130 to 160 hours with no overtime pay. In commercial project environments overtime always costs real\n  cash, and that surcharge is exactly what makes the crash slope steeper than the normal cost line.\n\nThat last sentence is the link back to the formula: the slope is not an abstraction, it is the premium\nrate showing up in the arithmetic.\n\n## Verification note\n\nAll eight slopes recomputed during ingest; every value matches the source, including D's 16,333 (49,000 / 3,\nrounded). Activity A is confirmed as the lowest-slope critical activity. The observation that the network\ndevelops a second critical path after one month of crashing was derived during ingest from the verified\nchapter-05 schedule and is **not** stated in the source guide.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What is project crashing?","a":"Shortening the project duration by adding resources — overtime, personnel, equipment — at increased direct cost."},{"q":"State the crash cost slope formula.","a":"Slope = (Crash cost − Normal cost) / (Normal time − Crash time) = (C₂ − C₁)/(T₁ − T₂)."},{"q":"Why are the cost and time subtractions in opposite order?","a":"Crashing raises cost and lowers time, so opposite ordering keeps the slope positive."},{"q":"What are the units of the slope?","a":"Money per unit time, for example KM per month — the price of buying one month."},{"q":"Why is crashing a non-critical activity pointless?","a":"It only consumes that activity's slack; the project duration is set by the critical path."},{"q":"Give three activities that cannot be crashed at any price.","a":"Concrete curing (3–6 weeks of hydration), bread baking (fixed 30-minute thermal process), a statutory 15-day legal complaint window."},{"q":"What is the maximum time one activity can contribute?","a":"T₁ − T₂, its maximum crash limit."},{"q":"State the least-cost rule.","a":"Among eligible critical activities, crash the one with the lowest slope first — then recompute the critical path."},{"q":"Why must the critical path be recomputed after each crash?","a":"Crashing can make a previously non-critical path become critical, changing which activity is cheapest to crash next."},{"q":"What is the parallel critical path trap?","a":"With two parallel critical paths, crashing one path alone does not shorten the project, because the other path keeps its original length."},{"q":"In the trap example, which option wins and why?","a":"Crashing the shared node D at 3,000 KM, which beats crashing A and B together at 2,000 + 1,800 = 3,800 KM."},{"q":"What does the trap teach about lowest slope?","a":"The lowest individual slope can be useless — B at 1,800 achieves nothing alone; a shared activity at a higher slope is paid once instead of twice."},{"q":"In the worked table, which activity is crashed first and at what cost?","a":"Activity A, at 750 KM per month, the lowest slope among the critical activities."},{"q":"Which activity offers the most crashable time, and at what rate?","a":"E, two months at 1,000 KM per month."},{"q":"Why can A only be used once?","a":"Its maximum crash is one month, so further savings must come from the next cheapest critical activity."},{"q":"How long is a regular shift and what premium does overtime attract?","a":"Eight hours; overtime typically costs 30% to 50% above the regular hourly wage."},{"q":"How does overtime relate to the crash slope?","a":"The premium rate is what makes the crash cost exceed the normal cost, so it is the slope's physical source."},{"q":"After crashing A by one month in the worked network, what happens?","a":"The project reaches 14 months and a second critical path appears — B→D→G→H is also 14, so two paths are now critical."},{"q":"Which activities are shared by the two critical paths after that first crash?","a":"G (slope 4,500) and H (slope 3,000)."},{"q":"Where should the second month be bought, and why?","a":"From H at 3,000 KM — crashing one activity from each path instead would cost E 1,000 plus B 4,000 = 5,000 KM."},{"q":"Why are C and E useless for the second month despite costing only 1,000 KM each?","a":"Neither lies on the B→D→G→H path, so crashing them leaves that path at 14 months and the project unchanged."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 09"},{"id":"a10","title":"Schedule Uncertainty & Z-Scores ★","slides":"2025/26 · week 12","md":"# 10 — Schedule Uncertainty and Z-Scores (Week 12) · ARCHIVE 2025/26 ★\n\n_The third calculation chapter and the last link in the chain. It answers the question a sponsor actually\nasks: **\"what are the chances we finish by then?\"**_\n\n## Why the project total is normally distributed\n\nIndividual activity durations follow an **asymmetric Beta** distribution (chapter 03). But the project\nduration is the **sum** of many such activities, and by the **Central Limit Theorem** a sum of many\nindependent random variables tends toward a **symmetric normal** distribution.\n\n> Activities: skewed Beta. Project total: symmetric normal.\n\nThat shift is what licenses the Z-score method. Do not use a Z-score on a single activity.\n\n```\n  probability\n    density                  mean T_E = 15\n       ▲                           │\n       │                      ███████████\n       │                   █████████████████\n       │                ███████████████████████\n       │             █████████████████████████████  T_s = 16\n       │          ███████████████████████████████│██\n       └──────────┬────────────────┬─────────────┼────► time\n                 −3σ              mean           +3σ\n                              Z = 0.57 → 71.57%\n```\n\n## The formulas\n\n**Project variance — critical path activities only:**\n\n> **σ²_p = Σ σ²_critical**\n\n⚠️ **The trap.** Do **not** add the variances of non-critical activities. The project duration is set by the\ncritical path, so only its activities contribute uncertainty to the total. Adding F's variance in the\nworked example would be wrong, no matter how uncertain F is.\n\nVariances add; standard deviations do not. Always sum the σ² values, then take the root **once** at the end.\n\n**Project standard deviation:**\n\n> **σ_p = √(σ²_p)**\n\n**The Z-score transformation:**\n\n> **Z = (T_s − T_E) / σ_p**\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| T_s | the **scheduled** target completion date you are asked about |\n| T_E | the **expected** project duration along the critical path |\n| σ_p | the project standard deviation |\n\nThen look Z up in a standard normal table; the value read off is **P(T ≤ T_s)**, the probability of\nfinishing on or before the target.\n\n**Reading the sign:** a positive Z means the target is later than expected, so the probability exceeds\n50%. A negative Z means the target is earlier than expected, so the probability is below 50%. Check your\nanswer against that before writing it down.\n\n## Worked example\n\nCritical path **A → C → E → G → H**, with T_E = 15 months from chapter 05.\n\n### Step 1 — activity variances along the critical path\n\n| Activity | σ² |\n|---|---|\n| A | 0.11 |\n| C | 0.11 |\n| E | 1.00 |\n| G | 1.78 |\n| H | 0.11 |\n\n⚠️ **These do not come from the chapter-05 table.** That table has a = m = b for every activity, which\ngives variance zero everywhere. The week-12 example quietly uses a richer data set for the same letters.\nWorking backwards through σ = (b − a)/6, these variances imply spreads of b − a = 2 for A, C and H, 6 for\nE, and 8 for G. If an exam gives you a/m/b values, compute the variances from them and ignore these.\n\n### Step 2 — project variance\n\n> σ²_p = 0.11 + 0.11 + 1.00 + 1.78 + 0.11 = **3.11 months²**\n\n### Step 3 — project standard deviation\n\n> σ_p = √3.11 = **1.76 months**\n\n### Scenario A — target 16 months\n\n1. **Z = (16 − 15) / 1.76 = 0.57**\n2. Z-table, row 0.5, column 0.07 → **0.7157**\n3. **P(T ≤ 16) = 71.57%**\n\nThere is a 71.57% chance of finishing within 16 months. Sanity check: the target is later than expected, Z\nis positive, probability above 50%. ✓\n\n### Scenario B — target 13 months (the instructor's homework)\n\n1. **Z = (13 − 15) / 1.76 = −1.14**\n2. Z-table, row −1.1, column 0.04 → **0.1271**\n3. **P(T ≤ 13) = 12.71%**\n\nOnly a 12.71% chance of finishing within 13 months. Sanity check: target earlier than expected, Z negative,\nprobability well below 50%. ✓\n\n> ⚠️ **A rounding subtlety that changes the answer.** √3.11 = 1.7635, not exactly 1.76. If you keep full\n> precision, Z = −2 / 1.7635 = **−1.134**, which rounds to **−1.13** and gives **12.92%**. The instructor's\n> 12.71% comes from rounding σ_p to **1.76 first** and then dividing, giving −1.1364 → −1.14.\n>\n> **Follow the instructor's convention: round σ_p to two decimals before computing Z.** Both answers are\n> defensible arithmetic, but only one matches the answer key. Scenario A is unaffected — it gives Z = 0.57\n> either way.\n\n## How to use a Z-table\n\nThe table is **provided in the exam**, so the skill is reading it, not memorising it.\n\n1. Compute Z and round to **two decimal places**.\n2. The **row** is the value to one decimal (0.5, or −1.1).\n3. The **column** is the second decimal (0.07, or 0.04).\n4. The cell is the cumulative probability **P(Z ≤ z)**.\n\nFor a \"what is the probability of finishing **later** than T_s\" question, take **1 − the table value**. For\n16 months that would be 1 − 0.7157 = 28.43%.\n\n## Running it backwards\n\nAn exam may invert the question: *what completion date gives 90% confidence?* Rearrange:\n\n> **T_s = T_E + Z · σ_p**\n\nLook up the Z for the probability you want (Z ≈ 1.28 for 90%), then T_s = 15 + 1.28 × 1.76 = **17.25\nmonths**. The structure is the same formula solved for a different unknown.\n\n## Verification note\n\nRecomputed during ingest. The variance sum (3.11) and σ_p (1.7635 ≈ 1.76) are correct, and Scenario A's\nZ = 0.567 → 0.57 → 0.7157 is confirmed exactly. Scenario B is the one genuine discrepancy found anywhere\nin this guide: the instructor's −1.14 / 12.71% requires rounding σ_p before dividing, where full precision\ngives −1.13 / 12.92%. Both the table values 0.7157, 0.1271 and 0.1292 were checked against the normal CDF\nand are accurate.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Why is total project duration normally distributed when activities are not?","a":"By the Central Limit Theorem, a sum of many independent random variables tends to a symmetric normal distribution even when the components are skewed."},{"q":"Which distribution applies to a single activity, and which to the project total?","a":"Asymmetric Beta for the activity; symmetric normal for the project total."},{"q":"State the project variance formula.","a":"σ²_p = Σ σ² over the critical path activities only."},{"q":"What is the trap in computing project variance?","a":"Including non-critical activities — only the critical path contributes to the project total's uncertainty."},{"q":"Can you add standard deviations directly?","a":"No — sum the variances, then take the square root once at the end."},{"q":"State the Z-score formula and define its terms.","a":"Z = (T_s − T_E)/σ_p, where T_s is the scheduled target, T_E the expected critical-path duration, σ_p the project standard deviation."},{"q":"What does the Z-table value represent?","a":"P(T ≤ T_s), the probability of finishing on or before the target."},{"q":"What does the sign of Z tell you?","a":"Positive means the target is later than expected so probability exceeds 50%; negative means earlier, so below 50%."},{"q":"In the worked example, what is the project variance and standard deviation?","a":"σ²_p = 0.11+0.11+1.00+1.78+0.11 = 3.11 months²; σ_p = √3.11 = 1.76 months."},{"q":"Compute the probability of finishing within 16 months.","a":"Z = (16−15)/1.76 = 0.57; the table gives 0.7157, so 71.57%."},{"q":"Compute the probability of finishing within 13 months.","a":"Z = (13−15)/1.76 = −1.14; the table gives 0.1271, so 12.71%."},{"q":"What rounding convention does the instructor use, and why does it matter?","a":"Round σ_p to two decimals (1.76) before computing Z — full precision would give Z = −1.13 and 12.92% instead of 12.71%."},{"q":"Why do the week-12 variances not match the week-6 activity table?","a":"The week-6 table has a = m = b, giving zero variance; week 12 silently uses a different data set for the same activity letters."},{"q":"How do you find the probability of finishing *later* than the target?","a":"Take 1 minus the table value, for example 1 − 0.7157 = 28.43%."},{"q":"How do you find the date for a given confidence level?","a":"Rearrange to T_s = T_E + Z·σ_p; for 90%, Z ≈ 1.28 gives 15 + 1.28×1.76 ≈ 17.25 months."},{"q":"How do you read a Z of −1.14 from the table?","a":"Row −1.1, column 0.04, giving 0.1271."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 10"},{"id":"a11","title":"Synthesis & Exam Rules","slides":"2025/26 · weeks 14–15","md":"# 11 — Synthesis and Exam Rules (Weeks 14–15) · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n_No new theory. This is the chapter that tells you how the pieces connect and what the instructor\nreportedly said about the exam._\n\n⚠️ Every \"instructor says\" item below comes from the **2025/26** run. Confirm it this year before relying\non it.\n\n## The integrated workflow\n\n```\n   PERT (a, m, b) ──► T_E and σ² ──► AON network diagram\n                                              │\n                                              ▼\n   time-phased budget ◄── Gantt baseline ◄── CPM forward/backward pass\n            │                                     │\n            ▼                                     ▼\n   crashing: least-cost slope ◄──────────── Z-score uncertainty\n```\n\nThe seven steps of a full problem:\n\n1. Convert the raw PERT estimates (a, m, b) into **T_E** and **σ²** for each activity. *(ch. 03)*\n2. Build the **AON network** from the predecessor table. *(ch. 04)*\n3. Run the **forward and backward passes** for ES, EF, LS, LF, slack and the critical path. *(ch. 05)*\n4. Convert the network into a **Gantt chart** for tracking and milestones. *(ch. 06)*\n5. Apply material prices and labour rates across the timeline for the **time-phased budget**. *(ch. 07)*\n6. Evaluate schedule uncertainty with **σ_p and a Z-score**. *(ch. 10)*\n7. Shorten the schedule with **least-cost crashing** on critical activities. *(ch. 09)*\n\n**The dependency is one-directional and unforgiving.** Step 3 feeds steps 4 through 7. If the forward pass\nis wrong, the critical path is wrong, so the budget timing, the variance sum and the crashing choice are\nall wrong with it. In a long exam problem, **check the CPM table before going on** — both slack formulas\nagreeing is a free verification.\n\n## The verbal trap\n\nThe week-15 notes single this out. A sponsor asks: *\"Can we shorten this project by 3 weeks?\"*\n\n```\n                      sponsor asks for 3 weeks\n                                 │\n              ┌──────────────────┴──────────────────┐\n              ▼                                     ▼\n    ✗ WRONG                                ✓ CORRECT\n    \"How much are you                      Calculate the exact\n     willing to pay?\"                      cost and resources\n                                                    │\n                                                    ▼\n                                      \"Yes — it needs X extra cost\n                                       and Y extra resources.\n                                       Are you ready to approve it?\"\n```\n\n**Never answer with \"how much are you willing to pay?\"** It sounds like negotiation but it is an admission\nthat you have not done the analysis. The correct sequence:\n\n1. Check whether **technical and legal constraints** physically allow the crash at all. (Concrete still has\n   to cure.)\n2. Identify the **critical path activities** and apply the **least-cost method**.\n3. Calculate the **exact extra cost** and the resources it buys — overtime hours, extra crew, equipment.\n4. Present the number: *\"Yes, we can shorten the project by 3 weeks. It requires X extra cost using Y\n   additional resources. Are you prepared to approve this budget increase?\"*\n\nThe underlying principle is the iron triangle from chapter 01. You are not refusing; you are pricing the\ntrade between time and cost, and the sponsor decides.\n\n## Exam logistics\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing** | **Pencils and erasers only. Pens are forbidden.** Forward and backward passes need correcting as you work, and pen produces a messy script that loses marks. |\n| **Calculator** | A standalone **non-programmable scientific calculator**. Phones and smart devices are prohibited. |\n| **Provided** | **Standard Z-score tables** come with the paper. |\n| **Scope** | Weighted toward **weeks 8–15**, while still assuming the network fundamentals from weeks 5–7. |\n\n## Exam structure\n\n**Theory questions** — PM definitions, Gantt versus network, risk response strategies, the quality and\nvariance principle.\n\n**Practical problems** — one or two comprehensive ones, requiring some combination of:\n- constructing the network diagram,\n- forward and backward passes,\n- slack and critical path identification,\n- PERT estimation,\n- crashing slope selection,\n- Z-score probability lookup.\n\n**Multiple choice warning.** The instructor has previously used deliberate answer-key patterns (A-B-C-D,\nA-B-C-B-D) and warns that patterns may be reused to detect irregular test-taking. If multiple choice\nappears, per-question time limits may be shortened to prevent collaboration during any \"extra minute\".\n\nThe practical implication is dull but real: **do not pattern-guess**, and expect to work at pace.\n\n## Formula sheet\n\n| Quantity | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| PERT expected duration | **T_E = (a + 4m + b) / 6** |\n| Triangular estimate | **E = (a + m + b) / 3** |\n| Activity variance | **σ² = ((b − a) / 6)²** |\n| Activity standard deviation | **σ = (b − a) / 6** |\n| Forward pass | **ES = max(EF of predecessors)**, **EF = ES + T_E** |\n| Backward pass | **LF = min(LS of successors)**, **LS = LF − T_E** |\n| Slack | **Slack = LS − ES = LF − EF** |\n| Crash cost slope | **Slope = (C₂ − C₁) / (T₁ − T₂)** |\n| Project variance | **σ²_p = Σ σ²_critical** |\n| Project standard deviation | **σ_p = √(σ²_p)** |\n| Z-score | **Z = (T_s − T_E) / σ_p** |\n| Target for a confidence level | **T_s = T_E + Z · σ_p** |\n\n**The four places to lose marks**, collected from the chapters:\n\n1. **max on the backward pass** instead of min. *(ch. 05)*\n2. **(T₂ − T₁)** in the slope denominator, giving a negative slope. *(ch. 09)*\n3. **Adding non-critical variances** into σ²_p. *(ch. 10)*\n4. **Not recomputing the critical path** after a crash. *(ch. 09)*\n\n## Verification note\n\nNo new calculations in this chapter. The formula sheet was checked against the chapters it summarises, and\nthe final row (T_s = T_E + Z·σ_p) is a rearrangement added during ingest rather than a formula from the\nsource.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"List the seven steps of a full integrated problem.","a":"PERT T_E and σ² → AON network → forward/backward pass → Gantt chart → time-phased budget → σ_p and Z-score → least-cost crashing."},{"q":"Why is the CPM table worth double-checking before moving on?","a":"The budget timing, variance sum and crashing choice all depend on it, so one pass error propagates into every later answer."},{"q":"What is the free verification available on a CPM table?","a":"LS − ES and LF − EF must give the same slack for every activity."},{"q":"A sponsor asks whether the project can be shortened by 3 weeks. What must you not say?","a":"\"How much are you willing to pay?\" — it admits the analysis has not been done."},{"q":"What is the correct response sequence to that question?","a":"Check technical and legal feasibility, apply least-cost crashing to critical activities, calculate the exact extra cost and resources, then present the figure for approval."},{"q":"What principle underlies the correct answer?","a":"The iron triangle — you are pricing the time-cost trade and letting the sponsor decide."},{"q":"What writing implement is required in the exam, and why?","a":"Pencil and eraser only; network passes need correcting as you work and pen produces a messy script."},{"q":"What calculator is permitted?","a":"A standalone non-programmable scientific calculator; phones and smart devices are banned."},{"q":"What reference material is supplied?","a":"Standard Z-score tables."},{"q":"What is the exam's scope?","a":"Weighted toward weeks 8–15, while still assuming the network fundamentals of weeks 5–7."},{"q":"What two question types appear?","a":"Theory questions on definitions and comparisons, plus one or two comprehensive practical problems."},{"q":"What is the warning about multiple-choice questions?","a":"Deliberate answer-key patterns have been used before and may be reused to detect irregular behaviour; per-question time limits may be shortened."},{"q":"Name the four most common calculation errors in this course.","a":"Max instead of min on the backward pass; reversed time order in the crash slope; adding non-critical variances; failing to recompute the critical path after a crash."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 11"},{"id":"axp","title":"Exam Prep: Question Types & Drills","slides":"2025/26 · the one problem to master","md":"# IE408 — Exam Prep · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n⚠️ **Built from previous-semester material.** The exam instructions below are what the 2025/26 instructor\nreportedly said. The formulas are standard and safe; the logistics and scope are not confirmed for 2026/27.\nTreat this as a strong prior, not a syllabus.\n\n## What the exam reportedly looks like\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Scope | Weighted toward **weeks 8–15**, assuming the network fundamentals of weeks 5–7 |\n| Theory | Definitions, Gantt versus network, risk strategies, quality and variance |\n| Practical | **One or two comprehensive problems** combining diagram, passes, slack, PERT, crashing and Z-score |\n| Writing | **Pencil and eraser only — pens forbidden** |\n| Calculator | Standalone **non-programmable scientific** calculator |\n| Supplied | **Z-score tables** |\n| Multiple choice | Answer-key patterns have been used deliberately; per-question time may be shortened |\n\n## The one problem to master\n\nAlmost everything calculable in this course is built on a single network. Learn it cold and you have\ncovered chapters 03, 05, 07, 09 and 10 at once.\n\n| Activity | Predecessors | T_E |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | none | 2 |\n| B | none | 3 |\n| C | A | 2 |\n| D | A, B | 4 |\n| E | C | 4 |\n| F | C | 3 |\n| G | D, E | 5 |\n| H | F, G | 2 |\n\n**Answers to check yourself against** (all verified during ingest):\n\n- Critical path **A → C → E → G → H**, duration **15 months**.\n- Slack: A, C, E, G, H = 0; B and D = 1; **F = 6**.\n- σ²_p = 3.11, σ_p = 1.76; **P(≤16) = 71.57%**, **P(≤13) = 12.71%**.\n- First crash **A at 750 KM**; after it, **two critical paths**, and the second month is cheapest from\n  **H at 3,000 KM**.\n- Budget **216,000 KM** over 15 months.\n\nPractise by regenerating the entire CPM table from the predecessor column alone, with no peeking. If you\ncan do that in under five minutes you are ready for the practical half.\n\n## Likely question types\n\n### Theory\n\n1. **Name the five process phases** and say which is not sequential. *(ch. 01)*\n2. **The iron triangle** — name the three constraints and state the interdependence. *(ch. 01)*\n3. **Gantt versus network diagram** — the comparison table. **The most predictable question in the\n   course.** Know purpose, phase, dependency treatment, progress treatment, milestones. *(ch. 06)*\n4. **Quality as inverse variance** — state Quality ∝ 1/σ² and explain why a consistently mediocre process\n   scores well. *(ch. 02)*\n5. **The eight dimensions of quality**, with reliability (MTBF) and serviceability (MTTR) distinguished\n   from durability. *(ch. 02)*\n6. **The seven risk responses**, four for threats and three for opportunities, with the avoid/mitigate\n   distinction and an example each. *(ch. 08)*\n7. **Qualitative versus quantitative risk analysis** and the two tools on each side. *(ch. 08)*\n8. **Dummy activities** — what they are, why zero duration, which convention needs them. *(ch. 04)*\n9. **Beta versus triangular** — which is used for time, which for cost, and why. *(ch. 03)*\n10. **The verbal trap** — what to say when a sponsor asks for three weeks. *(ch. 11)*\n\n### Practical\n\n11. **Compute T_E and σ² from a/m/b.** Usually the first part of a long problem. *(ch. 03)*\n12. **Draw the AON network from a predecessor table.** Single start, single end, no crossings. *(ch. 04)*\n13. **Full forward and backward pass**, then slack and the critical path. *(ch. 05)*\n14. **Convert to a Gantt chart**, marking milestones as diamonds and slack as a trailing gap. *(ch. 06)*\n15. **Build a time-phased budget**, monthly total = labour + materials, then a running cumulative. *(ch. 07)*\n16. **Compute σ_p and a Z-score**, then read the table for P(T ≤ T_s). *(ch. 10)*\n17. **Compute crash slopes and pick the activity**, respecting critical-path eligibility and crash limits.\n    *(ch. 09)*\n18. **Handle parallel critical paths** — compare a shared activity against one-from-each-path. *(ch. 09)*\n\n## Weak-spot drills\n\n**Do these with a pencil, not by reading.**\n\n1. **Regenerate the CPM table** from the predecessor list, from memory, in under five minutes. Repeat until\n   both passes need no thought. This is the highest-value drill in the course.\n2. **Deliberately make the backward-pass error** once — use max instead of min — and watch what it does to\n   C's slack. Seeing the failure makes the rule stick.\n3. **Crash the network twice in a row**, recomputing all four path lengths after each crash. The second\n   crash is where the trap lives.\n4. **Compute a Z-score both ways**, rounding σ_p first and not rounding, and confirm you can produce the\n   instructor's 12.71% on demand.\n5. **Write the seven risk strategies** from memory in two columns, threats and opportunities, with one\n   example each. Thirty seconds, full marks.\n6. **Reproduce the Gantt versus network table** from memory, six rows.\n\n## The four marks-losing errors\n\n| # | Error | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **max** instead of **min** on the backward pass | ch. 05 |\n| 2 | **(T₂ − T₁)** in the crash slope denominator, giving a negative slope | ch. 09 |\n| 3 | Adding **non-critical** variances into σ²_p | ch. 10 |\n| 4 | Not **recomputing the critical path** after a crash | ch. 09 |\n\n## Known problems in the source material\n\nFound while recomputing everything during ingest. All but one were transcription or consistency issues;\none is a genuine numerical fork.\n\n| Where | Issue |\n|---|---|\n| ch. 05 activity table | Every a = m = b, so **all variances are zero**. It is a pass-practice table, not a statistics one. |\n| ch. 10 variances | Uses **different, non-zero** variances for the same activity letters. The two examples are not the same data set. |\n| **ch. 10 scenario B** | **σ_p = √3.11 = 1.7635.** Rounding to 1.76 first gives Z = −1.14 → **12.71%** (the instructor's answer). Full precision gives Z = −1.13 → **12.92%**. Follow the instructor: round σ_p to two decimals first. |\n| ch. 02 dimension 4 | \"Operational operational lifespan\" — duplicated word in the source. |\n| ch. 08 tool lists | Numbering restarts mid-list in the source; the digest reorganises it. |\n\n## What is still unknown\n\nAsk at the first lecture, or wait for the syllabus:\n\n- [ ] **Grading breakdown** — no weights appear anywhere in the source.\n- [ ] **Quiz, midterm and final dates.**\n- [ ] Whether there is a **project or homework** component.\n- [ ] The **textbook**, if any.\n- [ ] Whether the **weeks 8–15 exam scope** still holds this year.\n- [ ] Whether the **pencil-only rule** still holds.\n","lesson":"","cards":[],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 XP"},{"id":"axf","title":"Flashcard Deck Map","slides":"2025/26 · 159 cards, how to drill them","md":"# IE408 Flashcards — deck index · ARCHIVE 2025/26\n\n_Cards live inside their own chapter files and the site reads them from there. This file is the map only —\nedit a card where it lives so there is never a second copy to keep correct._\n\n⚠️ These cards come from **previous-semester** material. The formulas and methods are standard project\nmanagement and will not change, but anything attributed to the instructor should be reconfirmed against\nthis year's lectures.\n\n## Deck map (159 cards)\n\n| Deck | File | Cards | Type |\n|------|------|-------|------|\n| 01 Process framework and scope | `01-process-framework-and-scope.md` | 12 | theory |\n| 02 Quality and variance | `02-quality-and-variance.md` | 12 | theory |\n| 03 PERT estimation | `03-pert-estimation.md` | 14 | ★ calculation |\n| 04 Network diagramming | `04-network-diagramming.md` | 13 | theory + drawing |\n| 05 Critical path method | `05-critical-path-method.md` | 15 | ★ calculation |\n| 06 Gantt and tracking | `06-gantt-and-tracking.md` | 12 | theory |\n| 07 Resources and budgeting | `07-resources-and-budgeting.md` | 14 | ★ calculation |\n| 08 Risk management | `08-risk-management.md` | 17 | theory |\n| 09 Crashing | `09-crashing.md` | 21 | ★ calculation |\n| 10 Schedule uncertainty | `10-schedule-uncertainty.md` | 16 | ★ calculation |\n| 11 Synthesis and exam rules | `11-synthesis-and-exam-rules.md` | 13 | theory + logistics |\n\n★ = a deck you must be able to *do*, not just recite. Reciting the crash slope formula is worth nothing if\nyou cannot pick the right activity to apply it to.\n\n## How to drill this course\n\nThe split is roughly 80 theory cards and 79 calculation cards, but they need completely different practice:\n\n- **Theory decks (01, 02, 04, 06, 08, 11)** respond to ordinary spaced repetition. Ask, answer, correct.\n  The highest-value ones are **08 risk** (seven response strategies, easy marks) and **06 Gantt versus\n  network** (the single most predictable theory question).\n- **Calculation decks (03, 05, 07, 09, 10)** need worked practice with pencil and paper, not recall.\n  Redo the chapter-05 network from the predecessor table until both passes are automatic, then layer the\n  budget, the Z-score and the crashing on top of it. It is one network; the whole course hangs off it.\n\n**If time is short**, chapter 05 first. Everything downstream inherits its numbers, and an error there\ncosts you every later part of a long problem.\n\n## The four error cards — drill these until they are reflexes\n\n1. Forward pass takes **max**; backward pass takes **min**.\n2. Crash slope denominator is **T₁ − T₂**, old minus new.\n3. Project variance sums **critical path activities only**.\n4. **Recompute the critical path after every crash.**\n","lesson":"","cards":[],"figs":[],"slideText":[],"track":"2025","tag":"’25 XF"}],"deliverables":[]};
